\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 A weakened West is imitating Chinese  \highlight22\uc2 interventionism \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,077 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's Chinese regime is waging an extraordinary attack on the country's biggest tech companies.\par \par \uc2 E-commerce giant Alibaba has been fined USD 2.8 billion for insisting on exclusive deals with sellers on its platform. The new regulatory rules propose to block user-targeted pricing and force tech platforms to host their competitors, while other companies are simply being told: reduce your market share.\par \par \uc2 That's not all. A new privacy law will control data collection by digital companies, thus leaving the Chinese Communist Party (CCP) itself with data powers. Companies that have sensitive information about their users will be banned from making listings on stock exchanges in foreign countries, limiting the companies' potential growth.\par \par \uc2 Western experts have been shocked by the extent of this attack to reassert government control over technology. But perhaps they should not be so surprised. One notorious aspect of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against Chinese digital platforms, delivery companies, and educational apps is how it reflects the unchecked, authoritarian impulse of the Western antitrust ethos: "big is bad."\par \par \uc2 For years, academics, antitrust lawyers, and politicians have said that \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , Apple, and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  are similarly too big and too powerful. Proposed remedies to that "problem," under the guise of encouraging competition, have included enforcing openness of platforms, tougher pricing rules, breaking up companies, and restrictions on data use.\par \par \uc2 Our existing laws and institutions have curtailed the worst of these demands. But this populist narrative gave rise to the antitrust agenda against "Big Tech" in the US and the digital markets drive in the UK. Unfettered by good manners, China has taken that reasoning further.\par \par \uc2 This example is illustrative of a broader pattern. For all the hands raised in protest at a "repressive" China in policy, in area after area of public policy, our climate of ideas increasingly resembles theirs.\par \par \uc2 Historian Niall Ferguson has noted how Ronald Reagan and \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  addressed the Soviet threat with security around the superiority of open economies, market-led innovation, and commitments to free speech. The left and the right today may point to China today as a geo-strategic and economic enemy. The difference? They seem to believe that replicating Chinese policy is what is required to achieve Western success.\par \par \uc2 Our leaders used to condemn Chinese practices of subsidizing industries, trade protectionism, and state-granted corporate privileges. Yet now we copy them: Congress recently authorized direct subsidies for plants that make semi-conductors, while \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has upheld Donald Trump's tariffs. Boris Johnson has promised a state-backed "green industrial revolution," with state subsidies for electric vehicles, renewable energy, and greening aviation technologies. So much for the power of markets over planning!\par \par \uc2 Chinese research and development efforts have been used as justification for huge increases in science budgets on both sides of the Atlantic. The Integrated Review of Security, Defence, Development and Foreign Policy endorsed the UK becoming 'a science and technology superpower' in part by invoking growing Chinese capabilities.\par \par \uc2 In infrastructure, Western nations seem ready to copy China at home and abroad as well. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Boris Johnson see transportation megaprojects as key to "building back better" and "moving to the next level." The G7 even committed to the "Build Back Better for the World" initiative in July as a "values-led" alternative to the transcontinental "\uc2 Belt and Road Initiative\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 But the imitation goes beyond economic development policies. China is pushing out its electronic renminbi - a digital \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and designed to expand state oversight and usurp payment systems built by tech companies. Now \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  are considering their own "BritCoin," urged by commentators who are worried about the "wild west" of private \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Hear that echo?\par \par \uc2 Yes, few in the West are in favor of totally copying China with a dystopian system of social credit and extensive identity verification. Also, we haven't given up freedoms in our lifestyles to the point that kids are banned from playing online video games beyond three-hour blocks on weekends, as China has recently done.\par \par \uc2 But paternalistic moralizing is on the rise, with our government increasingly willing to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  with bans and nudges for our own good. Where \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  calls video games "the opium of the minds", our politicians call fixed-odds betting terminals "the crack cocaine of gambling". Boris Johnson's government is banning "junk food" commercials before 9pm and implementing a code that, in the name of protecting children, will require websites to put age-verification filters on much benign content.\par \par \uc2 Epidemiologist Neil Ferguson even thinks China's actions facilitated the Western COVID-19 isolations. "If China hadn't done it, the year would have been very different," he said in December. True or not, the disdain for Chinese-style civil liberties is growing. The resolve to implement vaccine passports has strengthened even as cases of COVID-19 transmission among vaccinees weakens his argument.\par \par \uc2 Not so long ago, the Western response to economic planning and lifestyle controls in China would have been to proclaim the virtues of freedom.\par \par \uc2 We would defend Western digital platforms that have sharply reduced advertising costs, given users free content, and facilitated new competitive markets.\par \par \uc2 We would worry about the slippery slope of "national security" exceptions to free trade and point out that an industrial policy heavily concentrated on manufacturing is destined to fail, with overcapacity and underinvestment the inevitable consequence of state-led attempts to second-guess technologies and consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 A confident West would highlight how politician-led infrastructure produces the ghost cities and bridges that lead to nowhere in China. We would note that patents granted per unit of research and development investment are three times higher for private Chinese companies than for state-owned Chinese companies, reminding us that state-owned research crowds out more productive private efforts. And we would decry the chilling effects on entrepreneurship of impulsive prohibitions and dehumanizing paternalism.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is that today's intellectuals and politicians are generally unsure about the principles that have left us with a GDP per capita that is four times that of China. So the response to its rise is to defend capitalism with diluted Chinese characteristics.\par \par \uc2 This article was originally published in UK Telegraph (UK) on September 2, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Bourne\par \par \uc2 is R. Evan Scharf Professor for the Public Understanding of Economics at the \uc2 Cato Institute\uc2 . Tags: Chinese communismUnited StatesopinionXi Jinping\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210930eh9t00116\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscale