\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 US plans to create task force at State Department to monitor China\b0\par\par\uc2 1,482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 This is the China, land of the middle edition of the newsletter. Want to receive it every Friday in your email? Sign up below.\par \par \uc2 Follow the content about China on the blog and at folha.com/china\par \par \uc2 . China, Middle Earth Get the big issues in China explained and contextualized in your email; exclusive for subscribers\par \par \uc2 . Loading...\par \par \uc2 This week, a company hitherto unknown to most Brazilians entered the world media lexicon: Evergrande. The company was founded in 1996 in Guangzhou, southern China, and the founder, \uc2 Xu Jiayin\uc2 , was on the list of the ten richest men in China for years. Evergrande's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  consisted of mapping and acquiring land, developing large construction projects and using the buildings themselves as payment to the lot owners, while the remaining units were sold to finance the continuation of the work.\par \par \uc2 The megalomania became apparent as the company sank into debt. It had trouble selling the additional units it built. Thus it generated letters of credit with promises of interest far above market rates, erected more and more buildings to settle old debts (leaving more losses for the next project) and accumulated liabilities that today are estimated to reach US$300 billion, equivalent to almost 25% of Brazil's entire GDP last year.\par \par \uc2 According to Caixin magazine, the company developed several methods to hide its debts. It disguised debts as acquisitions and unpaid shares, manipulated balance sheets and signed loan contracts with high interest rates abroad to make up the annual cash flow.\par \par \uc2 Evergrande residential buildings under construction in Jiangsu province. - Aly Song/Reuters The\par \par \uc2 Chinese government is groping to gauge the size of the problem. Markets despaired at the possibility of a cascading effect on the world economy similar to the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in the United States in 2008. In China, regulators maintained a cautious silence. Local analysts speculate that the government has no idea of the extent of the problem and is groping for possible solutions that protect the market but send a signal of austerity and low tolerance for repeating the mistakes made by Evergrande. A partial response came on Wednesday (22). The \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced an injection of \u165\'00\'A5120 billion (about R$98.4 billion) into the banking system in reverse repurchase agreements (when the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues bonds linked to an agreement to buy them back in the future at a pre-fixed price). The company has also announced the payment of interests on a bond due, in the value of US$ 35,9 million. The measures seem to have calmed the mood in the stock market and there is speculation even about the possibility of nationalizing the company. However, the risk of bankruptcy remains, and the Chinese government has asked local governments to prepare for the developer's collapse.\par \par \uc2 Why it matters: The collapse of Evergrande does not represent as big a risk to the global financial system as \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  did. The Chinese economy is not very integrated with the rest of the world, the renminbi (the national \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is not totally convertible to dollars and the political system is capable of containing, at least in the short term, the fury of those who were defrauded (there are reports of protesters arrested and monitored by local police). This does not mean, however, that Brazil is immune to risks if China lets the developer go bankrupt. As Evergrande has over 1,300 projects and almost 160,000 direct employees, the possible collapse of the company will certainly take away a slice of the Chinese GDP. The slowdown in the construction sector, responsible for 25% of the national GDP, can also be reflected in Brazilian sales of iron ore, which represent 32% of exports to China last year (equivalent to US$ 20.5 billion).\par \par \uc2 what also matters \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  speaks in a video recorded for the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2 . - Huang Jingwen/Xinhua \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  does not usually address the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2  (the first and only time was in 2015). However, with the pandemic and the possibility of sending recorded speeches, the Chinese leader attended this year's edition with bold promises. The biggest one was a pledge not to fund new coal plants overseas. "China will increase support for other developing countries in producing green and low-carbon energy and will not build new coal-fired power projects overseas," Xi said. This is a grand statement. To comply with the limits negotiated in the Paris Agreement, China has begun to reduce fossil fuel use in its domestic energy mix, but compensated by building plants in nearby countries within the \uc2 Belt and Road Initiative\uc2 . It is estimated that 70 percent of all coal plants built globally have been at least partially funded by the Chinese. If implemented, the announcement could reduce 200 million tons of CO? emissions annually, but at the same time generate a loss of US$50 billion with the cancellation of more than 40 projects already underway.? The United States plans to create a task force and hire 20 to 30 State Department employees dedicated exclusively to monitoring China. The news was published by Foreign Policy magazine, which revealed the intention of American diplomacy to also increase the number of officers dedicated to the topic in various embassies. They would be in charge of observing how Beijing relates to other countries. According to the publication, the initiative, which has been nicknamed "China House," will guide diplomatic cooperation and military relations between the two nations. Initially, the State Department hopes to select officials who already work on issues related to China in other government agencies. The idea, however, is not unanimous internally. A similar initiative was gestated in the Trump administration in 2019. At the time, former State Department East Asia envoy \uc2 Susan Thornton\uc2  said that deploying officials to report on Chinese multilateral ties in various parts of the world would give rise to "all sorts of exaggerations and distortions about Chinese activities." She left the post, but talks continued, and the CIA itself began to structure a specific team to deal with Chinese espionage.?\par \par \uc2 keep an eye on The Saihan Tower of Saihanba National Park, located in north China's Hebei province. - Xinhua/Jin Haoyuan The week in China was marked by the celebration of the Mid-Autumn Festival, an important date in the national tourism industry calendar. Data revealed by CTrip platform, the Chinese version of \uc2 Trip.com\uc2 , shows that travel was positive, although the pandemic continues to impact people's leisure decisions. According to the portal, hotel bookings grew 20% over 2019, and car rentals, 77%. Still, 56% of all travel occurred close to tourists' homes, an indication of the population's wariness of further Covid outbreaks ravaging specific parts of the country. Why it matters:\par \par \uc2 When projecting what the post-pandemic world should look like, several tourism companies look to China. Perhaps the comparison is no longer as useful, considering that the country remains closed and with a zero-tolerance policy toward Covid cases, while the rest of the world is moving toward an easing. However, the sign that people remain fearful of displacement even when the pandemic is virtually no longer an issue shows how difficult it will be to negotiate a return of tourism to pre-Covid standards. to dig deeper \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  prides itself on non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a basic principle of its diplomacy. In this article for Foreign Affairs, Wilson Center researcher Charles Edel and former U.S. National Intelligence Council official David O. Shullman question this position and argue that China may be directly or indirectly financing the emergence of less democratic regimes and the breakdown of institutions. (porous paywall) New regulations, cancellation of IPOs, celebrities arrested, control of video game use... The news coming out of China this year indicates a resurgence in several areas of daily life in the country. In this article, the Wall Street Journal argues that this may all be a reflection of \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's attempt to restore the nation's core socialist values and create an entirely new alternative to Western capitalism. (free, English) The Chinese fast fashion giant Shein is a worldwide phenomenon, selling exclusive pieces at prices far lower than those charged by renowned brands. But all this has a cost: Sixth Tone reports on the terrible working conditions of the seamstresses who produce these clothes, subjected to minimal commissions, long hours and unhealthy work spaces. (free, in English)? In commemoration of the 72nd anniversary of the founding of the People's Republic of China, the Chinese Embassy in Brazil will hold an online event with performances by the Beijing Dance Academy and the Parais\u243\'00\'F3polis Ballet. This year's theme is "Hearts Together, Ties United," and the performance can be followed live this Saturday (25) at 11 a.m. on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . (free, in Portuguese)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210925eh9p004ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
