\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar choppy after Fed statement, Evergrande exhale lifts risk-sensitive  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par 21:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Updates with Fed policy statement)\par     By Chuck Mikolajczak\par     NEW YORK, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar was choppy on the heels of the latest policy announcement\par by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Wednesday, while risk-sensitive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Australian dollar and\par Chinese yuan strengthened after China's Evergrande said it would make a bond coupon payment. \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Wednesday cleared the way to reduce its monthly bond purchases "soon" and\par signaled interest rate increases may follow more quickly than expected, with half of the 18 U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policymakers projecting borrowing costs will need to rise in 2022.\par     "The tapering of quantitative easing seems very likely now in November but this was something of a given\par and remains couched in a lot of qualifying criteria in the event that various risks emerge, whether it is\par the debt ceiling debate, COVID outlook, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  property market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Steven Violin,\par portfolio manager at F.L.Putnam Investment Management Company in Wellesley, Massachusetts.\par     The dollar index rose 0.094%, alternating between gains and declines after the announcement, with\par the euro down 0.1% to $1.1711.\par     Property giant and Asia's biggest junk bond issuer Evergrande said it "resolved" one payment\par due on Thursday via a private negotiation, easing concerns of default and possible contagion risk, while the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  injected 90 billion yuan into the banking system to support markets. \par     "Being able to make tomorrow's bond coupon payment, that definitely lifted risk sentiment overnight and\par you saw a typical follow-through reaction in risk \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , so Canadian dollar high, Aussie dollar higher,\par Kiwi dollar higher - that was kind of an understandable reaction," said Erik Bregar, an independent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par analyst in Toronto. \par     Still, uncertainty remains whether the developer will be able to pay the coupon on its offshore dollar\par bonds, due on Thursday. \par     The Australian dollar rose 0.33% versus the greenback to $0.726 after rising as much as 0.49% to\par $0.7268 while the Canadian dollar rose 0.58% versus the greenback to 1.27 per dollar.\par     The offshore Chinese yuan strengthened versus the greenback to 6.4628 per dollar.\par     The safe-haven Japanese yen weakened 0.50% versus the greenback to 109.78 per dollar in the wake of the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's decision to keep policy on hold.\par     Sterling was last trading at $1.3637, down 0.16% on the day ahead of a policy announcement by the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Thursday, with expectations for a rate hike being pushed down the road by investors.    \par     In cryptocurrencies, Bitcoin last rose 6.93% to $43,409.48 following three straight days of\par declines. \par     \par ========================================================\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 2:41PM (1841 GMT)\par  Description      RIC         Last           U.S. Close  Pct Change     YTD Pct       High Bid    Low Bid\par                 