\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar choppy after Fed statement, Evergrande exhale lifts risk-sensitive  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 473 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2021\par 21:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates with Fed policy statement)\par \par \uc2 By Chuck Mikolajczak\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar was choppy on the heels of the latest policy announcement by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  on Wednesday, while risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian dollar and Chinese yuan strengthened after China's Evergrande said it would make a bond coupon payment.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  on Wednesday cleared the way to reduce its monthly bond purchases "soon" and signaled interest rate increases may follow more quickly than expected, with half of the 18 U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers projecting borrowing costs will need to rise in 2022.\par \par \uc2 "The tapering of quantitative easing seems very likely now in November but this was something of a given and remains couched in a lot of qualifying criteria in the event that various risks emerge, whether it is the debt ceiling debate, COVID outlook, the \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  property market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Steven Violin, portfolio manager at F.L.Putnam Investment Management Company in Wellesley, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index rose 0.094%, alternating between gains and declines after the announcement, with the euro down 0.1% to $1.1711.\par \par \uc2 Property giant and Asia's biggest junk bond issuer Evergrande said it "resolved" one payment due on Thursday via a private negotiation, easing concerns of default and possible contagion risk, while the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  injected 90 billion yuan into the banking system to support markets.\par \par \uc2 "Being able to make tomorrow's bond coupon payment, that definitely lifted risk sentiment overnight and you saw a typical follow-through reaction in risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so Canadian dollar high, Aussie dollar higher, Kiwi dollar higher - that was kind of an understandable reaction," said Erik Bregar, an independent \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 Still, uncertainty remains whether the developer will be able to pay the coupon on its offshore dollar bonds, due on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar rose 0.33% versus the greenback to $0.726 after rising as much as 0.49% to $0.7268 while the Canadian dollar rose 0.58% versus the greenback to 1.27 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The offshore Chinese yuan strengthened versus the greenback to 6.4628 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The safe-haven Japanese yen weakened 0.50% versus the greenback to 109.78 per dollar in the wake of the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's decision to keep policy on hold.\par \par \uc2 Sterling was last trading at $1.3637, down 0.16% on the day ahead of a policy announcement by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  on Thursday, with expectations for a rate hike being pushed down the road by investors.\par \par \uc2 In cryptocurrencies, Bitcoin last rose 6.93% to $43,409.48 following three straight days of declines.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Chuck Mikolajczak; Editing by Will Dunham and Andrea Ricci) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-9-22T19:02:55.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210922eh9m00d9t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
