\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Curious: China also pushes for a deal with the Monetary Fund\b0\par\par\uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By Carlos Burgue\u241\'00\'F1o cburgueno@ambito.com.ar China demanded Argentina to agree with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ). The Government of Beijing committed to support the country in the board of the organization, but insisted Buenos Aires to close a treaty of Extended Facilities, and that this one respects the general lines that are already negotiated with the financial organization managed by Kristalina Georgieva.\par \par \uc2 The claim was curious, but not surprising, not to be disregarded or overlooked. And it also represents a sign of the current times, an important precedent for the country, according to the interpretation that was closed within the Executive. Without China as support in the event of a possible failure in the negotiations with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , there is no possible destination for Argentina without closing an Extended Credit Facility. The request of \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  's government has been reiterated since March of this year in the multiple private meetings that Argentine officials regularly hold (there are many) with their Chinese peers and colleagues, ranging from structural and financial issues to investments in telecommunications and major public works. In one of the latter, corresponding to a large investment in yuan in the energy sector, it was made clear to local private and public interlocutors (at the national level and with the province involved), that the realization of the release of yuan will depend on the closing of negotiations with the \uc2 IMF\par \par \uc2 . And, if this does not happen, China would negotiate a different value of the official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the one quoted daily on the shelves of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the National Bank. China's position in favor of an agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  had two important moments. Last May, during a videoconference, the president of the Chinese Parliament, \uc2 Li Zhanshu\uc2 , conveyed his country's support "to the negotiations with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 " for the renegotiation of the debt.\par \par \uc2 It was during a videoconference he held with the head of the Chamber of Deputies, Sergio Massa, and the head of the Frente de Todos bloc, M\u225\'00\'E1ximo Kirchner. There, \uc2 Li Zhanshu\uc2  openly asked the country to sign an "agreement with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 " that "contributes to Argentina's economic and financial stability. He also said that "the Chinese side supports Argentina's negotiations with the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  on the debt valued at 44 billion dollars, because this contributes to Argentina's economic and financial stability. The relevant authorities of both countries can keep in communication in this regard". This position on China and the agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2  was reiterated in another meeting, but without representatives of the Eastern government. During the tour of Buenos Aires , Jake Sullivan, Director of National Security and envoy of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's government to explore Argentina's local and international political position, was received, among others, by President \uc2 Alberto Fern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\uc2 , Minister of Economy \uc2 Mart\u237\'00\'EDn Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n\uc2 , Foreign Minister Felipe Sol\u225\'00\'E1, Minister of Productive Development Mat\u237\'00\'EDas Kulfas, Minister of Health Carla Vizzotti and the Secretary of Strategic Affairs of the Presidency of the Nation Gustavo B\u233\'00\'E9liz. In several of these meetings Argentina explained the strategy to close an agreement with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , while the American visitor spoke about the country's relationship with China. \uc2 Alberto Fernandez\uc2  himself replied in one of the meetings that China was asking for an agreement with the Fund as much as the rest of the developed countries, and that it had already committed to vote in favor on the \uc2 IMF\uc2  board. Sullivan took that information back to Washington.\par \par \uc2 Strategically, Chinese support is important. But in terms of the Fund's board it is not so important. China holds only 6.35% of the votes (6% for Japan, 5.87% for Germany, 4.85% for France and the same percentage for the UK). However, it is known that its pressure on other blocs is important, and that in general it forms a tandem with Russia and the Arab countries. Curiously, it is mentioned that in its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is flexible with the states it considers its allies, but somewhat harsh with those it sees as part of the general strategic alliance of the United States. This is what many Latin American countries, close to the US position, perceived. In the Argentine case, there could be a curiosity: the US and China could defend the country with the same vehemence at the moment of discussing whether or not to support the reloaded Extended Facilities that are currently being negotiated.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210826eh8q001a0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal600
