r  IDR US$         14,480         40          INR US$             74.29        0.02\par  MYR US$         4.15           0.007       TWD US$             27.908       -0.002\par  CNY US$         6.464          0.0078      HKD US$             7.7635       0.0012\par  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --     \par  Commodities                                                                 \par  Spot Gold       1,763.91       -14.265     Silver (Lon)        25.78        -0.3135\par  U.S. Gold Fut   1,763.6        -17.1       Brent Crude         74.54        -0.14\par  Iron Ore        CNY 1,153      -43         TRJCRB Index        -            -\par  TOCOM Rubber    JPY231         -1          \f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2  Copper          9,352        45.5\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --  \par     ** indicates closing price \par     All prices as of 18:21 GMT\par     \par     EQUITIES\par \par     GLOBAL - Global shares edged lower on Tuesday, as new coronavirus outbreaks in Asia\par vied with strong U.S. consumer confidence, and as investors speculated about whether\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  would accelerate its timetable to end easy monetary policy\par     MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan fell\par 4.16 points or 0.59 percent, to 699.86, as some countries re-imposed lockdowns to\par contain the spread of the Delta variant of COVID-19.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     NEW YORK - The S&P 500 hit a record high for the fourth straight session on\par Tuesday, helped by shares of heavyweight technology firms and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 , while an\par upbeat consumer confidence report set a positive tone for key jobs data at the end of\par the week.\par     U.S. consumer confidence increased in June to its highest level since the COVID-19\par pandemic started more than a year ago, bolstering expectations for strong economic\par growth in the second quarter.\par     At 13:10 p.m. ET, the Dow Jones Industrial Average was up 47.41 points, or\par 0.14%, at 34,330.68, the S&P 500 was up 4.65 points, or 0.11%, at 4,295.26. The\par Nasdaq Composite was up 26.57 points, or 0.18%, at 14,527.08, hitting an\par all-time high for the sixth consecutive session.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - European shares ended higher on Tuesday after data showed economic\par sentiment improved sharply in June, while Adidas lifted the German index\par with a share buyback plan.\par     The pan-European STOXX 600 closed 0.3% higher at 456.37 points, after data\par showed euro zone economic sentiment hit a 21-year high in June as a steady vaccination\par programme saw several economies reopen.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japanese shares slumped on Tuesday, with weaker cyclical stocks outweighing\par gains in technology firms, as outbreaks of the highly contagious COVID-19 variant Delta\par raised concerns about a hindrance to global economic recovery.\par     The Nikkei share average fell 0.81% to 28,812.61, while the broader Topix\par  lost 0.82% to 1,949.48.\par \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - China stocks fell on Tuesday on concerns the infectious Delta virus\par variant could assuage global economic recovery, while investors refrained from placing\par big bets ahead of U.S. jobs data, which could sway the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy\par outlook.\par     The blue-chip CSI300 index closed 1.2% lower at 5,190.54, snapping a\par five-day winning streak, while the Shanghai Composite Index shed 0.9% to\par 3,573.18.\par \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - Australian shares closed marginally lower on Tuesday, with real estate,\par miners, and energy stocks witnessing a slide, as increasing COVID-19 curbs across the\par country dented sentiment.\par     The S&P/ASX 200 index closed down 0.08% at 7,301.2, recovering from 0.9%\par drop during the session.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - South Korean shares ended lower on Tuesday, hit by worries that a more\par infectious strain of COVID-19, the Delta variant, might derail the economic recovery,\par while investors awaited U.S. data later in the week. The won gained, while the\par benchmark bond yield fell.\par     The KOSPI closed down 15.21 points, or 0.46%, at 3,286.68.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par     NEW YORK - The U.S. dollar rose on Tuesday to a one-week peak, on track for its\par largest single daily gain in about two weeks, as new coronavirus outbreaks threatened\par to derail a global economic recovery with the Australian dollar and the British pound\par leading losses.\par     A decline in risk appetite benefits the dollar as a safe haven.\par     In late morning trading in New York, the dollar index, a gauge of its value\par against six major rivals, rose 0.6% to 92.124, on course for its biggest percentage\par gain since mid-June.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - China's yuan eased against the dollar on Tuesday, though trading was\par stuck in an extremely tight range as many investors moved to the sidelines awaiting key\par U.S. economic data for clues on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy outlook\par     Prior to market opening on Tuesday, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 set the\par midpoint rate at a one-week high of 6.4567 per dollar, 11 pips firmer than\par the previous fix of 6.4578.\par     In the spot market, onshore yuan opened at 6.4583 per dollar and was\par changing hands at 6.4609 at midday, 47 pips weaker than the previous late session\par close.\par \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - The Australian and New Zealand dollars weakened on Tuesday after\par cautious investors cut down on risk amid concerns over rising COVID-19 cases and as the\par greenback strengthened on growing bets of higher U.S. interest rates.\par     The Aussie dollar weakened 0.1% to $0.7557, falling for a second straight\par session. The unit dropped as deep as $0.7552 earlier in the day, a level not seen since\par June 23.\par     The New Zealand dollar was off 0.1% at $0.7032.\par \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - South Korean shares ended lower on Tuesday, hit by worries that a more\par infectious strain of COVID-19, the Delta variant, might derail the economic recovery,\par while investors awaited U.S. data later in the week. The won gained, while the\par benchmark bond yield fell.\par     The won ended at 1,128.5 per dollar on the onshore settlement platform,\par 0.16% higher than its previous close at 1,130.3.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TREASURIES\par     \par     NEW YORK - \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields edged higher on Tuesday in range-bound trading as\par the market focused on the release later this week of the U.S. employment report for\par June and how it might affect the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's monetary policy stance.\par     The benchmark 10-year yield was last up less than a basis point at\par 1.4816%. It notched its largest weekly gain since March last week, but has remained\par below 1.6% since early June.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - \f28 \uc2 \f2 German government\f28 \uc2 \f2  bond yields rose on Tuesday after Europe's largest\par economy recorded inflation above the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target for the bloc for\par the second month running.\par     Germany's annual consumer price inflation eased to 2.1% on a harmonised basis in\par June from 2.4% in May but was still above the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target of close\par to but below 2%, the Federal Statistics Office said on Tuesday.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japanese government bond (JGB) yields were steady to lower on Tuesday after\par U.S. Treasuries yield dropped due to month-end buying by investors, while firm results\par of two-year JGB auction also underpinned the market.\par     The 10-year JGB yield fell 0.5 basis point to 0.050%, stuck in its\par recent trading range between 0.04% and 0.06% as investors looked for more clues on the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's policy guidance.\par     Benchmark 10-year JGB futures price rose 0.06 point to 151.71.\par \par     For a full report, click on\par  \par     \par     COMMODITIES\par     \par     GOLD - Gold fell to its lowest since mid-April on Tuesday as the dollar\par strengthened in the run-up to this week's U.S. jobs report, which is expected to come\par in strong and could cement the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 's recent hawkish stance.\par     Spot gold fell 0.93% to $1,761.66 per ounce by 01:39 pm EDT (1739 GMT),\par after touching $1,749.20, its lowest since April 15. U.S. gold futures settled\par down 1% at $1,763.60.\par \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     IRON ORE - Iron ore futures dropped on Tuesday, weighed down by easing steel demand\par in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  due to unfavourable weather and threats of market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Chinese\par authorities seeking to cool high prices.\par     The most-traded September iron ore on China's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dalian Commodity Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par ended daytime trading 2.7% lower at 1,153 yuan ($178.57) a tonne, after four sessions\par of gains.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     BASE METALS - Copper prices fell on Tuesday as rising coronavirus cases\par strengthened the dollar and cast doubt on a speedy rebound of some economies, while\par plentiful supply of the metal also sapped its appeal.\par     Three-month copper on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 London Metal Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2  shed 0.3% to $9,352 per\par tonne by 1651 GMT after falling as low as $9,236. This was the metal's fourth straight\par session of declines.\par \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     OIL - Oil prices rose on Tuesday as broad hopes for a demand recovery persisted,\par fueled by comments from OPEC's secretary general, overshadowed travel curbs due to new\par outbreaks of the highly contagious Delta variant of the coronavirus.\par     Brent crude futures were up 33 cents, or 0.4%, at $75.01 a barrel by 11:49\par a.m. EDT (1649 GMT), having slumped by 2% on Monday.\par     U.S. West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude futures rose 28 cents, or 0.4%, to\par $73.19 after a 1.5% retreat on Monday.\par \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     PALM OIL - Malaysian palm oil futures closed at a two-week peak on Tuesday,\par reversing early losses, buoyed by stronger soyoil and hopes of higher June export data.\par     The benchmark palm oil contract for September delivery on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bursa\par Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Derivatives Exchange closed up 46 ringgit, or 1.3%, to 3,552 ringgit ($855.90)\par a tonne.\par     \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     RUBBER - Japanese rubber futures lost over 2% on Tuesday on growing concerns over\par weaker demand as some countries re-imposed lockdowns to contain the spread of the\par highly infectious Delta variant of the COVID-19 virus.\par     Osaka Exchange's rubber contract for December delivery, finished\par 5.3 yen, or 2.2%, lower at 232.0 yen ($2.1) per kg.\par \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par \par  (Bengaluru Bureau; +91 80 6749 1130)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-6-29T19:36:50.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210629eh6t03fgh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
