\par\par \b \uc2 LIVE MARKETS-China's 'implied messages'\b0\par\par\uc2 1,352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2021\par 14:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * European shares little changed\par \par \uc2 * UK, U.S. markets closed for holiday\par \par \uc2 * Asian shares edged up\par \par \uc2 May 31 - Welcome to the home for real-time coverage of markets brought to you by Reuters reporters. You can share your thoughts with us at markets.research@thomsonreuters.com\par \par \uc2 CHINA'S 'IMPLIED MESSAGES' (1214 GMT)\par \par \uc2 U.S.-China tensions have been on the back burner since Joe Biden won the presidential elections in late 2020. Still, investors are fully aware that issues between the two countries are far from being over.\par \par \uc2 Here are some of China's "implied messages" extracted from official comments by \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  economists.\par \par \uc2 The rapid appreciation of China's yuan against the U.S. dollar may have overshot and will not be sustainable, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 "That looks a further jaw-boning attempt to shift market expectations away from the idea that Beijing is going to allow CNY to keep appreciating," \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  economists say in a research note. "In short, this looks like a form of '\b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  War', a long-running source of tension between different economies."\par \par \uc2 They also note that The South China Morning Post has flagged "China calls for global action to stop significant flows of pandemic-fuelled hot money".\par \par \uc2 "A key message here is that despite Western delight at post-virus re-opening and ludicrous levels of market liquidity, our global system is still no more stable," they argue.\par \par \uc2 A second implied message is this: can the U.S. stimulate without bubbles and global inflation emerging?\par \par \uc2 "If the U.S. goes the ultra-easy route, then China perhaps cannot: and obviously that has serious implications when both behemoths have a political growth imperative."\par \par \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  economists also quote the editor of The Global Times talking about China's task of increasing nuclear warheads.\par \par \uc2 (Stefano Rebaudo)\par \par \uc2 \f2 *****\par               \par               \par               \par ECONOMIC RECOVERY UPDATES (1028 GMT)\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While economists are updating their forecasts of the expected economic rebound, some of them flag overheating risks. But stagflation is seen as very unlikely.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Berenberg analysts see a possible above consensus recovery in the euro zone with minor risks attached.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe broad-based surge in economic sentiment in May to within striking distance of its all-time high of December 2017 tilts the risk to our above-consensus calls for growth of 4.5% in 2021 and 4.4% in 2022 to the upside,\u8221\'20\'1D they say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone looks set to "propel GDP well beyond its pre-pandemic level by early 2022 at the latest," they add.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Furthermore, "demand is outstripping supply. Shortages of semi-conductors and other critical supplies are hampering production in some sectors.\u8221\'20\'1D\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 They think temporary supply shortages may "force some firms to shift some production into the second half of this year \u8211\'20\'13 or potentially even into early next year.\u8221\'20\'1D\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But they do not expect these temporary bottlenecks \u8220\'20\'1Cto hold back the rebound from the pandemic for good.\u8221\'20\'1D\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Stefano Rebaudo)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *****\par               \par               \par               \par               \par EQUITY CONSOLIDATION\u8230\'20\'26 YES, BUT ANY RISKS? (0904 GMT)\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Whatever you call it, slight correction or consolidation phase, there is an increasing consensus that the sentiment on equities will stay subdued for a while.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But what about downside or upside risks?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 UBS Global Wealth Management expects the cyclical recovery to persist in the second half of the year, and it likes large-cap value stocks. At the same time, it sees a potential for continued outperformance by U.S. small-caps and mid-caps over large-caps, given their more cyclical nature.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts sound more cautious, but they think equities are more in danger of melting up than seeing a bear market in the next six months, as only three of its 15 characteristics of a bubble are in place.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, the key risks which might trigger a factual correction are 10-year inflation breakevens rising above 3%, 10-year bond yields rising above 2%, they say in a research note.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Along with other investment banks, they see a \u8216\'20\'18dovish taper\u8217\'20\'19 by the Fed; namely, they expect "taper talk will be highly cautious and conditional" and to occur after the summer.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe key risk we see is when rates actually start rising as opposed to when taper talk starts,\u8221\'20\'1D they add.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  expects a \u8220\'20\'1CU.S. 10-year bond yield of 2% eventually, given that growth and inflation are likely to surprise.\u8221\'20\'1D\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bottom line, they recommend \u8220\'20\'1Cbuying into weakness\u8221\'20\'1D as they confirm a year-end Global MSCI target of 780, implying a 10% upside potential.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chart shows U.S. 10-year Treasury yields range-bound since March, while inflation linked yield falls.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Stefano Rebaudo)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *****\par               \par               \par               \par               \par "IT'S A SNOOZEFEST" (0734 GMT)\par               \par "Meanwhile in markets, it's a snoozefest as US and UK\par markets are both closed today for holidays".\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's how AFS analyst Arne Petimezas kicks off the morning note and his words couldn't be more appropriate to describe how European equities look like in the first minutes of trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pan-regional STOXX 600 index is trading around parity, being last down 0.05%, and moves are similarly muted across other country benchmarks and sub-sectoral indices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Even among single stocks nothing really stands out and swings on the STOXX are confined between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Endesa\f28 \uc2 \f2 's -4% and the Elekta's +3% with both moving without any apparent catalyst.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Here's your snapshot:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Danilo Masoni)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ****\par               \par               \par               \par               \par YUAN BULLS STEAL THE SHOW  (0700 GMT)\par               \par With London and Wall Street closed for holiday action on\par markets will most likely remain confined to Asia.\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yuan in fact was the big mover overnight with the bulls pushing its value against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to a 5-year high and adding further pressure on exporters in the world's No.2 economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That spurred fresh verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by local policymakers as data showed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's factory activity growth may have peaked.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Joining those cautioning against excessive speculation was the former \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regulator Guan Tao who spoke of rising signs of "cyclical herding" in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. Against the dollar the yuan hit a fresh 3-year high before paring all gains for the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Trade in other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was relatively quiet with short bets against the dollar hitting a 2-1/2 month high. In equities, Asian shares gained but stayed within recent ranges and in Europe, indexes were set for a mild pull back after last week's record close. Benchmark bond yields too didn't look eager to break new ground with even volatile Bitcoin seemingly lifeless.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The quiet mood was reflection of the festivities but also of investors sitting on the sidelines before an action packed week for U.S. data ending with much-awaited U.S. May payrolls data on Friday. After April's shock, another disappointment could put to rest, for the summer, any lingering concerns the Fed could pull the taper lever on its massive stimulus earlier than expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finally, gold prices tested four month highs, supported by concerns in global inflation, revived last Friday by a stronger than expected rise in U.S. consumer price for April. Inflation watchers will also keep an eye on German CPI data due later on.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Key developments that should provide more direction to markets on Monday:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's factory activity grows at slightly slower pace as raw materials costs rise\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's April factory output rises on capital goods demand\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Riksbank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Stefan Ingves\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German preliminary CPI\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OECD Economic Outlook 2021\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Emerging markets: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meetings in Israel and Ghana\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Danilo Masoni)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *****\par               \par               \par               \par EUROPE SET FOR WEAKER START (0510 GMT)\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European shares are set to open down slightly this morning after closing at record levels last week thanks to gains in financial stocks and prospects of fiscal stimulus in the U.S..\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 United States and Britain are closed for a holiday and activity will most likely be subdued throughout the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Futures on the euro STOXX 50 and DAX indexes are both down around 0.3%, although Asian shares managed to post slight gains with MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan last up 0.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Danilo Masoni)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ***** \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-5-31T13:14:42.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210531eh5v01uwd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
