\par\par \b \uc2 LIVE MARKETS-Yuan bulls steal the show\b0\par\par\uc2 518 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2021\par 09:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * European shares down 0.1%\par \par \uc2 * Asian shares edge up\par \par \uc2 * UK, U.S. markets closed for holiday\par \par \uc2 May 31 - Welcome to the home for real-time coverage of markets brought to you by Reuters reporters. You can share your thoughts with us at markets.research@thomsonreuters.com\par \par \uc2 YUAN BULLS STEAL THE SHOW (0700 GMT)\par \par \uc2 With London and Wall Street closed for holiday action on markets will most likely remain confined to Asia.\par \par \uc2 The yuan in fact was the big mover overnight with the bulls pushing its value against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a 5-year high and adding further pressure on exporters in the world's No.2 economy.\par \par \uc2 That spurred fresh verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by local policymakers as data showed \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's factory activity growth may have peaked.\par \par \uc2 Joining those cautioning against excessive speculation was the former \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulator Guan Tao who spoke of rising signs of "cyclical herding" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Against the dollar the yuan hit a fresh 3-year high before paring all gains for the day.\par \par \uc2 Trade in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was relatively quiet with short bets against the dollar hitting a 2-1/2 month high. In equities, Asian shares gained but stayed within recent ranges and in Europe, indexes were set for a mild pull back after last week's record close. Benchmark bond yields too didn't look eager to break new ground with even volatile Bitcoin seemingly lifeless.\par \par \uc2 The quiet mood was reflection of the festivities but also of investors sitting on the sidelines before an action packed week for U.S. data ending with much-awaited U.S. May payrolls data on Friday. After April's shock, another disappointment could put to rest, for the summer, any lingering concerns the Fed could pull the taper lever on its massive stimulus earlier than expected.\par \par \uc2 Finally, gold prices tested four month highs, supported by concerns in global inflation, revived last Friday by a stronger than expected rise in U.S. consumer price for April. Inflation watchers will also keep an eye on German CPI data due later on.\par \par \uc2 Key developments that should provide more direction to markets on Monday:\par \par \uc2 China's factory activity grows at slightly slower pace as raw materials costs rise\par \par \uc2 Japan's April factory output rises on capital goods demand\par \par \uc2 Riksbank\uc2  Governor \uc2 Stefan Ingves\par \par \uc2 German preliminary CPI\par \par \uc2 OECD Economic Outlook 2021\par \par \uc2 Emerging markets: \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings in Israel and Ghana\par \par \uc2 (Danilo Masoni)\par \par \uc2 \f2 *****\par               \par               \par               \par EUROPE SET FOR WEAKER START (0510 GMT)\par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European shares are set to open down slightly this morning after closing at record levels last week thanks to gains in financial stocks and prospects of fiscal stimulus in the U.S..\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 United States and Britain are closed for a holiday and activity will most likely be subdued throughout the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Futures on the euro STOXX 50 and DAX indexes are both down around 0.3%, although Asian shares managed to post slight gains with MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan last up 0.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Danilo Masoni)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ***** \f28 \par \par \uc2 Releas