\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Countervailing fee validity review imposed on imports of steel cables originating in China\b0\par\par\uc2 29,168 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Mai 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 DOF: 28/05/2021\par \par \uc2 Final RESOLUTION of the administrative procedure for reviewing the validity of the countervailing quota imposed on imports of steel cables originating in the People's Republic of China, regardless of the country of origin.\par \par \uc2 Download PDF\par \par \uc2 On the sidelines a seal with the National Shield, which says: United Mexican States.- Secretariat of Economy.FINAL RESOLUTION OF THE ADMINISTRATIVE PROCEDURE OF EXAMINATION OF VALIDITY OF THE COMPENSATORY FEE IMPOSED ON IMPORTS OF STEEL CABLES ORIGINALLY FROM THE PEOPLE'S REPUBLIC OF CHINA, INDEPENDENTLY OF THE COUNTRY OF PROCEDENTVisto to resolve in the final stage the administrative file E.C. 17/19 based in the International Trade Practices Unit (UPCI) of the Secretariat of the Economy (the "Secretariat"), this Resolution is issued in accordance with the following RESULTSA. Final resolution of the anti-dumping investigation1. On 16 December 2014, the Final Resolution of the Anti-Dumping Investigation on Imports of Steel Cables originating in the People's Republic of China ("China"), regardless of the country of origin ("Final Resolution"), was published in the Official Journal of the Federation (DOF). The Resolution determined a final countervailing fee of US$2.58 (two point fifty-eight) per kilogram. B. Notice of the validity of compensatory fees2. On 11 September 2018, the Notice on the Validity of Compensatory Fees was published in the DOF.\u160\'00\'A0Domestic producers and anyone of legal interest were informed, by this means, that the final countervailing quotas imposed on the products listed in that Notice would be eliminated from the expiry date indicated therein for each, unless a domestic producer expressed in writing his interest in initiating an examination procedure. The list included steel cables originating in China, which was the subject of this review. C. Manifestation of Interest3. On 30 September and 31 October 2019, \uc2 Aceros Camesa, S.A. de C.V\uc2 . ("Camesa") and Deacero, S.A.P.I. de C.V. ("Deacero"), respectively, expressed their interest in the Secretariat initiating the examination of the validity of the final countervailing quota imposed on imports by steel cables originating in China. D. Resolution of initiation of the examination of the validity of the compensatory fee4. On 2 December 2019, the Secretariat published in the DOF the Resolution declaring the comlencement of the review of the validity of the countervailing quota imposed on imports of steel cables originating in China ("Initiation Resolution"). It was set as a review period from 1 July 2018 to 30 June 2019 and as an analysis period from 1 July 2014 to 30 June 2019. E. Product under examination1.\u160\'00\'A0Product Description5.\u160\'00\'A0The product under examination is called steel cables. In general, it is classified as: i. simple cables or torones, which are formed by a group of crooked steel wires, and ii. composite cables, formed by a core or central axis called "soul" with twisted torons around it. 2. Features6. The fundamental characteristics of the steel cable are: diameter, construction, soul or core, finished, steel grade and twisting. The construction specifies the number of torons on the cable, the number of wires per toron, and the geometric arrangement of the wires on the toron. The simplest cable is constructed with a 2-wire (1\u215\'00\'D72) toron. For example, a 19\u215\'00\'D77 cable consists of 19 torons of 7 wires each, a 6x19S cable consists of 6 torons of 19 wires each toron and geometrically arranged "Seale". Steel cable structureSource: Deacero and Camesa.\par \par \uc2 3. \u160\'00\'A0 Tariff treatment7. \u160\'00\'A0 The product under review enters the domestic market for tariff fractions 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99 of the Tariff Coding of the General Import and Export Taxes Act (TIGIE), the tariff description of which is as follows:Tariff codification Tariff codingdescriptionChapter 73Melry, iron or steel manufacturesPartible 7312Sables, braids, slings and similar items, iron or steel, sinaislar for electricity. Subheading 7312.10Cables.Tariff fraction 7312.10.01Galvanized, with diameter greater than 4 mm, consisting of more than 5 wires and with un crooked cores of the same matter, except those falling within fraction 7312.10.07.Fraction 7312.10.05Of uncoated steel, with or without lubrication, except those caught in fraction 7312.10.08.Fraction 7312.10.07Galvanized, with a diameter greater than 4 mm but less than 19 mm , consisting of 7 wires, lubricated or uns lubricated. Fraction 7312.10.99The others. Source: Internet Tariff Information System (SIAVI).8. \u160\'00\'A0 According to SIAVI, imports of steel cables entering tariff lines 7312.10.05 and 7312.10.07 of TIGIE are subject to an ad valorem tariff of 5%, with the exception of imports originating in countries with which Mexico has concluded free trade agreements, which are exempt; however, and in accordance with the Comprehensive and Progressive Trans-Pacific Partnership (TPP) Treaty, goods originating in Vietnam are subject to an ad valorem tariff of 2%. For those entering for TARIFF fractions 7312.10.01 and 7312.10.99, tariff tariffs were left as of January 1, 2012, whatever their origin. 9. \u160\'00\'A0 Also, in accordance with the "Agreement amending the various agreement prohibiting the export or importation of various goods to the countries, entities and persons indicated", as of September 22, 2017, imports originating in the Democratic People's Republic of Korea entering through TIGIE tariff lines 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99 are restricted. 10. \u160\'00\'A0 The unit of measurement used by TIGIE is the kilogram. However, as stated in point 8 of the Final Resolution, Deacero stated that the unit of trade measure may be indistinctly both the meter and the kilogram. Therefore, it provided a conversion factor from kilograms to meters, which corresponds to the theoretical weight of each of the different types of cables according to their construction and diameter, this theoretical weight is not arbitrary, it is fixed according to the Mexican standard NMX-H-084-1983, whose background is the technical standard API 9-A of the \uc2 American Petroleum Institute\uc2  (API). Its application consists of multiplying the amount of meters by the theoretical weight, resulting in weight in kilograms. 4. Production process11. \u160\'00\'A0 Steel cables are made from wire rod or wire of varying steel grades, which may have a black or galvanized finish; souls or nuclei that can be made of steel, natural or synthetic fibers, and greases or lubricants. The production process of steel cables generally consists of the following stages:a. Pickling. The wire is cleaned to remove rust generated by the steel process and the environment. This process is carried out by applying chemical solutions. b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Drawing. The wire is stretched by several steps or several controlled reductions. It is climbed to give a smaller diameter and meet the required diameter characteristics. Also, it is climbed to reach a smaller diameter, and then re-drawn (re-drawn) to the required final diameter. c.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0patented. It is a heat treatment that helps regenerate the microstructure of the wire to continue its drawing at thinner diameters without losing its mechanical properties or ductility. d.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Galvanized (when applied). It consists of covering the wire to protect it from the environment, according to the specific wire requirement. The coating can vary in thickness, i.e. it can have thin or thick layers. It consists of a hot immersion of the wire in a zinc bath. Subsequently, a "draining" process is made by which the amount of zinc carried by the wire is controlled, then cooled and then "collected" in the presentation that is required, eitherroll carrier or reel. e.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Second climb. The second drawing, or "retrefilado", consists of taking a wire that has already been patented (which has already been regenerated the microstructure), and stretching it again through several reductions, until it reaches the final diameter with the mechanical and dimensional characteristics required for the wire. f.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0twisted. The wires, distributed according to their construction, are twisted longitudinally helically on one central one or more layers, once twisted, these wires together are called toron, in this process the corresponding lubricant required by the customer can be applied. g.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0closed. It is when the torons distributed according to their construction are twisted longitudinally helically around a soul that forms a unique body as an element of work; in this step of the production process the corresponding lubricant required by the customer can also be applied. 5. Standards12. \u160\'00\'A0 There are seventeen standards that apply to steel cables under review, three Mexican and fourteen international. In particular, API 9 describes the steel cable more fully, so the other standards are equivalent or complementary. However, steel cables originating in China can be imported even without subject to any standard. The following are the standards applicable to steel cables:StandardnameNMX-B-395-1990Steer wire cable cable, zinc coated (retaining cable). NMX-H-084-1983Surgical-toron products and steel cables. MNX-395 Zinc-coated steel wire cable cable. ASTM A-1023Note for general purpose carbon steel wire cables. ASTM A-475Note for zinc-covered cables. ASTM A-603Note for zinc-covered structural steel cables. ASTM A-586Note for parallel structural and helical steel wire cables coated with zinc. ASTM A-510Notes the general requirements for wires and round wire deael to carbon. 9-AE.S. Petroleum Institute API, cable specifications. ISO-2232 Non-alloy steel cable glands drawn for general use and large diameter cables. ISO-4345 Fiber soul specifications for steel cables. ISO-4346 Basic lubricant requirements for general purpose steel cables. ISO-17893Definitions and classification of steel cables. EN12385-4Sales-safety cable assemblies-part 4: string braiding for general lifting applications. BS302 Part 1Sn-braided british steel cable-Part 1.BS302 Part 4Ser-braided steel cable-part 4.DIN3051Scal-feature cables, technical conditions of steel supply.\u160\'00\'A013.In addition to the above \u160\'00\'A0 standards, ISO-4344:2004 (Steel wire ropes for lifts-Minimum requirements) also apply, which specifies the minimum requirements for the manufacture and testing of braided carbon steel cables used for elevator service and ISO-4101:1983 (Drawn steel wire for elevator ropes Specifications) that provide specifications (diameter, ovality, tensile strength, testing and acceptance conditions) of steel wire for use only with elevator cables, both from the \uc2 International Organization for Standardization\uc2  (ISO), as well as British Standard BS302 (Stranded steel wireropes). Specification for general requirements) that specifies the general requirements for elevator steel cables from the \uc2 British Standards Institute\uc2  (BS). 6. \u160\'00\'A0 Uses and Functions 14. \u160\'00\'A0 Among the main functions of steel cables are, serve as load or drag support, tension and perimeter guard elements. They are used in all sectors of the economy, mainly in industrial. The following are the main uses and functions of the steel cable:IndustryUses and applicationsElectric andtelecommunicationsPole tensioner, radio towers, power line protection, deconductor suspension, input for the manufacture of electrical conduction cables, telecommunications and fiber optics. Loading and transportFor mast and radial cranes (in the lift and in the shoulder straps), mobile cranes anddragas (on the shoulder straps, in the lines, for boom suspension), tower cranes (in the east, in the trolls, to assemble and lift the same crane, for mounting and lifting of the crane, on the shoulder straps), travel cranes, bridge cranes, hoists, blick lift, for passenger and cargo elevators (in suspension compensation and guidance thereof), in cable cars, ski lifts, ytelecabinas tele-seats, funicular railways and inclined planes to pull the carriages on rails. mineworkerTo drag and lift gondolas and baskets, for loading, balancing, counterweight, deepening and in winches. FishingFence, lift and drag on fishing boats, tuna and shrimp fleets, for fixing the bedbug, on the sea cranes. OilUsed in drilling lines, drilling oil wells, for gastile and winching, in anchor lines for drilling boats and rotary drills. agricultureSupport of greenhouse structures. Construction andengineeringFor the suspension of suspension bridges, on earth lines, lightning rods, earthen motion machinery such as dredgers and draglines, clam excavators, buckets, excavator shovels, backhoes and grazing machines, for the construction of road safety fences and fences for livestock, to hold towers and for manoeuvres in construction. otherLike lightning rods on poles.\u160\'00\'A0F. \u160\'00\'A0 Call and notifications15. \u160\'00\'A0 By publishing the Initiation Resolution, the Secretariat convened domestic producers, importers, exporters and anyone considering legal interest in the outcome of this review to appear to present the arguments and evidence they deemed relevant. 16.The Secretariat notified the parties to which it was \u160\'00\'A0 aware of the initiation of these proceedings and the Government of China. G. Interested Parties appearing17. \u160\'00\'A0 The accredited interested parties, who appeared in a timely and form of the present proceedings, are:1. Domestic producersAceros Camesa, S.A. de C.V.Margarita Maza de Ju\u225\'00\'E1rez No. 154Col. New Industrial VallejoC.P. 07700, Mexico CityDeacero, S.A.P.I. de C.V.Av. L\u225\'00\'E1zaro C\u225\'00\'E1rdenas No. 2333Col. OrienteC.P. Valley. 66269, San Pedro Garza Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, Nuevo Le\u243\'00\'F3n2. \u160\'00\'A0 Importer Servicables, S.A. de C.V.Dr. Balmis No. 91Col. DoctorsC.P. 06720, MexicoCity \u160\'00\'A0 H. First period of testing18. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat granted a fifteen-day extension at the request of Camesa and Deacero (as a whole "the National Producers") to submit their response to the official form, as well as the arguments and evidence for the first period of offering evidence. On 18 February 2020 Camesa and Deacero submitted their reply to the official form, as well as the arguments and evidence that it agreed to, which are contained in the reference administrative file, which were considered for the issuance of this Resolution. 19. \u160\'00\'A0 On 27 January 2020, Servicables, S.A. de C.V. ("Servicables") submitted its reply to the official form, as well as the arguments and evidence that it agreed to, which are contained in the reference administrative file, which were considered for the issuance of this Resolution. I. Replicas20. \u160\'00\'A0 On 7 and 24 February 2020, Camesa, Deacero and Servicables submitted their rebuttals and counter-arguments, which are contained in the administrative file of the case, which were considered for the issuance of this Resolution. J. \u160\'00\'A0 Information Requirements1. \u160\'00\'A0 Extensions21. \u160\'00\'A0 At Camesa and Deacero's request, the Secretariat granted two ten-day extensions to submit its response to the information requests made on 14 April and 17 August 2020, the deadlines expired on 14 May and 14 MaySeptember 2020, respectively.\u160\'00\'A02. Partiesa. Camesa22. \u160\'00\'A0 On 14 April 2020, the Secretariat requested Camesa, inter alia, to submit a methodology other than that provided for the calculation of the export price, since the methodology provided was from the domestic sales it made and imports from one of the importers; providing total import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for steel cables originating in China; submit information from the Chinese production company from which it obtained the quote provided; make certain clarifications of product coverage, and adjustments necessary for the price of export \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the ex-factory level; provide information, arguments and evidentiary evidence underpinning the prevailing industry that the conditions of a non-market economy prevail in the industry producing steel cables with respect to the manufacture, production and sale of that product, in accordance with paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  (China's "Protocol of Accession to the WTO") and Article 48 of the Foreign Trade Act (RLCE) Regulations; explain in detail the similarity between the technology used in the production process in China and Mexico;\u160\'00\'A0provide a percentage cost structure for steel cables in China during the review period or, where appropriate, use its own information as an alternative; point to the participation of each of the production factors: land, work, capital and energy, which are used intensively in the manufacture of steel cables; demonstrate distortion in the use of steel cable production factors by government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and how this gives the non-market economy character to china; present the production process in both China and the United States of America ("United States"), and price references for the United States, having failed to submit them, even though it provided it; make various justifications and clarifications to the information of the company from which it obtained domestic price quotes in China and that they are representative and a reasonable basis for the calculation of normal value, as well as adjustments to take it at the ex-factory level and the calculation methodology; clarifications of criteria of its methodology for debugging imports of steel cables; various details regarding the quote you used in your price projections, particularly with respect to the diameters used and, in general, explanations, corrections or confirmations in your projections of your economic and financial indicators. Camesa submitted her reply on May 14, 2020. 23. \u160\'00\'A0 On 17 August 2020, the Secretariat requested Camesa to correct aspects of form; clarify the amount of freight reported in the export price adjustments; for the U.S. domestic market to submit prices for certain types of cables, in addition, sales prices in China's domestic market, for the types of cables indicated above; for the prices of both countries, documentary support for the adjustments necessary to take them to an ex-factory level; which proves that the prices correspond to the domestic consumption of those countries and that they are representative of the country of origin and a reasonable basis for the calculation of normal value, as well as clarifications in their projections in the state of costs , sales and profits. Camesa submitted her reply on September 14, 2020. b. Deacero24. \u160\'00\'A0 On 14 April 2020, the Secretariat requested Deacero to correct various aspects of form;\u160\'00\'A0submit clarifications and, where appropriate, corrections to the methodology used for the calculation of the export price, since on the one hand it noted that it did not find imports of the product under review and, on the other hand, mentioned that it presents the export price based on import statistics, also, because the methodology used to obtain the prices it reported is from domestic sales made by a domestic producer, which provided another methodology; provide information, evidence and methodology to support the adjustments you calculated to take them to an ex-factory level; explain various aspects of the information it provided, related to its assertions that China is a non-market economy, and that the conditions of a non-market economy prevail in the steel cable industry in manufacturing, production and sale of that product, in accordance with paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO and Article 48 of the RLCE;\u160\'00\'A0explain in detail the similarity between the technology used in the production process in China and Mexico; provide a percentage cost structure for steel cables in China during the period of review or, where appropriate, use its own information as an alternative; point to the participation of each of the production factors: land, work, capital and energy, which are used intensively in the manufacture of steel cables; demonstrate distortion in the use of steel cable production factors, by government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and how this gives the non-market economy character to that industry; provide evidence of the mechanisms used by the Chinese government to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the steel wire industry and to identify which are the main companies producing steel wire rod, in which the government participates according to its own manifestations; explain how wire-producing companies transfer this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to steel cables; to support the relationship of several companies that referred that they were owned by the Chinese government and the percentage of their representation in the steel industry, to provide evidence of the impact on the steel cable production chain, generated by the relationship between the companies in comment and the profile thereof, to identify, of the products they produce, which corresponded to the steel cable examined; provide information to support the fact that in the production process of both China and the United States the technology used is similar; clarify criteria for its methodology for refining imports of steel cables; make various details regarding the quote you used in your price projections, particularly with respect to the diameters used and, in general, explanations, corrections or confirmation of the projections of your economic and financial indicators. Deacero submitted his reply on 14 May 2020. 25. On 17 August 2020, the Secretariat requested Deacero, inter alia, to clarify the amount of freight reported in the export price adjustments; provide information and evidence to prove distortion in the work factor; of the Chinese production companies that it pointed out, indicate which of them produced the steel cable under review and, of them, which were benefiting from the State; submit information that accounting practice in the United States is to maintain accounting through a single set of books; present prices for certain types of cables in the U.S. and Chinese market; for the prices of both countries, documentary support of the adjustments necessary to take them to an ex-factory level, in addition, which would prove that the prices correspond to the domestic consumption of those countries, which are representative of the country of origin and are a reasonable basis for the calculation of normal value, and clarifications and corrections in their projections in the state of costs, sales and profits. Deacero submitted his reply on 14 September 2020. c. Importer26. \u160\'00\'A0 On 14 April 2020, the Secretariat requested Servicables to submit information on its steel cable import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  through TIGIE tariff lines 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05 and 7312.10.99. Servicables submitted its response on 22 April 2020. 3. Parts27. \u160\'00\'A0 On 14 April 2020, the Secretariat required a number of importers and customs agents to submit import orders as well as the accompanying documentation. Deadlines expired on 28 April 2020. K. Second testing period28. \u160\'00\'A0 On 29 May 2020, the Secretariat notified Camesa, Deacero and Servicables of the opening of the second period of evidence, in order to present the arguments and supplementary evidence they consideredRelevant. The deadline expired on 8 July 2020. 29. \u160\'00\'A0 At the request of Camesa and Deacero, the Secretariat granted a ten-day extension for the submission of supplementary arguments and evidence. On 22 July 2020, Camesa and Deacero submitted their arguments and supplementary evidence, which are contained in the administrative file of the case, which were considered for the issuance of this Resolution. Servicables presented no additional arguments and evidence. L. \u160\'00\'A0 Other arraignments30. \u160\'00\'A0 On 27 January 2020, Ferretera Elizondo Hermanos, S.A. de C.V., stated that it had no interest in participating in the present proceedings, since it did not import steel cables originating in China during the period under review. 31. \u160\'00\'A0 On 10 February, 17 and 18 March 2020, Camesa and Deacero submitted a scope to the letter of reply and comments to the statements submitted by Servicables in its promotion of 13 March 2020, respectively, those which were considered at the stage of arguments and supplementary evidence. 32. \u160\'00\'A0 On 19 February 2020, at the request of the National Producers, the National Chamber of the Iron and Steel Industry (CANACERO) presented the methodology used to identify in the database the imports made by tariff fractions 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99, import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  corresponding to the product under review and products not under review. 33. \u160\'00\'A0 On 28 February and 13 March 2020, Servicables lodged a statement of statements concerning the letter submitted by Camesa and Deacero of 10 February 2020, and several statements reiterating those submitted on 24 and 28 February 2020, respectively, which were considered at the stage of supplementary arguments and evidence. 34. \u160\'00\'A0 On 5 October 2020, Camesa submitted additional information to that submitted in response to the request for information of 17 August 2020, however, the term phenorated on 14 September 2020, and that information was therefore not accepted in accordance with point 43 of this Resolution. M. Essential Facts35. \u160\'00\'A0 On 7 October 2020, the Secretariat notified Camesa, Deacero and Servicables of the essential facts of this procedure, which served as the basis for issuing this Resolution, in accordance with Articles 6.9 and 11.4 of the Agreement on the Implementation of Article VI of the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade 1994 (the "Anti-Dumping Agreement"). On 22 October 2020, only Camesa and Deacero submitted statements to the essential facts, which are contained in the administrative file and were considered for the issuance of this Resolution. N. \u160\'00\'A0 Public hearing36. \u160\'00\'A0 The public hearing of these proceedings was held on 15 October 2020. The domestic producers Camesa and Deacero, as well as the importer Servicables, participated, who had the opportunity to set out their arguments and replicate those of their counterparts, as recorded in the minutes that was raised for this reason, which constitutes a public document of full evidentiary effectiveness, in accordance with Article 46(I) of the Federal Law on Administrative Litigation Procedure (LFPCA). O. Allegations37. \u160\'00\'A0 On 22 October 2020, Camesa and Deacero submitted their pleas, which are contained in the administrative file of the case, which were considered for the issuance of this Resolution. Q. Opinion of the Committee on Foreign Trade38. \u160\'00\'A0 On the basis of Articles 89F fraction III of the Foreign Trade Act (LCE) and 19 fraction XI of the Internal Regulations of the Secretariat of the Economy (RISE), the draft of this Resolution was submitted to the opinion of the Committee on Foreign Trade, which considered it at its Fourth Ordinary Session of 4 May 2021. The project was viewed favorably by a majority. RecitalsA. \u160\'00\'A0 Competition39. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat has jurisdiction to issue this Resolution, in accordance with Articles 16 and 34 fractions V and XXXIII of the Organic Law of the Federal Public Administration; 1, 2(A), fraction II numeral 7, and 19 fractions I and IV of the RISE; 11.1, 11.3, 11.4, 12.2 and 12.3 of the AD Agreement, and 5 fraction VII, 67, 70 fraction II and 89 F of the LCE.\u160\'00\'A0B. Applicable Law40. \u160\'00\'A0 For the purposes of this procedure, the AD Agreement, the LCE, the RLCE, the Federation Tax Code, the LFPCA applied in accordance with the Second Transitional Article of the Decree issuing the LFPCA and the Federal Code of Civil Procedures (CFPC) are applicable, the latter three of additional application. C. Protection of Confidential Information41. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat may not publicly disclose the confidential information that the interested parties submitted, nor the confidential information that it itself received, in accordance with Articles 6.5 of the AD Agreement, 80 of the LCE and 152 and 158 of the L.L.R. D. Rights of defence and due process42. \u160\'00\'A0 Interested parties had ampl opportunity to present all kinds of arguments, exceptions and defences, as well as evidence to support them, in accordance with the AD Agreement, the LCE and the RLCE. The Secretariat valued them subject to the essential formalities of the administrative procedure. E. Information not accepted43. \u160\'00\'A0 This authority determined by Agreement AC.2001886 of 5 October 2020 not to accept the information and evidence referred to in point 34 of this Resolution, on the basis that it was not submitted within the time limit granted for this purpose, i.e. its submission was out of time. F. Responses to certain arguments of Parts1. \u160\'00\'A0 General aspects of procedure44. \u160\'00\'A0 Servicables stated during the public hearing that the National Producers, particularly Deacero, exhibited various magazine articles or newspaper notes, which are in a language other than Spanish without the corresponding translation, therefore they cannot be taken into account for research purposes. It added that when articles from magazines or newspapers were offered as evidence, they must comply with certain formalities, such as the ratification of the person who produced them. 45. \u160\'00\'A0 In this regard, the National Producers noted that the articles, newspaper notes or magazines that they submitted, these are private documentary evidence, which does not constitute a testimonial of the author thereof, hence it is not necessary or deemed appropriate that they are ratified by their author, that since the author's testimonial has not been offered it is evidence that is subject to valuation, and it is the authority that determines the value granted to them, in accordance with the AD Agreement and the applicable legislation. 46.In this regard, the Secretariat clarified that it verified the \u160\'00\'A0 information submitted by the interested parties and, where appropriate, it was required to provide the corresponding translations, in accordance with Article 271 of the CFPC, of additional application. For this reason, the information in the administrative file meets the requirements in a way required by applicable law. Also, with regard to the argument that the evidence exhibited by the National Producers, consisting of notes from magazines or newspapers, require ratification by their author, in order to be given a value, it is noted that those documents were accepted and had effects in the present proceedings, since they were not objected to or challenged by Servicables at the appropriate procedural time, that is, within three days of becomes knowledgeable of them, in accordance with Article 142 of the CFPC, of additional application. 2. \u160\'00\'A0 Desabasto47. \u160\'00\'A0 Servicables stated that only steel cable originating in China with diameters less than 3/8" imports, as it is not produced in Mexico, as well as the 7\u215\'00\'D719 3/8" and 1/2" galvanized cable, and the 7\u215\'00\'D719" stainless cablefrom 1/8" to 1/2", which cames from either camesa and which means it also does not manufacture Deacero because it has not been offered it. 48.In this regard, Camesa stated that domestic industry \u160\'00\'A0 has the productive capacity to manufacture steel cables imported by Servicables; it also noted that the main motivation for such imports is China's low and unfair prices. In addition, he stated that Deacero has the capacity to supply such cables, but clarified that domestic production could not be sold at China's low and unfair prices. 49. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers stated that, with the exception of stainless cables outside the product coverage of this review, the domestic industry produces and can produce and supply the cables imported by Servicables originating in China. To support his claims, Camesa submitted production reports in addition to extracts from the ASTM A-1023 technical standard with the specifications and cross-sectional diagrams of constructions 6\u215\'00\'D719 steel soul and 7\u215\'00\'D719. They also submitted catalogues of the sale of these goods. 50. \u160\'00\'A0 Servicables replied that, one situation is to produce and another is to be able to produce, because until the goods are produced, it does not exist on the domestic market, causing unsasure, and in the face of the need for consumption it is forced to import it. 51.It added that the elimination of \u160\'00\'A0 the countervailing quota did not result in continuation or recurrence of dumping because steel cables originating in China that it imported were not manufactured in Mexico, so it needed to continue importing those cables. Considers that the damage does not exist when these cables are not manufactured in Mexico; moreover, that the cable produced on national territory is not similar to the one that matters. 52.The Secretariat analyzed the information in the administrative record and noted that, during the period \u160\'00\'A0 analyzed, Camesa manufactured cable less than 3/8" and technically and commercially equivalent cables to galvanized 7\u215\'00\'D719 3/8" and 1/2", as they were of equal diameter but of construction 6\u215\'00\'D719. Both constructions are incorporated under asTM A-1023, contain fully interchangeable constructions, so their performance will be the same, share similar technical characteristics in resistance to breakage, fatigue, abrasion, among other elements. In addition, observed the following:a. during the period analyzed the National Producers manufactured steel cables with a diameter of less than 3/8",and b. the diameter cables 3/8" construction 6\u215\'00\'D719 and 7\u215\'00\'D719, although they record different resistances to rupture, these are similar, so, based on break resistance, they could have the same uses and functions, and be commercially interchangeable. 53. \u160\'00\'A0 In addition, in point 98(b) of the Final Resolution, the Secretariat determined that there was an equivalence between buildings 6\u215\'00\'D719 and 7\u215\'00\'D719, since its name addressed a different nomenclature. That is, in the TECHNICAL STANDARD ASTM A-1023, the first number of the nomenclature used to identify the construction of the cables, identifies the outer torons that make up the cable, and the second, identifies the number of wires in each toron. Thus, a 6\u215\'00\'D719 cable consists of 6 outer torons where each is composed of 19 wires. However, within the same standard, there is an exception to this nomenclature that includes 7\u215\'00\'D719 cables with diameter less than or equal to 3/8", where it refers that these cables are composed of 6 outer torons and 1 central toron, all composed of 19 wires. 54. \u160\'00\'A0 Further, in point 232 of the Preliminary Resolution of the anti-dumping investigation, the Secretariat determined that steel cables have similar basic uses and functions and that, within them, as well as between classes, they have such characteristics that they can be used in the same industries, and are generally commercially interchangeable. Under special conditions of use, they tend to reduce their commercial interchangeability and even to be goods produced on request, without implying that these are a different product. In this sense, the range of steel cables imported by Servicables and those of domestic production are similar in their uses and functions, so they are commercially interchangable and can be considered similar. 55.The Secretariat therefore concluded that, during the period under review, the domestic industry manufactured steel cables similar to those \u160\'00\'A0 imported by Servicables described in point 47 of this Resolution; because of this, contrary to what was stated by Servicables, there were no elements in the administrative record indicating that such steel cables were unsasured on the Mexican market. It also determined that, with the exception of stainless steel cables that are not part of the product coverage, cables imported by Servicables do not have such characteristics as to differentiate them from domestic produced goods. G. Analysis on continuation or recurrence of dumping56. \u160\'00\'A0 In the present proceedings, there were no Chinese exporting companies or the Government of China, so the Secretariat conducted the review of the recurrence or continuation of dumping on the basis of the information and evidence submitted by Camesa, Deacero, the servicables importer and the information reached by the Secretariat itself, in terms of the provisions of articles 54 second subparagraph and 64 last paragraph of the LCE. 1. Export price57. \u160\'00\'A0 At the request of Camesa and Deacero, CANACERO presented a database of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  entering through TARIFF fractions 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99, during the review period, which it obtained from the Tax Administration Service (SAT). 58. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers, on the basis of the purification methodology provided by CANACERO, indicated that the tariff fractions indicated above enter both the product under review and the unre examined. They added that the descriptions contained in the sat import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  database do not permit the exact identification of the goods under review, therefore, in order to prove the export price, estimated prices for various types and categories of cables on the basis of information submitted by an importing company appearing in this proceeding, that according to the National Producers, corresponds to cables with diameters less than 3/8", and obtained the difference between the average prices of sales made by domestic production in the Mexican market, corresponding to cables less than 3/8" and the prices reported by the importing company and, the resulting difference was applied to each type of cable with diameter less than 3/8" sold in Mexico by themselves. 59. \u160\'00\'A0 In this regard, the Secretariat required the National Producers to submit a methodology for calculating the export price, since the basis of the proposal to obtain the reported prices was sales to the domestic market and did not correspond to export transaction prices to Mexico. 60. \u160\'00\'A0 In response to the request, the National Producers submitted a proposal for the calculation of the export price, which they indicated corresponds to the estimated average price of all \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  originating in China, possibly corresponding to the product under review, corresponding to the period under review, for both black and galvanized steel cables. The price was obtained by debugging the database of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided to them by CANACERO. 61. \u160\'00\'A0 In order to identify the goods examined, the National Producers excluded import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  originating in the country under review, in accordance with the following criteria:(a) those which do not correspond to a definitive or temporary regime; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0the compensatory quota does not correspond to the goods examined; c. those with a description other than that of the goods examined, such as stainless steel cables, slings, screws, clamps, among others; d. products where it is not possible to identify the volume of the goods examined; e. steel cables with some type of coating other than galvanized, i.e. coated with copper, plastic, linings,nylon, among others; f. cables with fittings or attachments; (g) those whose volume is less than or equal to 70 kilograms, considering that this volume is used for samples and should not be considered normal commercial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in accordance with their salesdepartment, and h. \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in which the importer's turn corresponds to industries with uses and functions other than those of the goods examined, such as electrical, automotive or telecommunications industries. 62. \u160\'00\'A0 In order to obtain further detail on import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  originating in China during the period under review, the Secretariat was taken from the import statistics of mexico's Commercial Information System (SIC-M) that entered through TARIFF fractions 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99 of TIGIE. With the information provided by CANACERO, it checked the product description, the value in US dollars and the volume in kilograms, among other data, finding differences in the number of trades and, therefore, in value and volume. 63.Accordingly, the Secretariat determined to calculate the export price on the basis of SIC-M statistics, on the basis that the information contained in that database is obtained after validation of customs requests under an exchange of information between customs agents and agents, on the one hand, and the customs authority \u160\'00\'A0 on the other, which is reviewed by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  and is therefore considered to be the best available information. 64. \u160\'00\'A0 In addition, the Secretariat required importers and customs agents to provide import orders and accompanying documentation, and to identify the products entered by tariff lines 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99. It should be noted that the required information corresponds to more than 90% of the total volume that entered Mexico. 65. TheSecretariat considered it reasonable to use the methodology presented by \u160\'00\'A0 the National Producers, described in point 61 of this Resolution, with the exception of criteria that ruled out import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the volume of which was less than or equal to 70 kilograms and samples of the goods examined and \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in which the importer's turn corresponds to industries with uses and functions other than those of the goods examined, since they did not provide evidence to prove their said.\u160\'00\'A0Finally, by replicating that methodology on the basis of the SIC-M, it agreed that the import transactions correspond to goods which it entered definitively and temporarily, paid a countervailing quota and by its description transactions whose description does not correspond to the goods examined may be ruled out. As a result of the application of the criteria and with the information that was received from importers and customs agents, the Secretariat identified 15 types of steel cables of various diameters and constructions. 66.In addition, the National Producers submitted a quotation containing prices of the goods examined of various \u160\'00\'A0 diameters and constructions of a Chinese exporting producer, which they noted comprises most of the steel cables that are demanded in Mexico and are therefore representative of the export price. The quote is free on board (FOB), Shanghai port China and outside the period under review, so they proposed adjusting for inflation. 67.At the final stage of the proceedings, the National Producers noted that the export prices resulting from the 15 categories of cables identified in previous points are not relevant to the objective of this review, since these would not be those which in a compensatory quota elimination scenario would return in bulk and harmful and, in any event, those that would have that effect would be those \u160\'00\'A0 that identified and are contained in the quotation indicated in the previous point. 68. \u160\'00\'A0 They noted, therefore, that a source showing export prices should be used to reasonably infer the price levels to which the product under consideration would enter if the countervailing quota under consideration was eliminated or reduced, hence it is appropriate and justified to use the most representative construction and diameter cable export price information reflecting the experience of consumption of the domestic market compared to others, the latter being that provided in the quote. 69.In that regard, the Secretariat determined not to take into account the contribution \u160\'00\'A0 referred to in point 66 of this Resolution, since the fact that the types of cables identified and contained in that quotation are considered to be the most representative of national consumption, it is not a factor that determines the suitability or relevance to be considered for the calculation of the export price, on the contrary, the consumption of each type of cable, is a consequence of the needs of each buyer or customer, so it does not consider that in a scenario of reduction or elimination of the compensatory quota, as stated by the National Producers, imports of steel cables constructions other than those identified by the Secretariat , with the methodology they proposed themselves, would enter Mexico in a massive and harmful way. 70. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat therefore determined to use SIC-M information statistics, since the identification of the 15 types of steel cables, referred to, correspond to export \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  actually carried out originating in China, and as noted, these statistics are obtained after validation of customs requests that occur in a framework of exchange of information between customs agents and agents, in addition, the identification of the goods examined was carried out using the criteria proposed by the National Producers. 71.On the other hand, what is sought in an examination of \u160\'00\'A0 validity is to determine whether the elimination of the countervailing quota would lead to continuation or recurrence of unfair practice, in this regard, as the National Producers point out, the Secretariat should be pleased with reasonable information to validate the sales prices of exported goods with which the goods examined entered Mexico, which in this case were obtained from SIC-M and come from actual \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  originating in China, which were validated with the information from which the Secretariat arrived. 72. \u160\'00\'A0 Therefore, in order to obtain the export price, the Secretariat considered the price of the 15 types of steel cables it obtained from the import pediments for the period under review, since it verified that these were actually observed prices of the goods under review. 73. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on Articles 39 and 40 of the RLCE, the Secretariat calculated the weighted average export price per kilogram for each type of steel cable for the period under review. a. \u160\'00\'A0 Adjustments to export price74. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers stated that because imports correspond to customs value, they must be adjusted by terms and conditions of sale, in particular by land freight in China and freight and maritime insurance from China to Mexico. 75.In order to establish those adjustments, they submitted contributions for both domestic freight \u160\'00\'A0 and maritime freight and insurance, issued by carriers, at rates outside the period under review. Therefore, to bring quotation prices to the period under review, they used the Inflation Index of China they obtained from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ycharts.com/indicators/china_producer_price_index"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://ycharts.com/indicators/china_producer_price_index}}}\uc2 . 76. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat corroborated the data of the carriers on its website, verified their profile, as well as the different services they provided on the market, which were in the quote cited. It also noted that they were recognized companies with experience in the international freight transport service. i. Ground freight77. \u160\'00\'A0 For the calculation of land freight in China, the National Producers calculated the average cost in dollars per kilogram, corresponding to thetransport of the plant of an exporting producer of the examined goods, located in Jiangbei City, Chongquin to Shanghai Port, China, the land freight expense divided it by the volume normally carried in a 20-foot container, considering an average load of 19,950 kilograms. ii. Maritime freight and insurance78. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers calculated the average cost in dollars per kilogram, marking a trajectory from the port of Shanghai, China, to the port of Manzanillo, Mexico, for a 20-foot container, considering an average load of 19,950 kilograms. 79. \u160\'00\'A0 For the estimation of the insurance of the goods, they used the percentage corresponding to the deductible provided by the carrier, which is based on the customs value of the goods. b. \u160\'00\'A0 Determination80. \u160\'00\'A0 Pursuant to Articles 2.4 of the AD Agreement, 36 of the LCE and 53 and 54 of the RLCE, the Secretariat accepted the information provided by the National Producers and adjusted the export price for land freight and maritime freight and insurance. 2. \u160\'00\'A0 Normal valuea. China as \u160\'00\'A0 non-market economy81. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers stated that conditions of a non-market economy prevail in China. They emphasized that the steel sector, of which the steel cable industry is part, is influenced and benefited by government policies that promote the increase in its productive capacities, facilitating access to inputs with prices below market value and boosting its exports. 82.In support of the above, they presented arguments and \u160\'00\'A0 evidence at the macroeconomic, industrial and, specific, level of steel cables, in accordance with the criteria set out in Article 48 of the RLCE. i. That the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country under investigation be widely convertibleinto international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u160\'00\'A0 markets83. \u160\'00\'A0 They argued that the Chinese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is dormant and fully visible, based on the fact that the convertibility of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is limited for certain purposes, such as financial transactions.\u160\'00\'A0They submitted the document "Inquiry Into the Status of the People's Republic of China as a Nonmarket Economy Country Under the Antidumping and Countervailing Duty Laws", published by the \uc2 American Iron and Steel Institute\uc2  on May 10, 2017. 84.The existence of exchange controls and barriers to \u160\'00\'A0 capital flows in China provides the authorities with a key means of economic control, demonstrating the government's economic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market and, therefore, a strong indication that the economy in China is not governed by the principles of a market economy. The article "China is considering eliminating capital controls by 2020", published by El Pa\u237\'00\'EDs on October 20, 2015, reports that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not fully convertible or totally free, since its value is subject to the benchmark exchange rate established by the government, which constitutes clear interference by the Chinese government in its economy. 85.They added that the limitation of capital flow echoes in other \u160\'00\'A0 publications, such as in the article "China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 : The Yuan or Renmimbi" of October 2016, where it is found that the Chinese population cannot hold foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or buy assets abroad, preventing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from having a real flotation under market principles. 86. \u160\'00\'A0 The Chinese government acquires sovereign debt bonds issued by the United States to keep the value of the dollar above the yuan, thereby sustaining Chinese exports at low prices, allowing it to exert influence over political-economic measures in the United States, which aim to raise the yuan. They provided the newspaper note "China's Economy Facts and Effect on the U.S. Economy" published by The Balance on April 17, 2017. 87. \u160\'00\'A0 The document "Holding China Accountable for the Illegal Steel Trade", published by Lifezette, on 13 December 2016, notes that China's steel industry benefits from continued manipulation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Beijing government, as it abolishes the value of the yuan relative to the US dollar and allows it to deflate or reduce the cost of its exports. Such action has allowed China to sell its steel exports at a low cost in international markets. ii. \u160\'00\'A0 That wages be established through free negotiation between workers and employers88. \u160\'00\'A0 The \uc2 American Federation of Labor and Congress of Industrial Organizations\uc2  article "Labor Rights in China" notes that, over the past decade, China has been an attractive destination for global corporations, due to its low wages and labor laws that do not allow independent unions, which also limit the right to strike. Most workers at China's factories, mines, warehouses, docks and transport hubs still have little or nothing to say in selecting their trade union representatives. 89.The labor force in China has no wage \u160\'00\'A0 bargaining power, due to a legal framework established by the Chinese government that only allows workers to join trade unions under the control of the Chinese Communist Party. They provided the publication "2016 USTR Report to Congress On China's WTO Compliance", pages 173 and 174. 90.The Chinese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pressure government officials not to have work-based unrest, so they harass workers' families so that they do not go on strike; hence the government has come to arrest a dozen labour \u160\'00\'A0 rights specialists working in local organizations, which were key to responding to multiple disputes between employees and employers. They submitted the article "Refusing to Comply with Labor Rights Turns Against China," published by The New York Times, on May 12, 2016. 91. \u160\'00\'A0 The article "UN body urges China to allow labour activists to continue their job", published by the China Labour Bulletin on 14 November 2016, states that Chinese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits the bargaining power of workers, which is notorious to the United Nations, under which the \uc2 International Labour Organization\uc2  made a report, in which it reported that labour rights activists were arrested in China, where there are very significant legislative obstacles to ensuring freedom of association, because in its Trade Union Law they secontemplan articles that are contrary to fundamental principles of association. 92.Steelworkers in China do not have legitimate representation to participate \u160\'00\'A0 in a social dialogue with the government or business, as outlined in Adam Lee's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 Trade Union Advisory Committee\uc2  of 24 March 2017. 93.The labor oppression of the Chinese government \u160\'00\'A0 is exemplified by the fact that Ansteel imposed on the trade union leader, a situation reported by plant workers, as follows, according to the article "China Economy: Workers of the "Factory of the World" protest against pay cuts and layoffs", published by The Independent, on 2 March 2016. iii. \u160\'00\'A0 That industry or industry decisions under research on prices, costs and \u160\'00\'A0 supplies of inputs, including raw materials, technology, production, sales and \u160\'00\'A0 investment, are adopted in response to market signals and without significant interference from State94. \u160\'00\'A0 The Report of the Secretariat of the \uc2 World Trade Organization (WTO)\uc2 , of China's Trade Policy Review, Document WT/TPR/S/342 of 15 June 2016, provides that the Chinese government applies price controls to goods and services considered to be of direct importance to the national economy and the livelihoods of the population. In addition, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in banking, an important component for the general economy and the productive sectors, as the governmentcentral bank sets rates for basic commercial banking services, credit reporting services and bank letter transaction fees, because there is a lack of effective competition in such activities, which are monopolistic in nature. This indicates that costs and prices of different inputs and services are highly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government, and it is difficult for them to be established according to market signals. 95.In addition, they added that the report rated in the previous point also indicated that \u160\'00\'A0 the number of public enterprises in the industrial sector, which included mining, manufacturing and the production and supply of electricity, gas and water, has increased year by year since 2011. However, these companies account for 5% of industrial enterprises, government companies continue with nearly 40% of the sector's asset share in 2014, the government's unusually high share of industrial activity, indicating that inputs and costs are hardly established under market conditions. 96.\b \highlight22\uc2 China intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its stock markets, so there is artificial stability in the economy that does not allow prices and costs to be affected by market \u160\'00\'A0 behaviors, as stated in the article "\b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  Said to \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Stocks After $590 Billion Sell Off", published by Bloomerg News, on January 4, 2016. 97.They added that the main factors that have generated overca capacity are: (i) the desire of regions to be self-sufficient and competitive, resulting in doubling capacity at the national level; (ii) stimuli have had adverse effects, which have led to large steel mills adding capacity and closing \u160\'00\'A0 small ones; (iii) government enterprises are immune from losses, and (iv) energy subsidies offered by local governments. These measures have such an effect on the steel industry, which are capable of generating excess production overcapacity, so it can be concluded that the Chinese government directly influences the cost structure of that industry by granting it different stimuli and subsidiousness, why it cannot be considered to operate in accordance with the principles of a market economy, as noted in the article "The Overcapacity of Chinese Steel Changed Very Little in 2016", published by Handelsblatt Global, on February 12, 2017, and the report "Overcapcity in China an Impediment to the Party's Reform Agenda" by Roland Berger Strategy Consultans for the European Chamber, published in 2016. 98. \u160\'00\'A0 As noted in Wiley Rein's "Unsustainable: Government \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Overca capacity in the World Steel Industry", LLP., April 2016, noted that the Chinese government has ownership interests in nine of China's ten largest steel producers and that it also owns majority shares in much of the major steel producers, maintains a high degree of decision-making power over industry and continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of individual steel companies, for example recent reports indicate that local governments in China have instructed local steel mills to increase their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  exports and profits. 99.In addition, they noted that the \u160\'00\'A0 Chinese government has favored steel industry producers by providing funds, preferential loans, debt-for-equity swaps, tax refunds, and other indirect support policies, such as restrictions on foreign investment, as above in accordance with the document "Unsustainable: Government \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Overcapacity in the Global Steel Industry", April 2016. They added that four Chinese steel companies that ceased \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2015 for serious financial losses plan to restart their production after receiving investments from a government company, indicating that the government is not ready for massive steel mill closures. In addition, they argued that, based on the report "China's Excess Capacity: Drivers and Implications", by consulting firm Stewart and Stewart, updated to February 2016, overcaly capacity in China is due to the dominance of SOE's state-owned enterprises, as these are not only for commercial gain, but also for job creation and increased state assets. 100. \u160\'00\'A0 The study "Overcapacity in Steel, China's Role in the Global Problem", of the Center for Globalization, Government and Competitiveness, \uc2 Duke University\uc2 , September 2016, mentions that the overcapacity of China's steel industry has also resulted from the various subsidies and incentives it has received from the Chinese government, including energy and other input subsidies, access to cheap credits, and tax incentives. 101.They noted that as regards the production of steel cables, there are several cable companies in China that are owned by a Chinese government company, as in the case \u160\'00\'A0 of Baosteel Group Shanghai Ergang Co. Ltd, ("Ergang") which produces steel cables, is owned by the company "Baoshan Iron and Steel (Group) Corporation" ("Baosteel") which in turn is owned by the State.\u160\'00\'A0Ergang is intended for the distribution and marketing of steel cables in the domestic and export market. Baosteel-related companies produce the complete production chain and raw materials: billet and wire, wires, cables, etc., so that the Chinese government has in-depth \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the production factors of steel cable. iv. Allow foreign investment and co-investment with foreign firms102. \u160\'00\'A0 China's government uses the foreign investment approval process to restrict or delay the investment process, require foreign companies to have a Chinese partner, achieve significant value concessions as a price to enter the market, force technology transfer, and meet export or local content requirements, if they want their investment approved, even though these applications go against China's WTO commitments. In this regard, the National Producers presented document "2016 USTR Report to Congress on China's COMPLIANCE WTO" of January 2017. 103.The \uc2 European Union\uc2  explained that \u160\'00\'A0 in 2015 China adopted a negative list system, one for domestic investors and/or foreign investors with Chinese partners and one for foreign investors, however, this system is still discriminatory, as noted in the "Report of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  to the Council and the \uc2 European Parliament\uc2  on Barriers to Trade, Investment and Protectionist Trends" of 20 June 2016. 104.The restriction on investment in the steel sector in China can be given indirectly, through policies \u160\'00\'A0 that establish that strong mechanisms should be created in china to share technology, resources, sales channels, among others, situation that is contradictory to the principles governing a market economy, according to the document "Comments Regarding Foreign Trade Barriers To U.S. Exports for 2017", prepared by the \uc2 American Iron and Steel Institute\uc2  (AISI) on October 27, 2016. v. \u160\'00\'A0 That the industry under investigation has exclusively a set of \u160\'00\'A0 ledgers that are used for all purposes, and which are audited in accordance with \u160\'00\'A0 generally acceptedaccountingprinciples 105. \u160\'00\'A0 Although, in China, there might be accounting established in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles, the legal framework in China allows for more than one set of ledgers, one to publish and the other for the company.\u160\'00\'A0The above, according to Bio Technology, An Indian Journal: "Analysis of cause of Financial fraud and precautions" by Yiji Chen, Trade Science Inc, BTAIJ,10(20)2014. 106.The legal system and government participation in companies makes its financial and accounting information unreliable, including in the steel sector, in accordance with the article "A Guide to the Quality of \u160\'00\'A0 China's Financial Data", by Brandon Emmerich, Director of Granite Peak Advisory, of 14 December 2016. I saw. \u160\'00\'A0 That the costs of production and financial situation in the sector orindustry under investigation \u160\'00\'A0 are unstorted in relation to asset depreciation, uncobrable debts,barter trade, and debt compensation payments or \u160\'00\'A0 factorsdeemedrelevant 107.\u160\'00\'A0The financial situation of the Chinese steel industry is distorted, as the government grants incentives and supports, such as: preferential credits, credit guarantees and tax refunds, among others, which distorts the financial situation and cost structure of the Chinese steel industry, in accordance with the document "Comments Regarding Foreign Trade Barriers To U.S. Exports for 2017", prepared by the AISI, on October 27, 2016. 108.There are lay-wing-producing companies, whose financial situation benefited by the Chinese government through the granting of loans, under the promise of paying the interest owed by the company; debt restructuring, guaranteeing the payment of liabilities; granting money to the company; pressure on creditors to reduce the \u160\'00\'A0 company's debt and forgiveness of the company, within those companies are \uc2 Bohai Steel Group\uc2  ("Bohai"), Chongqing Iron and Co Steel. ("Chongqing"), Valin Steel Co. Ltd. ("Valin"), \uc2 Dongbei Special Steel Group Co. Ltd\uc2 . ("Dongbei"), and \uc2 Sinosteel Corp\uc2 .("Sinosteel") To support you, submitted the articles "China outlines rescue plan for Bohai Steel", "Steel industry on subsidy life-support as China economy slows", Financial Times, September 19, 2014 and "China's Zombie Companies Stay Alive Despite Defaults" , The Wall Street Journal, July 2016. 109.The distortion in the production cost structure of the steel cable industry lies in the fact that \u160\'00\'A0 the price of wire rod on the Chinese market is not determined in accordance with market principles, as producers of such input receive significant support from the Chinese government, according to the "Report of the Final Determination of the \uc2 U.S. Department of Commerce\uc2  on Imports of China's Carbon and Alloy Steel Wire" of November 13, 2014. 110.The Secretariat analyzed the information provided by \u160\'00\'A0 the National Producers and required them to submit arguments and evidence attesting that, in the production and sale of steel cables, manufactured by companies in the sector or industry in China, cost and price structures prevail that are not determined in accordance with market principles, pursuant to paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO and Article 48 of the RLCE, an explanation of the similarity between the technology used in the production process of steel cables in China and Mexico, the cost structure of steel cables, the participation of steel cable production factors, will demonstrate the distortion in these factors by government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  , point out how the implementation of public policies in China creates distortions in the allocation of resources in the value chain and this affects the determination of the costs and prices of steel cables, demonstrate how the Chinese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the steel wire and steel wire industry distorts the production chain, costs and prices of steel cables, in an up-to-date manner or, where appropriate, to justify why they are in force during the review period, as noted in points 22 and 24 of this Resolution. 111. \u160\'00\'A0 In response to the request, they noted that the information and evidence submitted is the most recent and corresponds to the information reasonably available to them. They added that China officially practices a socialist market economy, which is set out in various provisions of the Constitution, as well as in the General Program of the Communist Party Constitution, and that subsequently, these guidelines are translated into essential laws, plans and regulations that define and guide the situation of economic actors in China's market. In this regard, they argued and presented the following:a. based on articles 6 and 7 of China's Constitution, as well as on the General Programme of the Communist Party Constitution, China will be in the primary stage of socialism for a long time. The basis of China's socialist economic system is socialist public ownership of the means of production, i.e. the ownership of all people and collective ownership by workers; b. in the WTO Secretariat Report on China's Trade Policy Review of 12 October 2016, and according to the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document "Significant Distortions of the Chinese Economy" (SWD/2017) 483 final/2, of 20 December 2017, ("\uc2 European Commission\uc2  Document"), it was noted that the form of government organization in China has a structure of socialist market economy; (c) According to China's June 2018 Trade Policy Review, it remains preserved that the State and communist party wish to strengthen and expand through the creation of national champions, a comprehensive and sophisticated economic planning system, an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  government policy in the economy to implement these plans through the use of a wide range of tools, including catalog orientation, investment screening and financial incentives, among others; d. Some of the conclusions of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  Document refer to the Chinese Constitution's clearness that China practices a socialist market economy, that the state-owned economy is the main force, and when it comes to the private economy, the State does not merely encourage and support it, but guides it. Moreover, the single economic system gives the State, as well as the Communist Party, a decisive role in the economy. The basic characteristics of the socialist market economy are: (i) dominant state ownership, which the State and the Communist Party wish to strengthen and expand through the creation of national champions; ii. a comprehensive and sophisticated system of economic planning, and (iii). an \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  government policy in the economy to implement the plans through the use of a wide range of tools, including catalog orientation, investment screening and financial incentives, among others, leading to non-market-based resource allocations and the creation of overcapacity in many sectors; e. in the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  Document, the assets of foreign-owned entities centrally controlled by the Chinese government, both industrial and non-industrial, are estimated to be around US$5.6 trillion in assets, with another US$690 billion abroad, while total non-financial assets of SOEs at the central and subcentral levels exceed US$16 trillion.\u160\'00\'A0Only in terms of the industrial sector, according to the latest figures published by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (IMF), china's SOE's is estimated to account for 40% of total industrial corporate assets and more than half of total corporate debt; f. on the website of the State-owned Asset Supervision and Administration Committee of the State Council (SASAC, State-owned Assets Supervision and Administration Commission of the State Council, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://en.sasac.gov.cn/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://en.sasac.gov.cn/}}}\uc2 , mentions that a significant percentage of companies operate under the ownership, control, supervision or guidance of chinese authorities' policies, including several steel, wire and wire producers, main inputs of the subject merchandise; g. The book "How State-owned Enterprise Drag on Economic Growth Theory and Evidence from China" by Ruiming Liu examines that, during the transition era, the large number of beneficiaries of small- and medium-scale SOE's was created in a special market environment of asymmetric competition, in which the upstream factor market monopolized, while the downstream product market was in competition. Similarly, the monopoly of major government-owned enterprises in the factor market gave them the privilege of applying a stronger monopoly price, which amounted to an invisible tax, i.e. an invisible financial subsidy; h. land is owned by the state (collectively owned rural land and state-owned urban lands), thus allocating landdepends solely on the State that can pursue specific political objectives rather than free-market principles. In fact, there is evidence of the direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the State in the allocation of land or rights of use, in accordance with the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  Document; (i) the same Document states that China is currently the world's largest energy producer. About 50% of the generation capacity is state-owned, as well as the entire transmission network, and that 21 such enterprises, controlled by SASAC, are active in the energy sector; j. they added that, as part of the energy market reforms, central pricing was gradually withdrawn, however, prices are not yet market-based, as they remain state-controlled. One of the most important issues is how prices differ for different industries; differentiated prices exist for customers who consume large amounts of energy, or that it is used in periods of low activity; as well as differentiation between residential and industrial consumers, however, the price difference appears to favour certain industries. China has also subsidized coal production, triggering the construction of coal-fired power plants, to creating an excess supply of electricity from this source. Under the above, normal conditions do not prevail in the Chinese energy market, given state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its production and prices; k. with regard to capital, the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  Document notes that, although the liberalization of nominal interest rates achieved in October 2015, price signals are still not the result of free market forces, but are influenced by government-induced distortions. In fact, the proportion of loans below the benchmark rate still accounts for 45% of all loans, and the use of specific credit appears to have increased, as the proportion has grown markedly since 2015 despite worsening economic conditions. Artificially low interest rates result in insufficient prices and, consequently, excessive capital utilization; l. Raw materials and other inputs are affected, as China's government has the ability to price certain goods centrally. Although the centrally set price list has been greatly reduced, the government continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in cases where prices go against government policies. The example of the new rules governing the price of coal shows that gradual price easie can be reversed at any time. The above, in accordance with the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  Document; (m) In addition, the document states that the Chinese workforce is affected by the hukou household registration system. Only hukou holders have access to the full range of social security and public welfare benefits. Originally, this system restricted migration between rural and urban areas, although this restriction has been considerably relaxed. In 2014 a single national resident registration system was introduced, but there are different rules for obtaining a hukou, depending on the size and area of a city, in larger cities are more prohibitive rules; n. with respect to the Chinese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange rate that makes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  not widely convertible, they noted that the IMF's Annual Exchange Rate Report and Exchange Rate Restrictions for 2018 classifies restrictions into 12 categories: (i) capital transactions; (ii) capital market instruments; iii. money market instruments; iv. collective investment securities; v. derivatives; vi. commercial credits; vii. financial credits; (viii) similar warranties and instruments; ix. direct investments; x. liquidation of foreign investments; xi. real estate transactions, and xii. personal capital transactions, of which the Chinese government continues to maintain restrictions on most. In addition, they presented the Financial Times' "China's Capital Controls Dent Inbound Investment" note of April 18, 2017, which notes that Beijing began suppressing outgoing investments and preventing companies from sending capital abroad in an attempt to preserve their rapidly deteriorating foreign reserves below $3 trillion in January for the first time in five years; o. in relation to the common existence of a book set in China, they argued that Lehman Brown in the publication "Accounting & Bookkeeping in China" states that China does not have recognized international standards such as IFRS27 and US GAAP28 (International Financial Reporting Standards and US Generally Accepted Accountig Standards, respectively); p. the process of producing steel cables is intensive in capital and energy (gas and electricity), since in their production process they use furnaces and heaters for wire production and heat treatment of wires, raw materials for manufacturing (wires or wires and zinc) account for 80 and 90% of production costs, and production technology is widespread in the industry globally, regardless of whether they are integrated companies or not. In particular, Deacero is an integrated company that producesalambr\u243\'00\'F3n, the main raw material for the manufacture of the product examined, by merging scrap into an electric arc furnace; while Camesa is a non-integrated company and goes on the wire market to buy its raw material; q. To demonstrate the distortion of the cost of production in the steel sector, they explained that a company's production costs can be represented as a function that depends on the cost of inputs, so that at each level of production such a function has to be minimized. The economic objective of a company is to maximize the production function based on a given basket of goods. The cost function indicates the lowest cost of production for each possible production level, given the prices of production factors and raw materials. They added that, if the prices of inputs for manufacturing the product under review are distorted through chinese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the supply will increase artificially, therefore its domestic price will be lower than it would be in the absence of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; r. the cost function of steel cable producers in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , given a production level, can be represented as a C-C function (r, w, pl, pa, ps and pg), where "C" is the total cost of wire production;\u160\'00\'A0"r" the cost of capital;\u160\'00\'A0"w" the cost of labor;\u160\'00\'A0"pl" the rental price of the land;\u160\'00\'A0"pa" the cost of wire rod;\u160\'00\'A0"pe" the cost of electricity, and "pg" the cost of natural gas. They noted that it is necessary to show that the factorial prices (capital; labor; land; electricity, and natural gas) involved in the production of steel cables in China are distorted; s. some producers buy wire rod (main raw material) on the market, such companies are known as non-integrated. They presented a diagram of the production process of a Chinese company. On the other hand, other companies produce the steel wire in their own factories and use it as a starting raw material; these types of producers are known as integrated producers; t. provided the steel cable production cost structure during the period under review, which they developed on the basis of their own accounting records, as they stated that they did not have detailed information on the structure of production costs in China. According to its cost structure, Deacero noted that the raw material participates on average with 80% of production costs, while labor is approximately 3%; in the case of Camesa it is observed that 70% corresponds to the raw material and 13% to the labour force; u. as noted on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://angang.wspr.com.hk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://angang.wspr.com.hk/}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://eurometal.net/anyang-to-reduce-the-number-of-steel-companies/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://eurometal.net/anyang-to-reduce-the-number-of-steel-companies/}}}\uc2  websites, "11 steel companies in Anyang Henan, China, will be consolidated by four by 2020, with a special steel ratio reaching more than 30%. Anyang Steel of shagang Group and the Anyang government held talks this week on the integration of thekallanish points out. Of Anyang's 11 steel companies, there are 6 integrated plants. These are Anyang Iron & Steel, Shagang Yongxing, Fengbao Iron & Steel, Yaxin Iron & Steel, Xinpu Iron & Steel and Huixin Iron & Steel. Independent iron companies are Bosheng Steel, Xinyuan Steel and Lin Steel. The only electric arc furnace steelmaker is Boxin Steel and Linzhou Hexin Casting Industry that uses it to produce creed. The main finished products of the city are high strength wire rod for construction, high quality steel for machinery, pipe, hot rolled coil and cold rolled blade"; (v) government subsidies at the corporate level affect the wire rod, so that, if the company is integrated, the subsidy is transmitted directly, whereas, in the case of a non-integrated undertaking, it is reflected in the acquisition of the raw material at a lower price; w. An example of the above in an industry as atomized as the steel cable industry is Baosteel Group Xinjiang Bayi Iron & Steel Co. Ltd. ("Xinjiang Bayi Iron"), which is a subsidiary of Baowu Group and is a producer of steel rods, wires, hot-rolled plates, galvanized sheets and other steel products. The above according to the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.baosteel.com/group_en/contents/2763/36202.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.baosteel.com/group_en/contents/2763/36202.htm}}}\uc2 ; x. in this way the mechanism of transmission of government subsidies to subsidiaries that manufacture some products is evident, which, while they may not represent sufficient in the broad spectrum of products they manufacture, for steel cable-producing companies, such as Deacero, is relevant in their business scheme; And.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Baowu Group according to \uc2 World Steel Association\uc2  (WSA) information on its 2018 top steelmaker list is the second largest-producing steelmaker in the world, and is mostly owned by the Chinese government; z. Baowu Group companies benefit from the support provided to the group.\u160\'00\'A0The \uc2 China Baowu Steel Group Corporation\uc2  is one of 96 state-owned enterprises in accordance with SASAC, as follows from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://en.sasac.gov.cn/n_688.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://en.sasac.gov.cn/n_688.html}}}\uc2 ; aa.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Wuhan Iron and Steel Co. Ltd., Baosteel Group Nantong Co., Ltd., Baosteel and Baosteel Metal are producers of steel cables and subsidiaries of the Baowu Group and benefit from economic and financial support from the Chinese government and constitute the mechanism for translating such support to the sale price of steel cables by providing the essential raw materials for their manufacture; bb.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0to prove that the companies indicated in the previous point produce steel cables and are subsidious from the Baowu Group presented, for Wuhan Iron and Steel Co. Ltd., information they obtained from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ibaosteel.com/ibaosteel/productinfo/product_line10-1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.ibaosteel.com/ibaosteel/productinfo/product_line10-1}}}\uc2  where it is noted that, their main varieties of steel products are spring steel, cold steel, cable steel, galvanized steel wire for bridge cables, high carbon steel and welding steel, steel cables, among others;\u160\'00\'A0for Baosteel Group Nantong Co., Ltd. submitted information they obtained from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.baosteel-wire.com/en/about.asp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.baosteel-wire.com/en/about.asp}}}\uc2  where it is noted that it is dedicated to the manufacture of steel cables, for Baosteel they presented a catalogue of products within which the steel cables are located; for Baosteel Metal indicated that the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.baosteel.com/group_en/contents/2900/40050.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.baosteel.com/group_en/contents/2900/40050.html}}}\uc2  website notes that it is mainly engaged in the processing and production of various steel wires, among other things, the PC steel cable (galvanized steel wire) for bridges, andcc.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0reiterated that, under Article 5.2 of the AD Agreement, the source information to demonstrate that the Chinese steel cable industry operates under non-market conditions is one that was reasonably available. The information they submitted is the most recent and reasonably accessible and published on the subject. b. \u160\'00\'A0 Determination112. \u160\'00\'A0 For the purpose of validating the arguments that the conditions of a non-market economy with respect to the steel cable industry persist in China, the Secretariat analyzed the information provided by the National Producers described in points 81 to 111 of this Resolution. In principle, the Secretariat notes that, pursuant to paragraph 15(d) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, only Romai subparagraph (a)(ii) expired in December 2016.\u160\'00\'A0However, as a current text, subparagraph (a) and Romaite (i) of paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO remain. Subparagraph (a) provides for the possibility of applying a methodology based on prices or costs in China, of Chinese producers, or a methodology that is not based on those prices or costs. Thus, the Secretariat considers that the mere expiration of the validity of Romanite subparagraph (a)(ii) of paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO does not mean that there was no longer any possibility of using a methodology that was not based on a strict comparison with domestic prices or costs in China. 113. \u160\'00\'A0 Indeed, the methodological basis for determining price comparability in anti-dumping proceedings investigating products of Chinese origin is expressly contained in paragraph 15(a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, as well as, like Romaite (i), has not expired. Pursuant to subparagraph (a), there is a legal possibility to use producers' prices or costs in China, or to use a methodology that is not based on strict price or cost separation in China. 114. \u160\'00\'A0 It should be noted that, in the present proceedings, no Chinese exporters appeared.\u160\'00\'A0Consequently, the livelihood that in China and, in particular, the branch producing steel cables prevails the conditions of a non-market economy with respect to the manufacture, production and sale of that product, in accordance with paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, is subject to the analysis of the arguments and evidence provided by the National Producers. 115. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat considered that there was a legal basis for evaluating the Proposal of The National Producers to consider China as a non-market economy in manufacturing, production and sale of steel cables, in accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the RLCE and, consequently, to analyse the provenance of applying the substitute country methodology, mainly for the following considerations:(a. with respect to the exchange rate), the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not freely convertible, since it reflects \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls and capital flow barriers granted by the Chinese government. In addition, the IMF's Annual Exchange Rate Report and Exchange Rate Restrictions for 2018 indicates that China continues to maintain restrictions in most of the 12 categories eded in point 111(n) of this Resolution; b. with regard to labour, it is noted that workers in China do not have freedom of association, nor of negotiating their wages, because the Chinese government provides for such restrictions in its legislation, where they can only join trade unions under the tutelage of the Communist Party. In addition, the workforce does not receive the payment of benefits, because it is subject to a residence permit under the hukou labour system. The above, as noted in the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  Document; c. The National Producers presented evidence relating to steel cables and the main input for their manufacture, as well as the various ways in which the Chinese government directly or indirectly supports the production of steel cables, such as:(i. since the process of producing steel cables is intensive in capital and energy (gas and electricity), according to the information in the administrative record, it was noted that the State continues to influence markets, and this effect is reflected in \u160\'00\'A0 the existence of artificially low interest rates; the steel industry continues to benefit from incentives and supports,such as: preferential credits, credit guarantees and tax refunds; specifically there are wire-producing companies (main input of steel cables), whose financial situation benefited by the Chinese government through the granting of credits, under the promise of paying the interest owed by the company;\u160\'00\'A0debt restructuring, ensuring the payment of liabilities; granting money to the company; pressure on creditors to reduce the company's debt and forgiveness of it.\u160\'00\'A0These companies are Bohai, Chongqing, Valin, Dongbei and Sinosteel; (ii) in addition, according to the information provided by Deacero and Camesa, it is noted that wire rod, a raw material representing between 80 and 90% of the production costs in the manufacture of steel cables, operate under the ownership, control and supervision of the State; in particular, in accordance with the arguments of national producers regarding the integration of enterprises and the transmission of government support from the group to their subsidiaries, the Secretariat noted that the Baowu Group, a state-owned company, which is the second largest-produced steel mill in the world, has as its subsidiary Xinjiang Bayi Iron, a producer of wire rod, and also owns Wuhan Iron and Steel Co. Ltd., Baosteel Group Nantong Co., Ltd., Baosteel and Baosteel Metal, producers of steel cables, therefore it is possible to infer that the supports the group receives are moved through the value chain, until it reaches the steel cables, resulting in a subvaluation of the costs and prices of steel cables. The foregoing, as described in point 111(s) and (b) of this Resolution and corroborated by the Secretariat on the SASAC {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://en.sasac.gov.cn/directory.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://en.sasac.gov.cn/directory.html}}}\uc2  website, as well as with the paragraph 111(v) to (x) of this Resolution; iii. According to the "\uc2 U.S. Department of Commerce\uc2 's Final Determination Report on China's Carbon Steel Wire imports" 2014, dumping margins and subsidies above 100% are reported. While this element alone is not definitive for a positive determination in the present proceedings, by analysing together the evidence ad provided with respect to wire rod, there are sufficient elements to note that there are policies of government support on such primalmatter; (iv) the Secretariat also identified distortions in the allocation of other raw materials and inputs that also affect the allocation of resources in cable production, such as: (i) land, which is state-owned and its allocation depends solely on the State;\u160\'00\'A0(ii) energy, in which the State controls through SASAC 50% of the generating capacity and the transmission network, in addition to the fact that there are no market conditions in the prices, even though central pricing has been phased out, and (iii) raw materials, in which the State has the capacity to centrally set prices that go against government policies, such as coal, which has triggered the construction of coal-fired power plants, to create an excess supply of electricity from this source,and v. on the basis of the above, the Secretariat notes that there are support policies that translate into regulatory platforms , public policies or direct government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  that selectively discriminate between companies, which impact price formation, whether by repressing capital and energy costs, restrictions or the absence of land-owned markets; d. foreign investment in China is restricted because China uses an approval process that requires foreign companies to have a Chinese partner, forcing them to transfer technology and comply with export or local content requirements, if they want their investment approved, as reported in documents "2016 USTR Report to Congress on China's WTO Compliance" and "Comments Regarding Foreign Trade Barriers To U.S.Exports for 2017", prepared by AISI; e.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0China does not have generally accepted accounting principles, or recognized international standards such as IFRS27 and US GAAP28, according to the publication "Accounting & Bookkeeping in China", andf. the financial situation of the Chinese steel industry is distorted, as the government grants incentives and support to it, such as: preferential credits, credit guarantees and tax refunds, among others, which distorts the financial situation and cost structure of the Chinese steel industry, in accordance with the document "Comments Regarding Foreign Trade Barriers To U.S. Exports for 2017", prepared by the AISI. As noted above, this was observed in wire producers, the main raw material, used in the manufacture of deaero cables. 116. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat carried out the analysis of the information, arguments and evidence provided by the National Producers and contained in the administrative file of the case. On the basis of this information, in accordance with the justifications and, where appropriate, updates thereof, the Secretariat identified elements indicative of distortions in macroeconomic variables such as exchange rate, labour, foreign investment and accounting, based on the arguments and evidence provided by the National Producers. It also had elements that generate the presumption that in companies producing steel fuels in China, cost and price structures prevail that are not determined according to market principles, because distortions in the market were identified of factors affecting resource allocation in steel cable production and interfering with the determination of the costs and prices of the productive factors in which it is intensive. This is based on public policies that selectively discriminate between companies and provide them with support or transfer resources, the participation in the industry of SOEs producing state-owned wire rods, raw material representing between 80 and 90% of the cost of producing steel cables, and the transmission of distortions from those companies to the subsidiaries that manufacture the product under review. 117.As noted, and in accordance with \u160\'00\'A0 paragraph 15(a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 second paragraph of the RLCE, the Secretariat proceeded to consider the proposal to use a country with China's substitute market economy for the purposes of calculating normal value. c. \u160\'00\'A0 Substitute country selection118. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers proposed the United States as China's substitute country to determine normal value. To demonstrate that there are market conditions in that country in the steel cable industry, they indicated that the price of the product under review, as well as its inputs, is determined by the free participation of bidders and plaintiffs, there are no restrictions on the entry or exit of that product or the inputs used for its manufacture, nor is there government interference in the decisions of the steel cable sector, nor policies imposing price controls or quotas on production, marketing, import or export. They added that the criteria by which it is possible to know when facing a market economy are contained in the second paragraph of Article 48 of the RLCE, and presented the following arguments and evidence to justify the selection of the substitutecountry: a. that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  be widely convertible into international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\u160\'00\'A0They submitted the report of the Bank for International Payments, obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rbnz.govt.nz/-/media/ReserveBank/Files/News/2019/Statistical-Release-TriennialSurvey-2019.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.rbnz.govt.nz/-/media/ReserveBank/Files/News/2019/Statistical-Release-TriennialSurvey-2019.pdf}}}\uc2 , consulted on 9 February 2020; this report indicates that the US dollar has widespread convertibility and high demand, in such a way that it is the most widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions; b. wages should be established through free negotiation between workers and employers. According to the report of the American Federation of Labor and Congress of Industry Organizations (\uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 ), obtained from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/what-unions-do/empower-workers/collective-bargaining"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://aflcio.org/what-unions-do/empower-workers/collective-bargaining}}}\uc2  website, it is clear thatUnited States, wages are established by free negotiation between workers and employers; in no legal order is otherwise provided; free recruitment is entirely free and is governed by the principle of autonomy of people's will as supreme law. Moreover, freedom of association exists and practices in the United States and collective bargaining of working conditions between employers and employees is given as a system, without the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such processes; c. industry decisions under price, cost and supply research, including raw materials, technology, production, sales and investment, are taken in response to market signals and without significant interference from the State. They noted that fluctuations in US cost prices for raw materials and steel products respond to economic circumstances or phenomena linked to movements in their respective markets. They presented information from various publications analyzing the price movement of the inputs of the goods under review, corresponding to the metals industry, as well as electrical energy, obtained from the internet pages {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.metalbulletin.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.metalbulletin.com}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.crugroup.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.crugroup.com}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://business.directenergy.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://business.directenergy.com}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.plymouthenergy.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.plymouthenergy.com}}}\uc2 , in which it can be seen that input prices are affected by factors such as supply and demand; d. foreign investment and co-investments with foreign firms are permitted. According to the document prepared by \uc2 Hogan Lovells\uc2  entitled "Doing Business the United State 2019" it can be concluded that in the United States the investment regime is open, foreign private investment is allowed in all sectors except for national security reasons and in very few sectors is restricted. Foreign investors can be established through commercial companies and have branches throughout the country; e. that the industry under investigation exclusively owns a set of ledgers that are used for all purposes, and that are audited in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles. They noted that only one set of ledgers is used in the United States; However, depending on the structure, size and practices of each particular company, there can be multiple ways to report this information as long as they start from that single accounting source depending on the type of user to whom the information is addressed.\u160\'00\'A0They submitted information obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.thomaspc.com/blog/why-do-companies-have-a-second-set-of-books"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.thomaspc.com/blog/why-do-companies-have-a-second-set-of-books}}}\uc2  which corresponds to the firm Thomas, Thomas & Thomas P.C. The Secretariat confirmed that the firm conducted tax consultancies; documentary support points out that it is a common and legal practice especially for large companies, that various reports are maintained for different users, for example, one for tax purposes and others to inform investors about the financial health of thecompany, and f. that the production costs and financial situation of the industry under investigation do not suffer distortions in relation to asset depreciation, bad debts, barter trade and debt compensation payments or other factors deemed relevant. They noted that in the market for factors such as the product market, decisions by U.S. manufacturers are entirely free, respond to the free determination of supply and demand, and have no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or interference, direct or indirect from the State.\u160\'00\'A0As proof of the above, they presented profiles of various financial institutions, including: the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the United States, private banks and stock exchanges, which allows the Secretariat to assume that there is no bartering system and that the United States has a modern financial system that works under market principles. They presented the following websites to support their saying: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/structure-federal-reserve-system.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.federalreserve.gov/aboutthefed/structure-federal-reserve-system.htm}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://disfold.com/top-us-banks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://disfold.com/top-us-banks/}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.investopedia.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.investopedia.com}}}\uc2 . i. \u160\'00\'A0 The United States is a producer of119 steel cables. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers provided the production process in the United States from one of the leading steel cable production companies in that country. The Secretariat consulted the website provided and confirmed that the company was one of the largest producers of the goods examined, and provided the websites of 5 of the largest cable-producing companies in that country. ii. Similarity in the production process120. \u160\'00\'A0 To demonstrate similarity in the production process, they presented a diagram showing the different stages in the manufacturing process of steel cables in the United States and China, in addition, a table prepared with information from steel companies in both the United States and China. The table shows that the product manufactured in the United States is similar to the product under review, since both products have the same physical and chemical specifications, both are produced from the same inputs, the main input being the steel wire, and both are used for the same uses and functions. They added that the production process of both countries is mainly developed through the use of machinery, that is, it is a capital-intensive industry of mature and similar technology. They presented the internet pages corresponding to the production companies. iii. Availability of major inputs121.\u160\'00\'A0The National Producers noted that the United States and China are producers of steel wire rod, which is the base input for the manufacture of steel cables. They added that energy is also another important input in the production process and that both countries have this resource to supply their respective local demands. For each of the countries they presented statistical information on the steel wire obtained from the report "Steel-long-products-monitor-2019-july-prodcons", through the CRU Group website and the production and consumption of energy obtained them from theStatistical Yearbook of the website of the consultancy Enerdata Global Energy ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://yearbook.enerdata.net"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://yearbook.enerdata.net}}}\uc2 ). d. \u160\'00\'A0 Determination122. \u160\'00\'A0 The third paragraph of Article 48 of the RLCE states that a substitute country shall mean a third country with a market economy similar to the exporting country with a non-market economy. In this case, the Secretariat had elements that underpinned that the United States was a market economy country as the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely convertible into international markets; according to the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  in the United States, freedom of association exists and is practiced, allowing wages to be established through free negotiation between workers and employers; the prices of raw materials used in the manufacture of steel cables, are due to supply and demand; the investment regime is open, as private investment is allowed in all sectors; although it is a common and legal practice especially for large companies in the United States, that various reports are kept aimed at different users, companies in that country only use a set of ledgers, and the information provided regarding the financial system allows to assume that it works under market principles, as well as there is no bartering system. The Secretariat also analyzed the similarity between the substitute country and the exporting country, which would be reasonably defined, so that normal value in the exporting country could be approximated on the basis of domestic price in the substitute country, considering economic criteria. 123.The Secretariat conducted an analysis of the \u160\'00\'A0 information rated in points 118 to 121 of this Resolution provided by the National Producers to consider the United States as China's substitute country, based on the elements set out in the third paragraph of Article 48 of the RLCE. He noted:a. both countries manufacture steel cables and employ a similar, capital-intensive production process. The machinery used is automatic and mature technology; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0China and the United States have availability of major inputs for the manufacture of steel cables, such as wire rod and energy,and c. from what isit can be reasonably inferred that the intensity in the use of factors in the production of steel cables is similar in both countries. 124.Based on what was described in the previous points of this Resolution and in \u160\'00\'A0 accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the LLCE, the Secretariat confirmed the selection of the United States as China's substitute country for the purposes of calculating normal value in this proceeding. e. \u160\'00\'A0 Domestic prices in the United States125. \u160\'00\'A0 In order to calculate normal value in the United States, the National Producers submitted price references on that cable market similar to those presented in the quotation rated in point 66 of this Resolution, which indicated, in the period under review, come from one of the world's leading manufacturers of steel cables and correspond to \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out in the ordinary course of business, and should therefore be considered normal commercial transactions. They added that price references comprise the entire billing of steel cables by the supplier company and come directly from its accounting system. 126.In this regard, the Secretariat made an information request for The National Producers to submit \u160\'00\'A0 documentary support for the price references used in the calculation of normal value for the domestic market of the United States. Camesa provided an email from the production company providing the price references, with average prices selling steel cables for the period under review. The Secretariat confirmed that the company was a manufacturer of steel cables. 127.Subsequently, for the period of arguments and supplementary \u160\'00\'A0 testing, the Secretariat required the National Producers to submit for the U.S. domestic market prices comparable to the 15 types of cables identified with export \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Mexico, in order to make an equitable comparison between the export price and normal value in accordance with Article 2.4 of the AD Agreement. It should be noted that the types of cables required include black and galvanized finish. 128.In response to the request, the National Producers submitted price information on the United States internal market \u160\'00\'A0 of 6 steel cable constructions comparable to those requested, corresponding to the period examined at the ex-factory level, which they noted, obtained from the sales made by the same production company from which it obtained the references rated in point 125 of this Resolution. 129. \u160\'00\'A0 With regard to the remaining 9 types of steel cables, they noted that the manufacturer from which they obtained the price references rated in the previous point did not manufacture or sell steel cables for these types of cables. They added that they identified that 2 of these 9 types of cables remaining could correspond to electrical cables that are not commercially identified in the steel cable industry market. 130. \u160\'00\'A0 Derived from the foregoing, the National Producers made for the remaining 9 types of cables:a. a price estimate for 7 types of cables, based on the pricing and cost information of steel cables marketed by the manufacturer previously referred to in the United States domestic market, considering constructions and diameters closer to those requested by the Secretariat; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0although they considered that the other 2 types of product were not under consideration, they presented the estimated price of 1 of these types of cables,and (c) accordingly, the National Producers presented the price estimate of 8 types of steel cables that they considered comparable to those exported to Mexico. As documentary support they submitted an email of February 12, 2020. 131. \u160\'00\'A0 With regard to the above, the Secretariat considered that, in the case of no sales of 9 of the 15 types of cables requested, the proposal submitted by the National Producers therefore determined:(a) to take into account for the calculation of normal value the price estimate for the 7 types of cables rated in point 130 (a) of this Resolution, taking into account that the National Producers made such an estimate taking into account the variable costs of goods which they considered closest to the types of cables exported to Mexico and which were requested by the Secretariat; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0with respect to the 2 types of cables identified by the National Producers as a non-subject product, the Secretariat considered that there were no elements to be considered in such a way, since the information from various import requests and accompanying import documentation made it possible to identify that they correspond to steel cables, and it was not observed that these types of cables were electrical in type. For these types, the Secretariat considered:(i) for one of the 2 types, it accepted the estimate proposed by the National Producers as it took into account variable costs, and(ii).\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0for the remaining type of product, it obtained the average price in the U.S. domestic market by average galvanized cables, as it is the type of steel cable finish they did not present. The average was obtained from both the types of cables rated in point 128 and 130(a) of this Resolution. c. with the above, the Secretariat obtained prices for the remaining 9 types of steel cables. 132.Considering the prices and methodologies described in the previous \u160\'00\'A0 points, the Secretariat had information on the 15 types of steel cables comparable to those exported to Mexico, in accordance with Article 2.4 of the AD Agreement. f. \u160\'00\'A0 Determination133. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the information provided by the National Producers and the methodologies described in points 125 to 132 of this Resolution, the Secretariat calculated the normal value for the types of steel cables in dollars per kilogram on the United States market, in accordance with Articles 2.1 of the AD Agreement, 31 of the LCE, 39 and 40 of the L.L.L. g. \u160\'00\'A0 Prices in the domestic market of China134. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat analyzed the evidence submitted by the National Producers with respect to the prices of steel cables in China's domestic market, however, it considered not to use them in this proceeding, since, as stated in points 112 to 117 of this Resolution, the evidence establishes that the conditions of a non-market economy prevail in china's steel cable industry, and it is therefore reasonable to use the United States as China's substitute country for the purposes of calculating normal value, as noted in points 122 to 124 of this Resolution. 135.Notwithstanding the foregoing point, the Secretariat conducted an analysis of the information submitted by the National Producers with respect to domestic prices in China in the \u160\'00\'A0 administrative record, noting:(a) the National Producers submitted two price quotes for the Chinese domestic market in the first period of offering evidence, of which they noted that they are complementary to each other, and correspond to prices of steel cable producers in that country at the FOB level; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0they stated that the prices contained in both quotations correspond to normal commercial transactions, since those prices are those that prevail widely in the wholesale market in China, in the ordinary course of business between bidders and independent plaintiffs, so they should be considered a reasonable basis of normal value, in addition to being the types of steel cable with the highest volume of marketing volume in the Chinese market. Since the prices obtained correspond to information outside the period under review, they were adjusted using the China National Producer Price Index (INPPCh), which they obtained from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ycharts.com/indicators/china_producer_price_index"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://ycharts.com/indicators/china_producer_price_index}}}\uc2 ; c. National Producers adjusted land freight prices on Chinese territory, as these prices are reported at foB level. howdocumentary support presented the information contained in a quote from a carrier. The Secretariat corroborated the company's data on its website, verified its profile, as well as the different services it provided on the market, which were in the quote referred to. It also noted that it is a recognized company with experience in the international freight transport service; d. for the calculation of land freight in China, the National Producers calculated the average cost in dollars per kilogram, corresponding to the transport of the producer's plant, supplier of one of the quotes indicated in paragraph (a) of this Resolution, located in the city of Tongzhou road, Jiangsu to the Port of Quindao in China; the cost of land freight divided it by the volume normally carried in a 20-foot container, considering an average load of 19,950 kilograms. Since the quote is outside the period under review, they adjusted the rates using the INPPCh they obtained from the Internet{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ycharts.com/indicators/china_producer_price_index"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://ycharts.com/indicators/china_producer_price_index}}}\uc2 ; e. It should be noted that, in the case of one of the contributions, no information was available from the company that provided the prices to the National Producers, even after the Secretariat required additional information, only noted that the information provided came from domestic sources in China obtained through its status as a customer and participating agent in that market. In addition, the National Producers did not submit information that the price references correspond to normal commercial transactions, as they stated, and just pointing out that quotation prices are those that prevail widely in China's wholesale market in the ordinary course of business between bidders and independent plaintiffs, and should therefore be considered a reasonable basis of normal value for the purposes of these proceedings, if China has been regarded as a market economy, is not sufficient; (f) in addition to the foregoing, notwithstanding the secretariat requiring national producers to provide prices in China's domestic market for the 15 types of cables identified in the export price, as well as information necessary to bring prices to an ex-factory level, they only submitted price references for 9 of the 15 types of steel cables requested that they considered comparable to those exported to Mexico, 3 of which were obtained from the quotes rated in point 125 of this Resolution; for 6 more types of cables, they made a price estimate based on the cable information contained in the quotations at larger diameters and construction, which they considered similar to those requested; (g) for the remaining 6 types of cables requested by the Secretariat, they added that 2 types of cables could correspond to electrical cables that were not commercially identified in the steel cable industry market, so they did not provide prices for this type of cable. For 4 more they were not able to obtain specific references; (h) with respect to the 2 types of cables scribed in the previous subparagraph, the Secretariat reiterates what is described in point 131(b) of this Resolution,and (i) with respect to the estimate made for 6 types of cables, the Secretariat considers that the proposed methodology was not appropriate, because in accordance with Article 56 of the RLCE, where none of the goods sold in the country of origin were physically the same as the exported goods, the prices of domestic sales should be approved at the prices of export sales, making a difference adjustment considering the variable costs of production of both types of goods in order to make a fair comparison between the export price and normal value , contrary to what national producers propose, it cannot be assumed that price differences are proportionally equivalent to differences in variable costs, which are determined considering production and not sales. 136. \u160\'00\'A0 In accordance with point 134 of this Resolution, notwithstanding the analysis of the information made, the Secretariat considered not to use the evidence submitted by the National Producers with respect to the prices of steel cables in China's domestic market, because it identified elements indicating that conditions of a non-market economy prevail in the steel cable industry in that country, and it was therefore appropriate to use the United States as China's substitute country for the purposes of calculating normal value. h. \u160\'00\'A0 Conclusion137. \u160\'00\'A0 According to the information and methodologies described above, and based on Articles 11.3 and 11.4 of the AD Agreement, the second paragraph 54, the last paragraph and 89 F of the LCE, the Secretariat analyzed the export price and normal value information, and determined that there were sufficient elements to support the removal of the countervailing quota, the continuation or recurrence of dumping on exports to Mexico of steel cables originating in China would occur. H. \u160\'00\'A0 Analysis of continuation or recurrence of damage138. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat analysed the information provided by the parties to the investigation procedure, as well as the one that it itself arrived, consisting of the list of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the SIC-M, including import pediments, and which works on the administrative record, in order to determine whether there are elements to support the elimination of the final countervailing quota imposed on imports of steel cables originating in China, would result in continuation or recurrence of injury to the domestic steel cable industry. 139.The analysis of economic and financial indicators includes the information provided by the National Producers, since both undertakings constitute the domestic industry of the product similar to that under \u160\'00\'A0 review, as determined in point 143 of this Resolution. To carry out this analysis, the Secretariat considered the information for the period from 1 July 2014 to 30 June 2019, which included both the period analyzed and the period under review, as well as the estimates for the period from 1 July 2019 to 30 June 2020. Unless otherwise indicated, the behaviour of economic and financial indicators for a given year or period is stoned with respect to the previous immediate equivalent period. 1. \u160\'00\'A0 Domestic industry140. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers stated that, as a whole, they are the only two national companies producing steel cables. 141. \u160\'00\'A0 FOR its part, CANACERO submitted a letter inging the participation of camesa and deacero companies in domestic production during the periods July 2014 to June 2015, July 2015 to June 2016, July 2016 to June 2017, July 2017 to June 2018 and July 2018 to June 2019.\u160\'00\'A0According to this information, Camesa and Deacero accounted for 58% and 42% of domestic production respectively in the period July 2018 to June 2019. 142. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers noted that, during the period under review, they did not import the goods under review. In this regard, the Secretariat analysed the list of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of siC-M for tariff fractions 7312.10.01, TIGIE 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99 and noted that they did not import steel cables originating in China during the period under review. 143. \u160\'00\'A0 On the basis of the analysis of the information in the administrative file, In the light of the above points, the Secretariat determined that Camesa and Deacero constitute the domestic industry, by meaning all domestic production of steel cables similar to that under review, in accordance with Articles 4.1 and 5.4 of the LCE Ad Agreement, 40 and 50, as well as 60 and 61 of the LLCE. 2. \u160\'00\'A0 International market144. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers noted that they did not have specific information at their disposalinternational steel cable industry, providing production statistics and apparent consumption of the steel wire industry, primary input and main element of the cost of the product under review, obtained from CRU Group. 145.Based on the information described in the previous point, they stated that China has been the world's largest and largest producer of this raw material, with its production volume \u160\'00\'A0 in 2018 accounting for more than 65% of world production and was more than 50 times larger than Mexico's production volume, which ranks ninth in the major production countries of that input. In addition, China is among the top ten consumer countries worldwide for this raw material. 146.Further, on the basis of \u160\'00\'A0 general cable statistics, of TradeMap subheading 7312.10, they indicated that China is the main cable exporting country, which makes clear its large production and cable export capacity, in general and, therefore, of the cables under review. 147.The Secretariat analyzed the information submitted by \u160\'00\'A0 the National Producers, noting that, in 2018, the world's leading steel wire-producing countries were China, Germany, Japan, the Commonwealth of Independent States, Italy and South Korea, while the main consumer countries were China, Germany, Italy, Japan, the United States and the Commonwealth of Independent States. Mexico ranked ninth in the world's leading producer and consumer countries. In addition, it noted that, in the period 2014 to 2018, the world's leading cable exporting countries were China, Korea, Thailand, Italy, Malaysia and Germany, while the main importing countries were the United States, Germany, Korea, Japan, Thailand and Poland. Mexico ranked 30th and 18th, respectively. 148. \u160\'00\'A0 For the analysis of the international market, the Secretariat came from statistics on world exports and imports under subheading 7312.10, available in the United Nations Commodity Trade Statistics Database for the period 2014 to 2018, as it is the subheading corresponding to the most restricted product range contained in steel cables. This was because the Secretariat noted that no updated figures were available for 2019 in UN Comtrade statistics. 149. \u160\'00\'A0 Data indicate that world exports fell 5% cumulatively from 3,062.8 to 2,894.8 thousand tonnes from 2014 to 2018. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that in 2018 China was the world's largest exporter, concentrating 39% of the total, followed by Korea with 8%, Vietnam with 3.8%, Germany with 3.7% and Malaysia with 3.5%. Mexico ranked 30th with a 0.6% stake in the total. 150. \u160\'00\'A0 For its part, world imports increased cumulatively from 0.5% from 2,829 to 2,842.3 thousand tonnes, from 2014 to 2018. It was noted that, in 2018, the main importers were the United States with a 17% share of world imports, followed by Germany with 6%, Thailand with 4.3%, Korea with 4.2% and Poland with 3.6%. 3. Domestic market151. \u160\'00\'A0 In accordance with point 143 of this Resolution, Camesa and Deacero are the only steel cable production companies on the domestic market. 152.The National Producers noted that steel cable, both domestically manufactured \u160\'00\'A0 and imported, mainly supplies secondary industries, such as the oil industry, construction, communications, manufacturing, the electricity industry, and to a lesser extent the agricultural and fisheries sector. 153. \u160\'00\'A0 They stated that, during the period analysed, the domestic steel cable market was mainly supplied by domestic industry, as while the market contracted, the imports examined were reduced due to the countervailing quota, and total imports were also reduced. In addition, exports increased and dampened the contraction of the domestic market. 154. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the economic indicators provided by the domestic industry and the import figures obtained from the list of \b \highlight22\uc2 OPERATIONS\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the SIC-M, as indicated in points 164 and 165 of this Resolution, for the periods July to June 2014 to 2019, the Secretariat noted that the domestic steel cable market, measured through Apparent National Consumption (ANC), calculated as total domestic production plus least export imports, recorded a decline during the period analyzed. 155. \u160\'00\'A0 Indeed, decreased by 40% in july 2015-June 2016, increased by 2% in July 2016-June 2017, decreased by 14% in the July 2017-June 2018 period and increased by 14% in the period under review, resulting in a cumulative decrease of 39% in the period analyzed. In this regard, the performance of the ANC components was as follows:a. total imports decreased by 59% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased by 5% in July 2016-June 2017, decreased by 28% in the July 2017-June 2018 period and increased by 16% in the period under review, accumulating a 64% decrease in the period analyzed; b. during the period analysed, total imports of steel cables were imported from 59 countries. During the period under review, the main supplier was Korea, which accounted for 32% of total imports, followed by the United States, Thailand, Turkey and China, with 13%, 9%, 7% and 6%, respectively; c. domestic production declined by 21% and 2% in the July 2015-June 2016 and July 2016-June 2017 periods, respectively; an increase of 16% in the period July 2017- June 2018 and 9% in the period under review. So that it recorded a cumulative decrease of 2% in the period analyzed, andd. total exports decreased by 36% in the july 2015-June 2016 period and 9% in july 2016-June 2017, increased by 88% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 1% in the period under review, recording cumulative growth of 11% in the period analyzed. 156. \u160\'00\'A0 Moreover, domestic domestic production oriented to the domestic market (calculated as total domestic production minus export sales) declined during the period analysed of 8%, a 15% drop in the July 2015-June 2016 period, an increase of 1% in the july 2016-June 2017 period, a 4% decrease in the July 2017-June 2018 period and a 13% increase in the period under review. 4. \u160\'00\'A0 Actual and potential analysis of imports157. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers stated that, during the period under review, imports of steel cables were mainly concentrated from sources of supply other than China, while the subject merchandise was imported in ever smaller volumes, with the exception of the first year, when the domestic market did not have the countervailing quota that subsequently accounted for, but not sufficiently effectively, the subject imports. 158.In considering only import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the description of which imported \u160\'00\'A0 goods included the construction of the cable, the Domestic Producers were unable to identify imports of steel cables originating in China during the period under review. They also considered transactions with a generic description. 159. \u160\'00\'A0 Considering those transactions, they identified that, except in the initial period from July 2014 to June 2015, which observed a significant volume of subject imports amounting to 85.1% of total imports, in the subsequent four periods, these imports had a relatively smaller share of 26.3%, 42.2%, 38.9% and 16.4%, which, with respect to the ANC, made a contribution of 2.51%, 3.62%, 3.76% and 1.92%.\u160\'00\'A0160. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers noted that, in the period July 2014 to June 2015, the highest volume ofimports under review were related to the absence of the countervailing quota, which provides for the greater dynamism that such imports will have in a context of the absence of the countervailing quota. 161. \u160\'00\'A0 They added that, in the period analysed, the share of total imports was reduced from the ANC from 20.9% in the period July 2014 to June 2015 to 11.7% in the period under review. 162.At the request of \u160\'00\'A0 the National Producers, CANACERO submitted its database of imports from the SAT for tariff fractions 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99 for the period analysed. In this regard, they noted that these tariff fractions of TIGIE also enter products other than that examined, for example, stainless cables, with accessories or attachments, with non-zinc coating (copper, plastic, linings and nylon), with uses and functions other than the cable examined, pre-force products, slings, screws, clamps, among others. In addition, they indicated that it is normal to use a generic and common name to identify all cables entering through such tariff fractions. 163.The National Producers therefore \u160\'00\'A0 presented a methodology for identifying imports of steel cables originating in China and other countries, where they classified import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  as described below:a. subject merchandise (MOE): the description includes cable construction; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0non-NMOE: (i) \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside the analysis period, (ii) the pediment key does not imply actual income, whether final or temporary, (iii) the country whose origin is Mexico, (iv) other products, (v) multiple products, (vi) stainless steel, (vii) zinc-free coating, (viii) cables with accessories or attachments, (ix) pre-effort products, x) plasticized electricity conductors, and(c) goods in doubt by their generic description (MD): from the latter category NMOE: (i) samples whose volume is less than 70 kilograms, and (ii) the importer's turn corresponds to the electricity, automotive or telecommunications industries, industries with uses and functions other than those of the goods examined. 164. \u160\'00\'A0 In order to validate the methodology presented by the National Producers and CANACERO, the Secretariat was reached from the list of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of SIC-M, corresponding to imports made through TARIFF fractions 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99 of tiGIE within the period analyzed. It also made requirements for both customs agents and major importers of the subject merchandise, as well as obtaining information from various internet pages related to the steel cable industry. 165.In this regard, the Secretariat analysed the methodology referred to in point 163 of this Resolution and considered that the elimination of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  less \u160\'00\'A0 than 70 kilograms was not justified. However, the criteria for not including \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  involving products other than steel cables, in whole or in part, were considered so as not to overestimate the volume of imports that correspond specifically to steel cables. It also determined to consider the criterion proposed by the National Producers, concerning the include transactions with a generic description, as it identified that 85% of the volume reviewed through the requirements to customs agents and importers, the description of which is generic, actually corresponds to the goods under review. 166. \u160\'00\'A0 With the information released in points 163 to 165 of this Resolution, the Secretariat calculated the value and volume of imports of steel cables, noting that total imports decreased by 64% over the period analyzed: they decreased by 59% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased by 5% in July 2016-June 2017, decreased by 28% in July 2017-June 2018 and increased by 16% in the period under review. 167.With regard to \u160\'00\'A0 imports originating in China, they recorded the following behaviour in the period analyzed: they decreased by 91% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased by 6% in July 2016-June 2017, decreased by 1% in july 2017-June 2018 and increased by 12% in the period under review, resulting in a cumulative decrease of 89% in the period analyzed. It follows that imports of the goods examined, despite their declining behaviour during the period analysed, continued to enter the national territory, from an 18% contribution to total imports in the period July 2014-June 2015 to 6% during the period under review. 168. \u160\'00\'A0 Consequently, imports from sources other than China were similar in behavior to that of totals: they decreased by 52% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased by 5% in July 2016-June 2017, decreased by 29% in the July 2017-June 2018 period, and increased by 16% in the period under review, resulting in a 59% decrease in the period analyzed.\u160\'00\'A0However, in relative terms, its share of total imports over the period analyzed increased from an 82% contribution in the period July 2014-June 2015 to 94% during the period under review. 169.The Secretariat analysed the share of imports of steel cables and domestic domestic production oriented to the domestic market in the ANC over the period \u160\'00\'A0 analysed and noted that:(a) total imports participated in the ANC with 56% in the period July 2014-June 2015, 39% in the period July 2015-June 2016, 40% in the period July 2016-June 2017, and 33% in the periods July 2017-June 2018 and in the period under review, respectively, which meant a decrease of 23 percentage points in the period analyzed; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0imports originating in China, for their part, reached a 10% share of the ANC in July 2014-June 2015, and 2% in the july 2015-June 2016, July 2016-June 2017, July 2017-June 2018 periods and in the period under review, respectively, so cumulatively recorded a decrease of 8 percentage points in the period analyzed; c. in terms of imports from other origins, they decreased their share of the ANC by 15 percentage points in the period analyzed, from 46% in the period July 2014-June 2015 to 32% in the period under review (37% in the period July 2015-June 2016, 38% in the period July 2016-June 2017 and 31% in the period July 2017-June 2018), andd. as a result, domestic domestic production oriented to the domestic market increased its share by 23 percentage points in the ANC in the period analysed, from a 44% to 67% share (61% in the Period July 20 15-June 2016, 60% in the period July 2016-June 2017 and 67% in the period July 2017-June 2018). 170. TheNational Producers stated that, if the countervailing quota were eliminated, there would be a sudden return on imports originating in China in a very short period, motivated by the domestic and international economic situation, the conditions of \u160\'00\'A0 Chinese demand, the oversuffendum of Chinese industry and, in particular, the low price of Chinese imports, causing negative effects on domestic production and updating recurrence of injury. 171.They estimated that \u160\'00\'A0 Chinese imports would be equivalent to 19.5% of the ANC for the projected period, 17.3% of domestic production, 28.6% of domestic production oriented to the domestic market, and 33.7% of domestic sales of domestic industry. 172. \u160\'00\'A0 To support their assertions, they submitted their projections of the volume of imports of steel cables originating in China and other origins, for the period July 2019-June 2020, as well as their respective methodology, in a countervailing quota elimination scenario. 173. \u160\'00\'A0 They considered that, if the countervailing quota for imports originating in China were eliminated, the observed conduct of those imports would be repeated, at least half of the volume observed during the periodinvestigated the ordinary procedure. This is the volume reference for imports examined in a context where the countervailing quota did not apply. 174. \u160\'00\'A0 With regard to the volume of imports from other countries, they considered their behaviour during the analysis period and therefore calculated the average annual growth rate of the period analyzed and applied it to the volume observed in the period under review. 175.The Secretariat analyzed and valued the methodology proposed by the National Producers, as described in points 173 and 174 of this Resolution and considered it acceptable, on the basis that the estimated volumes were moderate and feasible, as it was based on the level of imports of Chinese origin that existed when \u160\'00\'A0 the countervailing quota was not yet applied, and on the level of imports from other origins observed during the period analyzed. 176.In replicating the calculations that the National Producers provided on their estimates for projecting imports originating in China, in the event of eliminating the countervailing quota, the Secretariat noted that the imports under review would reach \u160\'00\'A0 volumes that would allow them to participate in the ANC of 32% in the period July 2019 to June 2020. This would reflect an increase in the market share of imports originating in China compared to the 30 percentage point review period, while domestic production targeting domestic merchandise would account for 43% of the ANC over the same period, i.e. a decrease of 24 percentage points, relative to the period under review. 177.Based on the information and results of the analysis described above, the Secretariat concluded that there were sufficient elements to support the fact that if the countervailing quota imposed on imports of steel cables originating in China were eliminated, there would be a significant increase in \u160\'00\'A0 dumped imports, which would displace domestic sales and thus achieve significant market share, which would have a negative impact on the performance of relevant economic and financial indicators in the domestic industry. 5. \u160\'00\'A0 Real and potential effects on prices178. \u160\'00\'A0 The domestic industry considered that, in the period analyzed, prices increased to the extent that it was possible to cover the increase in costs experienced by the industry. This improvement is seriously affected by the economic context that is exacerbated in the projected period, and would be significantly aggravated in a countervailing quota elimination scenario, given the projection of the expected large volume of imports at significantly reduced prices compared to domestic industry. 179.In accordance with point 158 of this Resolution, in considering only import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  whose description includes cable construction, the National Producers were unable to identify imports of steel cables originating in China during the review period. They therefore offered two estimates of their price and undervaluation margin relative to the national price, as described below:a. based on the average import price that Servicables reported at its appearance, and Camesa's domestic sales prices for similarly measured cables, domestic production estimated a 20.8% undervaluation,and b. based on a quote from a Chinese exporting producer, in the diameters and constructions most accepted and consumed in the domestic market, estimated a 32% undercutting. 180. TheNational Producers stated that the quotation referred to in the previous point is a valid reference for export prices for the purposes of this review, since that quotation contains the most accepted and consumed diameters and configurations of cables on the domestic market, and constitutes the best information available to them to document prices of cable types at the wholesale level, without omitting to note that their source comes from \u160\'00\'A0 a well-known steel cable manufacturer in China. 181.The Secretariat analysed the price behaviour of steel cables, for this purpose it considered the information in the administrative record, including domestic prices of domestic sales by the domestic industry and the prices of imports of steel cables, the Secretariat determined to use the information on \u160\'00\'A0 volumes and values obtained as described in points 163 to 165 of this Resolution, as it was the best available information. 182.Based on the information described above, the Secretariat noted that the implied average price of imports of the product under review recorded the following behaviour over the period \u160\'00\'A0 analyzed: it grew 135% in the period July 2015 to June 2016 and 16% in the period July 2016 to June 2017, and decreased by 24% in July 2017 to June 2018 and 13% in the period under review, an accumulated increase of 81% in the period analyzed. For its part, the implied average price of imports from other origins increased by 88% in the period July 2015 to June 2016, decreased by 12% in July 2016 to June 2017, increased by 34% in July 2017 to June 2018 and 3% in the period under review, accumulating a 127% increase in the period analyzed. 183. \u160\'00\'A0 Furthermore, the Secretariat noted that the average domestic sales price of the domestic industry, measured in dollars, decreased by 13% in the period July 2015 to June 2016 and 9% in July 2016 to June 2017, and grew 12% in the period July 2017 to June 2018 and 13% in the period under review, accumulating a 1% decrease in the period analyzed.\u160\'00\'A0184. In order to assess undervaluation, the Secretariat considered the plant price of domestic sales in the domestic industry and compared it to the average price recorded by imports originating in China during the period analyzed, adjusted with tariff, customs duty and customs agent costs, as well as the payment of the countervailing fee. 185.The Secretariat noted that the average price of imports originating in China was below the domestic price \u160\'00\'A0 with undercut levels of 11% in the period July 2014 to June 2015, wherea in the periods July 2015 to June 2016, July 2016 to June 2017, July 2017 to June 2018 and in the period under review, it was above 149%, 212%, 119% and 71%, respectively. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that, while the price of imports originating in China increased from the domestic price, the price difference had been significantly shortened since the period before which it was under review. 186. \u160\'00\'A0 With regard to the average price of imports from other origins, the price of the product under review stood 15%, 48%, 92% and 13% above in the periods July 2015 to June 2016, July 2016 to June 2017, July 2017 to June 2018, respectively, and 3% below in the period under review. Import and domestic product prices\par \par \uc2 Source: Information provided by Camesa, Deacero and SIC-M187.Deacero stated that any elimination or reduction of the countervailing quota would result in the recurrence or continuation of injury to the domestic industry, since, of the foreseeable massive entry of imports of the goods examined under conditions of price discrimination, and the prices of China's exports to the world that has been given below the prevailing prices in Mexico during the \u160\'00\'A0 period of validity of the countervailing quota. 188.In order to estimate the projected price at which the subject imports would be recorded, if the countervailing quota were eliminated, the Domestic Producers used the quote of the \u160\'00\'A0 Chinese exporting producer, as rated in point 179 of this Resolution, so that in the projected period, the undercutting of the subject imports would be12.5%. 189.They also considered that the prices of imports originating in \u160\'00\'A0 other countries would behave similarly to those recorded in the period of analysis, so they calculated the average annual growth rate of the period analysed and applied it to the price observed during the period under review. 190.In addition, they projected that the price of their domestic sales would \u160\'00\'A0 be reduced by 22.5% from that observed during the review period, given that the increase in chinese unfair imports, at prices significantly lower than those observed by domestic industry, would require domestic production to reduce its price by a percentage equivalent to its operating margin. 191.The Secretariat considered appropriate the methodology that the National Producers used to estimate the prices of imports of steel cables originating in China, other countries and domestic prices, since the former would reflect the prices of the most accepted and consumed diameter and construction cables in the domestic market, the latter would reflect the level of prices observed during the period under review, while domestic prices would be adjusted down \u160\'00\'A0 to compete with the product under review. 192. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat retorted the calculations that the National Producers made for their estimates and noted that, if the countervailing quota were eliminated, the price of imports originating in China would be below the domestic price by 17% for the period July 2019 to June 2020. 193.For its part, the domestic sales price of the domestic industry would be \u160\'00\'A0 down 21% in July 2019 to June 2020 compared to the period under review. 194. \u160\'00\'A0 On the basis of the information and evidence in the administrative file, as well as the results of the analysis described in the previous points of this Resolution, the Secretariat concluded that there was a well-founded likelihood that, if the countervailing quota were eliminated, imports of steel cables originating in China would supply the domestic market at such price levels, which would negatively impact domestic prices on the domestic market, as they could achieve significant undercutting levels, forcing the domestic industry to lower its prices in order to compete and increasing demand for new imports, which would have negative effects on domestic sales and their income , which would affect the profits of the domestic industry. 6. Real and potential effects on the domestic industry195. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers noted that, during the period analysed, the domestic industry operated in a context of a reduction in its domestic market, with a modest recovery in the period under review and in the period prior to it, which did not allow to achieve the market volume that was held at the beginning of the term of the compensatory quota; recovery, which is also threatened in the projected period by the paralysis of the productive activity of the national consumer sector of steel cable, caused by the COVID-19 pandemic. 196.They added that the current economic context places them in a state of vulnerability, since the domestic market is expected \u160\'00\'A0 to have a contraction in the projected period; a situation that would adversely affect domestic sales and production, which, in a circumstance of elimination of compensatory share, would substantially aggravate the domestic industry in a condition of injury that would make it unworkable to continue its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . 197. \u160\'00\'A0 In order to assess the conduct of the domestic industry over the period under review, the Secretariat considered the economic and financial indicators provided by the National Producers. To analyze financial indicators, they presented their financial statements for the fiscal years 2014 to 2018, as well as the first half of 2018 and 2019, internal. They also provided their status of costs, sales and profits from sales of similar products to the domestic and export market for the period analyzed. 198.In order to make the figures comparable to each other, the Secretariat updated the information corresponding to the financial statements, as well as that of the status of costs, sales and profits, using the \u160\'00\'A0 method of changes in the general price level, using the National Consumer Price Index determined by the National Institute of Geography and Statistics (INEGI). 199.Based on the information described in the previous two \u160\'00\'A0 points of this Resolution, the Secretariat noted that, the domestic industry recorded a contraction in production in the period analyzed in the order of 2%, mainly explained by the negative behaviour of the July 2015-June 2016 and July 2016-June 2017 periods in which 21% and 2% fell, respectively, to subsequently observe a recovery of 16% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 9% in the period under review, insufficient to reach the level of production observed in the first periodof the validity of the compensatory quota. 200. \u160\'00\'A0 For its part, domestically oriented production of the domestic industry followed conduct similar to domestic production, albeit of a greater magnitude by reducing its volume by 8% in the period analysed, being its largest reduction in the period July 2015-June 2016 with a negative behavior of 15%, increased 1% in the period July 2016-June 2017, decreased 4% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and grew 13% in the period under review. Such conduct proved insufficient to achieve the volume observed in the period where the compensatory quota began to take effect. 201.In terms of \u160\'00\'A0 market share, the Secretariat noted, on the basis of the results described in point 169 of this Resolution, that domestic production oriented to the domestic market gained 23 percentage points of participation in the ANC during the period under review, from 44% in the period July 2014-June 2015 to 67% during the review period, while imports of steel cables originating in China decreased their share of the ANC in the same periods from a contribution of 10% to 2%, respectively, and imports originating in countries other than the one investigated increased from 46% of the ANC in the period July 2014-June 2015 to 32% during the period under review. This, in a context of domestic market contraction coupled with the validity of the countervailing share to imports originating in China. 202.The performance of the volume of domestic production was reflected in the performance of \u160\'00\'A0 its total sales (to the domestic and external market), which accumulated an 8% drop in the period analyzed: they decreased by 20% in the period July 2015-June 2016, and 1% in the period July 2016-June 2017, but increased 16% in the period July 2017-June 2018, and 1% in the period under review. 203.The Secretariat noted that \u160\'00\'A0 the performance of total sales of the domestic industry was explained by the conduct of domestic sales:a. domestic sales showed a declining trend during the period analyzed: they decreased by 13% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased by 2% in July 2016-June 2017, decreased by 6% in the July 2017-June 2018 period and increased by 1% in the period under review, resulting in a cumulative decrease of 17% in the period analyzed; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0exports decreased 36% in the july 2015-June 2016 period and 9% in July 2016-June 2017, but increased 88% in the July 2017-June 2018 period and 1% in the period under review, so that they accumulated an increase of 11% in the periodanalyzed, and c. relatively, domestic sales accounted on average for 66% of domestic production in the period analyzed, indicating that the domestic industry is more oriented to the domestic market, in which it would compete with imports of the dumped product, if the domestic industry were eliminatedcompensatory fee. 204. \u160\'00\'A0 The installed capacity of the domestic industry to produce steel cables remained unchanged during the period under review. As a result of the performance of installed capacity and production, the use of the first indicator decreased by one percentage point in the period analyzed, from 44% in the period July 2014-June 2015 to 43% in the period under review. 205. \u160\'00\'A0 In that regard, Deacero considered that the period of validity of the countervailing quota has not been sufficient to counteract the damage, since conditions prevail that put the domestic industry in vulnerable condition. One of the most important is the fact that, during the term of the compensatory quota, the utilization of installed capacity did not exceed 50%. Indeed, the Secretariat was able to find that the utilization of installed capacity averaged 39% during the period under review. 206. \u160\'00\'A0 The inventories of the domestic industry remained constant for the period July 2015-June 2016, decreased by 19% and 25% in the July 2016-June 2017 and July 2017-June 2018 periods, respectively, and increased by 42% in the period under review, so cumulatively they recorded a 14% decrease in the period analyzed. 207. \u160\'00\'A0 The domestic industry's employment decreased by 8% and 6% in the July 2015-June 2016 and July 2016-June 2017 periods, respectively, but grew by 11% in the July 2017-June 2018 periods and in the period under review, respectively, which cumulatively meant a 7% increase in the period analyzed. 208. \u160\'00\'A0 Domestic industry productivity decreased by 15% in July 2015-June 2016, grew 5% and 4% in the July 2016-June 2017 and June 2017-June 2018 periods, respectively, and decreased by 2% in the period under review, a decrease of 8% in the period analyzed. 209.The performance of the domestic industry's wage mass showed a \u160\'00\'A0 20% decrease in the period July 2015-June 2016, but increased 2%, 18% and 17% in the July 2016-June 2017, July 2017-June 2018 periods and in the period under review, respectively, so cumulatively recorded a growth of 12% in the period analyzed. 210. \u160\'00\'A0 As indicated in point 197 of this Resolution, the National Producers submitted cost, sales and utility statements of similar merchandise for the domestic and export markets for the production and sale of steel cables. Because the volume of sales in the export market, relative to total volume, accounted for 37% during the period under review, and on average 31% in the period analyzed, the Secretariat analysed the effects on financial performance from sales on the export market. 211.On the one hand, in the domestic market, as a result of the behaviour of sales volumes on that market \u160\'00\'A0 and domestic prices, the Secretariat noted that sales revenues decreased by 11.2%, 2.5% and 3.1% in the July 2015-June 2016, July 2016-June 2017 and July 2017-June 2018 periods, respectively, but increased 12.6% in the period under review, reflecting a cumulative decrease of 5.5% during the period analyzed. Moreover, operating or operating costs (sales costs plus operating expenses) decreased by 7.1% and 0.4% in the July 2015-June 2016 and July 2017-June 2018 periods, respectively, but increased 7.4% and 9.2% in the July 2016-June 2017 period and during the period under review, respectively, reflecting a cumulative increase of 8.4% during the period analyzed. 212. \u160\'00\'A0 As a result of the conduct of sales revenues and operating costs in the domestic market, as indicated in the previous point, operating results decreased by 21.1%, 31% and 15% in the periods July 2015-June 2016, July 2016-June 2017 and July 2017-June 2018, respectively, but increased 30.7% in the period under review, reflecting a decrease in operating profit of 39.6% over the entire period analyzed. 213. \u160\'00\'A0 Finally, in the domestic market, operating margin decreased by 3.2, 7.5 and 2.2 percentage points in the periods July 2015-June 2016, July 2016-June 2017 and July 2017-June 2018, respectively, but increased by 2.5 percentage points in the period under review, so that cumulatively decreased by 10.4 percentage points from a margin of 28.8% to 18.4% over the entire period analyzed. 214. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers stated that prices on the domestic market were recovered only to the extent that production costs increased. They presented unit production and sales cost statements, in pesos per kilogram. The Secretariat weighed the unit cost information of the producing companies with the information on the production volumes for each of them, and obtained unit costs per kilogram from the domestic industry. The Secretariat was able to verify what was indicated by the National Producers, as prices in the domestic market increased by 12%, in real terms, and unit costs by 11% for the period under review; wheree while, for the period analyzed, domestic prices increased by 13% and unit costs by 14%. 215. \u160\'00\'A0 On the other hand, in the export market, the Secretariat noted that sales revenues in that market decreased by 34.3% and 7.3% in the July 2015-June 2016 and July 2016-June 2017 periods, respectively, but increased 1.23 times in the July 2017-June 2018 period and 8.9% during the period under review, reflecting a cumulative increase of 47.4% during the period analyzed. In this regard, operating costs decreased by 30.8% and 6.5% in the July 2015-June 2016 and July 2017-June 2018 periods, respectively, but increased 93% and 11.1% in the July 2017-June 2018 period and during the period under review, respectively, which was reflected in a cumulative increase of 38.7% during the period analyzed. 216.As a result of the conduct of sales revenues and operating costs in the export market, as noted in the previous point, operating \u160\'00\'A0 losses decreased by 5.7%, 2.9% and 48.5% in the July 2015-June 2016 periods, July 2016-June 2017 and July 2017-June 2018, respectively, but increased 56.5% in the period under review, reflecting a decrease in operating loss of 26.1% over the entire period analyzed. 217. \u160\'00\'A0 Finally, in the export market, operating margin decreased by 6.1 percentage points in the period July 2015-June 2016, decreased by 0.9 percentage points in the period July 2016-June 2017, it increased by 16.1 percentage points in the July 2017-June 2018 period, but decreased by 2.1 percentage points in the period under review, so that, cumulatively, it increased by 7 percentage points from a margin of -14% to -7% throughout the period analyzed. 218.In addition, the Secretariat assessed the return on asset investment (ROA) variables, cash flow and capital-gathering capacity, from the financial statements of the National Producers, taking into account that they consider the most restricted group or range of products including the like product, in accordance with \u160\'00\'A0 Articles 3.6 of the AD Agreement and 66 of the RLCE. 219. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers indicated that there are no investments or projects associated with the production of similar goods.\u160\'00\'A0220.As noted in the information \u160\'00\'A0 described in the following table, the ROA of the domestic steel cable industry, calculated at the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  level, was positive in all years and semesters that are part of the period analyzed. Return on investmentsindex20142015201620172018Jan Jun2018Jan Jun2019Return on investment6.9%14.2%26.1%9.7%13.2%11.4%2.9%Source: The financial statements issued and internalNational Producers.221. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the cash flow statements of the National Producers, the Secretariat noted that cash flow at the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  level reported positive behavior during the years 2014 to 2018 and the first half of 2019. The flow of operating cash reflected, for each previous year, the following movements: increase of 33.3%, increase of 4.07 times, decrease of 2.31 times and increase of 1.88 times for the years 2015, 2016, 2017 and 2018, respectively; so that by 2018 compared to 2014, it reported a 6.83-bit increase in cash flow. Meanwhile for the first half of 2019, compared to its 2018 similar, cash flow increased 3.68 times. 222.The Secretariat measured the capacity of the domestic industry to obtain the financial resources necessary to carry out \u160\'00\'A0 productive activity through solvency, leverage and debt indices. In this regard, the following behaviour was observed. Solvency indicesindex20142015201620172018Jan Jun2018Jan Jun2019Circulating reason(times)2.302.101.552.782.031.611.66Acid test(times)1.821.421.071.971.331.211.14Leverage (times)1.991.711.671.781.801.891.73debt67%63%63%64%64%65%63%Source: Development of the Secretariat based on the financial statements issued and internal to theNational Producers.223. \u160\'00\'A0 In this regard, solvency and liquidity levels retained satisfactory levels (including under the acid test, i.e. current assets minus the value of inventories, relative to short-term liabilities); in general, a relationship between current assets and short-term liabilities of 1 to 1 or higher is considered appropriate. 224. \u160\'00\'A0 The leverage index has remained unmanifiable during the years 2015 to 2018, and the first half of 2018 and 2019. Normally a proportion of the total liability with respect to book capital, less than 100% is considered to be manageable, in this case leverage levels did not show that condition. As far as the debt level or total liability ratio is reached, total to total assets remained at acceptable levels, i.e. less than 100%. 225.Based on the analysis \u160\'00\'A0 described in the previous points, the Secretariat noted that, while the countervailing quota accounted for the growth of imports originating in China in the period analyzed, some of the economic and financial indicators showed negative signs, such as declining production, domestic market-oriented production, domestic sales, productivity, utilization of installed capacity, domestic sales revenues, operating profits and operating margin. TheRefore, the Secretariat considered that the state of national industry during the period under review was vulnerable to the elimination of the countervailing quota. 226. \u160\'00\'A0 On the other hand, Servicables argued that if domestic production "sold less and made less profit in 2019", it is not only due to the importation of Chinese steel cables, but also to the decline in the economy, for while in 2013 to 2018 the Mexican economy recorded growth of 1.4%, 2.2%, 2.5%, 2.4%, 2.2% and 2.02%, respectively, in 2019 it had a decline of -0.3%, an essential factor in the productivity and economy of the country, manufacturers and distributors of steel cables, as this decline necessarily influences the decline of the market. 227. \u160\'00\'A0 Servicables provided reports from the Bank of Mexico explaining issues relating to economic growth and Gross Domestic Product (GDP) and INEGI, where an annual variation of -1.5% in GDP from secondary activities is estimated for the fourth quarter of 2019. 228. The \u160\'00\'A0 National Producers replied that, indeed, the fall in the national economy argued by Servicables pays arguments that domestic production is vulnerable to any elimination or reduction of the countervailing quota, which would lead to an imminent and massive incursion of Chinese product at unfair price. 229. \u160\'00\'A0 That is, the widespread decline in economic activity of -0.3% reported by INEGI for 2019, affected a decrease in demand from the consumer steel cable sectors which, considering that they are concentrated in the secondary sector, is reflected in the estimated decline of -1.5% of that sector. 230. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers presented figures obtained from INEGI to show that domestic production of steel cable operated, during the examination period, in a market context, with a more significant reduction than the annual variation observed in 2019 of -0.3%. Thus, during the review period, the fisheries sector recorded a drop of -9.1%, mining a reduction of -6.4%, construction a contraction of -3.3% and the secondary sector in general a fall of -1.0%. There were also steady reductions throughout the period analyzed in branches such as mining, oil and gas extraction, and the construction of civil engineering works. 231. \u160\'00\'A0 In addition, as documented with BBVA Bank's "Mexico Regional Sectoral Outlook" report, domestic production predicts that the weakening of the domestic market will continue in the projected period, with an estimated 0.20% decrease in GDP in the secondary sector. 232. \u160\'00\'A0 Camesa added that, productive paralysis resulting from the health contingency caused by COVID-19, in industries such as construction, manufacturing and mining, has had an impact on the activity of the domestic steel cable industry, in such a way that the effects of a reduction or elimination of countervailing quota would now be sharper than those envisaged in its projections; it would open the way to increasing volumes of unfair imports of steel wire from China, generating severe and irreversible harmful effects on the deteriorating \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of domestic industry and forcing domestic production to close its \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term. 233.In this regard, the Secretariat considered that the decline in the domestic economy would aggravate the position of vulnerability of domestic production, in the face of a countervailing quota elimination scenario, which would result in an increase in the subject \u160\'00\'A0 imports, at dumping prices, a situation under the analysis described in points 176 and 177 of this Resolution. 234. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers stated that, in a countervailing quota elimination scenario, the share of domestic domestic production oriented to the domestic market in the ANC would be diminished. In addition, there would be impacts on domestic sales volume, domestic production volume, installed capacity use, employment and the cost of selling to the domestic market which, in the context of increased costs, would involve inadequate levels of profitability that would lead to unworkability in the manufacture of steel cables and the eventual closure of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . 235. \u160\'00\'A0 To support their assertions, they submitted the projections of their economic indicators for the period July 2019 to June 2020, if the countervailing quota was eliminated, as well as their methodology and criteria that they applied in their estimates:a. their exports would remain constant during the projected period; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0from BBVA Bank's "Mexico Regional Sectoral Outlook" report, they considered that the ANC would have a decrease of 0.20%, as it is the bank's estimate for the GDP of the secondary sector, where the highest consumption of steel cables in the international market is dispersed; (c) domestic production would be obtained by differentiating projected imports from the ANC, and by adding up the exports also projected; d. domestic domestic production oriented to the domestic market would be obtained by subtracting projected exports from domestic production; e. the volume of sales to the domestic market would be reduced by the same proportion as thedomestic production oriented to the domestic market in the projected period; (f) the price of sales to the domestic market would be that observed in the period examined minus the industry's operating margin in the same period; (g) inventories would have the rotation observed during the period under review; h. installed capacity would remain constant during the projected period; (i) employment would maintain the productivity observed during the period underreview, and j. wages would increase by the same proportion as the inflation reported by the Bank of Mexico for the projected period. 236. TheNational Producers also presented projections of their operating results \u160\'00\'A0 for the period July 2019 to June 2020, as well as their respective methodology, under the scenario without the continuation of the countervailing quota for the imports under review (under the situation of declining sales volume and domestic prices). 237. \u160\'00\'A0 For operating costs and expenses, they calculated the projected unit values of each element of the manufacturing cost and operating expenses, multiplying the unit cost of the period under review of each component by the unit to which the average annual growth rate of the respective component is added. 238.The Secretariat analyzed the methodology that the National Producers presented to project and considered the economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry to be acceptable, on the basis that \u160\'00\'A0 the projected indicators were based primarily on the historical performance they recorded during the period analyzed. 239.On the basis of the results of the analysis referred to in \u160\'00\'A0 the previous point of this Resolution, the Secretariat retorted the projections of the domestic industry for the period July 2019 to June 2020, with respect to the levels they recorded during the period under review, and noted that they would reflect the following impacts on the industry's relevant economic indicators: decreases in production (24%), domestically oriented production (36%), market share (24 percentage points), domestic sales (36%), employment (24%), wages (22%) and utilization of installed capacity (10 percentage points). 240.With regard to the financial information \u160\'00\'A0 projected by the domestic industry for the domestic market, for the period July 2019 to June 2020, compared to the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  results obtained during the period under review, the Secretariat noted a decrease in operating profit of 1.33 times (it would even become \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  loss), as a result of the decrease in both sales revenues of 29.6% and operating costs by 6.2%, resulting in a 27 percentage point reduction in operating margin by being -8.6% for the projection period. 241. \u160\'00\'A0 With regard to the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  results projected by the domestic steel cable industry for its export market, in the period July 2020 to June 2021 compared to the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  results of the period under review, the Secretariat noted an increase in operating loss of 61%, as a result of the increase in both sales revenues of 7% and operating costs by 10.5%, resulting in a 3.5 percentage point reduction in operating margin by being -10.5% for the projection period. 242.Based on the information and results of the analysis described above, the Secretariat concluded that the potential volume of imports originating in China, as well as the level of prices to which they would occur to the Mexican market, constitute objective elements that would establish the well-founded likelihood that in the face of the elimination of the countervailing quota, the domestic industry would have negative effects on the relevant \u160\'00\'A0 economic and financial indicators, resulting in the recurrence of injury to the domestic steel industry. 7. \u160\'00\'A0 Export potential of China243. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers mentioned that China's high production capacity coupled with its net export quality and low prices under conditions of price discrimination that have historically had the effect of undervaluing and displacing domestic industry leads them to conclude that, if the countervailing quota were eliminated, injury to domestic industry would continue. 244. \u160\'00\'A0 They argued that the productive potential of china's steel cable industry is such that the production capacity of only seven producers is significantly greater than the production capacity of all domestic production and the ANC of the domestic market. 245.They noted that they did not have specific information from China's steel cable industry at their disposal, and \u160\'00\'A0 therefore provided indicators of the steel wire rod industry, primary input, and main element of the cost of the product under review. Presented figures on China's steel production, apparent consumption and exports for the period 2014 to 2019, obtained from CRU Group; as well as the importation of wire rod from Mexico, obtained from the same source. Based on the information described, they stated that China has a high production capacity and export potential of wire rod, a condition that favors and supports the productive and export potential of the Chinese cable industry in general and, therefore, of the product under review. 246.Also, based on \u160\'00\'A0 overall cable statistics, of TradeMap subheading 7312.10, they indicated that China has been the world's largest exporter, with a nearly 40% share of world exports from 2014 to 2018. They argued that, during that period, Chinese exports increased by more than 154 million kilograms, a very significant amount compared to domestic industry sales volumes, and in turn denotes the interest and ability of Chinese industry to place its products in open markets. Moreover, declining increases in exports in this period reflect that some markets are closing or demanding fewer cable volumes; It is therefore clear that China will reorient its production volume to other export markets, particularly those that are open and have higher expectations for growth, such as the Mexican market, which ranks among the top 20 steel wire importing countries. 247. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers argued that the slowdown in china's trade war-induced economy between China and the United States caused a contraction in demand for steel in China, considered the world's leading consumer. Signs of weakness are seen in the country's major end-use steel (construction and automotive) markets as reflected in slow growth in real estate investment and falling car sales, clouding the outlook for steel demand in the Chinese market. 248. \u160\'00\'A0 They added that the expected weakness in 2020 for economies in the Asian region will limit the growth in demand for Chinese steel. In South Korea, demand will weaken due to slow construction and automotive sectors, while falling manufacturing needs will be the main weakening of Japan's steel demand. 249.WSA also estimated a significant reduction in the growth \u160\'00\'A0 in end-product consumption in China in 2020; a decrease in growth of 1% is expected in that year, contrasting with the 7.8% growth reported in 2019. 250. \u160\'00\'A0 In addition, they argued that, despite the slowdown in the Chinese economy, their steel mills continue to increase production, generating excess cheap Chinese steel, putting down pressure on their own and global prices, which will eventually be reflected in exports of finished goods, such as steel wire. 251. \u160\'00\'A0 They estimated that in 2019 China recorded an exportable surplus of 3.9 million tonnes of wire rod, which meant 203 times mexico's ANC during the review period. In addition, during 2018 China remains the largest producer of wire rod by representing 67% of the productionglobal; Which shows that, despite the installed overcapacity of steel production, China maintains its policy of continuing the increase in steel production.\u160\'00\'A0252. \u160\'00\'A0 On the other hand, they argued that, in the context of a declining domestic market, the federal government's "National Infrastructure Investment Agreement" which promotes private investment participation under fair trading conditions would follow the trend of that market, requiring the domestic industry to maintain adequate financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  health to participate constructively in the federation's infrastructure programmes, therefore, any elimination of the countervailing quota on China's steel cables, which would lead to the entry of a significant volume of imports of such goods under dumping conditions, would affect such financial and \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  health and prevent domestic and foreign industry from participating in such programmes in support of that agreement; hence it is important to maintain the countervailing share under review to encourage the participation of domestic and foreign participants in a market under fair conditions of trade. 253. \u160\'00\'A0 The National Producers noted that the recent determination of countervailing measures to Chinese steel cable by the Colombian government, coupled with the extension of european Union countervailing measures, in a context of elimination of the countervailing quota, would result in an imminent diversion of large volumes of Chinese steel cable that would find in Mexico an open and fertile market for unfair competition. 254.In addition, Camesa identified recent events that it believed exacerbated unfair practices in the \u160\'00\'A0 Chinese export of steel cables:a. despite the COVID-19 pandemic, China continued to produce steel during the first quarter of 2020, increasing its production to 234.45 million tonnes and bringing its inventories of finished and semi-finished steel by about 100 million tons per year. In the recovery of end-user demand, China has not been strong enough to absorb the surplus steel supply, which means greater inputs for the manufacture of steel wire and possibly exportable volumes of the product under review, with the Mexican market being an attractive destination in the eventual scenario of eliminating the countervailing quota; b. given this situation, there is increased pressure for China's steel cable production and export industry to face trade barriers to placing significant export surpluses on the world market, so china's export industry will seek to redirect shipments of the product under review to more open markets such as Mexican; c. Chinese production of finished products has had a rapidly growing dynamic, despite excess inventories and slowing global demand due to the COVID-19 pandemic, with a 31.6% increase in wire rods that cannot be absorbed by the Chinese domestic market; d. The Chinese government is implementing measures to address the decline in demand for COVID-19, increasing the rate of return on export taxes of 1,084 products from 10% to 13%, wire rod, the main raw material of the product under this review, which accounts for the difficult domestic status of the Chinese input industry and which irretrievably also affects the Chinese steel cable industry,and e. the recessionary conditions of the Chinese economy and steel cable consumer industries, coupled with surplus exportable capacity of the wire industry, will lead to the intensification of unfair trade practices , resulting in higher Chinese exports of steel cables, in the event of a suppression of the countervailing measure. 255. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the information described in the previous points, which constituted the best available information, the Secretariat noted the following:(a). indeed, the production capacity figures of only seven Chinese steel cable-producing companies, of which the National Producers submitted information by 2020, indicate that they have the capacity to produce more than 37 times the size of the Mexican market reported during the period under review, and that production capacity accounted for more than 16 times the installed capacity of the domestic industry in the same period; b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0in accordance with points 148 and 149 of this Resolution, the Secretariat was taken from the export statistics of tariff subheading 7312.10 obtained from UN Comtrade, which include the product under review, from which he noted that china's steel cable exports to the world increased 16% from 2014 to 2018, from 984 to 1,138.5 thousand tons, which meant that its share of world exports increased by 7 percentage points from 32% in 2014 to 39% in 2018; Indeed, throughout 2014 to 2018, China remained the world's leading exporter of steel cable with an average share of 36%; c. in relation to the Mexican market, during the review period, Chinese exports of steel cables registered in 2018 accounted for more than 39 times Mexico's domestic production and more than 39 times the CNA of steel cables; China exports vs domestic market\par \par \uc2 Source: National Producers, SIC-M and UN Comtraded. With regard to steel wire rod, primary input and main cost element of the product under review, according to CRU Group statistics, China remained throughout 2014 to 2018, as the world's leading producer of steel wire rods, with an average share of 67%, well above other countries such as Germany and Japan that had average shares of less than 3% in the same period. China's 2018 steel wire production was 144.7 million tonnes, representing a 65% share of world production; e. the export potential of this input from China in 2018, measured as the difference between its production and consumption, accounted for more than 182 times the size of the CNA of steel cables in Mexico, during the period under review; (f) the above figures show that China is a major producer of the primary input of the product under review, which it could allocate for the manufacture and export of steel cables to the Mexican market, if the countervailing quota is eliminated; (g) the determination of countervailing measures to Chinese steel cable by the Colombian government, as well as the extension of European Union countervailing measures, makes it possible to presume that Chinese exporters would have an incentive to place their exports in other countries, as the Mexican market would be,and h. despite the slowdown in the Chinese economy and global demand for steel due to the COVID-19 pandemic, China's steel industry continues to increase its production levels, which will invariably be reflected in a surplus of inputs for the production of goods, such as steel wire, which will potentially be exported at low prices.\u160\'00\'A0256.Based on the information and evidence on the administrative record of this investigation, as well as the analysis and results described in the previous points of this Resolution, the Secretariat concluded that \u160\'00\'A0 China's steel cable manufacturing industry has a considerable level of production and export potential, in relation to domestic production and the size of the Mexican market for the like product. This, coupled with the low prices at which the Chinese product would be dumped, on the domestic market, is sufficient evidence to consider that, in the face of the elimination of the countervailing quota, it would incentivize the entry of exports of steel cables to the Mexican market in significant volumes, resulting in a recurrence of injury to the domestic industry. I. \u160\'00\'A0 Conclusions257. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the analysis and results described in this Resolution, the Secretariat concluded that there were elementssufficient to determine that the elimination of the countervailing quota on imports of steel cables originating in China would result in a recurrence of dumping and injury to the domestic industry. Among the elements that led to this conclusion, without being limited to aspects identified throughout this Resolution, are:(a) There are sufficient elements to support the re-dumping of steel cables originating in China if the countervailing quota were eliminated. b.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0However, during the period under analysis, the application of the countervailing quota discouraged the presence of imports of steel cables from China, the projection of the imports under review in the face of the elimination of the countervailing quota, confirms the well-founded likelihood that they would re-account for the domestic market in considerable volumes that would enable them to achieve market share that would negatively impact the performance of the relevant economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry. c.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0The price of potential imports of steel cables originating in China could reach significant undercut margins relative to the domestic price of 17% in the period July 2019-June 2020, which would have a negative impact on the domestic price, since it would force the domestic industry to lower its price by 21%, in order to compete with increased demand for new imports. d.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Given the prices at which imports of steel cables originating in China would occur, they are expected to distort domestic prices and significantly displace the domestic product from the market, adversely affecting the performance of the relevant economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry. e.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0During the period analysed, some of the economic and financial indicators showed negative signs, such as declining production, domestic market-oriented production, domestic sales, productivity, installed capacity utilization, domestic sales revenues, profits and operating margin. Therefore, the state of the national industry in the period under review was considered vulnerable to the elimination of the compensatory quota. f.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Among the most significant impacts on the domestic industry that would cause the elimination of the countervailing quota in the projected period July 2019-June 2020 with respect to the levels recorded in the review period, decreases in production (24%), domestically oriented production (36%), market share (24 percentage points), domestic sales (36%), employment (24%), wages (22%), utilization of installed capacity (10 percentage points), domestic sales revenue (-29.6%), operating profit (-1.33 times), even would return to \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  loss, and operating margin (-27 percentage points) remaining at -8.6%. g.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0China has significant export potential in relation to the production and size of the Mexican steel cable market. In particular, during the review period, Chinese exports of steel cables registered in 2018 accounted for more than 39 times Mexico's domestic production and more than 39 times the ANC of steel cables. h.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0From 2014 to 2018 China remained at the top of major steel cable exporting countries; during 2018, its exports accounted for 39% of total exports, ranking as the world's leading exporter of the product. i.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0The determination of countervailing measures for Chinese steel cable by the Colombian government, as well as the extension of countervailing measures by the European Union, makes it possible to presume that Chinese exporters would have an incentive to place their exports in other countries, such as the Mexican market. 258.For the foregoing and on the \u160\'00\'A0 basis of Articles 11.1 and 11.3 of the AD Agreement, and 67, 70 Fraction II and 89 F Fraction IV, literal (a) of the LCE, the following SOLUTION is issued259.The administrative procedure for reviewing the validity of the countervailing quota imposed on imports of steel \u160\'00\'A0 cables originating in China is declared complete, regardless of the country of origin, which enter through TIGIE tariff fractions 7312.10.01, 7312.10.05, 7312.10.07 and 7312.10.99, or by any other. 260.The final compensatory fee referred to in point 1 of this Resolution is extended for five more years \u160\'00\'A0 from 17 December 2019. 261. It \u160\'00\'A0 is for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Public Credit to apply the final countervailing fee referred to in point 1 of this Resolution throughout the national territory. 262. \u160\'00\'A0 Pursuant to Article 66 of the LCE, importers who, under this Resolution, are required to pay the countervailing fee, shall not be obliged to pay the same if they find that the country of origin of the goods is different from China. The verification of the origin of the good shall be made in accordance with the provisions of the Agreement laying down the rules for the determination of the country of origin of the imported goods and the provisions for their certification, for non-preferential purposes (formerly the Agreement laying down the rules for the determination of the country of origin of the imported goods and the provisions for their certification, compensation quotas) published in the DOF on 30 August 1994, and its amendments published in the same broadcasting body on 11 November 1996, 12 October 1998, 30 July 1999, 30 June 2000, 1 and 23 March 2001 , 29 June 2001, 6 September 2002, 30 May 2003, 14 July 2004, 19 May 2005, 17 July 2008 and 16 October 2008. 263. \u160\'00\'A0 Notify this Resolution to the interested parties. 264. \u160\'00\'A0 Please contact this Resolution with the SAT for appropriate legal purposes. 265. \u160\'00\'A0 This Resolution shall enter into force on the day following its publication in the DOF. 266. \u160\'00\'A0 Arch yourself as a case who is totally and definitively concluded. Mexico City, as of May 11, 2021.- The Secretary of economy, Mtra.\u160\'00\'A0Tatiana Clouthier Carrillo.-Rubric.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210529eh5s0016l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
