\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 What you need to know this Tuesday before the market opens (+Podcast)\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Mai 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Financial Journal ? First Click May 25 China continues to move markets, especially commodities. The Commission for Reform and National Development (the planning of the Chinese economy) formally announced today that it will tighten the price control of iron ore, copper, grains and meats in its 14th five-year plan for the period 2021-2025.\par \par \uc2 At first, copper didn't react to ads. What's more, it closed with a 1.4% wing on the Shanghai stock exchange, the hand of other metals. But, as the hours went by, he reversed his trend, and erased the 1.2% rise with which he started the London session. At this time, copper falls 0.09% in \uc2 LME\uc2  and falls back 0.35% into comex.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also reaches the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Reuters reports that Chinese state banks went out to buy dollars to stop the yuan rally,which reached 6.40 against the dollar, its highest value since June 2018. But the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not the only one that can be seen today. The euro and sterling are moving towards their highest value in four months against the dollar, which is 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 The dollar weakens as does fear of an inflationary wave and the withdrawal of monetary stimulus.\u160\'00\'A0Three Fed members (Bullard, Brainard, Bostic) came out in choir to assure markets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not even see conditions to start thinking about a possible adjustment. The statements revived the appetite for risk, and gave impetus above all to technological actions. In Asia, Shanghai stock exchanges (+2.4%) Hong Kong (+1.75%) lived a rally. In Europe, with less exposure to technology, the rises are more moderate. The Stoxx600 rises 0.4% and reaches record level. Wall Street futures also trade upwards, led by the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  (+0.62%).\par \par \uc2 Also in the headlines this morning appears the US proposal for a global corporate tax.\u160\'00\'A0The G7 would be enlisting an agreement, which could be announced on Friday, to impose a 15% rate on profits. The rate is less than the 21% proposed by Joe Biden's administration, but would make it easier for \uc2 OECD\uc2  countries to take the measure as well.\par \par \uc2 On tax, Diario Financiero reports complications in the debate for a new mining royalty due to huge differences in estimates of the impact it would have on producers. In addition, DF reports that the Treasury would enter Congress today changes to the minimum wage. Meanwhile, the opposition increases the pressure in demand because the Executive immediately enters the promised projects as part of the so-called "common minimum agenda".\par \par \uc2 In this edition, DF interviews Francisco Sard\u243\'00\'F3n, CEO of Scotiabank Chile, and who moves to Peru to lead the bank in that country: "I leave Chile in the hope that its political process will lead it to development".\par \par \uc2 ATTENTION TODAY\par \par \uc2 Investors will pay attention to the U.S. Consumer Confidence Index. (08.00 hours)In Brazil, we will have inflation figures and formal job creation as of May.CEOs of major Wall Street banks testify before Congress about the role of their institutions in responding to the pandemic.Federal Reserve Supervisory Vice President \uc2 Randal Quarles\uc2  testifies of "Supervision and Regulation" before the Senate Banking Committee.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210525eh5p004l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw109\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1635\pichgoal60