sportation restrictions, public gathering prohibitions and temporary closures of non-essential businesses, which had put economic activities in a suspension mode until late March, in addition to the regular Chinese New Year Holiday. Although the COVID-19 pandemic has largely been contained in China since then and businesses have gradually resumed to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , China's economic recovery still faces great challenges. Against this background, we believe that China's out-of-home advertising market and our revenue in 2020 so far have been negatively affected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The COVID-19 has had a material adverse impact on our operating results for the first quarter of 2020. We had a year-over-year decrease of approximately 26% in revenue in the first quarter of 2020, according to unaudited financial information, and a potential slowdown in collection of accounts receivable in 2020. We have seen sales starting to increase in the second quarter compared with the first quarter in 2020. However, we do not expect a significant impact on our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financial results in the long run unless the COVID-19 pandemic is not contained in the year of 2020. The pandemic in China is currently under control. Businesses around China have largely returned to normal since April 2020. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of our customers and our supply chain have been back to normal since most cities in China have lifted the lockdown restrictions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following table shows our revenue, percentage of revenue, cost of revenue and gross margin, by revenue categories:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                        Year Ended December 31, 2019                  Year Ended December 31, 2018   \par                                                                      % of                                      Cost of