22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy Governor \uc2 Sharon Donnery\uc2  gives speech on COVID-19 in Dublin \u8226\'20\'22 16:00 Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta President \uc2 Raphael Bostic\uc2  speaks in Zoom webinar in Atlanta \u8226\'20\'22 15:00 Governor of the Swedish National Bank \uc2 Stefan Ingves\uc2  speaks in Stockholm \u8226\'20\'22 18:00 Federal Reserve Bank of New York President John Williams participates speaks in New York City Commentary and Analysis EUR/USD has a date with 1.1695 from where it may rally \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD drops through 1.1750 options to reach 1.1738 EBS \u8226\'20\'22 Pair set to meet min tech obj for Mar 2020-Jan 2021 rise at 1.1695 \u8226\'20\'22 More longs will have been pared since last CFTC data complied Mar 23 \u8226\'20\'22 Since March 23 EUR/USD has sped lower from 1.1940 \u8226\'20\'22 In process pair has broken below 200-DMA 1.1869 and 100-MMA at 1.1819 \u8226\'20\'22 Pressure upon EUR is evolution of this year's positive trend \u8226\'20\'22 With fewer longs and target met EUR/USD uptrend may resume Dollar hits one-year highs vs yen due to fundamentals and techs \u8226\'20\'22 Dollar hits 1-year high vs yen as inflation worries lift yields \u8226\'20\'22 USD's short exodus puts it on the cusp of bigger gains \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY chart is becoming much more bullish \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders snap up USD/JPY for a bigger bull cycle \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY has seen a 109.75-110.31 range on Tuesday, according to EBS data \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY, EUR/JPY 30-day log correlation remains under +0.5 GBP/USD holds below 1.38 after UST yields rise \u8226\'20\'22 Cable topped out just shy of 1.3783 (Asia high) after rallying off 1.3744 \u8226\'20\'22 1.3744 was Ldn am low, as higher UST yields lifted USD \u8226\'20\'22 10-year UST yield hit 14-month high near 1.78% during Ldn am \u8226\'20\'22 1.3847 was Monday's GBP/USD high -- before 10-year UST yield jumped to 1.72% \u8226\'20\'22 1.3730 and 1.3700 are support points below 1.3743 \u8226\'20\'22 1.3730 was Friday's low. 1.3800 is a resistance level beyond 1.3783 AUD/USD helped off one-week peak by higher UST yields \u8226\'20\'22 0.7625 marks the AUD/USD low since it scaled one-week peak of 0.7664 in Asia \u8226\'20\'22 Retreat to 0.7625 influenced by 10-year UST yield rise to 14-month high \u8226\'20\'22 0.7616-0.7655 was Monday's range (low before high) \u8226\'20\'22 There is a large 0.7640 option expiry for the 10am ET NY cut \u8226\'20\'22 Resource-rich Australia shows vagaries of any commodity super-cycle \u8226\'20\'22 RBA meets next week (April 6), dovish hold expected \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders snap up USD/JPY for a bigger bull cycle \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are becoming increasing comfortable holding USD/JPY, anticipating a much bigger bull cycle caused by a number of positive fundamental and technical factors. EUR/JPY is likely to head higher, too. USD/JPY's speculative long position has grown, according to IMM data -- the week ending Mar. 23 showed a futures market long an equivalent cash USD/JPY position of $6.2 billion, up from $4.5 billion the previous week. EBS flows data since March 23 indicate those longs may have grown even more. USD/JPY climbed a one-year high on Tuesday amid a spike in Treasury yields, as accelerating vaccinations and massive stimulus in the U.S. stoked inflation concerns. Spot has remained above the 109.56 Fibonacci level, a 61.8% retrace of the 111.71 to 102.60 (March 2020 to January 2021) EBS drop, which increases the odds for bigger gains. There is scope for USD/JPY gains to the March 2020 111.71 high in the weeks ahead. If the market falters below 109.56, however, that would be a sign that medium-term gains are coming to an end. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2PKbOtF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2PKbOtF}}}\uc2  ; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3sBa6JS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3sBa6JS}}}\uc2  ; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3fo0a2r"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3fo0a2r}}}\uc2  FTSE should rally and so should pound Compared with most of the world's major stock indexes, the FTSE is cheap, and the same stimulus that has driven those markets to record levels should fuel a bigger rise for the UK index. That's likely to be taken as positive signal for the UK, igniting demand for the pound. Sterling gains weigh on a stock index that contains many companies that account in dollars, but the FTSE is seriously lagging, other stock markets and in light of their stratospheric rises looks increasingly good value. While FTSE has lagged, it has become less vulnerable to the stronger pound. Trade-weighted GBP is close to its 2021 peak and so is FTSE. Both have recently risen in tandem. This month, FTSE could close over its 100-month moving average at 6,792 for the first time since the covid-19 virus set off a slump last year, exposing the 76.4% retrace of that slide at 7031, the gateway to last year's 7689 peak. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3fkZvyW"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3fkZvyW}}}\uc2  ; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2PgMGuI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2PgMGuI}}}\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-4-2T11:01:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210330eh3u01zj1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
