\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Asia stocks hit, rupiah around 4-month lows as U.S. bond yields rise\b0\par\par\uc2 582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2021\par 08:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Indonesia cenbank says ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Thai stocks rise as OPEC+ extends output cuts\par     * \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sets above 6% economic growth target \par \par     March 5 (Reuters) - Indonesia's rupiah was hovering around\par four-month lows on Friday as rising U.S. bond yields sent the\par dollar higher and hit appetite for Asia's emerging \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and\par stock markets.\par     Stock markets in Asia suffered early falls following\par overnight comments by Federal Reserve Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jerome Powell\f28 \uc2 \f2 ,\par who reiterated the Fed's policy stance as appropriate, even\par though he thought the recent bond sell-off was "notable".\par \par     By midday, Asian markets recovered some ground, with most\par standing less than half a percent lower, and Chinese stocks\par  gaining again. \par     "Any fallout has been limited in Asia thus far thanks to\par China," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jeffrey Halley\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a senior market analyst for Asia\par Pacific at \f28 \uc2 \f2 OANDA\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     China set a more than 6% growth target for 2021 at its\par parliamentary meeting, having skipped a target last year due to\par the pandemic.\par     The Chinese government said it aimed to create more jobs in\par 2021 than it did last year. It was the only major economy to see\par growth last year, at 2.3%, as it was the first to emerge from\par the pandemic.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indonesia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said it was ready to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par the market to keep the weakening rupiah steady. The\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was down 0.3% on Friday.\par     Yields on Indonesian 10-year bonds were last at\par 6.609%, while stocks edged 0.1% higher. Indonesia has\par some of the highest-yielding debt in emerging markets.\par     Analysts at Maybank said if U.S. 10-year yields\par cross the 1.61% level hit last week, then sentiment in Asia,\par particularly on the rupiah and South Korea's won,\par could be undermined further. \par     At last look, U.S. 10-year yields were up at 1.56%, slightly\par below the recent 1.61% high. \par     In the Philippines, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said a spike in\par inflation to its fastest rate in 26 months was temporary,\par suggesting it was not in a rush to reverse its accommodative\par monetary policy stance.\par     Manila stocks and the peso were flat. \par     Thai shares rose 0.7% as a surprise OPEC+ decision\par to not increase oil supply in April boosted prices and supported\par energy stocks on the index.  \par         \par     HIGHLIGHTS:\par     ** Malaysia's 3-year benchmark yield up 11.4 basis points to\par 2.157%\par     ** Thai oil and gas producer \f28 \uc2 \f2 PTT Pcl\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose around 2%\par     ** Analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  say inflation will now start to rank\par higher on the priority list of central banks in emerging markets\par     \par   Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at 0653 GMT\par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2      INDEX    STOCKS   STOCKS\par                           DAILY %  YTD %           DAILY %  YTD %\par  Japan                    -0.26    -4.63           -0.23    5.17\par  China                    -0.07    +0.82           0.00     0.88\par  India                    +0.08    +0.40           -0.57    7.24\par  Indonesia                -0.28    -1.82           0.06     5.27\par  Malaysia                 -0.34    -1.20           0.36     -2.48\par  Philippines              +0.00    -1.15           -0.02    -3.62\par  S.Korea                  -0.09    -3.54           -0.57    5.32\par  Singapore                -0.03    -1.25           0.12     6.14\par  Taiwan                   +1.34    +2.12           -0.32    7.62\par  Thailand                 -0.26    -1.54           0.73     6.62\par  \par  (Reporting by Nikhil Kurian Nainan in Bengaluru; Editing by\par Simon Cameron-Moore and Subhranshu Sahu)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-3-5T08:21:28.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210305eh35016r0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
