\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan hits one-week low as recovery hopes lift dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2021\par 05:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SHANGHAI, Feb 5 (Reuters) - China's yuan weakened to touch\par one-week lows against the dollar on Friday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a weaker fixing, and as expectations of a strong U.S.\par economic recovery put the greenback on course for its best\par weekly gain in three months.\par     Traders said that dollar selling by corporates ahead of the\par Lunar New Year holiday nevertheless limited the downside for the\par yuan, which they expect to continue to fluctuate within a range\par around U.S. dollar index moves.\par     Before the market open, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the\par midpoint rate of the yuan's daily trading band at\par 6.471 per dollar prior, more than 100 pips weaker than the\par previous fix of 6.4605. \par     Spot yuan opened at 6.4691 per dollar and touched\par 6.4822 per dollar, its weakest point since Jan. 28, before\par paring losses to 6.4767 by midday. That was slightly weaker than\par a late session close on Thursday of 6.4720.\par     The offshore yuan dipped against the greenback to\par 6.4835 per dollar. \par     "I feel like the weakening of the midpoint shows regulators\par hope the yuan will have two-way fluctuations," said a trader at\par a foreign bank. He added that the market has been cautious in\par recent days as investors wait for clearer signs of the new U.S.\par administration's approach to relations with China.\par     U.S. President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joe Biden\f28 \uc2 \f2  on Thursday signaled a more\par aggressive approach to China, calling Beijing "our most serious\par competitor" in a speech promising a new era after the\par scattershot foreign policy of his predecessor.\par     The yuan's weakness on Friday also came amid a bout of\par dollar strength on investor views that the U.S. economic\par recovery is on relatively strong footing. \par     Democrats in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Senate\f28 \uc2 \f2  were poised for a marathon\par session aimed at overriding Republican opposition to a $1.9\par trillion COVID-19 relief proposal.\par     The global dollar index rose to 91.567 on Friday from\par Thursday's close of 91.527. \par     Analysts nonetheless see room for a stronger yuan in the\par medium term. \par     "The PBoC would like to stabilise its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  but it would\par be complicated given current inflows following the inclusion of\par China bonds in global bond index," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Natixis\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists Jianwei Xu\par and Nordine Naam said in a note, adding that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is unlikely\par to strongly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market due to its Phase 1\par agreement with the U.S.\par     They said they expect the dollar to weaken this year against\par most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and expect the yuan to trade at 6.30 per dollar\par within one year.\par \par \par     The yuan market at 4:10AM GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.471    6.4605    -0.16%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.4767   6.472     -0.07%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    0.09%              \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       0.80%\par  Spot change since 2005                27.79%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \p