\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan firms as dollar falters, shrugs off rise in COVID-19 cases\b0\par\par\uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2021\par 05:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SHANGHAI, Jan 13 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed on\par Wednesday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a stronger midpoint\par guidance and as the greenback weakened. \par     The dollar nursed losses on Wednesday as a retreat in U.S.\par yields sapped momentum from its recent rebound and investors\par cautiously resumed bets that it will continue its 2020 slide.\par  \par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate\par at 6.4605 per dollar prior to the market open, 218 pips firmer\par than the previous fix of 6.4823. \par     In the spot market, the yuan opened at 6.4607 and\par was changing hands at 6.4561 at midday, 72 pips firmer than the\par previous late session close. \par     Still, traders said the day's midpoint was weaker than\par expected, as regulators wanted a reasonable yuan rise to reflect\par China's economic fundamentals and dollar weakness. \par     "The U.S. is actually happy to see the dollar weakness which\par could boost its exports, while China is not that willing to see\par a too rapid yuan rise which could hurt the country's exports,"\par said Wang Jianhui, chief economist at Capital Securities. \par     "Though \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  much during the recent yuan\par rally, as it wants to avoid \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation accusations by\par the U.S.," he added. \par     New bank lending in China fell in December from the previous\par month, but lending for all of 2020 hit a record, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept its policy stance accommodative to speed the economy's\par recovery from the coronavirus shock early last year.\par  \par     There was muted reaction to data showing China has recorded\par the biggest daily jump in COVID-19 cases in more than five\par months. Authorities are moving aggressively to avoid a national\par outbreak, with more than 28 million people are under lockdown in\par four cities. \par     The yuan's appreciation has not reached an end despite\par rising concerns about exporters' profit erosion, Becky Liu, head\par of China macro strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard Chartered Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (HK) Limited,\par said in a report.\par     China's economy continues to recover and external\par geopolitical risk and uncertainty would likely ease with U.S.\par President-elect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Joe Biden\f28 \uc2 \f2 's inauguration next week, she said.\par     The Thomson Reuters/HKEX Global CNH index, which\par tracks the offshore yuan against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a\par daily basis, stood at 96.01, weaker than the previous day's\par 96.2. \par     The global dollar index fell to 89.97 from the\par previous close of 90.025. \par     The offshore yuan was trading at 6.4474 per dollar.\par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par (NDFs), considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.5817, -1.84 percent away from the midpoint.\par     One-year NDFs are settled against the midpoint, not the spot\par rate.\par \par \par     The yuan market at 4:02AM GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.4605   6.4823    0.34%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.4561   6.4633    0.11%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    -0.07%             \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       1.12%\par  Spot change since 2005                28.20%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \par  Item            Current     Previous  Change\par                                        \par  Thomson         96.01       96.2      -0.2\par  Reuters/HKEX                          \par  CNH index                             \par  Dollar index    89.97       90.025    -0.1\par  \par     \par     \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2% from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.4474    0.13%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              6.5817    -1.84%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore\par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par  (Reporting by Luoyan Liu, Jindong Zhang and Andrew Galbraith;\par Editing by Kim Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2021-1-13T05:30:54.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020210113eh1d00jg1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
