\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Chinese yuan threatens to 'eat' the dollar and end its global hegemony\b0\par\par\uc2 1,341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Januar 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 China's rapid recovery from the pandemic has revived the eternal debate over how long the 50-year dominance of the dollar can persist in world markets.\par \par \uc2 America's struggle to control the coronavirus and revive its economy stands in stark contrast to the Asian nation, where growth has soared. This divergence, which saw the dollar's worst performance since 2017 as the yuan progressed, has reinforced China's penchant for dollar hegemony, with investors flocking to assets in the country, testing the renminbi for trade, and even looking at it as a reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The disappearance of the dollar as a global reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been speculated and predicted for years.\par \par \uc2 Before the yuan, all the upheaval referred to the euro as a successor to the dollar. However, nothing managed to make a dent in the twin forces that underpin the supremacy of the dollar: America's role as an engine of global growth and a first-choice refuge for investors during crises. So powerful were these two pillars that years ago they were given a catchy nickname in trading circles: the "dollar smile."\par \par \uc2 But recently, that smile has looked more like a grimace, with the virus eroding the two traditional coin supports. Instead, it is the yuan that benefits from demand for higher economic performance and isolated assets from the consequences of the pandemic, which refocuses the long-term outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The center of the world economy is moving from the North Atlantic, where it has been for 500 years, to the Pacific," said \uc2 Marc Chandler\uc2 , Chief Market Strata at Bannockburn Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are going to reflect that over time."\par \par \uc2 The Yuan smilesIt's a somewhat ironic ending to President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's quest for a weaker dollar. Despite frequently admonishing Beijing officials for keeping their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under control to support Chinese exports at the expense of the United States, and starting a full-blown trade war to force their hand, it took a pandemic to change the tide.\par \par \uc2 China is reaping the rewards. The world's second-largest economy is now ready to depose the United States as the leading engine of growth in 2028, five years earlier than expected just a year ago after better weathering the pandemic, the Center for Economic and Trade Research said last month.\par \par \uc2 While U.S. production is ready to recover in 2021, growing by 3.9 percent, China is on track to expand by more than 8 percent. And its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering tightening monetary policy, in stark contrast to the Fed's promise to remain accommodative, which has helped drag the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, some see China's economic success in itself, particularly as it became an axis of the global supply chain, reinforces the trend at low interest rates elsewhere and increases divergence.\par \par \uc2 "The United States and other countries remain dependent on the Chinese supply chain to control the pandemic while vaccines are being implemented," said Ben Emons, managing director ofMedley Global Advisors macro strategy. That advantage is what "keeps the G-10's central banks highly accommodative."\par \par \uc2 Money speaksInvestors have certainly realized, injecting $135 billion into Chinese bonds in the 12 months ended September 30, according to data compiled by Bloomberg. Stocks have also become popular, attracting $155 billion over the same period. While the Recent \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2 's confusion over the exclusion from the list of several Chinese companies served as a reminder of the regulatory and trade-related risks surrounding the nation's assets, investors were not greatly unmoved.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, it is the record stash of negatively yielding assets in other parts of the world that has made Chinese debt particularly attractive. China's 10-year bonds yield more than 3 percent, compared to just over 1 percent of similarly maturity Treasury bonds. With these values also joining an increasing number of international references that help investors set their profitability goals, the renminbi is getting another look like a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for both short- and long-term commitments.\par \par \uc2 Greater influenceWhile more than 60 percent of the world's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are denominated in dollars, as they have been for more than two decades, dollar holdings fell to the lowest level since 1996 at the end of the third quarter. The euro, pound and yen have benefited from this fall, but only the yuan has seen allocations increase, to 2.1%, over the past three quarters.\par \par \uc2 That causes some analysts to reconsider their approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \uc2 HSBC Holdings Plc\uc2  believes that the yuan increasingly influences weekly price changes in the pound and commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while \uc2 Societe Generale SA\uc2  strategists see that it affects the feeling of risk.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now the fifth most widely used \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for global payments, accounting for about 2% of transactions, according to data from the Global Interbank Financial Telecommunications Society, which handles cross-border payment messages for more than 11 eleven thousand financial institutions in 200 countries.\par \par \uc2 While that's still a small part, when Swift started tracking coins this way in 2010, the renminbi was ranked 35th.\par \par \uc2 Risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionOf\b0 \highlight\uc2  course, investor frenzy over all things \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes with a big warning: these flows could push the yuan to strengthen too much, too quickly, and precipitate government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt export-damaging profits. Historically, officials have advocated for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 In fact, \uc2 Citigroup Inc\uc2 . predicts that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could soar to its strongest level in nearly three decades against the dollar in 2021, \uc2 Goldman Sachs Group\uc2  forecasts an increase to levels not seen since 2015, and \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  describes renminbi betting as one of its "greater conviction" elections for this year.\par \par \uc2 It would not be the first time that officials have acted to curbRecognition. In 2015, Beijing unexpectedly devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , resulting in the biggest one-day yuan loss in 20 years. So far, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only moved to slow down, rather than stop, the yuan's advance by making it cheaper for traders to bet against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and by relaxing capital restrictions to allow for more exits.\par \par \uc2 But if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rises to less desirable levels, policymakers could act more strongly. They could also further delay the opening of domestic markets, a prerequisite for the yuan to become a truly global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "At the local level, the momentum of exchange rate actions suggests a desire for a slower pace of RMB appreciation," Nomura strategists, including Craig Chan, wrote in a December report.\par \par \uc2 Long gameAny \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would slow not only the progress of the yuan, but also its challenge to the dollar. Despite bullish calls for the yuan of some of the most important names in the financial industry, the average forecast is for a breakthrough of less than 1 percent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2021. That would delay the expected earnings of more than 3 percent for the ruble and more than 6 percent for the Brazilian real, show data compiled by Bloomberg.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's hegemony could also find an ally in former Fed President \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , who is elected by President-elect \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to lead the Treasury department. The incoming administration could once again promote a dollar stronger, restarting a policy that had been maintained since 1995 until it failed during Trump's presidency.\par \par \uc2 Still, the increased appetite for Chinese assets suggests greater international comfort with the renminbi, and the nation's superior economic performance is likely to drive greater use, even if the dollar remains the dominant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for trade, capital markets, and reserves for some years to come. .\par \par \uc2 "We are extremely optimistic in emerging markets, which is based primarily on the view that the Chinese economy will be strong in 2021 and that China has done a great job dealing with the virus," said \uc2 John Malloy\uc2 , portfolio manager and co-director of the emerging and border markets team overseeing $10 billion at \uc2 RWC Partners\uc2 . "There will be continuous pressure on the yuan to strengthen."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210108eh180030z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
