\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 China increases control over the private sector: "We have no choice but to follow the party"\b0\par\par\uc2 2,196 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , who long distrusted the private sector, is moving firmly to dominate it.\par \par \uc2 China's most powerful leader in a generation wants even greater state control in the world's second-largest economy, and private firms of all sizes are expected to align. The government is installing more Communist Party officials within private companies, depriving some of credit and requiring executives to adapt their businesses to achieve state goals.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, it takes full care of companies it considers undisciplined and absorbs them into state-owned enterprises.\par \par \uc2 The momentum is led by a growing conviction within the country's leadership that private markets and entrepreneurs, while important to China's rise, are unpredictable and cannot be fully trusted. The view that state planners better manage a complex economy has become more popular this year, and Beijing relies heavily on state directives to design a V-shaped recovery of the Covid-19's impact.\par \par \uc2 Xi has made his priorities especially clear in recent months. In September, the party issued new guidelines for private companies, reminding them to serve the state and promising to use education and other tools to "continually improve the political consensus of private entrepreneurs under party leadership."\par \par \uc2 Just weeks later, Xi \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  personally to block \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's initial $34 billion public offering from one of China's largest private firms, Ant Group, in part because of concerns that it was too focused on its own profits rather than the state's goal of controlling financial risk.\par \par \uc2 The message goes unnoticed by entrepreneurs, who are reorienting their businesses to appease the state or completely relinunting private enterprise.\par \par \uc2 "For us small businesses, we have no choice but to follow the party," says \uc2 Li Jun\uc2 , a 50-year-old owner of a fish farming company in eastern Jiangsu Province. "Still, we don't benefit at all from government policies."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Li recently closed a seafood processing plant because he could not obtain bank loans, a persistent problem for private companies, despite Beijing's repeated promises to make credit more available to them.\par \par \uc2 The risk to China is that Xi's vigorous assertion of steritist prerogatives will hinder the kind of innovation, competitive spirit, and unbridled energy that drove China's explosive growth in recent decades. Economic policies that helped nurture e-commerce giant \uc2 Alibaba Group Holding Ltd\uc2 ., tech conglomerate \uc2 Tencent Holdings Ltd\uc2 . and other global success stories seem to be coming to an end, economists say inside and outside China. As a result, they say, Chinese companies are becoming less like American companies, which are driven by market forces and rely on private innovation and consumption.\par \par \uc2 The State Council information office, China's cabinet, did not respond to the written questions for this article.\par \par \uc2 The percentage of Chinese investment in manufacturing and infrastructure from private companies, after growing in recent decades, peaked in 2015 with more than half of total investments in fixed assets andhas been shrinking ever since.\par \par \uc2 As a result, China's economy has become less efficient. The amount of capital needed to generate a unit of economic growth has almost doubled since 2012, when Xi came to power, according to China Dashboard, a data project between research firm Rhodium Group and the \uc2 Asia Society Policy Institute\uc2 . This is partly because China's largest state-owned enterprises are often less productive than private companies, official data show.\par \par \uc2 Party officials, for their part, see an opportunity to curb the excessive risk-taking, debt and corruption that accompanied the rapid rise of private companies. Xi's kind of state capitalism, which mixes markets with intensified state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has survived a trade war with the United States and recently outperighed free-market economies, based on economic growth rates.\par \par \uc2 In one of the clearest signs of China's leadership, more state-owned enterprises are devouring private companies, redefining a government initiative called "mixed ownership reform." The original idea, which dates back to the late 1990s, was to encourage private capital to invest in state-owned enterprises, which brought more private sector acumen to China's often inflated state-owned enterprises.\par \par \uc2 Now, with Xi, the process often works the other way around, with large state-owned enterprises absorbing smaller ones to keep them going and reconfiguring the strategies of smaller companies to serve the state.\par \par \uc2 Transactions involving state-owned enterprises buying private companies exceeded $20 billion last year, more than double the 2012 level, in industries including financial services, pharmaceuticals, and technology, the revelations of publicly traded companies show.\par \par \uc2 "State-owned enterprises must play a leading role and important influence in the healthy development of private enterprises," says a new central government action plan for the next three years, requiring more mergers between state and private enterprises.\par \par \uc2 Beijing OriginWater Technology Co\uc2 ., a wastewater treatment service provider that competes with companies such as \uc2 General Electric Co\uc2 ., was one of the target companies. It was started in 2001 by Wen Jianping, an engineer who had studied in Australia. He was eager to help clean Up China's contaminated water supply and take advantage of the country's increasingly open business environment.\par \par \uc2 As demand for water purification grew, Mr. Wen's business prospered. An initial public offering in 2010 helped make him a billionaire. In 2018, he made Forbes magazine's list of the richest people in China, with a reported net worth of more than US$1.1 billion.\par \par \uc2 Over time, Wen took more risks, committing his actions to get more loans and finance larger projects. A government "deleveraging" campaign launched under Xi's government to curb excessive risk-taking forced companies to reduce debt and caused stock markets to fade, bringing down the value of Wen's stock. Your lenders started applying for loans.\par \par \uc2 In addition to Wen's problems, the government in 2018 began reversing an initiative that united private investors with local governments to build costly infrastructure projects, citing fears of spendingExcessive. Companies like Wen's were left with unfinished projects and a debt that was maturing quickly.\par \par \uc2 A subsidiary of \uc2 China Communications Construction Co\uc2 ., a large state contractor for Beijing-led infrastructure projects abroad, slapped and bought a control stake in Beijing OriginWater for more than $440 million. Mr. Wen's share fell to around 10%, from 23%.\par \par \uc2 Now, instead of focusing on the domestic market, Beijing OriginWater says it plans to help facilitate the \uc2 Belt and Road Initiative\uc2  of party leadership, a huge infrastructure program promoted by Xi to bring Asian, European and African nations into Beijing's orbit.\par \par \uc2 Several long-standing board members were replaced by designated persons approved by the State-owned Asset Management and Supervision Commission, which regulates and owns majority stakes in large state-owned enterprises, including \uc2 China Communications Construction\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A notice posted on the company's regulator's website late last year, when The \uc2 China Communications Construction\uc2  subsidiary began acquiring shares in OriginWater, sets the ratings for project managers. Among them: candidates must reveal their political affiliations and must have an "unwavering fighting spirit."\par \par \uc2 In response to questions, \uc2 China Communications Construction\uc2  described the acquisition of Mr. Wen's company as an "alliance of the strong." Wen declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with a Chinese weekly, China Times last year, Wen compared state-owned enterprises to trees and private companies to shrubs. "In the future, trees can get bigger and bigger, absorbing more land, water and sunlight," he said. "The shrubs will transform, become a branch of the tree or an grass, and the grass will die."\par \par \uc2 Zhuji Water Group Co., a city government-run water services company in Zhejiang Coastal Province, spent 147 million yuan last year for a 28% stake in Zhejiang Great Southeast Co., a publicly traded plastic packaging company, after that company got into debt trouble.\par \par \uc2 Zhuji's government has been trying to make Zhuji Water a kind of conglomerate by having the company take over hotels, real estate and other assets. His acquisition of the Greater Southeast is also a way for Zhuji Water to be listed, says a company official.\par \par \uc2 Most of the time, however, government officials only want to make sure that large private companies adhere to the state's objectives and policies. To that end, the state is installing more Communist Party committees in corporate offices and encouraging them to play more assertive roles in decision-making.\par \par \uc2 Sanyue Industrial Co, a private electronics manufacturer in the southern city of Dongguan, formed the first party committee in October in the company's 11-year history. He did so after the government told the company he needed it, says company executive Huang Shengying.\par \par \uc2 The committee, which is made up of five party members already working at the company, including two from management, plans to meet often to "study the spirit" of Xi's government policies and speeches, Huang says. "We need to better understand politics to survive. The construction of theparty, we are told, is good for corporate development."\par \par \uc2 Three other private companies in Dongguan also recently established party cells, including an electronics manufacturer, an auto parts manufacturer and a chemical company. A Dongguan official, Zhao Zhijia, calls party committees "red charging stations" and says that "these companies will integrate party construction into their corporate culture. It's a win-win."\par \par \uc2 These party committees often succeed over the decision-making of management and corporate directories. A party cell at Baowu Steel Group, a state-owned company that is China's largest steel producer, held 55 meetings over the past two years and reviewed some 137 commercial and other proposals submitted by management, according to company documents. He reviewed 16 of the proposals before sending them to Baowu's board of directors.\par \par \uc2 He also rejected some, including one related to a fundraising proposal for a subsidiary of the company, saying that the need for more capital was unclear, according to an article published on Baowu's website.\par \par \uc2 The party committee has ordered the company to reserve more funds to help the poor even though profits from Baowu's listed branch declined by 42% the previous year. Eliminating poverty is one of Mr Xi's main policy objectives.\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials say That Xi does not intend to crush entrepreneurship or eliminate market forces. He promised to support the private sector, which contributes half of the government's tax revenue and employs 80% of urban workers.\par \par \uc2 Unlike its predecessors, which constantly expanded the private economy, Xi focuses on attracting entrepreneurs to the party's fold.\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials close to the leadership say Xi's thinking has been influenced by the excesses that arose under predecessors \uc2 Jiang Zemin\uc2  and \uc2 Hu Jintao\uc2 , when corruption and environmental degradation were rampant, and by market disruptions that shook Xi in the early years of his rule.\par \par \uc2 Initially, Xi had been open to pushing for market reforms that began in China under Deng Xiaoping in the 1980s. In late 2013, Xi's leadership promised to give market forces a "decisive role." He blessed market-minded regulators who talked about stock investment and relaxed government control over the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . His administration even considered a proposal to have professional managers rather than party representatives running state-owned enterprises.\par \par \uc2 One after the other, these reform plans led to chaos. In the summer of 2015, a large wave of stock market sales hit markets and embarrassed Xi. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to release the Chinese yuan further frightened the public.\par \par \uc2 In closed-door meetings with his subordinates, Xi made clear his displeasure, according to officials close to leadership, and unleashed state forces to solve what he saw as market problems.\par \par \uc2 Senior state-sector officials successfully pressured Xi's leadership to eliminate plans to bring more market-oriented managers to state-owned enterprises.\par \par \uc2 Beijing now directly oversees 128 state-owned enterprises. Although that is less than around 140 in 2012, companies have become larger, further invading the private sector, amid consolidationsgovernment-led aimed at creating national corporations. Local governments run thousands more businesses.\par \par \uc2 Until last year, \uc2 Xu Zhong\uc2  was head of the research department of \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It publicly blamed China's poor governance and market distortions at the state's hand in credit allocation, causing private companies to be deprived of financing.\par \par \uc2 "The first institutional problem leading to financial chaos is the unclear boundaries between government and the market," he wrote in an article published in December 2017. At a high-level economic forum in February 2019, he called on the government to be held accountable when it reached market reforms.\par \par \uc2 A short time later, he was transferred to a new position outside the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a market trader's association.\par \par \uc2 "The field of market reform is practically gone," says an economist advising the government. "By now, it's pretty clear what kind of reform the leader really wants."\par \par \uc2 There was no doubt about the changing winds of September, when Liu He, the leading economic adviser to leadership with a reputation for supporting market reforms, summed up Beijing's plans for the state sector for the next three years.\par \par \uc2 "State-owned enterprises," he said, "must become the competitive core of the market."\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020201211egca000us\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
