\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-EXCLUSIVE-China comfortable with yuan rises for now as economy recovers, sources say\b0\par\par\uc2 747 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2020\par 00:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Repeating Friday story with no change to text)\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Dec 4 (Reuters) - Chinese policymakers are comfortable with the yuan's rise to 2 1/2-year highs as a rebound in the world's second-biggest economy accelerates and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives the market greater leeway in setting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value, sources said.\par \par \uc2 But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might take action if further rises in the closely managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially if they were rapid, were to hurt the country's exports, the sources involved in internal policy discussions told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Amid broad expectations for further yuan gains and more prolonged weakness in the dollar, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's acceptance of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise runs counter to recent market speculation that the bank might take steps to stabilise the rising yuan.\par \par \uc2 "Yuan appreciation is supported by China's economic fundamentals," said \uc2 Yu Yongding\uc2 , an influential government economist who previously advised the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We don\u8217\'20\'19t need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as we haven't seen any shocks from big capital inflows or outflows," Yu told Reuters. "The exchange rate is mainly driven by the current account."\par \par \uc2 The yuan, which closed at 6.5302 to the dollar on Friday, has gained about 6.6% against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, although that is largely explained by a nearly 6% decline in the dollar's value against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The yuan is on course to post the biggest annual rise since its 6.8% appreciation in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Some policy advisers think the yuan may strengthen to 6.4 per dollar next year, a further 2% rise. The authorities could still take steps to brake a rapid rise if Chinese exporters were to feel the pinch or speculative "hot money" were to flood in, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has recently taken some technical steps that analysts read as a willingness to allow some weakness in the yuan but are not aimed at actively depressing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The relatively light approach to a stronger yuan may reflect the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's efforts to give market forces a greater role, part of longer-term reforms to make the yuan a more international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , insiders said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not respond to Reuters request for comment.\par \par \uc2 EXPORTERS IN MIND\par \par \uc2 Policymakers believe this year's yuan climb is supported by the sharp recovery from the economic damage of COVID-19, which emerged in China a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Beijing countered the disease with aggressive lockdowns and the downturn with a raft of stimulus measures. China's economy is expected to grow 2.1% this year, the only major economy to expand - although at its slowest since 1976 - then surge by 8.4% in 2021, a recent Reuters poll showed.\par \par \uc2 Still, policy insiders do not expect the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  to tighten monetary policy any time soon, as domestic demand remains weak, a new wave of coronavirus infections could cloud global recovery prospects and tensions with the United States may not ease in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Also supporting the yuan are solid exports, fund inflows and a widening gap between Chinese and U.S. interest rates.\par \par \uc2 "We should not worry too much about yuan rises," said a policy insider.\par \par \uc2 "The yuan is still within a normal range and there is no big deal if it rises a bit further," this source said. "There could be some impact on exports going forward, but the impact is not big now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Further gains are indeed expected.\par \par \uc2 Bullish positions in the yuan were at their highest in almost three years, according to the latest fortnightly Reuters poll of analysts and fund managers.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is likely to keep weakening for at least six months as investors continue to shift to risky assets and seek higher returns, a Reuters poll of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 A source close to the commerce ministry, a staunch defender of Chinese exporters, said those companies might be hurt if the yuan strengthens beyond 6.5 to the dollar, but other insiders believe there is greater official tolerance over yuan gains.\par \par \uc2 A major concern would be the speed of further gains.\par \par \uc2 "We cannot let it rise too fast," one insider said. "The main concern is exports. It could also affect market expectations and lead to more hot-money inflows." (Reporting by Kevin Yao; Editing by William Mallard) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-12-7T00:00:05.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201206egc60134e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
47
