\par\par \b \uc2 Post-Covid World -- Barron's\b0\par\par\uc2 By Reshma Kapadia \par \uc2 2,571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2020\par 03:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 26\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Louis-VIncent Gave\par \par \uc2 Chief Executive, Gavekal Hong Kong\par \par \uc2 Louis-Vincent Gave, CEO of Gavekal, is a go-to source for institutional investors trying to interpret global macro risks such as the financial implications of China's rise. Gave, 46, was born in Paris, educated at \uc2 Duke University\uc2 , and based in Hong Kong before the pandemic. Gavekal provides independent research and manages $1.7 billion in Asian fixed-income and equities strategies, primarily for European institutions.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: What investment trends will be most prominent after the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 Louis-Vincent Gave: If I ask what the most important development was in 2001, most people would say it was 9/11. With the benefit of hindsight, it was China joining the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2 , which changed the world for the following 20 years. If I ask about 2007, you'd say it was the start of the subprime crisis. With the benefit of hindsight, it was the launch of the smartphone.\par \par \uc2 With hindsight, what will people say about 2020?\par \par \uc2 So far, the Covid response in the U.S. has been a $12,800 increase in debt per capita; in the United Kingdom, it's $7,000, and in Germany and France, $5,300. In China, it's $1,200. The Western world responded with massive increases in budget deficits, which could constrain future policy options, while Asia, especially China, hasn't.\par \par \uc2 Western policy makers have no choice but to embrace yield-curve controls; they can't let interest rates go back up. You had Japan and Europe in the yield-curve control gang. The big change now is that the U.S. has joined them. Once the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  went down this [path], the euro tanked. Once we are on the other side of Covid-19 and it becomes clear the U.S. has no other choice, the dollar will collapse.\par \par \uc2 What will be the best investment opportunity post-Covid?\par \par \uc2 Investing in Asian fixed-income markets, in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Governments there have broadly been more efficient at dealing with Covid-19. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  balance sheets and government spending haven't grown out of control. Just as water flows downhill, capital is attracted to positive real [inflation adjusted] rates. Today, these are mostly found in Asia.\par \par \uc2 What is the most pressing public policy issue the U.S. will face?\par \par \uc2 How to fund runaway debt. For now, everyone's answer is through modern monetary theory [which posits that governments that control their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can spend freely]. Once the debt is monetized by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there are no historical examples, outside of Japan, where that doesn't lead to massive and very fast inflation, massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debasement, or both.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean for the dollar's reserve-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  status?\par \par \uc2 I look at \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like computer operating systems. Most Gavekal clients use \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  because everyone else uses it. The dollar is \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . Go back to 2005-06, when Apple was trading at nine times earnings and viewed as making a niche product. In 2007, Apple said it would create a parallel system and went straight to the consumer, who took [Apple] not because it was cheaper but because it was easier.\par \par \uc2 So the renminbi is Apple.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing the rollout of Chinese fintech solutions across Southeast Asia, the Middle East, and Africa through \uc2 WePay\uc2  and Alipay. Then, tack on the digital renminbi and look forward to a future where an Indonesian businessman goes to Singapore and pays for his taxi with Alipay and the transaction isn't settled through Swift or the dollar but through digital renminbi. The pushback I get is that no one is going to trust the digital RMB -- or, who wants the Chinese government to know how and where you spend your money? That's a big roadblock, but if you told me 10 years ago people would put Alexa in their homes voluntarily....\par \par \uc2 Aren't you worried about China's debt or social instability?\par \par \uc2 For the past 10 years, I've been told that Chinese debt was about to implode and there would be riots in the street. In the past 10 years, we have seen riots in France and the U.S -- and in Hong Kong -- but China has been remarkably stable. We have been told that the Chinese government would have no choice but to nationalize big parts of the economy and the renminbi would collapse. That scenario has unfolded in Europe and the U.S. [The U.S.] has increased debt by $4.2 trillion, three-quarters of which was funded by the Fed. Meanwhile, the renminbi has been the strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  year to date and over 10 years.\par \par \uc2 What is a key concern for Asia-based investors?\par \par \uc2 The decoupling of the U.S. and China is a massive change, and Taiwan is an important fault line. Taiwan wasn't too much of an issue when the U.S. and China got along and all China produced were cheap plastic toys and bicycles. But this year, the market cap of the global semiconductor industry is above that of the energy sector. \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing\uc2  [ticker: TSM] said it is already manufacturing a generation of chips that \uc2 Intel\uc2  [INTC] has said it won't be able to fabricate until as late as 2023. If you think semiconductors matter more than energy, Taiwan Semi is one of the most important companies in the world.\par \par \uc2 What are the longer-term ramifications of President Xi's crackdown in Hong Kong?\par \par \uc2 The core thesis is that Xi is a transformational president -- the first imperialist president since the Ming Dynasty. If you are Xi and you hear your companies won't have access [to U.S. markets], Hong Kong sounds like a great way to internationalize the renminbi and do a digital renminbi. Most Westerners saw the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the death of Hong Kong, but \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  guaranteed Hong Kong would be China's capital markets for the foreseeable future. [Xi] has no choice but to make it a success, which is why the Hong Kong dollar is stuck at the high end of its [trading] band.\par \par \uc2 Chinese internet stocks have been hit by increased regulatory scrutiny, including the scuttling of the oversubscribed planned public offering of Ant Group. Does this mark a turning point for these companies?\par \par \uc2 Since the [suspension] of the Ant IPO and new antitrust [guidelines], we also had a state-owned coal company default on one billion renminbi, or $150 million. One big issue for China has been a trade surplus of $60 billion and enormous inflows into China tech and bonds driving the renminbi higher.\par \par \uc2 In the Western world, we would raise rates [to deal with potential bubbles]. In China, they have regulatory weapons. They managed to cool the tech stocks in China and inflows into Chinese bonds. They got their message through.\par \par \uc2 You have been living in Vancouver during the pandemic. What is the one place on Earth that you'd most like to visit when the pandemic ends?\par \par \uc2 I have to get back to my Hong Kong and Beijing offices. I miss my colleagues and my friends there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Share your thoughts on the post-pandemic world: What do you think will be the greatest investment opportunity post-Covid? What will be the most important public policy issue that the U.S. will face? Where would you most like to visit once the virus is no longer a threat to travel? Click here to share your thoughts with us.\par \par \uc2 Write to Reshma Kapadia at reshma.kapadia@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 To subscribe to Barron's, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/subscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/subscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 5 Dec 2020 09:11 ET  Post-Covid World -- Barron's\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Reshma Kapadia \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Louis-VIncent Gave\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chief Executive, Gavekal Hong Kong\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Louis-Vincent Gave, CEO of Gavekal, is a go-to source for institutional investors trying to interpret global macro risks such as the financial implications of China's rise. Gave, 46, was born in Paris, educated at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Duke University\f28 \uc2 \f2 , and based in Hong Kong before the pandemic. Gavekal provides independent research and manages $1.7 billion in Asian fixed-income and equities strategies, primarily for European institutions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Barron's: What investment trends will be most prominent after the pandemic?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Louis-Vincent Gave: If I ask what the most important development was in 2001, most people would say it was 9/11. With the benefit of hindsight, it was China joining the \f28 \uc2 \f2 World Trade Organization\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which changed the world for the following 20 years. If I ask about 2007, you'd say it was the start of the subprime crisis. With the benefit of hindsight, it was the launch of the smartphone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With hindsight, what will people say about 2020?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 So far, the Covid response in the U.S. has been a $12,800 increase in debt per capita; in the United Kingdom, it's $7,000, and in Germany and France, $5,300. In China, it's $1,200. The Western world responde