\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-Europe  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - October 22\b0\par\par\uc2 1,658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2020\par 06:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Risk mood sours as US stimulus doubts return Market Briefs \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. coronavirus aid prospects uncertain after Trump blasts Democrats \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. intelligence agencies say Iran, Russia have tried to interfere in 2020 election \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. economy\u8217\'20\'19s rebound sets up test of Fed\u8217\'20\'19s new pledge \u8226\'20\'22 Fed Beige Book - U.S. economy recovering slowly, but some sectors struggling \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 St. Louis Fed\uc2  Bullard \u8211\'20\'13 U.S. can wait on fiscal aid, businesses adapting \u8226\'20\'22 Fed Brainard - Warns economic disparities may hold back US recovery, more fiscal support needed \u8211\'20\'13 FT \u8226\'20\'22 China and U.S.\par \par \uc2 economies diverge over coronavirus response \u8211\'20\'13 Reuters ANALYSIS \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese buy net Y419.8 bln foreign bonds, sell Y162.8 bln foreign stocks Oct 17 week \u8226\'20\'22 Foreign investors buy net Y392.4 bln JGBs, sell Y94 bln Japanese stocks, Y554.8 bln Japan bills Oct 17 week \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowing reaches record in first half of fiscal year, cash requirement climbs to \u163\'00\'A3246bn - FT \u8226\'20\'22 Over half Europe's small firms fear for survival, survey finds \u8226\'20\'22 RBNZ's Robbers \u8211\'20\'13 Bank resilience may be tested as loan losses rise Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 06:00 DE Nov Gfk Consumer Sentiment, -2.8 f'cast, -1.6 prev \u8226\'20\'22 06:45 FR Oct Business Climate Mfg, 96 f'cast, 96 prev \u8226\'20\'22 10:00 GB Oct CBI Trends - Orders, -45 f'cast, -48 prev \u8226\'20\'22 14:00 EZ Oct Consumer Confidence Flash, -15 f'cast, -13.9 prev Looking Ahead - Events, Auctions, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 07:00 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's \uc2 Fabio Panetta\uc2  speaks at the ECB Conference in Frankfurt \u8226\'20\'22 08:30 BoE's chief economist \uc2 Andy Haldane\uc2  attends a conference in London \u8226\'20\'22 09:25 BoE Gov Bailey attends The Waterline summit 2020 in London Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY in bounce after overnight plunge, to be limited? \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY down to as low as 104.35 overnight, Asia sees bounce back to 104.75 \u8226\'20\'22 More upside likely limited on option expiries, recent trend \u8226\'20\'22 Massive expiries nearby today - 104.50-55 USD2.85 bln, 104.90-95 1.1 bln \u8226\'20\'22 Some in between, massive below/above too, expiries continue into next week \u8226\'20\'22 US yields off highs but firm, supportive, Treasury 10s @0.810% \u8226\'20\'22 Risk off in Asia, Nikkei -0.7% @23,468 despite hopes for US fiscal relief \u8226\'20\'22 JPY crosses also mostly on back-foot with USD/JPY, GBP/JPY exception \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY 123.90-124.08, AUD/JPY 74.18-46, heavy, GBP/JPY 137.31-55, buoyant EUR/USD-Gives back some ground as Asia risk-off \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened 0.34% higher at 1.1861 after USD fell across the board \u8226\'20\'22 Asia was risk-off as hopes for a US aid deal faded following Trump Tweet \u8226\'20\'22 E-minis fell 0.65% and AXJ equity index was down around 0.50% \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD drifted down to 1.1838 and was just above 1.1840 into the afternoon \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD support at yesterday's 1.1822 low and 10-day MA at 1.1785 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance at 1.1915/20 where 76.4 fibo and Sept 10 high converge AUD/USD-Grinds lower in gloomy Asia \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened 0.98% higher at 0.7117 after USD fell across the board \u8226\'20\'22 Risk-off mood gripped Asian session as hopes of US aid bill faded \u8226\'20\'22 E-mini futures and AXJ equity index down around 0.65% \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD grinded through bids and technical support between 0.7090/95 \u8226\'20\'22 Low so far has been 0.7089, but move is orderly \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance at 10-day MA at 0.7130 and close above would be bullish \u8226\'20\'22 Next level of decent support at 61.8 of 0.7021/0.7138 move at 0.7065 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD underpinned by bearish shift in USD sentiment GBP/USD-Cools as risk sours after key resistance held \u8226\'20\'22 -0.1% - USD led on soured US COVID-19 aid E-mini S&P -0.65% \u8226\'20\'22 Trades towards the base of a 1.3128-1.3152 range with reasonable interest \u8226\'20\'22 Brexit talks resume - chances of a comprehensive deal are slim \u8226\'20\'22 Charts; momentum studies, 5, 10 & 21 DMAs head higher - positive setup \u8226\'20\'22 1.3142 upper 21 day Bolli indicates overbought - buy dips not breaks here \u8226\'20\'22 1.3173, 61.8% of the September fall capped Wednesday - major resistance \u8226\'20\'22 Close below 1.2937 21 DMA, a recent base, would end the topside bias \u8226\'20\'22 1.3091, 38.2% of Wednesday's rise and Asian 1.3152 high support-resistance Brexit may be a 'buy the rumour, sell the fact' for GBP Sterling jumped 1.5% on Wednesday on news that Brexit negotiations will resume, but with both sides still far apart on key issues, a very limited deal that would still hurt the UK economy seems likely. Thus the resumption of talks could end up being a case of 'buy the rumour, sell the fact' for the pound. As a second COVID-19 wave ravages Europe and British industry groups campaign for a compromise to mitigate the economic impact of Brexit, it is no surprise that negotiations between the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and UK are resuming. To just walk away with no deal would be political poison. Yet the key issues of the \uc2 EU\uc2 's 'level playing field' and the UK's 'sovereignty' remain unresolved and it will take a significant change in stance to find a comprehensive deal. More likely is a limited agreement that will still have a significant negative economic impact, especially on the UK. Technically, the GBP/USD picture has turned positive, as momentum studies, and 5-, 10- and 21-day moving averages head higher. Wednesday's rally was capped around 1.3173, 61.8% of the September fall; a sustained break would target the 1.3481 September high. A close below the 1.2937 21 DMA, a recent base, would end the topside bias. USD/JPY bounces an opportunity in a falling market Having skidded from a high of 105.75 on Tuesday to a 104.34 low overnight, USD/JPY has bounced a bit, as was the case with similar spikes lower on July 31 and Sept 21. While this bounce could continue, the ceiling for USD/JPY is progressively moving lower with each plunge, and the trend remains for more JPY strength than not. In the current downtrend, bounces have been limited to just above the descending 55-day moving average. Those shorting USD/JPY on moves to and above have been rewarded, and there is no reason to believe this sell-rally strategy will not continue to work. A plethora of nearby option expiries is helping to limit moves lower and subsequent rebounds. Massive expiries are scheduled Thursday , and will remain large going forward. At the same time, the gradual downtrend is being facilitated by institutional and other investor intentions, both domestic and overseas. Japanese investors have been more inward-looking with the Nikkei's recent outperformance, and foreign inflows have also been evident. However, higher-yielding foreign bonds remain in favour with Japanese investors, especially given recent upticks in U.S. yields. Buying of USD by this bloc on dips will continue to support USD/JPY, helped by on-off haven flows. USD/CNH returns to shore for those who missed the boat For hesitant traders who have watched USD/CNH sail further south each day, fearful of selling at the low, there may be one more chance to get on board before it cascades through the 6.6000 psychological barrier. USD/CNH has bounced early Thursday after falling for seven straight days, the longest stretch since March. Last at 6.6540, up from Wednesday's 27-month low of 6.6275, the pair now faces a key technical resistance level: offers are likely moored at the top of the daily Bollinger downtrend channel, currently near 6.6800. If that barrier successfully deflects USD/CNH lower, it would validate the bearish chart pattern, spurring shorts to pile in. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's acknowledgement that yuan strength is due to \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's impressive economic recovery will soothe any fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  will act to dampen the pace of yuan appreciation, any move to push it lower would be at odds with Beijing's goal of attracting more foreign capital. Indeed, the speed of the move has been fast. The offshore yuan has gained 8.6% against the USD since notching a record low of 7.1966 in May. A pullback appears healthy at this stage, but it is unlikely to last long. RBI in renewed drive to trigger INR depreciation The \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 's rumoured buying of U.S. dollars versus the Indian rupee this week runs counter to its stated \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb extreme volatility, and appears to be an active push to weaken the INR. Since early September the lower parameter of the RBI's tolerance level for USD/INR appeared to be 73.00, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was rumoured to be a persistent buyer of dollars, ostensibly to absorb sizable inflows. Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via state-run banks this week has prevented the INR from appreciating beyond 73.30. The purchases mark a continuation of the RBI's broad efforts to bolster INR competitiveness, particularly versus the Chinese yuan as India looks to attract companies seeking manufacturing sites outside China. They also fit with the RBI's goal of building dollar reserves. The RBI action has taken traders by surprise, occurring against the backdrop of a broadly weaker USD triggered by a rising CNY and buoyant global risk appetite. Many had also expected the RBI to tolerate a further modest appreciation of the rupee in its fight against stubbornly high retail inflation. With the downside thus limited, USD/INR is likely to test the higher end of its 73.00-74.00 range but only a break of 74.35-50 signals a fresh uptrend.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-10-25T04:52:34.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020201022egam00iwl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
