\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Asian stocks,  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  steady after rough week\b0\par\par\uc2 601 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2020\par 07:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Graphic: World \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     * Graphic: Foreign flows into Asian stocks \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3lKhL5I"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/3lKhL5I}}}\uc2 \f2 \par     * S. Korea stocks on course for worst week since March\par     * MYR flat as Malaysia on FTSE Russell bond index watchlist\par \par     By Anushka Trivedi\par     Sept 25 (Reuters) - Most Asian emerging stocks and\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eked out modest gains on Friday, steadying as signs\par overnight of moves to deliver more U.S. fiscal stimulus calmed\par some of the worst bouts of selling on global stock markets in\par months.\par     Wall Street had closed higher on signs that Democrats in the\par House of Representatives were working on a $2.2 trillion\par coronavirus stimulus package that could be voted on next week.\par \par     "Today is largely consolidative after consecutive days of\par decline," said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Christopher Wong\f28 \uc2 \f2 , senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Maybank\par in Singapore.\par     India and Indonesia were the best performers with gains of\par more than 1% in morning trade. Jakarta's main index,\par weighed down a new wave of coronavirus infections and concerns\par over moves to change its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  law, had shed more than 4%\par this week.\par     Other Asian equity markets made more modest gains, with the\par Philippines, Malaysia and South Korea adding\par between 0.4% to 0.8%, and were still set to register heavy\par losses for the week.\par     South Korea's KOSPI was down 5.2% for the week, its worst\par performance since March.\par     Concerns about a second round of COVID-19 infections that is\par spreading across Europe and weak economic indicators have made\par markets bearish this week. \par     "The increasingly worrying 'second round' COVID risks, which\par have showed up in Services PMIs in Europe, point to a sputtering\par and uneven recovery without appropriate and timely policy\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Riki Ogawa said.\par     In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , a strong influence over many of the region's\par markets, the yuan rose 0.2% after news that China's\par government bonds would be included in FTSE Russell's flagship\par World Government Bond Index (WGBI).\par     Once included, China's market yield will be the second\par highest in the FTSE WGBI index based on current prices, bringing\par in inflows of about US$140 billion, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  analysts said.\par     In the same announcement by FTSE Russell, Malaysian bonds\par remained on a waiting list for possible removal, holding back\par the Malaysian ringgit, which has also suffered from a\par fresh political turbulence in Kuala Lumpur this week. \par     \par     HIGHLIGHTS\par         \par     ** Malaysia's 3-year benchmark yield is up 1.6 basis points\par at 1.969%\par     ** Top gainers on the Jakarta stock index include\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indofarma Tbk PT\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 24.89% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pyridam Farma Tbk PT\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par  up \u8203\'20\'0B24.84% \par     ** Top gainers on the Singapore STI include \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mapletree\par Logistics Trust\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 1.49% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Comfortdelgro Corporation\par Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 1.41% \par     \par     \par  Asia stock indexes and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                         \par  at  0424 GMT                                                      \par  COUNTRY      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RIC      \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DAILY %  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YTD %    INDEX    STOCKS   STOCKS\par                                                           DAILY %    YTD %\par  Japan                         -0.03     +3.02               0.43    -1.98\par  China                         +0.15     +2.14              -0.24     5.42\par  India                         +0.00     -3.40               1.22   -10.12\par  Indonesia                     -0.03     -6.53               1.41   -22.04\par  Malaysia                      +0.02     -1.82               0.34    -5.22\par  Philippines                   +0.10     +4.56               0.47   -24.85\par  S.Korea                       +0.17     -1.22               0.69     4.13\par  Singapore                     +0.01     -2.23               0.46   -23.61\par  Taiwan                        +0.39     +3.09              -0.29     1.93\par  Thailand                      +0.19     -5.14              -0.31   -21.28\par  \par  (Reporting by Anushka Trivedi in Bengaluru, Editing by Patrick\par Graham & Simon Cameron-Moore)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-25T06:46:03.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200925eg9p00ku1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
