\par\par \b \uc2 Trump says aides rejected his request to adjust value of dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2020\par 03:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DAYTON, Ohio (Reuters) - U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on Monday said he was rebuffed when he asked officials to adjust the exchange rate of the dollar to counteract what he described as repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation by China of its yuan.\par \par \uc2 Trump told thousands of supporters at a political rally in Dayton, Ohio, that his policies were saving jobs in the political battleground state after years of inaction to confront China's aggressive behavior in global markets.\par \par \uc2 "I go to my guys, 'What about doing a little movement on the dollar?'" he said, but they countered that was not possible. "'Sir, we can't do that. It has to float naturally.'"\par \par \uc2 The Republican president, who is seeking reelection to a second term in the Nov. 3 national poll, repeated his claim - which China denies - that Beijing deliberately changes the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain competitive advantage in global markets.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has denied \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yuan and lower the cost of its exports to the United States. The yuan has firmed for eight weeks straight against a softening dollar.\par \par \uc2 Trump gave no details on his conversation about tweaking the dollar's value, and no comment was immediately available from the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2 , which is expected to release its long-delayed semi-annual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report in coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 The United States, like most global economies, allows the price of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float, meaning it is set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market based on supply and demand relative to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trump in May reversed course and backed a "strong dollar" after years of railing against the dollar's relative strength as a factor harming U.S. competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department in January dropped its designation of China as a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator", days before U.S. and Chinese officials signed the Phase 1 trade deal, saying Beijing had agreed to refrain from competitive devaluation.\par \par \uc2 Washington's unexpected decision in August 2019 to label China a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator" came at the height of trade tensions between the world's two large economies, but was widely criticized as not based in fact.\par \par \uc2 Before that, Treasury had not labeled China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator since 1994.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Jeff Mason; writing by Andrea Shalal; Editing by Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-22T01:34:16.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200921eg9l04ck5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
