\par\par \b \uc2 Heard on the Street: Risk Builds for the Other Chinese  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  The Other Chinese  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Manipulation Doubt Question -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mike Bird \par \uc2 571 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2020\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B11\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (September 18, 2020).\par \par \uc2 The accusations of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation against the People's Republic of China are well-known. But risks surrounding the other Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the New Taiwan dollar -- are building up, too.\par \par \uc2 With the U.S. dollar falling, Taiwan's closely controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at its highest level in 2 1/2 years, and a rally of 1.5% would push it to an all-time high. The Taiwanese government and its \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Republic of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  have tried to avoid significant appreciation in the past, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets with the aim of preserving Taiwan's export competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  posted a note to its website confirming that it had asked banks to try not to sell too many dollars in a single transaction.\par \par \uc2 In January, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  warned that Taiwan was close to returning to the department's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -monitoring list, an additional level of observation that could lead to a charge of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation.\par \par \uc2 The whole picture for Taiwan is altered by the gargantuan $600 billion in foreign assets, largely U.S. dollar bonds, that its life-insurance companies have accumulated. Insurers have searched for a combination of returns and a larger investable market than the island economy can provide, with \uc2 Brad Setser\uc2  of \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2  and blog Concentrated Ambiguity producing the canonical explanation of the intricacies of that market.\par \par \uc2 As with the Chinese yuan, the market for Taiwanese dollars is bifurcated into offshore and onshore markets because of domestic restrictions on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's use. Insurers have done most of their hedging in the onshore market, the use of which is restricted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But a major development this year has come in the form of much higher hedging costs in the offshore market in nondeliverable forwards. That has meant much or all of the yield pickup that insurers were looking for in the first place has evaporated, once the heightened costs of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  protection are added in.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, there has been a notable slowdown in the accumulation of foreign assets, which made up 56.4% of the sector's total holdings in July, down from an all-time high of 59.3% 18 months earlier.\par \par \uc2 Analysts are divided over what happens next: \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  strategists argue that insurers continue to accumulate foreign assets, but on an unhedged basis. \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 's interest-rate-derivative researchers say the change in hedging costs will likely mean a prolonged slowdown in accumulation by the lifers.\par \par \uc2 Such a slowdown could have a major effect on Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 : With fewer U.S. dollar bonds being purchased, demand for the greenback would fall and the Taiwanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would strengthen further.\par \par \uc2 That seems likely to prompt more action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to compensate. Without a little help from its insurers, Taiwan might find it more difficult to avoid additional accusations of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 Write to Mike Bird at Mike.Bird@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 18, 2020 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200918eg9i000an\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
