\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan hovers at 16-month high, \uc2 PBOC\uc2  yet to send strong signal to rein in strength\b0\par\par\uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 September 2020\par 06:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SHANGHAI, Sept 3 (Reuters) - The yuan hovered at a 16-month\par high against the dollar on Thursday after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par lifted its official midpoint rate for the eight straight trading\par day, but it has yet to send any strong signal to rein in\par strength in the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Prior to market opening, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China (PBOC)\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 set the midpoint rate at a new 16-month high of\par 6.8319 per dollar, 57 pips or 0.08% firmer than the previous fix\par of 6.8376. \par     Thursday's official fixing was the strongest since May 13,\par 2019, but came in slightly weaker than market projections of\par 6.8293, similar to Wednesday. But many market participants said\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not yet robustly used its counter-cyclical\par factor in the midpoint formula as it did in the past to\par counteract one-way bets in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     In the spot market, onshore yuan opened at 6.8323\par per dollar and was changing hands at 6.8297 at midday, up 78\par pips or 0.11% from the previous late session close.    \par     "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has in the past mainly relied on yuan\par midpoint to guide market expectations, but recent fixings have\par not clearly shown such intentions and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par reiterated that it had exited frequent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," analysts\par at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Construction Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (Asia) said in a note.\par     They expect the yuan will continue to appreciate, but at a\par slower pace, in a range of 6.7 to 6.9 per dollar for the time\par being.\par     Several domestic \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders said despite some\par fluctuations in the greenback in global markets in recent\par sessions, domestic investors have broadly shaped a consensus of\par a strong yuan. \par     One trader at a Chinese bank said long yuan positions have\par become crowded and there remained room for further rises in the\par yuan.\par     Global investment banks are revising up their forecasts for\par the yuan, betting that its recent rally will be sustained due to\par persistent dollar softness and continued capital inflows.\par \par     Foreign investors in August raised their holdings of China\par bonds for the 21st month in a row, lured by a rising yuan and\par attractive yield premiums.\par     The global dollar index rose to 92.833 at midday,\par when the offshore yuan was trading at 6.8298 per\par dollar. \par     \par     The yuan market at 0339 GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.8319   6.8376    0.08%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.8297   6.8375    0.11%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    -0.03%             \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       1.95%\par  Spot change since 2005                21.18%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \par  Item            Current     Previous  Change\par                                        \par  Thomson         93.35       93.18     0.2\par  Reuters/HKEX                          \par  CNH index                             \par  Dollar index    92.833      92.693    0.2\par  \par     \par     \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2% from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.8298    0.00%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              6.9863    -2.21%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore\par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par \par \par  (Reporting by Winni Zhou and Andrew Galbraith; Editing by Kim\par Coghill)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-9-3T05:18:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200903eg9300fa1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
