\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Countervailing Quota Review Imposed on Imports of Coal Coal Connections for Butt Welded originating in China\b0\par\par\uc2 21,952 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2020\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 FINAL RESOLUTION of the Administrative Procedure for Reviewing the Effective Of the Countervailing Quota imposed on imports of coal-to-butt steel connections originating in the People's Republic of China, regardless of the country of origin.\par \par \uc2 On the sidelines a stamp with the National Shield, which says: United Mexican States.- Secretariat of Economy.FINAL RESOLUTION OF THE ADMINISTRATIVE PROCEDURE OF EXAMINATION OF THE VALIDITY FEE IMPOSED ON THE IMPORTS OF STEEL CONNECTIONS TO CARBON TO SOLD TO TOPE ORIGINATING IN THE POPULAR REPUBLIC OF CHINA, INDEPENDENTLY OF THE COUNTRY OF PROCEDENCEVisto to resolve in the final stage the administrative file E.C. 13/19 based in the International Trade Practices Unit (UPCI) of the Secretariat of the Economy (the "Secretariat"), this Resolution is issued in accordance with the followingRESULTANDOSA. Final resolution of the anti-dumping investigation1.\u160\'00\'A0On 4 August 2004, the Final Resolution of the Anti-Dumping Investigation on Imports of Coal Butt Steel Connections originating in the People's Republic of China ("China") was published in the Official Journal of the Federation (DOF), regardless of the country of origin. The Resolution determined a final countervailing fee of 81.04%. B. Review of the compensatory fee2. On 7 November 2006, the final resolution of the review of the countervailing fee referred to in the previous point was published in the DOF. It was determined to modify the countervailing fee from 81.04% to US$2.07 ("dollars") per kilogram. C. Previous effective exams3. On 2 February 2011, the Final Resolution of the first review of the countervailing quota was published in the DOF. It was determined to modify the countervailing fee from $2.07 to $1.05 per kilogram and extend it for five more years. 4. On 2 July 2015, the Final Resolution of the Second Review of the Effective Compensation Quota was published in the DOF. It was determined to extend it for five more years. D. Notice on the validity of compensatory fees5. On 11 September 2018, the Notice on the validity of compensatory fees was published in the DOF.\u160\'00\'A0The national producers and any person having a legal interest were thus informed that the final countervailing contributions imposed on the products listed in that Notice would be eliminated from the expiry date indicated therein for each, unless a domestic producer expressed in writing his interest in initiating an examination procedure. The list included coal steel connections for butt welding, which is the subject of this examination. E. Manifestation of interest6. On 24 June 2019, \uc2 Tubos de Acero de M\u233\'00\'E9xico, S.A\uc2 . (TAMSA) expressed its interest in the Secretariat initiating the effective review of the final countervailing quota imposed on imports of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding originating in China. F. Resolution initiating the third review of the validity of the compensatory fee7.\u160\'00\'A0On 17 July 2019, the Secretariat published in the DOF the Resolution declaring the beginning of the review of the countervailing quota imposed on imports of coal butt steel connections originating in China (the "Initiation Resolution"). The review period was set from 1 July 2018 to 30 June 2019 and as an analysis period from 1 July 2014 to 30 June 2019. G. Product under review1.\u160\'00\'A0Product Description8.\u160\'00\'A0The product under review is thecarbon steel connections for butt welding with external diameters in the range of 1/2 to 16 inches, including both, and includes elbows, "tees", reductions and caps (elbows, tees, reducers and caps). They are also known as coal steel fittings for butt welding, or by their name "fittings". 9. In addition to the dimensions of the product, other physical and chemical properties that are also described by product under review are ductility, impact resistance and static or dynamic loads, as well as durability, which are depending on their final use. 2. Tariff treatment10. The product under review enters the domestic market by tariff fraction 7307.93.01 of the General Import and Export Tax Act (TIGIE) Tariff, which is as follows:Tariff codingDescriptionChapter 73Admel, Iron or Steel Handpartition 7307 Pipe fittings (e.g. fittings, elbows, sleeves), cast iron, iron or steel.\u160\'00\'A0\u8211\'20\'13 Others:Subheading 7307.93\u8211\'20\'13 Butt welding accessories. Fraction 7307.93.01Accessories for butt welding. Source: Tariff Information System Via Internet (SIAVI). 11. The unit of measurement used in THE TIGIE is the kilogram, although commercial \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are usually carried out by parts. 12. Based on SIAVI's information, the Secretariat noted that imports of coal-to-butt coal connections entering tiGIE tariff fraction 7307.93.01 are duty-free. 3. Productive process13. The process of making coal-fired steel connections for butt welding is basically the same worldwide. It starts with cutting the steel pipe and sheet plate, in sections and circles, respectively, in appropriate sizes, according to the type of connection to be manufactured and their size.\u160\'00\'A0It can be finished by heat treatment, beveling, blasting, stamping or painting, or not finished. 14. The process to produce each connection is as follows:a. Elbows: the pipe sections are passed over a chuck that provides the elbow shape by means of force and heat. Automatic hydraulic presses and natural gas furnaces at controlled temperature with an accuracy of +/- 3 degrees Celsius are used. b. "Tees": the third branch is formed from the section of the tube by extrusion. Heat and force is required in automatic double-acting presses to perform this process. c. Reductions: the pipe sections are preheated, then placed in a mold where, by means of an automatic press, the reduced part is formed. The product obtained is heat treated to remove the efforts from the forging. d. Caps: by dice and bullets the cap shape is provided to the circle of plate on sheet. The process is done cold or hot. The parts are heat treated for standardization. 15. By means of automatic blasting and finishing machines, the elbows, "tees" and reductions are cleaned and their ends beveled. The lids are bevelled in lathes. 16. The main inputs for manufacturing the product under review are the seamless carbon steel pipe with which elbows, "tees" and reductions are produced, and the sheet plate with which the caps are manufactured. Other raw materials include electric power, natural gas, paints and varnishes. 4. Standards17. Coal-fired steel connections for butt welding are normally manufactured according toASTM A 234 / A 234 M-00, issued by the \uc2 American Society for Testing and Materials\uc2  (ASTM, "\uc2 American Society for Testing and Materials\uc2 ") and ANSI/ASME (B16.9-2001 and B16.28-1994), issued by the American Institute of National Standards and the \uc2 American Society of Mechanical Engineers\uc2 ("ANSI" and "ASME", respectively), although, as stated by TAMSA, they are usually manufactured without complying with any specific standard. 18. Various international standards set the maximum carbon, phosphorus, sulphur, manganese, silicon and chromium that must contain carbon steel connections for butt welding. For example, B16.9-2001 provides that a steel connection to elbow-type carbon manufactured with grade B steel and in diameters from 21.3 to 1,219 millimetres must have a maximum carbon content, manganese, phosphorus, sulfur, silicon and chromium 0.30, 1.06, 0.05, 0.058, 0.10 and 0.40%, respectively. 5. Uses and functions19.\u160\'00\'A0The main function of elbows, "tees" and reductions is to connect the ends of two or more tubes to change the direction, branch and reduce or increase the diameter of the pipe line, respectively. The function of the caps is to close the pipe line or the end of a mechanical or hydraulic vessel. 20. These goods are mainly used in pipe lines for the conduction of various fluids (water, steam, petrochemicals and gas, mainly) in various systems, e.g. industrial, heating, plumbing, air conditioning and irrigation. They are also commonly used as inputs to manufacture boilers or heat exchange equipment, or to connect pipe lines to some appliance or machinery. H. Call and notifications21.\u160\'00\'A0By publishing the Initiation Resolution, the Secretariat convened domestic producers, importers, exporters and anyone considering to have a legal interest in the outcome of this review to appear to present the arguments and evidence they deemed relevant. 22. The Secretariat notified the parties to the parties that it was aware of and the Government of China to the commenced the initiation of the present proceedings. I. Interested Parties appearing23. He appeared to the procedure in a timely manner only the national producer:\uc2 Tubos de Acero de M\u233\'00\'E9xico\uc2 , S.A.Campos El\u237\'00\'EDseos No. 400, 17Col floor. Chapultepec PolancoC.P. 11560, Mexico CityJ. First trial offer period24.\u160\'00\'A0At TAMSA's request, the Secretariat granted it a ten-business extension to submit its response to the official form, as well as the arguments and evidence for the first period of offering evidence. On 9 September 2019, TAMSA submitted the reply to the official form, as well as the arguments and evidence agreed to by its right, which are contained in the administrative reference file, which were considered for the issuance of this Resolution. K. Replicas25.\u160\'00\'A0As no counterparties appeared in domestic production, no aftershocks were presented. L. Information Requirements1. Overtime26.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat granted ten skilled experts to submit its response to the request for information made on 16 October 2019. The deadline expired on 13 November 2019, respectively. 2. Domestic producer27. On 16 October 2019, the Secretariat required TAMSA, among other issues, to submit the methodology for calculating themaritime freight adjustment, as well as those for the term of sale cost and freight (CFR); provide, with respect to the product under review, elements to prove that cost and price structures that are not determined in accordance with market principles, the similarity between the technology used in the Chinese and Mexico production process, its production cost structure and the participation of each of the production factors used intensively in its manufacture prevail in its production and sale; explain the distortion in the use of many factors from government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , how distortions in the production of seamless coal steel pipe are transferred to the product under review, whether steel connection companies in China are state-owned or privately owned, and whether there is any degree of integration with seamless coal steel pipe producers; provide elements to infer that China's export price to Hong Kong can be considered as an internal price from China; support the documents that served to develop the "China Analysis as a Non-Market Economy" that it submitted, as well as those it used to calculate the reported amounts for inputs and other manufacturing expenses, sales and administration expenses, financial expenses, reported interest and performance, and to establish that the United States imposed countervailing measures on China; evidence to support the eliminate of the countervailing quota to the product under review, the dumping practice would continue or be repeated; that the steel connection industry in the United States is operating under the principles of a market economy; the similarity between the product sold in the United States market and the product under review, its production process, an explanation as to whether the intensity in the use of production factors is equivalent in both countries and whether they are major producers of sheet plate, main input for the manufacture of caps, and energy; provide support for the documents that served to develop the market study for the determination of normal value, prepared by \uc2 White & Case\uc2 , clarify why it made a margin adjustment and whether the prices of the producers it reported are at the ex-factory level; information on imports of the product under review, indicators from the domestic industry and its company for the review period, as well as the four previous comparable periods and projections for the periods July 2019 to June 2020 and July 2020 to June 2021. With respect to its projections, it would provide an explanation of the adjustment for internalization costs it proposed, on the bank of Mexico's use of Gross Domestic Product (GDP) growth forecast to project Apparent Domestic Consumption (ANC), and arguments on the mechanism of transmission of China's export potential to domestic industry. It submitted its reply on 13 November 2019. 28. On 28 January 2020, the Secretariat requested TAMSA, among other issues, to explain and provide documentary support for freight prices per container of 20 feet from various ports in China to the United States; documentary support for the maximum load of a 20-foot container; an explanation of the methodology you used to estimate the inflation rate for October 2019 and the inflation rate for the period July 2018-June 2019, with data from the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (IMF); market study for normal value determination will clarify whether the reported pricescorrespond to quotations or price lists, present the worksheets for each distributor, as well as the documentary support that the products considered to calculate the discount adjustment correspond to those identified as higher consumption products in Mexico, and clarify various aspects in relation to the deflactation that it presented to make the adjustments for discount and margin of marketing.\u160\'00\'A0It submitted its reply on 11 February 2020. 3. No parts29. On 16 October 2019, the Secretariat requested CANACERO to clarify various aspects of the methodology it used to debug imports of steel connections during the analysis period. It submitted a response on 6 November 2019. 30. On 23 October and 14 November 2019, the Secretariat requested a number of customs agents and importing companies, respectively, to submit import pediments and accompanying documentation. The deadline expired on 6 and 29 November 2019, respectively. M. Second trial offer period31.\u160\'00\'A0On 21 November 2019, the Secretariat notified TAMSA of the opening of the second period of offering evidence. On 15 January 2020, TAMSA presented additional arguments and evidence, which are in the administrative file of the case, which were considered for the issuance of this Resolution. N. Other appearances32.\u160\'00\'A0On 3 September 2019, CANACERO submitted a study for the determination of the export price of the product under review, as well as the methodology used for this calculation and for the analysis of imports, drawn from statistical information on imports that entered through TIGIE tariff fraction 7307.93.01 during the period July 2014 to June 2019, provided to it by the Tax Administration Service (SAT). O. Essential Facts33. On 8 May 2020, the Secretariat notified TAMSA of the essential facts of this procedure, which served as the basis for issuing this Resolution, in accordance with Articles 6.9 and 11.4 of the Agreement on the Implementation of Article VI of the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade 1994 (the "Anti-Dumping Agreement"). TAMSA did not present any manifestations of the essential facts.\u160\'00\'A0Q. Public hearing34. On 15 May 2020, the public hearing of this proceedings was held. Only TAMSA participated, who had the opportunity to present its arguments, as stated in the minutes that it was raised for that reason, which constitutes a public document of full evidentiary effectiveness, in accordance with Article 46(I) of the Federal Law on Administrative Litigation Procedure (LFPCA). Q. Allegations35. On 19 May 2020 TAMSA submitted its pleadings, which were considered to be issued in this Resolution. A. Opinion of the Committee on Foreign Trade36. On the basis of Articles 89 F. III of the Foreign Trade Act (LCE) and 19 fraction XI of the Internal Regulations of the Secretariat of the Economy (RISE), the draft of this Resolution was submitted to the opinion of the Committee on Foreign Trade, which considered it at its meeting on 22 July 2020. The project was welcomed by a majority. RecitalsA. Competition37. The Secretariat has jurisdiction to issue this Resolution, in accordance with Articles 16 and 34 fractions V and XXXIII of the Organic Law of the Federal Public Administration; 1, 2 paragraph A number II number 7 and 19 fractions I and IV of the RISE; 11.1, 11.3, 11.4, 12.2 and 12.3 of the AD Agreement, and 5fraction VII, 67, 70 fraction II and 89 F of the LCE. B. Applicable Law38. For the purposes of this procedure, the Anti-Dumping Agreement, the LCE, the Regulations of the Foreign Trade Act (RLCE), the Federation Tax Code, the LFPCA applied in addition, in accordance with the Second Transitional Article of the Decree issuing the LFPCA, as well as the Federal Code of Civil Procedures, the latter three of supplementary application, apply the Act. C. Protection of Confidential Information39. The Secretariat cannot publicly disclose the confidential information that the interested parties submitted, nor the confidential information that it itself received, in accordance with Articles 6.5 of the AD Agreement, 80 of the LCE, and 152 and 158 of the ERC. D. Law of defence and due process40. Stakeholders had ample opportunity to present all kinds of arguments, exceptions and defences, as well as evidence to support them, in accordance with the AD Agreement, the LCE and the ERCE. The Secretariat assessed them subject to the essential formalities of the administrative procedure. E. Analysis of continuation or recurrence of dumping41. The Secretariat conducted the review of continuation or recurrence of dumping on the basis of the information and evidence submitted by the domestic producer TAMSA, as well as the one reached, in accordance with the provisions of the second paragraph and 64 last paragraph of the LCE. During the review procedure, the Secretariat gave the exporting companies ample opportunity, as well as the Chinese government to state what their right would agree, however, did not appear. 1. Export price42. TAMSA stated that, in an effective review, the AD Agreement does not require a new determination to calculate the margin of price discrimination. In this regard, it cited the Report of the Appellate Body of the \uc2 World Trade Organization (WTO) \uc2 in US\u8211\'20\'13Sunset Review of Anti-Dumping Duties on Corrosion Resistant Carbon Steel Flat Products from Japan (WT/DS244/AB/R) of 15 December 2003, that in paragraph 123 it states that Article 11.3 of the AD Agreement "does not expressly require the authorities in an examination by inspection to calculate new dumping margins, nor does it expressly prohibit them from relying on pre-calculated dumping margins". In addition, it added that paragraph 127 of the same report also states that "However, if investigating authorities choose to rely on dumping margins when making their likelihood determination, the calculation of these margins must be consistent with the disciplines of Article 2.2". 43. TAMSA proposed six scenarios for accrediting the export price, four of which were submitted by CANACERO and drawn up on the basis of the import statistics it obtained from the SAT.\u160\'00\'A0Since TIGIE's tariff fraction 7307.93.01 also provides non-TIGIE product, CANACERO used automatic notices from the Comprehensive Foreign Trade Information System (SIICEX) to verify the dimensions, standards, grade of steel, as well as that the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  correspond to elbow, tee, reduction and cap forms, in order to identify imports for the product under review that paid compensatory fee, during the review period. 44. The two remaining export price scenarios, presented by TAMSA, correspond to export quotes from steel connection manufacturers in China, and to the export price of China to India,as the third country, which it obtained from China's export statistics, China's export statistics, the latter, as an alternative option and to confirm that China continues to export the subject merchandise at discriminated prices. 45. However, TAMSA stated that, in order to prove the export price, it considered one of the scenarios provided by CANACERO, which contains only the purified imports of product under review shipped directly from China. 46. For its part, the Secretariat reached the list of total imports that entered by TIGIE tariff fraction 7307.93.01 during the review period, which it obtained from the Mexico Trade Information System (SIC-M). It compared this information with the information provided by CANACERO, finding differences in the number of transactions that paid compensatory fees and, therefore, in value and volume, so it determined to use the SIC-M's base of \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on the basis that the information contained in that database is obtained prior validation of the customs pediments given in a framework of exchange of information between customs agents and agents, on the one hand, and the authority to provide on the other, which is reviewed by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  and is therefore considered as the best available information. 47. In addition, in order to have greater elements regarding the identification of the product under review, the Secretariat required importers and customs agents to provide import pediments and supplementary information on \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that paid compensatory fee during the review period. The information obtained represented 94% of the total volume of imports that paid countervailing fee. 48. On the basis of the above, the Secretariat identified 89 types of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding, taking into account the type of connection and the outer diameter, i.e. elbows, "tees", reductions and caps, in diameters in the range of 1/2 to 16 inches. 49. Based on the information and documents indicated, the Secretariat calculated the dollar-weighted average export price per kilogram for each of the 89 types of coal-to-butt steel connections for the review period in accordance with Articles 39 and 40 of the RLCE. The volume in kilograms for each type of connection was identified in the invoices and in the attached documents. a. Adjustments to the export price50. TAMSA noted that, derived from the information that CANACERO provided, it became aware that prices were expressed at the border of entry level (customs value) and also at commercial value, i.e. invoice value. It considered that such an invoice price did not require adjustment for maritime freight, so no adjustment to the export price was necessary. 51. For its part, the Secretariat identified the terms and conditions of sale of each transaction in import pediments or sales invoices. According to the revised information, imports were made free-to-board (FOB), cost, insurance and freight (CIF) and agreed destination (DAP). 52. In this regard, the Secretariat requested TAMSA to submit the information and methodology for calculating the maritime freight adjustment, as well as those for the cfR sales term. In its response to the REQUEST for information TAMSA only provided the calculation for maritime freight adjustment, so the Secretariat,depending on the terms of sale, it adjusted the export price for that concept, based on the information provided by TAMSA. i. Maritime freight53. TAMSA submitted an estimate of the dollar cost per ton incurred from transporting the goods to Mexico in a 20-foot container during the review period. To document the cost of freight he used the monthly price reports reported by the company Drewry on its website and information it obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://es.thetimenow.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://es.thetimenow.com}}}\uc2 , to obtain the distance of threedistort ports in China to the port of Manzanillo, Mexico. With the above information, the Secretariat estimated the dollar cost per kilogram for the review period to transport the goods from China to Mexico. ii. Determination54. On the basis of Articles 2.4 of the AD Agreement, 36 of the LCE and 53 and 54 of the RLCE, the Secretariat accepted the information provided by TAMSA and adjusted the export price by sea freight. 2. Normal value.\u160\'00\'A0China as a non-market economy55.\u160\'00\'A0TAMSA noted that non-market conditions prevail in China and the butt-welded steel connections industry, in accordance with the Protocol of Accession of the People's Republic of China to the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  (the "China Protocol to the WTO") and Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the ERCE. 56. It indicated that paragraph 15(a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO provided for the possibility of using prices or costs in China or a different methodology for price comparability purposes. In this regard, it considers that under subparagraph (a) of paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, which remains in force, the use of domestic prices in China is subject to the condition that the producers under investigation clearly demonstrate that market conditions prevail in the industry. On the other hand, the expiration of Roma (ii) of paragraph 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO does not prevent the authority from using a different methodology in the event that the respective sector is not shown to operate under market conditions. 57. Added that, in the case of China, the Secretariat had emphasized that, although some clauses of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO were no longer applicable, the possibility of China being considered a non-market economy had not disappeared, and that this determination depended on the arguments and evidence presented by the interested parties in the proceedings. 58. In this regard, TAMSA presented the following arguments and evidence to show that in China and in the butt-welding steel connections sector, structures and costs that are not determined in accordance with market principles prevail, in accordance with the criteria of Article 48 of the RLCE:a. The exchange rate is under the control of the State:i. TAMSA noted that the Chinese authority's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the exchange market remains considerable. It provided the following graphs with the historical evolution of the real and nominal exchange rate, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  portal Oanda and The Hindu BusinessLine magazine;\par \par \uc2 ii. explained that the graph on the left compares the historical evolution of the real and nominal exchange rate from 2011 to 2015. The graph illustrates how in the long run the Chinese authorities set a exchange rate that tends to be rigid compared to fluctuationsthe real evolution of the purchasing power of coins. The graph on the right, published in The Hindu BusinessLine magazine for the period 2007 to 2018, shows a gap between the actual versus nominal exchange rate. In particular, it is noted that, in the short term, the authorities have appreciated their local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but in such a way that the nominal exchange rate remains considerably separated from the real exchange rate.\u160\'00\'A0In short, the graphs show a management of the exchange market that does not reflect contact with market signals; iii. argued that the role of the Chinese government is not boiled down to determining the exchange rate, it encompasses severe control, through a rigorous dual control mechanism, since, although there is nominally a single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is handled in two separate markets: onshore (CNY) and offshore (CNH), as noted in the document "The Dual Exchange Rate System (CNY vs CNH)" published by \uc2 Mizuho Bank, Ltd\uc2  in June 2018; iv. according to the document "CNH vs CNY: What are its main differences between the two yuan", published in the journal DAILYFX, on 12 September 2018, the CNH refers to the Chinese yuan in the external market, which is located outside Mainland China, which includes the traditional centres of Hong Kong, Singapore, London and newly developed centres such as Luxembourg, while in mainland China the CNY is managed. The exchange of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the yuan can only be carried out in certain state-owned banks, in strictly limited quantities and according to the exchange rate established by Chinese government; (v) while companies may exchange \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for CNH, they must prove that they require it for international trade \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  or financial movements, as the share of entities in the CNH market is restricted to qualified companies, as indicated in the CME Group Report "Chinese Renminbi Offshore Market (CNH)", published on the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.cmegroup.com/trading/fx/emerging-market7usd-cnh.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.cmegroup.com/trading/\b  \highlight22fx\b0 \highlight22 /emerging-market7usd-cnh.html}}}\uc2 , of 4 March 2014; since 2016, the IMF has incorporated the yuan as part of the international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket that makes up the financial instrument called Special Drawing Rights (SDO), however, the IMF itself has noted that China's monetary reforms have been insufficient. According to the imf's "IMF Country Report No. 18/240", the renminbi remained stable against the basket published by China's Exchange Trading System in 2017, but with a greater fluctuation compared to the dollar and has appreciated by about 2% in real effective terms in the first half of 2018. It points out that it is necessary to implement continuous structural reforms; in particular, it stresses that the central parity mechanism for the daily flotation band must be transparent and mechanical, with the exchange rate influenced by the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the external exchange market and by public communication where necessary, rather than by administrative measures, andvii.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0Article 48 of the RLCE does not require an analysis of this criterion at the industry level, but clarified that the centralized determination of the exchange rate and restriction on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  convertibility affects the steel sector and, in particular, the industry under review. In that regard, it indicated that, according to the information it obtained from the Internet pages of steel producers in China and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.made-in-china.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.made-in-china.com}}}\uc2 , these companies typically exported between 30% and 90% of their total production. Under these conditions, Chinese connection producers guide major shipmentscommodity that is usually quoted in dollars per tonne on international markets. Thus, if the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued, this will result in a competitive advantage. b. Wages and labour flow are under the control of the State:i. TAMSA referred that wages are another economic variable that are subject to rigorous state control. According to information from the Observatory on International Labour Relations at the \uc2 Complutense University of Madrid\uc2 , China refuses to ratify the Conventions of the International Labour Organization ("ILO"), concerning the right to freedom of association, as well as fundamental instruments related to the abolition of forced labour, labour inspection and others; ii. the most recent status information on the relationship between trade unions, government and single party remains the same, i.e. the trade union organization is in theory permitted, but should be integrated into a single trade union body, controlled in turn by the Communist Party of China (PCCh), according to China Bussines Review's report, "Trade Union Law and Collective Bargaining in China", published on the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.chinabusinessreview.com/trade-union-law-and-collective-bargaining-in-china/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.chinabusinessreview.com/trade-union-law-and-collective-bargaining-in-china/}}}\uc2 , in April 2017; (iii) according to the article "Obsolete urban passports still govern the lives of rural citizens of China", published on the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/politics/outdated-urban-passports-still-rule-the-lives-of-china-s-rural-citizens-a7517181.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/politics/outdated-urban-passports-still-rule-the-lives-of-china-s-rural-citizens-a7517181.html}}}\uc2 , on 13 January 2017, the hukou system is an internal migration control mechanism that centrally regulates the flow of labour, which was imposed during industrializing policy since the 1950s by theCH and which persists to date; iv. TAMSA presented the following graph illustrating the fact that the migrant workers sector has lagged behind and its gap in urban workers has widened, so it can be seen as an anchor for the average cost of labour in China, according to the study "A Simple Model of the Hukou Chinese System and Some Ongoing Reforms", published by the Institute for Economics Research and Business Administration at The University of Kobe, Japan in 2017:\par \par \uc2 (v) The same document notes that hukou reform is one of the most complex issues in the Chinese transition, and is closely related to the urbanization of millions of peasants, the migration of urban residents through cities, land ownership and the equity of the education system; vi. TAMSA provided the article "The Chinese Hukou System at Age 60: Continuity and Reform" by Kam Wing Chan, professor at the University of Washington, published on February 2, 2018. The document states that, despite dramatic social and economic changes over the past four decades, the hukou system remains an institution that endures. Its continued importance indicates how far or even how little China has strayed from its socialist path. Beyond the control of migration, the hukou system was the mechanism for organizing labor for rapid industrialization in the first three decades in that country. The immobilization of the peasantry allowed the state to organize farmers' crops to support the first primary objective of industrialization. In the era of reform, the hukou system has beenreshaped to serve the state's new imperative on export-oriented industrialization, contrary to the previous immobilization strategy, the peasantry was liberated to create a huge class of cheap and mobile labor, andvii.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0It added that Article 48 of the RLCE did not require an analysis of this criterion at the industry level, but the centralized determination of labour costs the steel sector and the product industry under review. It indicated that labour was an integral part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the steel sector, under these conditions, if wages were not determined according to market conditions, this would result, in conjunction with the other factors under consideration, of an artificial competitive advantage. c. Prices, costs and wares are under state control:i. TAMSA argued that in China the State continues to centrally and planly control macroeconomic variables that determine prices and costs, particularly in the steel sector. According to China's Trade Policy Review Report WT/TPR/S/342, published by the WTO, on 15 June 2016 (China Trade Policy Review 2016), a basic economic model remains in China in which public ownership remains the central pillar of the economy, although parallel developments in various forms of ownership are permitted, sectors of strategic importance (e.g. energy, utilities, transport, financial, telecommunications, education and health care services) remain only partially open to private investment; ii. China's Trade Policy Review of 2016 also notes that the Chinese authorities may apply price controls to goods and services considered to be of direct importance to the national economy and livelihoods of the population, and that these price controls are established by the \uc2 National Development and Reform Commission\uc2  at the central level, and by the Provincial Development and Reform Commissions and the Office for Product Prices in each province; iii. in accordance with China's Trade Policy Review Report number WT/TPR/S/375, published by the WTO on 6 June 2018 (China Trade Policy Review 2018), this situation continues, because even though the government reports direct control over only a limited number of goods and services, in fact Section 18 of China's Pricing Act allows government pricing to "products that have significant influence on the national economy and livelihoods of population," as well as services and products that the government determines are key to its economy; iv. at the industry and industry level, TAMSA argued that the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , in the document "On significant distortions in the economy of the People's Republic of China for the purposes of trade defence investigations", of 20 December 2017, confirmed that in the acero sector state-owned enterprises play a central role. The \uc2 European Commission\uc2  estimated that about half of the companies in the sector are direct state-owned (51% private and 49% state-owned when measuring production, and 56% private and 44% state-owned when measuring capacity); (v) the document notes that there is also a significant presence of state-owned enterprises in the mining industry, supplier of raw materials for steel production; for example, large steel production companies such as Anshan Iron & Steel Corporation, \uc2 Panzhihua Iron &Steel Corporation\uc2  and Benxi Steel are companies in the steel sector and also own iron mines; he added that the government exercises control over the sector through state-owned enterprises.\u160\'00\'A0According to the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 's report, these companies play a leading role in the steel sector, so that private companies cannot operate under market conditions; vii. mentioned that the supplies of basic inputs for the steel sector are made through state-owned enterprises that handle prices determined by the authorities. In addition to oil and gas, electricity is another of the basic inputs of the steel sector and is also controlled by the state. The authorities have implemented reforms to liberalize electricity prices (especially industrial ones), but transmission and distribution tariffs are officially established in accordance with the principle of costs plus reasonable benefits at different voltages, with the government calculating that cost and determining the reasonableness of those benefits, in accordance with China's Trade Policy Review 2018; viii. presented electricity and gas prices that it obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.statista.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.statista.com}}}\uc2  and the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 , respectively. He mentioned that, by comparing international electricity and gas prices with China's prices, it is noted that China reports artificially low prices. The effects of artificially low prices on inputs can be seen in the prices of billets or billets that can be used to manufacture seamless pipe, the main input of the connections under review. The prices of inputs used to manufacture the key raw material of butt welding steel connections are significantly deviate from international prices; (ix) according to the document "On significant distortions in the economy of the People's Republic of China for the purposes of trade defence Investigations", of December 2017, the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  noted that coke, together with iron ore, were subject to an export tax of 40%, as well as the imposition of quantitative restrictions; According to the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , these mechanisms have led to a situation in which the price of the raw material continues to be the result of State \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which it considers to continue to affect steel producers, including seamless pipe producers, which are the ones supplying the raw material for steel connections or are themselves producers of the product under review; x. stated that authorities such as the \uc2 United States Department of Commerce\uc2  (USDOC), the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , and Canada, have conducted subsidy investigations for a number of Chinese steel companies, where countervailing measures have been imposed.\u160\'00\'A0It added that the subsidies identified directly affect the inputs, raw materials and final prices of the sector under review, in addition, the amount of subsidies had been valued as significant by the international authorities; xi. explained that from the world steel association's (WSA) list of the top steel producing countries, identified the two main Chinese producers of seamless pipe, Valin Group and Baotou Steel, which are state-owned. He added that DOC found that these companies receive round steel billets, electricity, and land use at distorted prices; support by transferringfunds for industry technology projects, regional subsidies, tax benefits with accelerated depreciation programs, exemptions on equipment importation and reduced regional taxes, as well as tax credits on domestic equipment procurement; (xii. stated that information on the steel connection manufacturing industry is highly dispersed and untransparent, however, the available data would conclude that the economic distortions that have also been described also characterize this subsequent stage of the production chain; xiii. indicated that Chinese exporters of steel connections face anti-dumping measures on the international market. In particular, in the case of the United States, they face countervailing fees ranging from 35% to 182.9% since July 1992. The above, according to the \uc2 United States International Trade Commission\uc2  (USITC) document "Investigation Nos. 731-TA-308-310 and 520-521 (Fourth Review)" publication 4628; xiv. based on the document "Factsheet-multiple-forged-steel-fittings-AD-CVD-final", USDOC, available on the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://enforcement.trade.gov"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://enforcement.trade.gov}}}\uc2 , indicated that Chinese producers of forged steel connections have been subject to countervailing measures, as THE USDOC found that these producers enjoy subsidies of 13.41%, in addition to incurring price discrimination of between 8% and 142.72%. It clarified that those manufacturers also often produce the steel connections under review; xv. \uc2 European Commission\uc2  Implementing Regulation (EU) 2017/141 states that in the European Union Chinese exporters of stainless steel connections face anti-dumping measures ranging from 30.7% to 64.9%. He clarified that producers of coal steel connections are often also offering stainless steel connections; xvi. presented a list of Chinese companies producing butt-welded coal steel connections that it produced with information it obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.made-in-china.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.made-in-china.com}}}\uc2  and from the companies themselves. In this regard, it mentioned that 20 identified companies are direct manufacturers of steel connections in China, and in turn 50% of them are located in the so-called economic development zones, which are strategic areas in which the government implements policies such as tax exemptions, debt forgiveness and direct transfers of funds, among other government support programs; xvii. from the previous analysis, identified The company Hainan Huatongxinda Trade Co., Ltd., which reports greater production capacity, and who, in turn, is an integrated company that also manufactures seamless pipe. According to its information, this company has long-term relationships with government-owned oil companies and exports between 71% and 90% of its production. It also identified Guangzhou Kingmetal Steel Industry Co., Ltd and Chengdu Derbo Steel Co., Ltd. as producers of steel connections and pipe offerings, the latter with an export level of 90%. Regarding China NorthIndustries Corp., which faces an anti-dumping quota of 154.72% in steel connections in the United States, it said it is a 100% state-owned corporate, which is listed as one of the 500 largest companies in the country; 20 producers of connections identified, noted that 13 of them (65%)\u160\'00\'A0they also produce forged steel connections, products that are manufactured with the advantage of government supports in the form of subsidies. He indicated that thesesubsidies are not necessarily limited to an isolated product line, so it concluded that there are reasonable indicators that these companies, as manufacturers of the connections under review, operate with such artificial advantages; (xix) concluded that information at the industry level shows that steel connection manufacturers enjoy state support via the supply of seamless pipe at non-market prices. In addition, they enjoy similar benefits in the subsequent phase of the production chain, as indicated by the area of geoeconomic development in which they are located, as well as the fact that the main producers of connections have strategic links with state-owned enterprises, or are themselves state-owned, and that they are integrated enterprises and/or with parallel production lines that, in both cases, operate with substantial government support; xx. in addition, presented the following table showing that prices of connections in the Chinese domestic market, a sale price from China to its autonomous province called the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region, are significantly below international prices. This, based on statistical information published by \uc2 the International Trade Centre\uc2 , United Nations Trade Map:\par \par \uc2 xxi. in this regard, he explained that the price of connections in Chinese territory, as it is below international benchmarks, occurs in parallel with the existence of subsidies and other forms of public fund transfers, both to the Chinese steel sector, to its seamless pipe industry and to connection manufacturers in particular. d. Foreign investment is under state control:i. at the macroeconomic level, TAMSA indicated that, China's Trade Policy Review 2018, confirms that the Foreign Investment Catalogue (the "Investment Catalogue"), which is the main instrument for guiding foreign direct investment in that country, continues to operate. The 2017 version of the Investment Catalogue classifies them into the recommended category, or in a negative list containing a list of industries where foreign direct investment is restricted or prohibited; ii. projects that are outside the negative list require the presentation of requests for registration or are not allowed. For their part, projects in the recommended category are eligible for preferential treatment, for example, they enjoy exemptions from customs duties for the importation of equipment; iii. in China's Trade Policy Review of 2018, the WTO noted that China continued to encourage foreign direct investment in the central and western regions, where projects can benefit from favourable policies, in addition to the application of subjection to administrative approval and registration processes; iv. the "\uc2 OECD\uc2 's FDI restrictiveness index, 2010 updata" document of the Organization for Economic Co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Development (\uc2 OECD\uc2 ), presents the index on restriction of foreign direct investment, which quantifies four types of a country's measures in 22 different sectors: (i) restrictions on foreign capital, (ii) selection and prior approval requirements, (iii) rules for key personnel, and (iv) other restrictions related to the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of foreign enterprises. According to this index, the score for China represents a wide range ofrange of sectors virtually closed, including the financial sector, being that China is the worst-rated country, with key sectors of the economy absolutely beyond the scope of foreign investment; (v) At the sectoral and industrial level, TAMSA added that the steel sector is not explicitly indicated in the Investment Catalogue of the GOC, which means that, although it is not a prohibited sector, it is also not considered encouraged, so it is subject to control mechanisms via registration and examination of the authorities; (vi) according to information from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scmp.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.scmp.com}}}\uc2 , foreign companies wishing to invest in China's steel sector must have intellectual property rights, have an annual general steel production capacity of more than 10 million tonnes, or at least one million tonnes of special alloyed steel; in addition to having capital strength and relatively high credibility, verifiable with supporting documentation from banks and accounting firms; vii. on the website referred to above, it is also noted that foreign companies must comply with government restrictions designed to limit new capacity, so that they can only invest in old plants, renew projects or invest in new plants while closing equivalent capacity in outdated facilities; thus, despite China's strategic place as the world's largest consumer and producer of steel, foreign companies have only limited investment in a Chinese steel sector by state-backed enterprises; viii. TAMSA argued that foreign investment management policy also affects the steel sector via its impact on companies that provide raw materials, inputs and services.\u160\'00\'A0According to China's Trade Policy Review of 2018, there is state control over gas and electricity, inputs required by the steel industry, as well as in the mining sector, which is a supplier of inputs in steel production. The distribution of natural gas is mainly controlled by state-owned \uc2 China National Petroleum Corporation\uc2  and \uc2 China Petrochemical Corporation\uc2 , while private companies play a larger role only in gas retail sectors; ix. China's Trade Policy Review of 2018 also notes that the foreign investment regime in the electricity sector is regulated by the 2017 Investment Catalogue, which stipulates that the construction and \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of networks, including transmission and distribution of electricity, falls into the restricted category and must be controlled by the Chinese government. Foreign investors can participate in the construction and \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of networks through partnerships with Chinese companies, but the networks must be controlled by the government. It is the authorities that define transmission and distribution rates officially, which naturally helps to restrict the flow of investment to this energy sector; x. in the same document indicates that the mining sector is included in the prohibited category in the Investment Catalogue and remained unchanged between 2015 and 2017; this includes the exploration and exploitation of tungsten, molybdenum, tin, styb, fluorite, rare earths and other minerals that can be used by the steel sector. Some changes have been made for the recommended and restricted categories related to mining, but the exploration and exploitation of special and rare types of coal and graphite, as well as the smelting and separation ofrare earths and tungsten smelting fall into the restricted category, andxi.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0investment regulation also affects industrial consumers of seamless pipe, raw material of steel connections. According to the above-mentioned document, most line pipe networks, which are used to transport oil and natural gas, belong to three state-owned enterprises, which are listed on the Hong Kong Stock Exchange and are allowed to participate in the minority share of foreign investors, but are subject to national security review. e. Accounting does not follow international standards:(i) in accordance with China's Trade Policy Review of 2018, the practice of accounting services in China is permitted only in the form of limited liability companies or companies, established and administered by certified public accountants licensed by the Chinese authorities. Foreigners may take China's national accounting exam and foreign accounting firms may join Chinese firms and enter into contractual agreements; ii. according to the document "Managing your accounting and bookkeeping in China", by the international accounting services firm Dezan Shira & Associates, the presence of foreign accounting firms is in the category of encouraged in the Investment Catalogue, but subject to the principal partner being a local Chinese company. This source notes that the "China Accounting Standards" system remains the mandatory system of use in China, and notes that rules have been issued called Generally Accepted Accounting Practices in China, which seek greater convergence with international accounting, however, these are published by china's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which decides whether a standard issued by an international body is incorporated into the Chinese accounting system, implying regulation by the government; iii. in the 2017 Annual Financial Report of Hunan Valin Group, which integrates seamless pipe production companies, it is stated that the 2017 annual financial report has been audited by Tianjian certified public accountants, but adds that, during the reporting period, the accounting firm issued an unqualified standard audit report to the company and clarifies that the accounting standards for commercial enterprises No. 42, issued by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 28 May 2017, have been implemented in that report. TAMSA concludes that, although it is a company listed on the local stock exchange, accountants certified by the authority that made the report do not apply the standard accounting rules and, rather, the accounts centrally formulated by that country's financial authority, and(iv).\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0there is no availability of accounting information from companies in the sector, either it is scarce or unreliable. In general, international observers point out that accounting standards in China follow rudimentary registration patterns or follow government guidelines. With regard to the connection industry, it noted that none of the identified companies disseminates basic financial data or annual reports, and that their accounting information is not accessible to the public. f. Cost and financial distortions:(i) at the macroeconomic level, TAMSA noted that China's Trade Policy Review of 2018 indicates that financial control in China remains unchanged, where major monetary policy decisions on loans and feesrequired to be approved by the State Council, and are established directly by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China\uc2 ; ii. the banking sector continues to be characterized by the existence of predominant state-owned categories. According to the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 's working paper, "On significant distortions in the economy of the People's Republic of China for the purposes of trade defence investigations" of December 2017, government control in the Chinese banking sector is seen in large commercial banks, commercial stock banks and state policy banking. Government banking, under some of these modalities, accounts for nearly 70% of China's total banking assets. The rest mainly corresponds to smaller rural or urban commercial banks, mostly owned by local or provincial governments. Foreign-invested banks remain negligible in China; iii. the same \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document explains that the Chinese legal framework provides that banks are mechanisms for implementing China's economic policy, as Article 1 of the Banking Act stipulates that banks must promote the development of the socialist market economy and Article 34 provides that commercial banks must conduct their business in accordance with the needs of national economic and social development, and under the guidance of the State's industrial policies; iv. the Commission's working paper concludes that, in the financial aspect, there is a distorted situation which is not de facto comparable to other market-based economies. China's current financial system is characterized by: (1) a strong presence of state banks, and (2) a widespread state influence that imposes a large number of policy objectives on the financial system, in particular for the implementation of its sophisticated economic planning system; (v) at the sector and industry level, TAMSA noted that enterprises in the Chinese steel sector are generally required to comply with State guidelines, the supply of raw materials is regulated by the government, the use of geographical areas through subsidies is encouraged, export incentives or taxes are applied, and prices do not reflect international market signals, receive financial support from the state, and preferential loans despite their low profitability; vi. to support the above, TAMSA relied on USDOC data showing that producers of seamless steel pipe (Wixu, Jiangsu, Tianjin, and others) receive financial support from the State in the form of preferential rate loans, as well as debt forgiveness, subsidies, among others, which, in its saying, by affecting seamless steel pipe, necessarily affect the production of steel connections used as raw material; vii. to demonstrate the price impact of cost distortion and financial situation, TAMSA estimated a constructed value of butt-welding steel connections. It noted that, according to its data, the raw material corresponds to 69% of the cost of manufacture, so for this item it considered the prices of seamless pipe in dollars per tonne reported by exporters from Southeast Asia (without China), for the review period, based on statistical information published by Trade Map. To calculate sales, administrative and financial expenses and profit margin, he considered financial information from The Chinese companies Baotou Steel and Valin Group, which manufacture seamless steel pipe, obtained from the pageInternet {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.financials.morningstar.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.financials.morningstar.com}}}\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 viii. in this regard, indicated that the estimation of the constructed value, based on the international prices of the raw material, made it possible to observe the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the steel connection industry. It added that the comparison of the constructed value with the prices observed in China's domestic market makes it possible to appreciate the degree of distortion through which producers operate in China. 59. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat analysed the information provided by TAMSA and required it to present additional arguments and evidence attesting that, in the production and sale of butt welding steel connections manufactured by industry or industry companies in China, cost and price structures that are not determined in accordance with market principles, in accordance with Article 48 of the RLCE, an explanation of the similarity between the technology used in the production process of butt-welding steel connections from China and Mexico, the cost structure of steel connections to coal for butt welding, the participation of production factors that are intensively used in the manufacture of steel connections to weld to butt , to demonstrate the distortion in these factors by government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and to point out how distortions in the production of seamless coal steel pipe are transferred to the coal-fired steel connections for butt welding. 60. \u160\'00\'A0 In this regard, TAMSA presented the following arguments and evidence to demonstrate that China's butt steel connections industry had structures and costs that are not determined according to market principles:a. commented that the technological process of the product under review is determined by the need to comply with \uc2 ASTM international\uc2  standards or their equivalents, which govern their commercialization. It clarified that the particular details of the Chinese production process were not available to it, as they are considered confidential by companies. However, the information available to us makes it possible to infer that the production process and technology, as determined by manufacturing equipment and machinery, is similar to that of other international producers, including TAMSA; b. to support the above, it submitted information from Chinese companies indicating on their Internet pages that the butt-welded steel connections meet the specifications of asME/\uc2 ASTM international\uc2  standards, as well as illustrative examples of the production process and machinery used by Chinese producers, also provided the production process in Mexico for the manufacture of the product under review, and the testimony of the electrical mechanics engineer with experience in the butt welding connection market, Edgar Alberto GarayAcevedo, which indicates that the connections, production processes and manufacturing equipment in China are the same or very similar to those in Mexico; c. provided the production cost structure of the butt-welded steel connections, during the period under review, which it developed on the basis of its own accounting records, as it stated that it did not have detailed information on the production cost structure of the product under review. According to the cost structure, it is observed that the raw material represents 68.5% of the manufacturing cost of the product under review. As a documentary support, it provided screen impressions of its cost system; d. reiteratedthat there are subsidies in the supply of steel in China, particularly to pipe producers who provide the raw material for the production of connections, or who produce them themselves, as well as in the supply of electricity and natural gas, important components of the cost of production. He added that other components such as labor, financial resources and land use are also distorted, given China's economic structure, regulations and the weight of the state as a regulator, owner and supplier of strategic products; e. stated that distortions in the production of seamless coal steel pipe were transferred to the product under review, on the basis that the pipe is the main input in the development of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding, corresponding to about 69% of the total manufacturing cost of the product under review. These distortions are evidenced by the fact that pipe manufacturers are eventually also manufacturers of connections, so distortions in input prices are transferred via supply of the basic raw material or directly, because it is a production line line lined up inside the company's plant; f. presented the following table, which it produced with information it obtained from the statistics published by Trade Map, which compares the price of Chinese exports of pipe with the international price in the Southeast Asian region:\par \par \uc2 (g. in that regard, it explained that, in comparing these prices, it is noted that the prices of Chinese exports of pipe were 37% below the international price. Thus, a producer who uses a raw material at such low levels gains an artificial competitive advantage by placing its costs about a quarter below its normal levels; (h) provided price references for steel pipe to seamless coal for the Chinese domestic market, which it obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.alibaba.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.alibaba.com}}}\uc2 . In this regard, it noted that the average price of the raw material for making connections is $676.96 per tonne, which is 57% below the international prices of this input in the Southeast Asian region; (i) reiterated that the information provided, referred to in previous points, makes it possible to infer that steel connection companies have a dependency relationship with SOEs, which are their suppliers or strategic customers, and that there is indeed integration with seamless coal steel pipe producers; (j. to support the that domestic prices in China of butt-welded steel connections are distorted, he stated that, according to the reconstructed value of the product under review, it is noted that the cost of producing steel connections, in a scenario in which Chinese producers faced the levels set by the international market in the prices of their inputs, would result in an operating cost of $2,613.68 per tonne, so when comparing that cost with the price of the final product in the domestic market, taking the price ofChina to Hong Kong as a reference, the price of the product under review is about half the operating cost, andk. presented steel connection price references in the Hebei regions , Shanxi and Tianjin, which he obtained from the internet pages {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.alibaba.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.alibaba.com}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.made-in-china.com.In"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.made-in-china.com.In}}}\uc2  that regard, it noted that domestic prices, during the review period, are on average $965.33 per tonne, so, by comparing this price with the constructed value, it is clear that the price of the Chinese final product does not cover the raw material cost of $1,585.18 per tonne. b. Determination61. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat carried out a comprehensive analysis of the arguments and information provided in this review and which were in the administrative record. It noted that, pursuant to paragraph 15(d) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, only Romanite(a) expired in December 2016. However, as a current text, subparagraph (a) and Romanite (i) of paragraph 15 of China's Accession Protocol to the WTO remain. Subparagraph (a) provides for the possibility of applying a price or cost-based methodology in China, Chinese producers, or a methodology that is not based on those prices or costs. Thus, the Secretariat considers that the single expiry of the validity of paragraph 15(a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO does not mean that there has been no longer any possibility of using a methodology that is not based on a strict comparison with domestic prices or costs in China. 62. \u160\'00\'A0 Indeed, the methodological basis for determining price comparability in anti-dumping proceedings investigating products of Chinese origin are expressly contained in paragraph 15 (a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, as well as, like Romanite (i), has not expired. Pursuant to subparagraph (a), there is a legal possibility to use producer prices or costs in China, or to employ a methodology that is not based on strict price or cost separation in China. 63. \u160\'00\'A0 In this area of thought, it is important to note that no Chinese exporters or any other interested party, in addition to the domestic producer, appeared in this proceeding. Consequently, the support that in China and, specifically, in the butt welding steel manufacturing industry, cost and price structures that are not determined according to market principles are subject to tamSA's analysis of the arguments and evidence provided. 64. \u160\'00\'A0 Accordingly, the Secretariat considers that there was a legal basis for assessing TAMSA's proposal, concerning considering China as a non-market economy in the production and sale of butt welding steel connections, and analyzing the provenance of applying the substitute country methodology in accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 RLCE, mainly for the following reasons:(a) the information provided by TAMSA shows which steel connection manufacturers are located in economic development zones, areas where the government implements various government support programs, such as: tax exemptions, debt forgiveness and direct transfers of funds; they are either state-owned enterprises or have strategic links with state-owned enterprises; b. there are integrated companies that also produce seamless steel pipe, the main input in the production of connections. These enterprises may enjoy state support through the supply of steel pipe, since, according to DOC information, companies producing this input receive financial support from the State, either through preferential rate loans, debt forgiveness, subsidies or others. Elements that, insideof the Comprehensive Analysis carried out by the Secretariat, allowed it to conclude that the prices of the main input in the production of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding are not adopted in response to market signals, but are fixed through significant state interference; c. distortions in the prices of seamless coal steel pipe can be transferred to steel connections to coal for butt welding, by virtue of productive integration and because seamless coal steel pipe is the main input in the production of steel connections, accounting for 69% of the cost of production; d. coupled with government support programs or subsidies provided by the Chinese government, either to steel connection companies or to companies producing seamless coal steel pipe, add to the various support that the state government grants to the steel sector in general, through the supply of electricity and natural gas, land use, labour, among others, or because steel companies are owned by the state, which play a central role in that sector, according to the information of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , while all this creates distortions in the prices and costs of steel connections due to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the Chinese government; e. on the other hand, based on the information provided by TAMSA, it is apparent that the prices of connections in the Chinese domestic market (prices in the Hebei, Shanxi and Tianjin regions and selling From China to its autonomous province of Hong Kong), are below the international prices of steel connections to coal for butt welding; (f) In addition to the above, the significant presence of state steel companies in raw material suppliers is observed:i. in relation to gas, its distribution is mainly controlled by China NationalPetroleum Corporation and \uc2 China Petrochemical Corporation\uc2 , state-owned enterprises; ii. in terms of electricity, transmission and distribution rates are officially established in accordance with the principle of costs plus reasonable benefits at different voltages, with the government calculating that cost and determining the reasonableness of those benefits.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, it is a sector that falls within the restricted category within the 2017 Investment Catalogue, and(iii).\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0iron, together with coke, are subject to an export tax and the imposition of quantitative restrictions, which is a clear example that the price of these raw materials is affected by state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (g. the Secretariat cautioned that another relevant aspect was the restriction on foreign direct investment, since China was the most restrictive country on the \uc2 OECD\uc2  list, a situation that translates into the requirement of meeting difficult-to-meet requirements for foreign enterprises wishing to invest in China's steel sector. The Secretariat noted that the mining sector, which provides raw materials for steel production, is also in the prohibited category of the 2017 Investment Catalogue; (h) In addition, the Secretariat confirmed that, at the macroeconomic level, there were also distortions in variables such as foreign direct investment, exchange rate and wages, as follows:(i) the Investment Catalogue continues to operate as the main instrument for guiding foreign direct investment in China. In this catalogue, investments areclassified into recommended or in a list where foreign direct investment is restricted or prohibited.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, the Chinese government continues to encourage foreign direct investment in regions of the country where projects can benefit from favorable policies; ii. the exchange rate policy established by the Chinese government, which affects all sectors of the economy in general, results in exchange rate containment by allowing it to fluctuate only within a band determined by it, in order to boost its exports. This policy results in exports with prices below international prices set by supply and demand laws, and(iii).\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0labour, being subject to residence checks by the Chinese government, does not allow wages to be established through free negotiation between employees and employers, resulting in their containment. (i) this demonstrates that active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Chinese government causes distortions that come from government policies, regulatory provisions or direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that selectively discriminate between companies owned or by capital participation, by region or by product type, which have an impact on the formation of prices and costs of production factors where the manufacture of coal-fired steel connections is intensive to weld to the butt, either by cracking capital and energy costs, and restrictions on labour mobility. 65. \u160\'00\'A0 On the basis of the foregoing, the Secretariat considers that the information provided by TAMSA raises the presumption that, in companies that produce coal-fired steel connections for butt welding in China, cost and price structures that are not determined according to market principles prevail, because market distortions were identified from the factors affecting resource allocation in the production of steel connections to coal to be welded to butt and interfere with the determination of the costs and prices of the productive factors in which it is intensive. 66. \u160\'00\'A0 On the basis of the foregoing, and pursuant to paragraph 15(a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the ERC, the Secretariat proceeded to consider the proposal to use a substitute country in China for the purpose of calculating normal value. c. Substitute country selection67. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA proposed the United States as a substitute country for China to determine normal value.\u160\'00\'A0To support market conditions in the United States, it provided the \uc2 US Department of State\uc2 's "The Economy in Synthesis" from the \uc2 United States Department of State\uc2 , which it obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://usinfo.state.gov"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://usinfo.state.gov}}}\uc2  and the "United States Country Sheet", from the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, \uc2 European Union\uc2  and Cooperation of Spain, in June 2019, which it obtained from the Internet{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.exteriores.gob.es"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.exteriores.gob.es}}}\uc2  page. 68. \u160\'00\'A0 On the basis of the third paragraph of Article 48 of the RLCE, TAMSA argued that:a. with respect to the widespread convertible of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, it noted that the US dollar is a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that constitutes a widely convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in international financial markets, and that it functions internationally as one of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the international reserves of central banks; b. in relation to wages being established through free negotiation betweenworkers and employers, argued that wages in the United States are set directly through the interaction between workers and their employers; the government establishes the existence of minimum wages per region, and are usually set above this benchmark in the industrial sectors.\u160\'00\'A0In some industrial sectors, trade unions whose affiliation is voluntary operate, under a legal regime that establishes freedom of association; c. that industry or industry decisions under price research, costs and supply of inputs, including raw materials, technology, production, sales and investment, are adopted in response to market signals and without significant interference from the State, presented the market study for the determination of normal value, prepared by \uc2 White & Case\uc2 , indicating that companies operating in that sector offer their prices through the publication of price lists, which are adjusted according to the signals in that country's market. It added that these are companies that make their decisions to purchase inputs, production levels and final price determination in response to the supply and demand interaction of the product in the market; (d) in connection with allowing foreign investment and co-investment with foreign firms, noted that the United States is one of the nations with the highest degree of openness to foreign investment; e. because it makes the industry under investigation exclusively possess a set of ledgers that are used for all purposes, and which are audited in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles, indicated that in the United States companies must have unique ledgers, which must follow generally accepted accounting principles, given existing tax obligations, andf.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0that the costs of production and financial situation of the sector or industry under investigation are unchanged in relation to asset depreciation, bad debts, barter trade and debt compensation payments, or other factors deemed relevant, stated that the companies in the sector at issue operate in market business relationships, where there are no bartering mechanisms, but purchase transactions for sale under market conditions. i. The United States is a producer of steel connections69.\u160\'00\'A0TAMSA said that coal-fired steel connections are manufactured in the United States for butt welding. To prove it, he presented a market study on the butt-welding steel connections industry in the United States, conducted by consultans \uc2 White & Case\uc2 , which notes that production in the United States focuses on four coal-fired coal-to-but-welding companies. 70. It also provided the profile of the consulting firm that produced the market study and indicated that \uc2 White & Case\uc2  is a legal services company that provides international trade consultancy and has a professional team specialized in the field. The Secretariat corroborated this information on the company's website and noted that the consultanus had a presence in more than 30 countries and has more than 100 years of experience in various areas, including the international trade area. 71. \u160\'00\'A0 The market study produced by \uc2 White & Case\uc2  indicates that the steel connections made in the United States, are similar to those of Chinese manufacturing, comply with the samestandards (ASTM and ANSI) and follow the same standardized stages in their production process. 72.In addition to the \u160\'00\'A0 above, TAMSA presented a comparative picture of the similarity of the butt-welded steel connections manufactured in both countries. It also submitted information obtained from the Websites of Chinese coal-fired steel producers and FROM U.S. producers, describing the characteristics of that product. ii. Similarity in the production process73. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA noted that the production process for the manufacture of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding in China, the United States and the rest of the world, follows a similar scheme.\u160\'00\'A0It starts with cutting the steel pipe and sheet plate, in sections and circles, respectively, in appropriate sizes, according to the type of connection to be manufactured and their size. These cuts are processed in presses and, where appropriate, in molding machines that reduce or make up connections. The resulting connections may or may not be finished with heat treatment, beveling, blasting, stamping or paint. 74. In order to demonstrate similarity in the manufacturing processes of each type of butt welding steel connections, TAMSA presented the production process diagrams in China and the United States, obtained from the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.steeljrv.com/industrial-standards-pipe-fittings.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.steeljrv.com/industrial-standards-pipe-fittings.html}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://hardhat"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://hardhat}}}\uc2  engineer.com/pipe-fittings/pipe-fittings-manufacturing-process Internet pages, respectively. iii. Availability of major inputs75. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA stated that the main inputs used to produce carbon steel connections for butt welding are the seamless carbon steel pipe with which elbows are produced, "tees" and reductions, as well as the sheet steel plate with which the caps are manufactured. Other raw materials are electric power, natural gas, paints and varnishes. 76. With regard to the production of seamless coal steel pipe, TAMSA submitted information from the "Steel Statistical Yearbook 2018" report, which shows that in 2017 China and the United States were among the top three producers worldwide. 77. With regard to sheet steel plate production, TAMSA presented the statistic obtained from the "Steel Statistical Yearbook 2018" report on the top five producing countries worldwide, where China was noted to be first in 2017 while the United States was fourth. 78. Based on information from the energy consulting firm \uc2 Canadian Enerdata Ltd\uc2 ., which it obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.enerdata.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.enerdata.com}}}\uc2  publices statistical yearbooks in each country, TAMSA noted that, in 2018, China and the United States maintained a dominant position in energy, both as producers and consumers of electric power internationally. iv. Other elements79.\u160\'00\'A0TAMSA noted that there are no anti-dumping or countervailing measures in the WTO concerning coal steel connections for butt welding, to support the above, submitted the 2018 WTO Committee on Anti-Dumping Practices to support the above. d. Determination80.\u160\'00\'A0The third paragraph of Article 48 of the ERCE states that a substitute country shall mean a third country with a market economy similar to the exporting country with a centrally planned economy. The similarity between thesubstitute country and the exporting country shall be reasonably defined, so that normal value in the exporting country can be approximated on the basis of the domestic price in the substitute country, taking into account economic criteria. 81. \u160\'00\'A0 In order to comply with this provision, the Secretariat conducted a comprehensive analysis of the information provided by TAMSA to consider the United States as a substitute country for China. The Secretariat noted that both countries made coal-fired steel connections for butt welding and that there was similarity in production processes. With regard to the availability of inputs needed for the manufacture of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding, both in China and the United States, there is a significant production of seamless coal steel pipe and weatch steel plate, the main inputs for the manufacture of coal-to-butt steel connections. From the above, it can be reasonably inferred that the intensity in the use of the factors of the production of steel connections to coal for butt welding is similar in both countries. 82. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the information and evidence submitted by TAMSA and the analysis described above, in accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE, 48 of the ERCE, the Secretariat confirmed the selection of the United States as China's substitute market economy country for the purpose of calculating normal value. e. U.S. Domestic Market Prices83. \u160\'00\'A0 To prove normal value in the U.S. domestic market, TAMSA presented the market study produced by \uc2 White & Case\uc2 . It was a source of specialized information in the seamless steel pipe market in the United States, and therefore reliably and re-representatively demonstrated the market price levels used by major producers in that country.\u160\'00\'A0This market study includes product catalogs and price lists of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding (elbows, "tees", reductions and caps) of four U.S. production companies. 84. According to the market study produced by \uc2 White & Case\uc2 , the prices reported are in dollars per piece and are at the ex-factory level. The volume in kilograms for each type of connection was obtained from the product catalogues of the production companies themselves and from the factors of conversion from parts to kilograms presented by TAMSA. Prices were in effect during the review period. 85. The Secretariat verified on the Websites of each of the production companies the information that TAMSA submitted. It noted that these companies are manufacturers of coal-to-butt steel connections in various diameters, that their facilities are located in the United States, and that they have been engaged in production for more than 60 years, and therefore considered that such information constitutes a reasonable basis for determining prices in the domestic market in the United States. 86. \u160\'00\'A0 In addition, the Secretariat noted that, according to the market study, the four coal-fired steel connections for butt welding in the United States accounted for 90% of the domestic industry in 2016, and therefore corresponded to the main manufacturers of coal-to-coal connections for butt welding. 87. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the information provided by TAMSA, the Secretariat identified 87 types of steel connections to butt welding coal exported to Mexico, taking into account the type of connection and the outer diameter ofbased on the information described in point 48 of this Resolution, and calculated a normal value for each type of connection, in dollars per kilogram. Normal value information was not available for two types of connections. 88. \u160\'00\'A0 The foregoing, on the basis of Articles 2.1 of the AD Agreement, 31 of the LCE, 39 and 40 of the RLCE. f. Normal setting89. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA proposed to make adjustments to normal value for discount and margin of marketing. To support these adjustments, it presented the market study developed by \uc2 White & Case\uc2 . i. Discount90.\u160\'00\'A0According to the market study produced by \uc2 White & Case\uc2 , purchases to producers are, as a rule, made through distributors, in addition, it is usual for products to be offered at discounted prices, so, to obtain the net sale price, TAMSA applied a discount adjustment. 91. \u160\'00\'A0 In order to allocate the discount applicable to the prices of each of the coal-fired coal-producing companies for butt welding, TAMSA presented in the market study the prices of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding of two US distributors, as well as the worksheets where it estimated an average of the percentage discount for each producer. Since distributor prices are outside the review period, TAMSA submitted the price index to the producer of the sector to which the butt-welded coal steel connections belong, obtained from the \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , with which it took prices to the review period. ii. Marketing margin92. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA noted that it considered it reasonable to adjust prices by marketing margin. It stated that the prices of US producers are generally list prices, which are often adjusted according to market signals, purchase volumes and other aspects of commercial policy applied by manufacturers to their distributor customers. To estimate the amount of the adjustment, it considered it reasonable to compare the price offered by distributors to the end customer with the list price offered by the manufacturer. 93. \u160\'00\'A0 In that regard, the Secretariat did not consider the margin adjustment in the calculation, because the percentage of the discount it obtained based on the information on the distributors' quotes included that concept, so that the adjustment would be doubled if applicable. iii. Determination94. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat considered the information and methodology provided by TAMSA for the calculation of discount adjustment, and applied the adjustment for each of the 87 types of butt-welded coal steel connections it identified, as noted in point 87 of this Resolution. The foregoing, on the basis of Articles 2.4 of the AD Agreement, 36 of the LCE and 51 and 58 of the RLCE.\u160\'00\'A03. Conclusion95.\u160\'00\'A0In accordance with the information and methodology described above, and based on Articles 11.3, 11.4 of the AD Agreement, 54 second paragraph, 64 last paragraph and 89 F of the LCE, the Secretariat analyzed export price and normal value information, and determined that there were sufficient evidence to support the re-dumping of dumping in exports to Mexico of coal-to-butt connections originating in China. F. Analysis ofcontinuation or repetition of damage96.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat analysed the information in the administrative file and that which it itself received, regarding the list of SIC-M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , import pediments and united Nations Commodity Trade Statistics Database (UN Comtrade) statistics, in order to determine whether there are elements to support the elimination of the final countervailing quota, imposed on imports of coal connections for butt welding originating in China, would result in continuation or recurrence of injury to the domestic industry of the like product. 97. The analysis of economic and financial indicators includes the information that TAMSA provided, since TAMSA constitutes the domestic industry of the product similar to that under review, as determined in point 100 of this Resolution. To carry out this analysis, the Secretariat considered the information for the period from 1 July 2014 to 30 June 2019, which includes the review period, as well as the estimates for the periods from 1 July 2019 to 30 June 2020 and 1 July 2020 to 30 June 2021. Unless otherwise indicated, the behaviour of economic and financial indicators in a given year or period is analysed with respect to the previous immediate equivalent period. 1. Domestic industry98. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA said it is the only company in Mexico that produces coal steel connections for butt welding. To support it, he submitted a letter from CANACERO on 12 June 2019 confirming this. 99. \u160\'00\'A0 According to statistics on the list of SIC-M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , corresponding to TIGIE tariff fraction 7307.93.01, the Secretariat noted that TAMSA and its related company did not import the product under review during the period under review. 100. \u160\'00\'A0 On the basis of the above points, the Secretariat determined that TAMSA constitutes the domestic industry, meaning all domestic production of coal steel connections for butt welding, in accordance with Articles 4.1 and 5.4 of the LCE Anti-Dumping Agreement, 40 and 50 and 60 and 61 of the RLCE. 2. International market101. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on crude steel production information obtained from the \uc2 World Steel Association\uc2 , in its Steel Statistical Yearbook 2018 and 2019 reports, TAMSA argued that China is the world's leading producer of crude steel and also for derivative products, such as steel connections to coal for butt welding. He added that China is the main exporter of steel, demonstrating that it has a purely exporting steel policy. 102. \u160\'00\'A0 According to information from the International Energy Statistics 2018, TAMSA argued that countries with high volume of oil production are the main consumers of butt welding connections, so the United States represents the main consumer, followed by Saudi Arabia, Russia, Canada, China and Iraq. 103. \u160\'00\'A0 It also noted that China is the leading producer of seamless steel pipe, the main input for connection manufacturing, followed by the United States and Japan, according to data from the \uc2 World Steel Association\uc2 . He added that the production of tubes and connections in China occupied between 2014 and 2017 approximately 67% of the production ofWorld. 104. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA provided UN Comtrade statistics on imports and exports under subheading 7307.93 of the Harmonized Commodity Description and Coding System established by the \uc2 World Customs Organization\uc2 , where the product under review is classified. China argued that, with respect to coal connections for butt welding, China is not among the major importing countries, but continues to remain the world's leading exporter, because the price levels at which it exports are lower than those of countries that do not engage in unfair international trade practices. 105. Based on this information, the Secretariat identified that China, South Korea, Italy, Thailand and Malaysia were the main exporting countries for coal-to-butt coal connections, with a contribution of 38%, 13%, 12%, 5% and 4% of world exports, respectively, during the period 2014 to 2018. This same source shows the United States, Russia, the United Arab Emirates, Italy and Japan as the largest importing countries, with a share of 21%, 4.5%, 4.4%, 3.7% and 3.6% of world imports, respectively. 3. Domestic market106. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA indicated that it is located in the port of Veracruz, and that from there it has access to the main consumers of the country who are located in the areas where oil well drilling is carried out, or close to the main refineries. 107. Noted that the areas with the highest consumption of connections were the northern, central, southern and marine regions; each is headquartered in Ciudad Reynosa, Veracruz/Poza Rica, Villahermosa and Ciudad del Carmen, respectively. He added that other important consumers of steel connections are the most industrialized cities in the country, such as Mexico City, Monterrey, Guadalajara and Puebla, where the main distributors are headquartered. 108. The Secretariat carried out the analysis of the domestic market for coal-fired steel connections for butt welding based on the domestic production and export information that TAMSA provided, as well as the import figures in the list of SIC-M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , obtained as indicated in points 116 to 119 of this Resolution, for the period July 2014 to June 2019. 109. The Secretariat noted that the domestic market for coal connections for butt welding, measured through the ANC, calculated as domestic production, plus imports, minus exports, recorded a cumulative increase of 5% in the period analyzed: it decreased by 18% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased by 1% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 3% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 24% in the period of review. 110. Total imports accumulated an increase of 8% in the period analyzed: they decreased by 1% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased by 18% in the period July 2016-June 2017, decreased by 24% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and increased by 21% in the review period.\u160\'00\'A0During the period analyzed, total imports of coal connections to butt welding were imported from 43 countries. In the period July 2018-June 2019, the main supplier was Germany, which accounted for 26% of total imports, followed by Taiwan (21%), Malaysia (10%), South Korea (10%) Cambodia (10%). 111. The volume of total domestic production decreased by 51% in the period July 2015-June 2016 and 37% in the period July 2016-June 2017,increased by 172% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 25% in the review period, so that it accumulated an increase of 5% in the period analyzed. 112. \u160\'00\'A0 Exports accumulated an increase of 7% in the period analyzed: they decreased 52% in the period July 2015-June 2016 and 27% in the period July 2016-June 2017, increased 150% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 22% in the review period. 113. \u160\'00\'A0 Domestic domestic production, which is oriented to the domestic market, calculated as total domestic production minus exports, increased by 1% in the period analyzed: it decreased by 49% and 55% in the periods July 2015-June 2016 and July 2016-June 2017, respectively, but increased 237% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 30% in the review period. 4. Actual and potential analysis on imports114.\u160\'00\'A0TAMSA stated that the countervailing quota has contained Chinese imports of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding, however, if that measure is eliminated, they could enter at discriminated prices in significant quantities. 115. It indicated that by tiGIE tariff fraction 7307.93.01 other products that are not under review, such as: alloy steel connections larger than 16 inches, with a standard other than A 234, coples, stainless steel connections, grooved, flanges, threads, niples, sockolet and weldolets, among others. Therefore, and in the absence of the specific import volumes and values of the coal-fired steel connections for butt welding under review, it proposed to use the information that CANACERO provided to it. 116. \u160\'00\'A0 For its part, CANACERO presented the SAT import base, corresponding to TIGIE tariff fraction 7307.93.01, for the period July 2014 to June 2019 and the methodology it used to calculate the volumes and import values of the product under review, both from China and from other origins. It explained that, in order to debug imports, it considered the automatic notices of SIICEX and managed to reconcile 56% of the registrations and 74% of the total volume of imports in the period analyzed, as follows:a. formed 4 groups of importers: China, the United States, South Korea and the rest of the world and identified: (i) those import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that paid compensatory fee, in the case of China, and (ii) that, according to the product description of the automatic notices or the SAT database, comply with ASTM standard A 234, with the grade of carbon steel (WPB or WPC), diameter from 1/2 to 16 inches and elbow shapes, , reduction and cover; b. identified and excluded goods which do not correspond to the product under review, for example, the alloy steel connections described in point 115 of this Resolution; c. with respect to imports of TAMSA and its related company, CANACERO noted that none of the companies acquired butt-welded coal steel connections originating in China.\u160\'00\'A0TAMSA imported pipes and its related company, only 5% of its imports corresponded to steel connections originating in Malaysia, Italy, the United States, South Korea and Vietnam, the rest of the imports are from alloyed connections or with diameters greater than 16 inches, andd.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0for import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the description of which does not allow the determination of whether they correspond to the product under review, calculated the volumes and values from a proportion factor specific to each group ofImporters. 117. \u160\'00\'A0 From the information described in the previous point, TAMSA indicated that the volume of product under review represented 50.5% of total imports in the period analyzed. 118. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat considered that the methodology proposed by CANACERO was based on relevant information. The Secretariat also came from the siC-M import statistics for tiGIE tariff fraction 7307.93.01. In order to have greater elements regarding the identification of the product under review, it required a number of importers and customs agents to provide import pediments and accompanying documentation for \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out during the review period. 119. \u160\'00\'A0 Of the information provided by CANACERO, TAMSA and that reached by the Secretariat, 98% of the volume imported from China and 66% of the volume imported from other countries, which entered the country during the period analyzed, were reviewed. The Secretariat identified that 9% of the volume imported from China corresponded to the product under review, while 56% of the volume imported from the other origins corresponded to the coal connections to butt welded analyzed. 120. Based on the results of the information, the Secretariat noted that total imports of coal connections to butt welding decreased by 1% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased 18% in the period July 2016-June 2017, fell 24% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and grew 21% in the period July 2018-June 2019, which equates to an increase of 8% in the period analyzed. 121. \u160\'00\'A0 Imports originating in China have maintained a limited presence in the domestic market with the application of the countervailing quota, since in the period analyzed they accounted for on average 3% of total imports. In the period analyzed they recorded an increase of 3%: they grew 179% in the period Of July 2015-June 2016, although they decreased 5%, 31% and 44%, in the periods July 2016-June 2017, July 2017-June 2018 and July 2018-June 2019, respectively. 122. \u160\'00\'A0 Imports originating in other countries increased by 8% in the period analyzed: they decreased by 4% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased 19% in the period July 2016-June 2017, fell 24% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and grew 23% in the period July 2018-June 2019. 123. \u160\'00\'A0 In terms of the domestic market, since the volume of imports originating in China was negligible, they recorded an average share of 2% in the period analyzed: 1% in the period July 2014-June 2015, 4% in the period July 2015-June 2016, 3% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 2% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 1% in the period July 2018-June 2019, while the participation of other countries in the ANC, in the same periods, it was 62%, 73%, 86%, 64% and 64%, respectively, so in the period analyzed they increased their share by 2 percentage points. 124. In relation to the volume of domestic production, imports originating in China accounted for 1% in the period July 2014-June 2015, 6% in the period July 2015-June 2016, 8% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 2% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 1% in July 2018-June 2019. Meanwhile, the share of domestic domestic production oriented to the domestic market in the ANC was 37% in the period July 2014-June 2015, 23% in the period July 2015-June of2016, 10% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 34% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 35% in the period July 2018-June 2019, so in the period analyzed their share decreased by 2 percentage points. 125. \u160\'00\'A0 In order to estimate the likely volume of imports of the product under review, if the countervailing quota is eliminated, TAMSA provided projections for the periods July 2019-June 2020 and July 2020- June 2021 of imports of coal connections to butt welding, originating in China and other origins. To this end, he considered the following:a. argued that the steel connections under review are a product used to connect pipelines for the conduction pipelines of oil, natural gas and various petrochemicals and that, the demand for connections is determined by the behaviour of the economy as a whole, so it estimated the ANC in line with growth expectations for GDP. That is, the ANC projected from a growth rate of 1.47% and 1.94%, in line with the GDP growth expectations published by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  for the following two annual periods, 2020 and 2021; b. in a scenario that considers the elimination of the compensatory quota, it made its estimates on the basis of an economic exercise of comparative static and partial equilibrium, based on the following assumption: if the prices of a good become relatively cheaper, its consumption will increase, compared to that of its substitute products. He noted that the parameter measuring this change was substitution elasticity, which measured the percentage in which the relative product quantity changes, as a result of a percentage change in relative prices, andc.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0It indicated that this type of model has already been used in other anti-dumping investigations in Mexico, as well as in WTO arbitration proceedings, as a proposal from the Mexican authority itself. 126. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the above point, TAMSA projected that imports originating in China would grow to a 12% and 22% share of the ANC in the following two periods comparable to the review period, pursuant to:(a) considered the share of imports originating in China in the ANC and the undervaluation with respect to the domestic price they recorded during the period investigated, in the original investigation, indicators that served to calculate China's (ratio) of participation in the ANC and average undervaluation; b. with the above information, the prices it designed and the economic exercise of comparative static and partial balance, noted above, obtained the share of imports originating in China in the ANC, and(c).\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0to project total and originating import volumes from China for the periods July 2019-June 2020 and July 2020-June 2021, applied the respective shares resulting from the economic procedure described to the projected ANC. 127. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat responded to TAMSA's proposed methodology for projecting the imports under review, since: (i) it is based on the expected increase in the ANC, in line with the expectations of GDP growth that the Bank of Mexico estimates and, (ii) the proposed economic model, takes into account the interchangeability of the product under review and its similar on the basis of the economic principle of substitution elasticity, which define the demand for the product and replace it in addressing new conditions inthe supply and arrival of imports at a lower price. It should be noted that the Secretariat replicated the method on the basis of import volumes and values obtained as described in points 116 to 119 of this Resolution. 128. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat noted that, if the countervailing quota were eliminated, imports originating in China would increase 12 times in the period July 2019-June 2020 and 22 times in the period July 2020-June 2021, compared to the period under review, where they were negligible. This would result in the estimated volume going from 1% to 12% and 22% of the ANC in those same periods, respectively. With regard to domestic production, the share of the subject imports would behave similarly as they increased their share by 10 and 21 percentage points, from a contribution of 1% in the review period to 11% and 22% in the two projected periods. 129. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the information and results of the analysis described above, the Secretariat concluded that there were sufficient evidence to support the fact that, if the countervailing quota imposed on imports of butt-welded steel connections originating in China were eliminated, there would be an increase in such dumped imports and would achieve market share greater than that observed in the period analyzed, to the detriment of domestic production. 5. Real and potential price effects130. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA stated that the national price shows a downward trend throughout the period analyzed and that favorable cost adjustments mitigated the adverse performance of the domestic industry's profit margins. He added that in recent periods the prices of imports from Asian countries increased, a situation that slowed the fall in TAMSA prices. In this regard, in December 2018 it increased the prices of its steel connections. 131. TAMSA indicated that domestic production is vulnerable, because the market under review involves goods sold under conditions of total commercial interchangeability and customer purchases respond to price changes. 132. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the information described in points 116 to 119 of this Resolution, the Secretariat calculated the implied price of imports of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding. In this regard, it noted that the average price of imports originating in other countries fell 53% in the period analyzed: it decreased by 47% and 6% in the periods July 2015-June 2016 and July 2016-June 2017, respectively, and increased 14% in the period July 2017-June 2018, to decrease again by 18% in the review period. 133. \u160\'00\'A0 The average price of imports originating in China decreased by 4% in the period analyzed: it increased 91% in the period July 2015-June 2016, 15% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 3% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and decreased by 57% in the review period. 134. The average domestic sales price of the domestic industry, measured in dollars, decreased by 18% in the period analyzed: it decreased by 8% in the period July 2015-June 2016 and practically maintained its level in the period July 2016-June 2017, however, it decreased 9% and 1% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and in the review period, respectively. 135. In order to assess the existence of undervaluation, the Secretariat consideredthe plant price of domestic sales of domestic production and compared it to the average price recorded by imports originating in China during the period analyzed, to which customs processing duty was added. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that the average price of imports originating in China was 4% below the domestic price in the period July 2014 to June 2015, during the remainder of the analysis period the domestic price was above that of imports originating in China. 136. \u160\'00\'A0 It is important to note that the countervailing quota contained the growth of imports originating in China in dumping during the period analyzed, as a result, the volume they recorded was virtually negligible, accounting for only 3% of total imports of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding, a situation that was reflected in their price performance. 137. Moreover, TAMSA argued that in a scenario where the compensatory quota is eliminated, low prices for The Chinese product would enjoy an unfair advantage which would result in a shift in the domestic product and a deterioration in its prices and performance indicators. He added that it will face lower orders for merchandise and/or customer pressures to reduce its prices by undercutting on the prices of the imported product. 138. Noted that prices originating in China to other export destinations, such as Indonesia or Russia, demonstrate the prices so low at which China could export, should the countervailing quota be eliminated in Mexico. He indicated that there were constant offers from Chinese steel manufacturers at discriminated prices. 139. TAMSA estimated the import price of the product under review. For the period July 2019-June 2020, it considered the sales price that the Chinese customs reported to a third country (India, the main destination for Chinese exports) during the review period, and for the period July 2020-June 2021, the price of quotes that have been obtained for export sales from China. It noted that both prices were added to internment costs, such as maritime freight and customs processing rights.\u160\'00\'A0For the estimate of the price of the domestic product, it added to the reported prices the inflation predicted by the Bank of Mexico. 140. The Secretariat considered acceptable the methodology that TAMSA used to estimate domestic price and imports originating in China, as it is based on the estimated growth in domestic prices and the prices at which China usually exports to its main markets and actual quotes. The Secretariat therefore responded to TAMSA's proposal and noted that import prices for the connections under review would have undercutting margins of 16% and 30% in the July 2019-June 2020 and July 2020-June 2021 periods, respectively. 141. Based on the available evidence and the results of the analysis described above, the Secretariat concluded that there was a well-founded likelihood that, if the countervailing quota is eliminated, imports of butt-welded carbon steel connections originating in China are at price levels such that they would have a negative impact on domestic prices to the domestic market, as they could achieve significant undercutting levels that would increase demand for new imports, which would have negative effects on domestic sales and profits of the domestic industry, as described insubsequent points of this Resolution. 6. Actual and potential effects on the domestic industry142. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA indicated that the countervailing quota contained injury to the domestic industry, since imports originating in China were significantly reduced and if such a measure is eliminated, Chinese imports could enter at discriminated prices and in significant quantities, resulting in a recurrence of injury to the domestic industry. 143. \u160\'00\'A0 In order to assess the conduct of the domestic industry during the period under review, the Secretariat considered the economic and financial indicators, including in the state of costs, sales and profits to the domestic market that TAMSA provided, for coal-to-coal connections similar to those under review, for the period July 2014-June 2019.\u160\'00\'A0It also took into account its financial statements, corresponding to the fiscal years 2014 to 2018, as well as the financial statements of an internal nature for the periods January-June 2018 and January-June 2019. The Secretariat updated the financial information in order to make its figures comparable, which was done through the method of changes in the general price level, based on the National Consumer Price Index published by the National Institute of Statistics and Geography. 144. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the information described in the previous point, the Secretariat noted that the volume of production of the domestic industry increased by 5% in the period analyzed: it decreased by 51% in the period July 2015-June 2016 and 37% in the period July 2016-June 2017, increased 172% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 25% in the period July 2018-June 2019. Domestic market-oriented production of the domestic industry behaved similarly with 1% growth in the period analyzed: it decreased by 49% in the period July 2015-June 2016 and 55% in the period July 2016-June 2017, increased 237% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 30% in the period July 2018-June 2019. 145. \u160\'00\'A0 In terms of market share, the domestic market-oriented production of the domestic industry had a 37% share of the ANC in the period July 2014-June 2015, 23% in the period July 2015-June 2016, 10% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 34% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 35% in the period July 2018-June 2019. This meant that in the period analyzed it lost 2 percentage points of market share. 146. \u160\'00\'A0 In terms of the total sales of the domestic industry, there was an increase of 10% during the period analyzed: they decreased by 45% in the period July 2015-June 2016 and 24% in the period July 2016-June 2017, increased 94% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 36% in the period July 2018-June 2019. 147. \u160\'00\'A0 Sales to the external market of domestic production accumulated growth of 7% in the period analyzed: they decreased by 52% in the period July 2015-June 2016 and 27% in the period July 2016-June 2017, increased by 150% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 22% in the period July 2018-June 2019. It is important to note that, in relative terms, exports accounted on average for 68% of domestic production in the period analyzed. 148. \u160\'00\'A0 Sales to the domestic market for domestic production accumulated a16% growth in the period analyzed: they decreased by 28% in the period July 2015-June 2016 and 20% in the period July 2016-June 2017, increased 9% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 85% in the period July 2018-June 2019. 149. The positive performance of production and total sales in the period analyzed was reflected in the performance of the level of employment of the domestic industry, as this indicator recorded an increase of 33% in the period analyzed: it decreased 2% and 38% in the periods July 2015-June 2016 and July 2016-June 2017, respectively, and increased 25% and 77% in the july 2017-June 2018 and July 2018-June 2019 periods, respectively. 150. \u160\'00\'A0 The productivity of the domestic industry, as measured as the quotient of production and employment, fell 21% in the period analyzed: it decreased by 50% in the period July 2015-June 2016, increased 2% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 118% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and fell 30% in the period July 2018-June 2019. 151. \u160\'00\'A0 The performance of the domestic industry's wages shows a declining trend in the period analysed: they decreased by 33% and 46% in the july 2015-June 2016 and July 2016-June 2017 periods, respectively, and grew 58% and 63% in the July 2017-June 2018 and July 2018-June 2019 periods, respectively, resulting in a decrease of 6% over the period analyzed. 152. Inventories of the domestic industry decreased by 13% in the period July 2015-June 2016 and 67% in the period July 2016-June 2017, increasing 3.6 times in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 25% in the review period; resulting in a cumulative increase of 29% over the period analyzed. 153. \u160\'00\'A0 With regard to the installed capacity of the domestic industry, the Secretariat noted that it remained constant throughout the period under review. For its part, the utilization rate was 36% in the period July 2014-June 2015, 18% in the period July 2015 to June 2016, 11% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 31% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 38% in the period July 2018-June 2019, which meant a cumulative increase of 2 percentage points in the period analyzed. 154. The described performance of the domestic sales volumes of the domestic industry and its prices was reflected in the conduct of its revenue. In this regard, the Secretariat noted that revenue from such sales decreased 22%, 16.1% and 8.7% in the periods July 2015-June 2016, July 2016-June 2017 and July 2017-June 2018, respectively, and increased 81.3% in the period July 2018-June 2019, so they accumulated an increase of 8.4% in the period analyzed. 155. \u160\'00\'A0 For its part, operating costs decreased by 24.5%, 14.1% and 12.5% in the periods July 2015-June 2016, July 2016-June 2017 and July 2017-June 2018, respectively, but increased by 85.3% in the period under review, thus accumulating an increase of 5.1% in the period analyzed. 156. As a result of the performance of sales revenue and operating costs, the Secretariat noted that operating results increased by 0.82 times, 1.83 times and 1.24 times in the July 2015-June 2016 periods, July 2016-June 2017 and July 2017-June 2018, respectively, but decreased 3.5 times in the period under review, reflecting a decrease in operating loss of 69.3% in the periodAnalyzed. 157. This resulted in the operating margin increasing by 3.4 percentage points in the period July 2015-June 2016, decreasing by 2.5 percentage points in the period July 2016-June 2017, increase by 4.4 percentage points in the period July 2017-June 2018 and decrease by 2.1 percentage points in the period under review, so it accumulated an increase of 3.2 percentage points in the period analyzed from a margin of -4.4% to -1.2%. 158. In addition, the Secretariat assessed the performance variables on investment in assets (ROA, Return of the Investment in Assets), cash flow and capital raising capacity, based on TAMSA's ruling or internal financial statements, taking into account that they consider the production of the most restricted group or range of products that include the product similar to the subject matter under review, as described in Articles 3.6 of the Anti-Dumping Agreement and 66 of the RLCE. 159. TAMSA indicated in its reply to the official form that there are no investments or projects associated with the production of the like goods. 160. \u160\'00\'A0 With regard to the ROA of the domestic coal steel connection industry, calculated at the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  level, the Secretariat noted that it was positive in all the years that were part of the period analyzed. Index20142015201620172018Jan-Jun2018Jan-Jun2019Return on Investment (ROA)36.2%14.9%4.9%9.1%14.6%7.7%7.3%Source: TAMSA's internal and ruling financial statements. 161. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on TAMSA's cash flow statements, the Secretariat noted that cash flow at the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  level reported negative performance during the period 2014 to 2018, and the first half of 2019. Operating cash flow reflected the following movements: 11%, -33%, -57.3% and 20.3% for 2015, 2016, 2017 and 2018, respectively, so that in 2018, compared to 2014, it reported a decrease of 61.7% in cash flow. In the period January-June 2019, compared to the same period in 2018, it decreased 22.6%. 162. \u160\'00\'A0 The ability to raise capital is analyzed through the behaviour of solvency, liquidity, leverage and debt indices. Below is a summary of the behavior in these indicators:Index20142015201620172018Jan-Jun2018Jan-Jun2019Circulating ratio1.030.880.941.021.171.081.17Acid test0.650.480.510.540.630.570.57Leverage102%91%79%81%70%79%73%Debt50%48%44%45%41%44%42%Source: TAMSA's internal and ruling financial statements. 163. \u160\'00\'A0 In this regard, the Secretariat noted that solvency and liquidity levels retained unsatisfactory levels (mainly under the acid test, i.e. the working asset minus the value of inventories, relative to short-term liabilities); in general, a relationship between current assets and short-term liabilities is considered appropriate if they have a ratio of 1 to 1 or 1 or 1 or 1Upper. 164. \u160\'00\'A0 The leverage index has remained at manageable levels during the period 2015 to 2018, and in the first half of 2018 and 2019. Normally a proportion of the total liability with respect to book capital, less than 100% is manageable, in this case the leverage levels were manageable (except in 2014). So it is up to the level of debt or ratio of total liability to total asset remained at acceptable levels. 165. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the analysis of the domestic industry's indicators, the Secretariat noted that, while the countervailing share contained Chinese imports of the product under review, when considering the period under review, some of the economic indicators show negative signs, such as domestic price, market share, wages, inventories and productivity. At the financial level, operating-level losses were observed (except in the period July 2017-June 2018) and operating cash flow was affected with limited capital raising capacity. Accordingly, the Secretariat considered that the state of the national industry in the period analyzed was vulnerable to the elimination of the compensatory quota. 166. TAMSA argued that the domestic market is of great importance to exporters because energy reform has driven and will continue to drive investment in Mexico, both in the energy sector, and in industries that provide inputs necessary for the exploration and exploitation of deposits, the construction of distribution networks and pipelines, and other necessary infrastructure, such as the steel industry. 167. \u160\'00\'A0 It indicated that, under the AD Agreement, a countervailing quota review does not require a new injury determination, nor of the causal link between dumping and probable injury, but a prospective analysis. In this regard, it argued that the WTO Appellate Body report determined this and, as an example, provided the case of US-Anti-Dumping Measures on Oil Drilling Pipelines from Mexico. 168. TAMSA noted that, in a scenario without a countervailing fee, dumped prices would lead to a greater preference for the imported product, so it would have lower orders for goods and/or pressures to reduce its prices. In this regard, for the prospective analysis, TAMSA projected the effect of its economic and financial indicators, taking into account the following strategies for competing with dumped imports, if the countervailing quota is eliminated:a. a significant reduction in domestic sales volumes and, consequently, production if their prices do not adjust with a downward opportunity; b. a downward adjustment of their prices with cushioned impacts on sales volumes, but not on values and which, in any case, will lead to negative results in terms of profitability, andc.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0a fall in the value and volume of sales and, as a result, also production, the presence of the domestic product on the market, the use of installed capacity, as well as pressures to reduce wages and the labour plant. 169. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the scenario described in subparagraph (a) of the previous point, concerning a reduction in sales volume, TAMSA designed the domestic industry's indicators for the periods July 2019-June 2020 and July 2020-June 2021, as follows:a. production to the domestic market, from the beginning of the domestic market, from theparticipation of domestic production in the projected ANC; b. domestic production was estimated from the sum of the volume of production to the domestic market, plus projected exports; c. sales to the domestic market were estimated from the ratio of domestic sales and production to the domestic market of the review period, by production to the projected domestic market; (d) inventories, based on the quotient of this indicator and the domestic production of the review period, by projected domestic production; e. wages, based on the level they showed in the review period for the Bank of Mexico's inflation forecast; f. considered that personnel employed directly in domestic production of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding, export sales and installed capacity would remain at the same level as they observed during the review period, andg. unit operating costs and expenses for the review period. 170. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat considered it appropriate to replicate the methodology of estimating the indicators provided by TAMSA and determined that the projections were acceptable, as they were based on the scenario described in points 125 to 124 of this Resolution. It should be noted that the analysis of subparagraphs (b) and (c) of point 168 is not included, on the basis that TAMSA did not provide the methodology used.\u160\'00\'A0Also, in the second probationary period, TAMSA presented a new projection of the effect of eliminating the compensatory quota on the domestic industry's indicators of coal-fired coal connections; however, in that estimate it considered the volume and prices of total imports, not those for the product under review. The Secretariat therefore considered as better information that described in points 168 and 169 of this Resolution. 171. \u160\'00\'A0 In this regard, under TAMSA's strategy of not adjusting its domestic prices downwards, the Secretariat noted that the estimate of the domestic industry's indicators would have the following impacts:a. comparing the period July 2019-June 2020 with respect to the levels of the review period would reflect impacts on: production (3%), domestic market-oriented production (10%), total sales (3%), domestic sales (10%), market share (4 percentage points), productivity (3%) utilization of installed capacity (1 percentage point).\u160\'00\'A0In addition, operating loss would increase (1.78 times), as a result of the decrease in both sales revenue (6.4%) and operating costs (4.1%), which would result in a reduction in operating margin (2.5 percentage points) by remaining at -3.7%, andb.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0in the period July 2020-June 2021 with respect to the review period, impacts on indicators of the domestic industry would be aggravated by falls of: production (6%), domestic market-oriented production (18%), total sales (5%), domestic sales (18%), revenue (16%), market share (7 percentage points), productivity (6%) utilization of installed capacity (2 percentage points). In addition to the increase in operating loss (3.45 times), as a result of the decrease in both sales revenue (12.2%) and operating costs (7.8%), which would result in a reduction in the operating margin (5.1 percentage points) by remaining at -6.3%. 172. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on information and informationresults of the analysis described above, the Secretariat concluded that the potential volume of imports originating in China, as well as the price level at which they would be in the Mexican market, are objective elements that would establish the well-founded likelihood that, in the face of the elimination of the countervailing quota, the domestic industry would have negative effects on the relevant economic and financial indicators, resulting in a recurrence of injury to the domestic coal-fired steel supply industry to weld. 7. China's export potential173. \u160\'00\'A0 TAMSA noted that the imposition of the countervailing quota resulted in China's exports being significantly reduced. He added that there were factors that demonstrate the likelihood of a repeat of unfair practice in the event of a possible elimination of the countervailing quota, such as: the export potential of the Chinese steel industry and its large installed capacity. 174. It argued that, during the review period, China's domestic production outperformed Mexican production. This, even though China did not use its total installed capacity which is much larger than the installed capacity in the Mexican market. 175. It indicated that during the review period, Chinese producers exported globally equivalent to 10% of China's total production of butt-welding steel connections, or 16 times the ANC of the Mexican market. 176. TAMSA added that China's surplus capacity and export potential causes China to trade abroad at discriminated prices. In this regard, it noted that in the period July 2018-June 2019, the price at which China exported to Mexico was $1,702 per tonne and, over the same period, China exported to other countries with even lower prices, for example, exported to Indonesia ($1,381 per tonne), Malaysia ($1,323 per tonne), Brazil ($1,316 per tonne), Thailand ($1,303 per tonne) or Cambodia ($1,255 per tonne). 177. Moreover, it estimated the production and installed capacity of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding from China, based on the seamless pipe indicators, with information from the World Steel Association and Steel Business Briefing (SBB), respectively:a. to estimate the production of steel connections to coal for butt welding, considered the proportion of line and conduction pipe in seamless pipe exports published in UN Comtrade, andb. to estimate installed capacity, TAMSA used data from China's total seamless steel pipe production, with the level of \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  utilization of plants in China, adding that the installed capacity of seamless pipe has not changed in the last four periods of the period analyzed. 178. \u160\'00\'A0 With the information described in the previous point, TAMSA estimated the proportion of the production and installed capacity of steel connections to the total line and conduction pipe, this proportion was taken from the Final Resolution of the anti-dumping investigation. 179. \u160\'00\'A0 For export and import statistics, he used information from the Tenaris Information Centre, which he obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://teamwork.tenaris.net"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://teamwork.tenaris.net}}}\uc2 . TAMSA added that this information considers the entire universe of connections to weld butt, so, to estimate the indicators of the product under review, it proceeded to apply the proportion in which the connections ofbutt-welded coal steel under review, for the universe total imports into Mexico, made by TIGIE tariff fraction 7307.93.01, which it obtained from the study conducted by THECANACERO. 180. In addition, TAMSA argued that China remains the main exporter of steel connections, according to UN Comtrade data. Therefore, China is constantly looking for destinations to place goods that have restrictions in other markets and, coupled with Mexico's proximity to the United States and refinery construction and rehabilitation projects driven by the Mexican administration, the Mexican market is an attractive destination for Chinese goods in dumping, in the event of a possible elimination of the countervailing quota.\u160\'00\'A0181. \u160\'00\'A0 The Secretariat considered that the methodology TAMSA used to estimate China's production and installed capacity was relevant and represents the best available information, as it was based on reliable publications and sources of information. In this regard, it was noted that during the period under review:a. China's exports to the world accounted for more than 16 times the ANC, 15 times domestic production and 5 times installed capacity; b. China's production accounted for more than 170 times the ANC and 156 times domestic production; c. China's freely available capacity (installed capacity minus production) was equivalent to more than 270 times the ANC and 249 times domestic production, and(d).\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0the asymmetries between these indicators provide sufficient evidence to determine that the use of a portion of China's production, or its export potential, could be significant in shifting production and the Mexican market. Exports from China vs. China National market indicators(Tons)\par \par \uc2 Source: TAMSA and SIC-M.182. With respect to China's export profile, UN Comtrade's export statistics under subheading 7307.93, which include coal-fired steel connections for butt welding, indicate that during the period 2014 to 2018, China's exports accounted on average for 38% of totals worldwide, becoming the leading exporter. 183. TAMSA argued that there are additional factors that increase the likelihood of a repeat of unfair practice in the event of a possible elimination of the countervailing fee. In this regard, it indicated that Chinese exports, both of the product under review and the main input for its production (seamless steel pipe), are subject to the payment of compensatory fees in different countries.\u160\'00\'A0As regards anti-dumping connection investigations, trade remedy measures were established by Argentina, the European Union, Japan and Turkey. As regards input (seamless steel pipe), the measures were implemented by Argentina, Brazil, Canada, the European Union, India, Mexico, Turkey and the United States. 184. TAMSA indicated that there are other trade restriction measures in the steel sector globally, such as:a. the United States government's trade measures consisting of extraordinary tariffs in relation to the protection of its national security imposed on seamless pipe, input of the product under review, and(b) trade measures by which trade measures through which trade measuresthe United States has subjected certain goods from China to additional tariffs, citing intellectual property violations. 185. It added that, together with the trade defence measures in force in other markets, this creates incentives for China to divert its exports under price discrimination to other markets such as Mexico, should Mexico eliminate the current countervailing quota. 186. Based on the information and analysis described above, the Secretariat concluded that the steel connection manufacturing industry in China has significant export potential and freely available capacity, so that, in the face of a slight diversion of its trade to the domestic market, it would be able to supply the domestic market several times. This, coupled with undervaluation levels, indicates the well-founded likelihood that, in the face of the elimination of the countervailing quota, China's exports to the Mexican market would increase, in absolute and relative terms significantly, leading to a recurrence of injury to the domestic industry. G. Conclusion187. \u160\'00\'A0 Based on the analysis and results described in this Resolution, the Secretariat concluded that there were sufficient evidence to determine that the elimination of the countervailing quota to imports of coal connections for butt welding originating in China would result in a recurrence of dumping and injury to the domestic industry. Among the elements that led to this conclusion, without being limiting the aspects identified throughout this Resolution, are the following:a. There are sufficient evidence to support the avoiding dumping of exports to Mexico of coal-to-butt steel connections originating in China. b. Notwithstanding the dumping period of the application of the countervailing quota, the volume of imports of butt-welded coal steel connections originating in China, under dumping, projections of these imports in the face of the possible elimination of the countervailing quota, confirm the well-founded likelihood that they would return to the domestic market in considerable volumes, which would displace domestic production and achieve considerable market share. c. There is a well-founded likelihood that, in the face of the elimination of the countervailing quota, the price of potential imports originating in China, will reach a level of undervaluation with respect to the national price of 30%, which would have a negative impact on economic and financial indicators, since it would force it to decrease them by facing operating losses. d. The elimination of the countervailing quota and the likely increase in imports originating in China would result in negative effects on the domestic industry. Among the most significant effects in the second projected period, with respect to the levels achieved in the review period, production declines (6%), domestic market-oriented production (18%), total sales (5%), domestic sales (18%), revenue (16%), market share (7 percentage points), productivity (6%), utilization of installed capacity (16%), market share (7 percentage points), productivity (6%), utilization of installed capacity (2 percentage points), revenue (12.2%), as well as an increase in operating loss (3.45 times) and reduction in operating margin (5.1 percentage points) with -6.3%. e. China has a free capacityexport potential for coal-fired steel connections for heavily higher butt welding to the domestic market. In particular, the freely available capacity of the review period was equivalent to more than 270 times the size of the Mexican market and more than 249 times the domestic production of the same period. f. China ranks as the leading exporter of coal-fired steel connections for butt welding, as during the period analyzed its exports accounted for an average of 38% of global totals. g.\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0\u160\'00\'A0China's exports of coal-to-butt steel connections are restricted in various countries by trade remedy measures by Argentina, the European Union, Japan, Turkey and the United States, making it possible to presume that the investigated country reorients shipments of the product under review to more open markets such as Mexico. 188. As set out above and on the basis of Articles 11.1 and 11.3 of the AD Agreement, and 67, 70 fraction II and 89 F fraction IV literal to the LCE, the following RESOLUTION is issued189. \u160\'00\'A0 The administrative procedure for reviewing the validity of the countervailing quota imposed on imports of coal-to-butt steel connections originating in China, regardless of the country of origin, entering through TIGIE tariff fraction 7307.93.01, or any other, is declared to be completed. 190. The term of the final countervailing fee referred to in points 3 and 4 of this Resolution is extended for a further five years from 5 August 2019. 191. It is for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Public Credit to apply the final countervailing fee referred to in points 3 and 4 of this Resolution throughout the national territory. 192. Pursuant to Article 66 of the LCE, importers who under this Resolution are required to pay the compensatory fee shall not be obliged to pay the compensation fee if they verify that the country of origin of the good is different from China. The verification of the origin of the good shall be carried out in accordance with the provisions of the Agreement laying down the rules for the determination of the country of origin of the imported goods and the provisions for their certification, for non-preferential purposes (before the Agreement laying down the rules for determining the country of origin of the imported goods and the provisions for their certification, compensatory quotas) published in the DOF on 30 August 1994, and its amendments published in the same broadcasting body on 11 November 1996, 12 October 1998, 30 July 1999, 30 June 2000, 1 and 23 March 2001 , 29 June 2001, 6 September 2002, 30 May 2003, 14 July 2004, 19 May 2005, 17 July 2008 and 16 October 2008. 193.Notify the interested parties of this \u160\'00\'A0 Resolution to the appearance. 194. \u160\'00\'A0 Contact this Resolution to the SAT for applicable legal purposes. 195. This Resolution shall enter into force on the day following its publication in the DOF. 196. Archive as a total and definitively completed case. Mexico City, August 5, 2020.- The Secretary of Economy, Graciela M\u225\'00\'E1rquez Col\u237\'00\'EDn.-Rubric.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200819eg8j006px\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngb