\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-Europe  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - July 30\b0\par\par\uc2 1,225 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2020\par 06:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Majors steady in month-end post-Fed, USD remains on back-foot Market Briefs \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. economy likely suffered historic plunge in Q2; outlook murky as COVID-19 cases surge \u8226\'20\'22 Fed chief says coronavirus surge slowing U.S. economic recovery \u8226\'20\'22 Fed buying spree could move to long end of yield curve \u8211\'20\'13 analysts \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. jobless benefit at risk as Congress coronavirus talks stalled \u8226\'20\'22 White House to lure U.S.\par \par \uc2  firms to Latam from Asia in nearshoring drive, senior adviser says \u8226\'20\'22 Macron's popularity shoots up after EU recovery deal -opinion poll \u8226\'20\'22 BOJ won\u8217\'20\'19t rule out deeper negative rates to combat COVID effects \u8211\'20\'13 BOJ Dep Gov Amamiya \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese sell net Y565 bln foreign bonds July 25 week, Y149.6 bln stocks too, foreign investors buy Y337.4 bln JGBs \u8226\'20\'22 Japan June retail sales -1.2% y/y, better than -6.5% eyed, off 4th straight month, to stay weak \u8226\'20\'22 AU Jun Building Approvals, -4.9%, 0.0% f'cast, -16.4% prev \u8226\'20\'22 AU Q2 Export Prices, -2.4%, 2.7% prev; Import Prices, -1.9%, -1.0% prev \u8226\'20\'22 NZ Jul NBNZ Business Outlook, --31.8, -34.4 prev Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 06:45 FR Jun Producer Prices MM, -0.1% prev \u8226\'20\'22 07:55 DE Jul Unemployment Chng SA 43k f'cast, 69k prev; Unemployment Rate 6.5% f'cast, 6.4% prev \u8226\'20\'22 08:00 DE Q2 GDP Flash QQ SA -9.0% f'cast, -2.2% prev; Flash YY NSA -10.9% f'cast, -1.9% prev; SA -11.3% f'cast, -2.3% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:00 EZ Jul Consumer Confidence Final, -15.0 f'cast, -15.0 prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:00 EZ Jun Unemployment Rate, 7.7% f'cast, 7.4% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:00 EZ Jul Economic Sentiment, 81.0 f'cast, 75.7 prev; Industrial -17.0 f'cast, -21.7 prev; Services -25.0 f'cast, -35.6 prev \u8226\'20\'22 12:00 DE Jul CPI Prelim YY 0.2% f'cast, 0.9% prev; YY 0.4% f'cast, 0.8% prev Looking Ahead - Events, Auctions, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 13:00 13:00 BoE's Sarah Breeden discusses "Non-systemic UK banks\u8230\'20\'26" in London Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY pivots @105.00 in Asia after fall to 104.77 \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY fell some more to 104.77 EBS o/n, steadies in Asia around 105.00 \u8226\'20\'22 Range so far 104.92-105.15 EBS so far, quiet post-Fed, into month-end \u8226\'20\'22 Caution on COVID, Japan data shrugged off, flows likely to ebb in August \u8226\'20\'22 105.00 strike option expiries yesterday, today, tomorrow a tether \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance from 105.13 descending 55-HMA, soggy US yields too \u8226\'20\'22 Some do see Japanese institutional investor buys below but still iffy EUR/USD-Eases on profit taking but trending higher \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened 1.1792 after USD broadly eased - sustained by dovish Fed \u8226\'20\'22 USD clawed back some lost ground in early Asia on profit-taking \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD traded down to 1.1766 before steadying above 1.1175 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD trending higher ahead of key 61.8 of 1.2556/1.0634 move at 1.0822 \u8226\'20\'22 Support at yesterday's 1.1714 low with break ending 8-days of higher lows \u8226\'20\'22 Sentiment remains bullish and buying dips is the favoured strategy AUD/USD-Settles slightly lower after early profit taking \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened 0.7189 and after trading at 0.7190 it slipped lower \u8226\'20\'22 USD broadly firmed on light profit taking after hitting multi-year lows \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD traded as low as 0.7171 before settling around 0.7180 \u8226\'20\'22 Spike in Melbourne COVID-19 cases and Aus data had no impact on price \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD hourly support at 0.7155/60 with 10-day MA (0.7116) key support \u8226\'20\'22 Sellers tipped ahead of 0.7200 with minor resistance at 0.7200/10 \u8226\'20\'22 Break above 0.7210 targets 2019 high at 0.7295 GBP/USD-Technical resistance holds, but bias still higher \u8226\'20\'22 -0.05% with a 1.2973-1.2998 range in a low key Asia - USD a touch firmer \u8226\'20\'22 UK car output plunges to lowest level since 1950s \u8226\'20\'22 Cable strength remains driven by USD weakness and Euro strength \u8226\'20\'22 Techs - momentum studies, 5, 10 & 21 daily and weekly MAs head north \u8226\'20\'22 1.2992 upper 21 day Bollinger suggests cable is overbought short term \u8226\'20\'22 Strong trending setup targets 1.3020, 76.4% 2019/20 fall, which capped in NY \u8226\'20\'22 Sustained 1.3020 break opens the door to 1.3210 January/March range top \u8226\'20\'22 Close below 1.2802 10 DMA needed to undermine topside bias USD/JPY heavy post-FOMC as yen remains better bid USD/JPY looks set to remain heavy with the yen drawing some pandemic-related haven demand as the dollar loses its lustre and U.S. yields continue to head lower, albeit gradually, following a dovish \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  meeting. From 108.16 on July 1, USD/JPY hit a low of 104.77 on Wednesday, falling below the 61.8% Fibonacci retracement of its March 9 to 24 move from 101.18 to 111.71, at 105.20. With U.S. yields remaining soggy or moving lower, any USD/JPY upside could be limited in the near term. Nearby option expiries at 105.00 should work to tether for now. There were chunky expiries at this strike Wednesday and more are scheduled Thursday and Friday. USD/JPY could pivot near here into month-end but will likely remain heavy with Tokyo flows, from both Japanese importers and exporters, falling off into August and the summer holidays. Some Japanese investment demand could emerge at recent lows and below towards the March spike low, but U.S. yields are lower and less of an enticement. Higher yields in Australia may attract some but that market is far smaller and AUD/JPY is still relatively expensive, suggesting further buy-side flows will likely be limited to summer carry trades. How long will China ignore the yuan's hidden weakness? The yuan's weakness - apparent to market watchers observing its performance on a trade-weighted basis - does not appear to have raised concern in Beijing. Yet the authorities could soon step in, as a depreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dampens China's appeal for foreign investors. Superficially, USD/CNY continues to decline, displaying some bearish technical signals hinting at further yuan appreciation versus the dollar, as the latter remains under broad pressure after predictably dovish comments from U.S. Federal Reserve Chair Powell. But the yuan index has been plummeting since last week, triggered by China's retaliatory closure of a U.S. consulate amid worsening bilateral ties, which sank Shanghai stocks. The index's drop to its lowest since October, despite the dollar index slumping to two-year lows, has not gone unnoticed. The TR/HKEx renminbi index is approaching the level at which the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stave off further losses. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday manoeuvred the daily USD/CNY midpoint to discourage bearish yuan bets) - as it did in October in response to U.S. accusations of intentional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation. As the U.S. continues coaxing firms and investments away from China, the last thing Beijing needs is a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that could accelerate the trend.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-8-2T04:49:37.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200730eg7u00lzp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\p