ital, Byculla WestZip Code 400011, Mumbai, Maharashtra, IndiaValson Polyester Ltd.Twin Complex, Phase II, Ist Floor, Opp. Marol Fire BrigadeMarol Naka, Andheri (E)Zip Code 400059, Mumbai, IndiaWellknown Polyesters Ltd.Nirmal, B-Wing, 18th Floor, Backbay ReclamationNariman PointZip Code 400021, Mumbai, IndiaWujiang Hengyu Textile Co. Ltd.Ditang Road 1677Zhenze, WujiangZip Code 215231, Jiangsu, ChinaWuxi Autofibre Textile Co. Ltd.Dadi Industrial Park, Hudai Town, Binhu, WuxiZip Code 214161, Jiangsu, China Xiamen Jin Xia Technology Co. Ltd.Fanghu North 2nd Road No. 1507Huli, XiamenZip Code 361010, Fujian, ChinaXianglu Chemical Fiber Co. Ltd.Maqing Road 1268, Haicang Investment ZoneXiamenZip Code 361026, Fujian, ChinaZhangjiagang Hengshuo Trading Co. Ltd.Renmin Middle Road 70Zhangjiagang, SuzhouZip Code 215600, Jiangsu, ChinaZhejiang Hemei Leisure Produc\u237\'00\'EDs Co.Shanbei Village, DangshanXiaoshanZip Code 311245, Hangzhou, ChinaZhejiang Hengyi Petrochemicals Co.North Shixin Road No. 260, Nan An MingXia ZhuoshanZip Code 311215, Hangzhou, ChinaZheJiang Advanced Huaxin Materials Co. Ltd.Xinwan Industry Zone, Xiaoshan, HangzhouZip Code 311228, Zhejiang, ChinaZhejiang Shengyuan Chemical Fibre Co. Ltd.Hongyang RoadDongsha, Yinong, XiaoshanZip Code 311247, Hangzhou, ChinaZhejiang Sunmarr International Transportation Co. Ltd.Shixin Middle Rd No.819, Lvdu World Trade Plaza 14FXiaoshanHangzhou, ChinaZhejiang Xinghuali Fiber Co. Ltd.Huashe Street, Industrial ZoneShaoxingZip Code 312033, Zhejiang,China3.\u160\'00\'A0GovernmentsSeed of China in MexicoPlate No. 317Col. PolancoC.P. 11560, Mexico CitySed of India in MexicoMusset No. 325Col. PolancoC.P. 11550, Mexico CityE. Information requirements18. \u160\'00\'A0On 26 November 2019, the Secretariat requested the Chemical Industry Association, A.C. (ANIQ), to confirm that Akra was the only national producer of PFTT in the period July 2016-June 2017, as well as to provide figures on PFTT's domestic production volumes for the period analyzed. Responded on December 3, 2019.RECITALSA. Competition19. \u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat has jurisdiction to issue this Resolution, in accordance with Articles 16 and 34 fractions V and XXXIII of the Organic Law of the Federal Public Administration; 1, 2(A), fraction II, numeral 7, and 19 fractions I and IV of the Internal Regulations of the Secretariat of the Economy; 5 and 12.1 of the Agreement on the Implementation of Article VI of the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade 1994 (the "Anti-Dumping Agreement"), and 5 fraction VII and 52 fractions I and II of the Foreign Trade Act (LCE), and 80 and 81 of the Regulations of the Foreign Trade Act (ERC). B. Applicable Law20. \u160\'00\'A0For the purposes of this procedure, the Anti-Dumping Agreement, the LCE, the RLCE, the Federation Tax Code, the Federal Administrative Litigation Act and the Federal Code of Civil Procedures, the latter three of supplementary application, apply. C. Protection of Confidential Information21. \u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat may not publicly disclose the confidential information submitted to it by interested parties, nor the confidential information that it itself collects, in accordance with Articles 6.5 of the AD Agreement, 80 of the LCE and 152 and 158 of the ERC. However, interested parties may obtain access to confidential information, provided that they meet the requirements set out in Articles 159 and 160 of the RLCE. D. Procedural Legitimacy22. \u160\'00\'A0In accordance with points 170 to 173 of this Resolution, the Secretariat determines that Akra is entitled to request the initiation of this investigation, in accordance with Articles 5.4 of the AD Agreement and 50 of the LCE. E. Period researched and analyzed23. \u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat determines to determine as a period of investigation from 1 July 2018 to 30 June 2019 and as a period of injury analysis from 1 July 2016 to 30 June 2019, the same periods that were proposed by Akra, since these are consistent with Article 76 of the ERC and the recommendation of the Committee on Anti-Dumping Practices of the \uc2 World Trade Organization (WTO) (\uc2 document G/ADP/6 adopted on 5 May 2000). F. Price Discrimination Analysis1.\u160\'00\'A0Export price24. \u160\'00\'A0To prove the export price, Akra provided a list of total PFTT import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided to it by the Tax Administration Service (SAT) through an ANIQ, which were entered for TIGIE tariff fraction 5402.33.01 during the period investigated. 25.\u160\'00\'A0Akra noted that because the subject merchandise enters through a specific tariff fraction, all import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  should correspond to investigated product;\u160\'00\'A0however, he identifiedimports that by their description do not correspond to the goods investigated, therefore, in order to consider those corresponding to PFTT, based on the product description field presented in the list of imports of the SAT, Akra excluded: i. product that is not 100% polyester (\b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that include the words nylon, nylon, acrylic, spandex, cotton, elastane, cotton and polyamide);\u160\'00\'A0ii. product that is not imported into coils or is not filament (\b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  that include the words rolls, cord and cones); iii. product that is sewing thread or thread for sewing or embroidery, and iv. products such as ligas, yarn, padding, fabric, vinyl and polyester mesh. 26.\u160\'00\'A0Akra calculated a weighted average export price for China and India based on the customs value in dollars per kilogram. He noted that import prices are net of discounts, refunds and bonuses. 27.\u160\'00\'A0In order to replicate the Applicant's methodology, the Secretariat came from the list of total imports of PFTT made during the investigated period reporting the Mexico Trade Information System (SIC-M). 28.\u160\'00\'A0Because some differences in value and volume were found between the two statistics, the Secretariat determined to use the basis of the import statistics reported by the SIC-M to calculate the export price, on the basis that the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  contained in that database are obtained upon validation of the customs pediments given in a framework of exchange of information between customs agents, on the one hand, and the customs authority on the other, which are reviewed by the \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  and is therefore considered as the best available information. It depurated the database according to the methodology proposed by domestic production. a. Adjustments to the export price29. \u160\'00\'A0The Applicant stated that for the calculation of the export price it used the customs value which is equivalent to the Cost, Insurance and Freight (CIF) value. 30.\u160\'00\'A0Noted that the Customs Law defines the customs value as the transaction value, which includes, in addition to the price paid, the amount of the following charges: i. fees and brokerage costs, except purchase fees; (ii) the cost of packaging; (iii) packaging costs, both for labour and materials, and (iv). transport costs, insurance and related expenses, such as handling, loading and unloading, incurred in connection with the transport of the goods. 31.\u160\'00\'A0Proposed adjusting the export price by terms and conditions of sale, in particular by maritime freight and costs of origin for China, as well as freight and maritime insurance for India. 32.\u160\'00\'A0For both countries, it provided contributions that support the amount of proposed adjustments based on carriers and applied them to all import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . 33.\u160\'00\'A0In the case of China, the maritime freight and cost of origin adjustments, as specified in the same quote, correspond to the stretch from the Chinese port of Shanghai to that of Manzanillo, Mexico. He calculated a simple average in dollars per kilogram from quotes he contributed for each month of the period investigated. 34.\u160\'00\'A0With reference to India, the maritime freight adjustment corresponds to the stretch from the Indian port of Nhava Sheva to that of Manzanillo, Mexico. Calculated the amount of the adjustment in dollars per kilogram. To get the insurance adjustment amount, he applied thespecified in that quote. 35.\u160\'00\'A0With regard to domestic freight adjustment in both countries, it stated that it did not have the respective information. b. Determination36. \u160\'00\'A0Pursuant to Articles 2.4 of the AD Agreement, 36 of the LCE, and 53 and 54 of the RLCE, the Secretariat accepted the information submitted by Akra, as it corresponds to data from the period investigated, as well as to the ports through which it entered the largest volume. It adjusted the export price for maritime freight, costs of origin for China and freight and maritime insurance for India, considering the customs value reported in the SIC-M.2 statistics. Normal valueto.\u160\'00\'A0Chinai.\u160\'00\'A0China's status as a non-market economy37. \u160\'00\'A0Akra stated that it provided information and evidence in order to establish the presumption that companies producing PFTT in China prevail cost and price structures that are not determined in accordance with market principles, therefore, in accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the ER, as well as paragraph 15 of the Protocol of Accession of the People's Republic of China to the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  (the "China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO"), the prices of a third market economy country that can be considered as a substitute for China should be considered as normal value. 38.\u160\'00\'A0China noted that China remains a country with an economy that does not reflect market principles. He provided a study called "China's Assessment of Non-Market Polyester Industry" (the "Study"), produced by Akra in September 2019. The study analyses the overall and particular situation of the PFTT industry in China based on the economic criteria set out in Article 48 of the ERC. The points contained in the Study are discussed below. (1). On the country investigated39. \u160\'00\'A0Akra noted that, based on China's Constitution (the "Constitution"), the country maintains a socialist market economy, under the leadership of the Chinese Communist Party (PCCh). Pursuant to Article 7 of that legal order, it states that the socialist economy is state-owned, that is, the State is the leading force through the PCCh, with guidelines communicated through five-year plans. 40.\u160\'00\'A0He mentioned that the five-year plans are a series of social and economic development initiatives, which set growth objectives and project reforms. 41.\u160\'00\'A0It indicated that, according to economic theory, the factors of production are land, labor and capital.\u160\'00\'A0It noted that under article 10 of the Constitution, land in China was owned by the State.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, based on the european Commission Working Group document "On Significant Distortions in the People's Republic of China's Economy for Purposes in Defence in Trade Investigations", December 2017 (SWD (2017) (the "\uc2 European Commission\uc2  Document 2017"), particularly in Chapter 9, land allocation depends solely on the State, which pursues specific policy objectives rather than free-market principles, i.e. state-owned enterprises have received their land for free or through fictitious single-participant tenders, obtaining land-use rights at a very low price. He pointed out that state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in only one of the factors of production results in direct distortions in the production of goodsEnd. 42.\u160\'00\'A0It abounded that in that \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document 2017, the state sector in China is a pillar of the economy in which state enterprises account for approximately 40% of all industrial assets and account for half of the bank loans, not counting the figures of companies with a mixed public-ownership and state-owned linkage. 43.\u160\'00\'A0The document develops a number of cross-cutting distortions, which concludes that: "State-owned enterprises are increasing their presence in the service economy and a strong presence of public enterprises in public services, the financial sector, telecommunications, the transport industry and a wide range of manufacturing industries, including steel and chemicals, can also be observed". 44.\u160\'00\'A0China submits that, although the government has announced and implemented to varying degrees of reforms apparently to allow market forces to come into play, government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains widespread and varied. Akra mentioned that in order to determine whether China is a centrally planned economy, the criteria of Article 48 of the ERC should be considered in a comprehensive manner and not individually because the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventional\b0 \highlight\uc2  effect on one of the criteria may be sufficient to affect the cost and price structure of the investigated product. 45.\u160\'00\'A0Akra argued that under paragraph 15(d) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, subparagraph (a) Romaite (ii) expired in December 2016, but the application of the initial part of subparagraph (a) and the Romanite (i) remains in force. 46.\u160\'00\'A0It noted that China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO was clear, since subparagraph (a) provides for the possibility of applying a methodology based on the prices or costs of Chinese producers, or a methodology that is not based on those prices or costs. 47.\u160\'00\'A0It explained that the Study set out the elements to conclude that Chinese imports must be considered as originating in a country with a centrally planned economy, therefore, in accordance with Article 33 of the LCE, price references from a third country with a market economy that can be considered as a substitute can be taken as normal value. 48.\u160\'00\'A0It added that for the purposes of Article 33 of the LCE, Article 48 of the ER ISR is part of two fundamental premises: (i) if the economy is non-market, and (ii). to support it, the analysis and evaluation of each of the criteria set out in the second paragraph of that article. 49.\u160\'00\'A0It argued that in the Initiation Resolution on Balloon Research, as well as in Polyester Short Fiber urged against China, published in the Official Journal of the Federation (DOF) on 26 June 2017 and 6 February 2018, respectively, the Secretariat expressed its view for the interpretation of Article 15 of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO and its relationship to Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the ERCE, stating that, in initiating an anti-dumping investigation, it is for domestic production, in the first instance, based on the best available information, to provide evidence leading to a reasonable presumption that at the product level under investigation , industry or sector in China does not meet the criteria contained in Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the ERC and, secondly, to Chinese exporting producers the opportunity to demonstrate that they operate under market economy conditions with respect tothe manufacture, production and sale of such a product. 50.\u160\'00\'A0It added that, by economic logic, beyond the criteria laid down in Article 48 of the ERCE, any aspect that directly impacts production factors defined as land, labour and capital, has an impact along the production chains, at the sector, industry and final product level.\u160\'00\'A0It stated that the distortions identified in the Chinese market, according to the Study, have an impact on the determination of the costs and prices of the investigated product and, therefore, on the manufacture, production and sale of the investigated product. 51.\u160\'00\'A0In the Study, Akra includes a section on the current situation in China. He also mentioned that at the end of 2012 President Xi Jinping established in his speech that China had suffered unusual difficulties and sacrifices in the modern history of the world and that with his mandate he would achieve the "great rejuvenation of the Chinese nation". However, according to Yang, Yuan and Liu Nian, in its note published in the Financial Times in May 2019, over the years it seems to reveal a different reality in which problems emerge, for example, in the financial system, as the government turned to banks to provide large loans during the boom years, which is now trying to cut back because of the negative consequences of investing in unprofitable companies and deal patterns. 52.\u160\'00\'A0In the recent international context, trade disputes between the United States and China have brought back the debate over the conditions of political and economic control that were the thrust of China's accelerated growth. Issues related to structural reforms have been highlighted, such as the rewriting of a Chinese foreign investment law that includes the prohibition of forced transfer of technology, the protection of the intellectual property rights of foreign investors, and national treatment of foreign investors. The issue of industrial subsidies to state-owned enterprises (SOEs), the prevention of monetary manipulation, including, in accordance with the article "EU Trade Policy in the midst of the China-US Clash: Caught in the Crossfire?" of Gonz\u225\'00\'E1lez, Anabel and V\u233\'00\'E9ron, Nicolas of 2019, has also emerged. 53.\u160\'00\'A0He added that according to Ma Jian's article, "The dark side of the chinese dream. Ma Jian on the short path from utopia to dystopia," Literary Hub (2019), the promise of the Chinese dream that would make the new government lead to greater economic wealth and restore China to its past glory, has become a vague and hazy slogan. It pointed out that, for the purposes of the analysis, it was necessary to consider the costs and distortions that these actions have involved for the other nations with which China has trade relations. 54.\u160\'00\'A0It argued that, in October 2017, the \uc2 United States Department of Commerce\uc2  (USDOC) (the "USDOC 2017" determination) published the results of an investigation into China's status, confirming that non-market economy conditions prevail in China, the analysis of which is consistent with the criteria provided for by Mexican law. (2). On the sector or industry under investigation55. \u160\'00\'A0Akra stated that PFTT is a product of the petrochemical industry, and the products of that industry are a subset of industrial chemicals derived from petroleum, ethane and naphtha or natural gas.Petrochemicals account for 90% of the total demand for raw materials in today's chemical production. 56.\u160\'00\'A0He explained that a differentiation must be made on high-value chemicals (HVCs), such as light olefins (ethylene and propylene) and aromatic chemicals (BTX), such as benzene, toluene and mixed xylenes, which are often co-produced in a process such as steam cracking. 57.\u160\'00\'A0The demand for HVCs is driven by PET derivatives, plastics, synthetic fibres and rubber products. In fact, the demand for plastics is higher and growing faster than that of any other group of bulk materials. This is relevant because the polyester industry derives from the chain links that start from HVCs.58. \u160\'00\'A0Synthetic textile products are made of fibers that are polymerized from the same resins used in the other plastic segments, with PET and polypropylene being the most common, and polyester fibers and filaments are the highest production volume worldwide. 59.\u160\'00\'A0The value chain for producing polyesters is one of the most complex. The links range from the production of BTX to xylenes, PTA with ethylene oxide and MEG, concluding in variants such as fibers and polyester filaments, resins or packaging, among other specialties. It provided a value chain diagram for polyester production, ranging from natural gas, oil, paraxilene (PX), PTA and MEG, based on the information "The Polyester/PET Value Chain: Process Technology Improvements from BXT to Xylenes and PTA /DMT to Resins/Fibers, 2016-2025. 60.\u160\'00\'A0It argued that the structure of the petrochemical industry within which the polyester products sector is of greater relevance, makes it possible to understand how the control and manipulation exercised by the State in the global-macroeconomic and local-microeconomic field from the first links of the chain, such as those related to the impact on the precursors of the petrochemical industry, results in distortions in the polyester products sector and, consequently, the PFTT. (3). Criteria laid down in Article 48 of the ERC61. \u160\'00\'A0To support the support that China is a non-market economy, Akra presented in the Study, arguments and evidence at the macroeconomic, industrial and PFTT level, in accordance with the criteria contained in Article 48 of the RLCE:(a) that the foreign country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under investigation be widely convertible into international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets62. \u160\'00\'A0Akra noted that a convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widespreadly one that can be freely exchanged to another for any purpose without regulatory restrictions. Convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are associated with open and stable economies, and their prices are determined through supply and demand forces in the market. A non-convertible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is understood as the opposite, that is, it does not behave freely because it is controlled or manipulated by the State.\u160\'00\'A063.\u160\'00\'A0It indicated that, in China's most recent Trade Policy Review of 2018, issued by the WTO Trade Policy Review Body, contained in document WT/TPR/S/375/Rev.1, it was held that there was an optimistic trend that the People's Bank of China and the State \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Administration continued to take steps to establish a greater degree of liberalization andConvertibility. 64.\u160\'00\'A0However, WTO Members continued to recommend to China to improve its conditions of access and eliminate restrictive practices, to the extent that some countries, such as the United States, show that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot be considered to be widely convertible into international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. 65.\u160\'00\'A0It noted that, pursuant to Section 701 of the Trade Facilitation and Trade Enforcement Act of 2015, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  (the "Treasury Department") publishes a report twice a year analyzing whether countries are manipulating their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In its 2019 report, it assessed the interchangeability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting, under the following arguments, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 : "the Chinese authorities have recognized that they have extensive control over the RMB (renminbi) exchange rate". Moreover, that China has "a long history of facilitating the undervaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 " and would have taken "concrete steps to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in order to gain an unfair competitive advantage. 66.\u160\'00\'A0It added that the Treasury Department continues to urge China to take the necessary measures to avoid a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting, moreover, that China has not resolved actions that lead to market distortion, including subsidies and SOE's, requiring an improvement in economic fundamentals and the configuration of a structural policy to prop up towards a stronger renminbi (RMB). In summary, it notes that the Treasury Department reached the following conclusions:a. \u160\'00\'A0 continues to ensure placement on the "Monitoring List" with which the United States reviews certain economies that deserve special attention derived from its monetary practices; b. \u160\'00\'A0 monitor and review, over the next 6-month period, in light of the trade imbalance between China and the United States, the undervaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; c. \u160\'00\'A0 concerns persist about exchange rate practices, particularly around misalignment and subvaluation of the RMB relative to the dollar; d. \u160\'00\'A0 China must make improvements in the transparency of its reserve and exchange rate management \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and objectives, and(e). \u160\'00\'A0 exchange rate problems are identified, as the RMB has fallen against the dollar by 8% over the past year, in the context of a large and growing bilateral trade surplus. 67.\u160\'00\'A0Akra stated that while China has made progress in recent years in converting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the government continues to participate in exchange rate formation.\u160\'00\'A0It noted that, based on the usDOC's conclusions (2017), the Chinese government still maintains significant restrictions on capital account transactions and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly in markets and retains strict approval requirements for all major capital account transactions, while limiting the extent of price divergence between domestic and external \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. (b) That wages in that foreign country be established by free negotiation between workers and employers68. \u160\'00\'A0It indicated that on the basis of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document 2017, China had not ratified all international agreements or labour conventions. Of the eight conventions that the \uc2 International Labour Organization (ILO) \uc2 classifiesas fundamentals, it has ratified only four, pending Conventions 29, 87, 98 and 105, relating to forced labour; ii. freedom of association and the protection of the organization, iii. the right to organize and collective bargaining, and (iv). abolition of forced labour, respectively. 69.\u160\'00\'A0It stated that the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document 2017 considered that Conventions 87 and 98 are considered to be critically important to the structure of the labour market, since they confer rights on workers and employers, in addition, they promote market-based wages. 70.\u160\'00\'A0It added that on the basis of the document "China's Investment Policy Consequences for Workers", developed by Meixner, Warneck, Morvannou and Mestre in 2016, workers' rights in China are mainly based on the Labour Act 1994, the Trade Unions Act 1992 (as amended in 2013), the Arbitration Act, the Dispute Resolution of 2008 and the Employment Promotion Act 2008. 71.\u160\'00\'A0He pointed out that, based on the Observatory on International Labour Relations, it consisted of a research group of the professional school of labour relations of the \uc2 Complutense University of Madrid\uc2  ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.observatoriorli.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.observatoriorli.com}}}\uc2 ), although, in China there may be almost two million trade unions, in practice there is only one, since all must be integrated into the Federation of Trade Unions in China (ACFTU). That is, in real terms, in China "there is no freedom of association and there is only one union legally enabled under the control and direction of the Communist Party" (Meixner, et al, op. cit. 2016). 72.\u160\'00\'A0It noted that collective bargaining in China was still a long way from the ideal. It indicated that the China Labour Bulletin (2018) (the "Bulletin 2018"), on its website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://clb.org.hk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://clb.org.hk}}}\uc2 , reports that there is no formal mechanism for collective bargaining at the national level, and since trade unions cannot effectively represent workers in the negotiation, workers have been forced to take matters into their own hands by organizing strikes. These events have increased from less than 200 in 2011 to 1,256 in 2017, according to Thomlinson Harvey in the note "The Communist Party of China is abandoning workers," published in The New York Times, in April 2018. 73.\u160\'00\'A0With regard to the above, Akra made a comparison of strike movements in 2011 and 2018 in manufacturing, and concludes that the uprisings of Chinese workers have not served to push to improve their working conditions, on the contrary, the government has reacted with arrest and repression. 74.\u160\'00\'A0Akra noted that, according to Bulletin 2018, the effect of worker uprisings transcends collective bargaining of wages, and it has been found that, despite workers' efforts, "law enforcement has weakened over the past decade as the economy slowed down and governments sought to protect local investment. Following the implementation of the Labour Contract Act in 2008, there was an initial push by local governments and the union to ensure that the most vulnerable workers, rural migrants, signed formal employment contracts as required by law. But the initiative never gained momentum." 75.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, it submitted that, based on the 2019 China Labour Bulletin, during that year, most disputeswere in nationally owned enterprises, without or with a union controlled by the administration of the company. These disputes were mainly due to the large imbalance of power in industrial relations that has allowed employers to delay the payment of wages and social security contributions, refuse to pay severance or relocation compensation, arbitrarily reduce wages and benefits, and dictate working conditions. 76.\u160\'00\'A0On this subject, Akra pointed out that: (i) every trade union is under the supervision and direction of the Federation of Trade Unions in China, which is subordinate to the Communist Party, that is, controlled by the State; (ii) the role of the Federation is ineffective in meeting the real needs of workers, and the private sector cannot develop representative trade unions, and (iii).\u160\'00\'A0before the monopoly on trade union representation maintained by that Federation, there are distortions and bad signs in the labour market. 77.\u160\'00\'A0The Applicant added that the USDOC 2017 agrees that there are still significant institutional constraints in China, on the extent to which wage rates are determined through free negotiation between workers and enterprises. The Chinese government prohibits the formation of independent trade unions to represent workers, using this ban in their favor to impose themselves in collective bargaining, mainly derived from unilateral control of the ACFTU and the Communist Party. Even though there are certain legal remedies for an individual to challenge the employment contract and wage-related violations in particular cases, institutional barriers limit their effectiveness. 78.\u160\'00\'A0Akra concluded that the arguments put forward in this regard are a clear indication that working conditions in China are not established through free negotiation between workers and employers, but are directly and permanently influenced by the Chinese political system. In addition, in the Preliminary Resolution of the Anti-Dumping Investigation on Imports of Short Fiber Polyester originating in China, published on 30 November 2018, the Secretariat noted that although the Chinese Chamber of Commerce for textiles imports provided some examples of full-time employment contracts established between one of the exporters and their workers, these did not indicate the minimum conditions of recruitment, such as benefits, breaks, holidays, social security, dispute resolution, among other elements that dispute the employment relationship, resolving for this purpose, that control mechanisms persist in China that limit labour mobility and the free negotiation of wages between employers and workers as set out in Article 48 of the RLCE. (c) That decisions of the sector or industry under research on prices, costs and supply of inputs, including raw materials, technology, production, sales and investment, be taken in response to market signals and without significant interference from State79. \u160\'00\'A0In relation to the PFTT industry, Akra stated that the technical relationship from raw material to final product is virtually the same worldwide. He explained that chemicals in general, and hence petrochemicals such as PFTT, are constituted in the relationship of chemical reactions and the "quantitative study of reagents and products" of the same, which is defined as stoichiometry. This means that for the production of aPFTT unit, about 0.86 PTA units and 0.34 MEG units are used. He clarified that in practice there may be some decimal variations in material performance, due to factors related to specific conditions in the processes of each production plant. 80.\u160\'00\'A0Noted that the technology used for the manufacture of PFTT worldwide is similar in any country, except minor and marginal differences; the raw materials used, PTA and MEG, are also the same; the technical relationship of raw material to final product is virtually the same; the chemical, petrochemical and polyester industry under investigation is capital intensive, so the capital-to-work ratio is very high and is an industry characterized by strong installed capacity. 81.\u160\'00\'A0He added that the value of a good or service, whether at the cost or price level, is determined by the factors of production, i.e. land, labor and capital, from which there is evidence that the Chinese government interferes. 82.\u160\'00\'A0He pointed out that with respect to the sector or industry under investigation, there is evidence showing that the chemical industry in China does not respond to market signals, due to direct government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . 83.\u160\'00\'A0It argued that based on the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document 2017, of the 20 largest chemical companies in China, 11 are SOEs, including the 6 most important. He added that these companies are directly related along the value chain and petrochemicals, so the origin control of large state conglomerates has undeniable effects on end products, upstream downstream. The 20 largest chemical companies in ChinaNo.Company nameProperty1ChemChinaSOE2Henan Energy Chemical lndustry Group Co. Ltd.SOE3China PingMei Shenma Energy Chemical Co. Ltd.SOE4Tianjin Bohai Chemlcal lndustry Group.\u160\'00\'A0Co. Ltd.SOE5Hubei Yihua Group Co. Ltd.SOE69Yuntianhua Group. Co. Ltd.SOE7Shandong Dongming Petrochemical Group Co.Privada8Shandong Jingbo Holding Co. Ltd.Private9China National Chemical Engineering Co.SOE10Shanghai Huayi GroupSOE11Wengfu (Group) Co. Ltd.SOE12Wanda Holding Co. Ltd.Private13Shanxi Yangmei Chemical Industry Investment Co.\u160\'00\'A0Ltd.SOE14Lihuayi Group Co. Private Ltd.15Jiangying Chengxing Industry Group Co. Private Ltd.16Jiangsu Sanfanggang Group. Co. Private Ltd.17Shandong Haike Chemical Industry Group Co. Private Ltd.18Hengli Petrochemical (Dalian) Co. Private Ltd.19Sinochem International (Holding) Co. Ltd.SOE20Shandong Huatai Group Co. Private Source Ltd. : \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document 2017.84.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, Akra provided pfTT, PTA and MEG production capacity statistics for 2018 and 2019, years corresponding to the period of investigation. The information was obtained from Wood Mackenzie Chemicals Data Extract. Production capacity data is broken down by manufacturer, in volumes per quintile, from 2016 to 2019. 85.\u160\'00\'A0From this data, it noted that:a. \u160\'00\'A0 the PFTT industry and its raw materials consists of a large number of small enterprises, which in 2019 there were 227 companies with the capacity to produce filament, 52 for PTA and 63 for MEG; b. \u160\'00\'A0 there are a high number of companies that do not have the optimal installed capacity size to produce efficiently, sowhich are presumed to be economically unviable facilities, moreover, it should not be overlooked that this industry is capital-intensive and, therefore, the balance point implies a high utilization of installed capacity; c. \u160\'00\'A0 at least three groups on the Henan Energy Chemical Industry Group Co. list. Ltd., Tianjin Bohai Chemical Industry Group Co. Ltd. and Hubei Yihua Group Co. Ltd.), designated as SOEs, have the capacity to produce PTA, MEG, film, polymer, fiber and filament; d. \u160\'00\'A0 there are 10 major polyester filament producers with installed capacity, which on average exceeds one million tons per year; E. \u160\'00\'A0 with respect to the PTA, the main manufacturers with installed capacity were identified, which on average exceeds a volume of 1.5 million tons per year, andf. \u160\'00\'A0 in the case of the MEG, the main producers exceed an installed capacity of 400 thousand tons per year. 86.\u160\'00\'A0It explained that, based on Wood Mackenzie's production capacity data, it proceeded to identify ownership of the main manufacturers. He searched the Internet pages about the profile of those companies. It noted that, out of a total of 16 raw material manufacturers, it identified 5 PTA and 6 MEG producers with a history of state control. 87.\u160\'00\'A0It stated that, on the basis of the information of those undertakings on their websites, it noted that, in some cases, the relationship with the main energy precursors, namely oil, gas and electricity, is indicated. 88.\u160\'00\'A0It found that based on the document called "State Enterprises in the Current Chinese Economy: Paper, Reform and Evolution", from the Institute of China and the \uc2 University of Alberta\uc2  (2018), 3 major oil and derivatives-producing companies (NOCs) are from the National Oil Companies and designated as SOEs: i. \uc2 China National Petroleum Corporation (CNPC); \uc2 ii. Sinopec, China Petroleum and Chemical Corp, and iii. \uc2 China National Offshore Oil Corporation\uc2  (CNOOC). Also, according to the document "China's Oil Industry Structure: Past Trends and Future Prospects," published by the \uc2 University of Oxford\uc2  in May 2016, NOCs, despite the opening reform followed by the Chinese government, are far from independent and there is still intensive state involvement in its structural decisions. 89.\u160\'00\'A0It pointed out that according to the report of the "\uc2 Group of 20\uc2  China 2016", these NOCs, in the extraction of oil and natural gas, represent a control over the inputs that is transferred to the final products. 90.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, Akra presented a diagram prepared by the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  2017 on distortions in the chemical industry in China, which highlights the following:(a). \u160\'00\'A0 there is high installed capacity not used, especially in PTA production; b. \u160\'00\'A0 local governments protect outdated plants and allow them to artificially scale up to prevent them from closing; c. \u160\'00\'A0 the state imposes government control over production capacity when it overeste you,andd. \u160\'00\'A0 the five-year plan establishes policy support measures to ensure the connection between fiscal, financial, trade, and other policies. It plans to expand financing to key companies and projects and continue improving industry and technological transformation work.\u160\'00\'A0It envisages other instruments in the commercial area that have the potential to influence market prices, costs and the structure of the value chain. Financial policy will support the chemical industry. Central and local governments have established a large amount of investment funds to provide priority sectors. 91.\u160\'00\'A0The Applicant added that, on the basis of the Final Resolution on Subsidy Investigation in Exports of Fine Denier Polyester Short Fiber originating in China, published on 16 January 2018, for the main raw materials, PTA and MEG, the USDOC concluded the existence of conflicts by the Chinese government in the following terms:a. \u160\'00\'A0 the Chinese government identified 17 state-owned enterprises among 32 MEG producers. In the case of the PTA, it identified state-owned enterprises that it claims accounted for 29% of the industry in terms of number of companies, andb. \u160\'00\'A0 determined that the government owns or controls all domestic production of MEG and PTA in China, so significant government participation in these markets results in significant distortion of the prices of these raw materials. 92.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the publication of Wood Mackenzie and other sources such as Independent Commodity Intelligence Independent Commodity Inteligence Service (ICIS) and PCI, Akra provided a price comparison for MEG and PTA. Noted that, according to the different sales terms reported in such information sources, China's prices compared to those in other regions such as Asia, North America, Europe, among others, are consistently lower. 93.\u160\'00\'A0Akra stated that, under the following premisses, it is not difficult to conclude that such government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in NOCs distorts the production of the entire petrochemical chain, including the subject merchandise:a. \u160\'00\'A0 the petrochemical industry has two essential characteristics: i. the stochiometric ratio of raw materials to final product is essentially the same worldwide. Therefore, its production is highly related to the availability and costs of raw materials, and ii. it is capital-intensive, so state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially facilitates elements related to this factor of production (e.g., the cost of land income and the cost of financing), and have an effect on the advantage of that production chain versus that of those originating in countries where market rules are met, andb. \u160\'00\'A0 government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the main precursors, as in the case of NOCs, shifts distortions to the entire value chain of the PFTT under study. It added that, as a particular case, SOE, Sinopec Group, made it possible to assume that its costs and selling prices of its products (including the MEG) were not determined by the free interaction of supply and demand. 94.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the arguments set out above, Akra concluded that government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  distorts the cost structure from integrated precursors to gas and oil, to raw materials toend products; mainly because the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in decisions related to the price of productive factors. It therefore emphasizes that industry or industry decisions under research on prices, costs and supply of inputs, including raw materials, technology, production, sales and investment, are not taken in response to market signals and have significant state interference. (d) That foreign investment and co-investment with foreign firms be permitted95. \u160\'00\'A0Akra noted that according to notes published in June 2018 and February 2019 by Xinhuanet and Wong Dorcas, respectively, China announced that it would introduce a "negative list" dividing the Catalogue for the Guidance of Foreign Investment Industries into two parts: the recommended list as a new catalogue and the negative list. In addition, it noted that on March 15, 2019, the Congress of China passed a new Foreign Investment Law that would come into force on January 1, 2020, according to the \uc2 Latham & Watkins\uc2  source, "China introduces new foreing investment law, negative lists, and encouraged industries catalog."\u160\'00\'A0Client alert, 2019. 96.\u160\'00\'A0He argued that while China has indicated that it is in the process of facilitating administrative approval processes, the "Catalogue for the Guidance of Foreign Investment Industries" (the "Catalogue") is still in force. The Catalogue indicates in which sectors foreign investment is encouraged, restricted and prohibited, but also under what conditions investments are allowed.\u160\'00\'A097.\u160\'00\'A0It stated that, in accordance with Appendix 1 of the Catalogue, foreign investment in textile industry, manufacture of chemical raw materials and chemicals and in the manufacture of chemical fiber is encouraged, however, inputs or the production of pet synthetic fibres are not contemplated. 98.\u160\'00\'A0By the contaria, in Appendix 2 of that catalogue, the gas and energy sectors that are precursors of raw materials for the manufacture of PFTT are located in Part I of the Catalogue of Restricted Industries for Foreign Investment. 99.\u160\'00\'A0He argued that China has a history of attracting foreign technology by allowing investment in specific sectors, then preventing more entries once the required technology has been transferred and Chinese industry has become competitive. He added that the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document 2017 concludes that "while the Chinese government is implementing reforms affecting both domestic investment and foreign investment, including an ambitious ongoing effort to unify the country's legal regime under the Single and Comprehensive Foreign Investment Act that codifies recent reform trends, these reforms do not reduce the state's role in managing private investment. On the contrary, they seem to be used as a means to strengthen the hand of the state by making its influence on the economy more specific and more efficient." (e) That the industry under investigation possesses exclusively a set of ledgers that are used for all purposes, and which are audited in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles100. \u160\'00\'A0Akra argued that, in the international context, the notion of accounting standards has appeared in China since the early 1990s. It argued that according to Dezan Shira & Associates' article "Chinese accounting standars: a primer for foreing investors", published in China Briefing (2018), it confirms that, in China,"Accounting systems are comparatively less mature than those of developed economies" and "it is still difficult to understand the financial statements prepared by China-based entities under Chinese accounting standards." 101.\u160\'00\'A0It noted that accounting in China is governed by the Chinese Accounting Standards (CAS), which is based on two standards and, although it has been sought to unify these standards, there are still differences with respect to International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS). 102.\u160\'00\'A0He added that based on an article produced by LehmanBrown "Accounting & bookkeeping in China" (2016), this firm specializing in accounting, China-based business advisory and tax issues, there are no standards or IFRS, or Generally Accepted Accounting Principies (GAAP), however recent reforms have brought its accounting system closer to these principles, the accounting standards followed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not comparable with IFRS. 103.\u160\'00\'A0Abounded, accounting standards or CAS in China prevail over international standards, which shows the possibility of distortion in the financial statements, since the legal framework in that country permits the existence of more than one set of ledgers. According to a Chen article, "Analysis of cause of finanacial fraud and precautions." published in Trade Sciece Inc Journal (2014), the imperfection of the Chinese company's accounting system is evident, in part because "the company usually makes two sets of ledgers, the fake for publication and the true for the company". 104.\u160\'00\'A0He emphasized that Shen Hong and Zhou Wei published in 2019 the note "Chinese auditors are on he hook after clients caught cooking the books" in The Wall Street Journal, which notes that in dozens of Chinese companies their audits were questioned for alleged violation of securities law. Similarly, in the note, Amy Un, a senior Capital Securities analyst based in Shanghai, said that "the cost of breaking the law was too low in China." This results in an incentive to manage ledgers at its discretion. 105.\u160\'00\'A0Akra concluded that there is no certainty in China that a book set exists and that it is audited in accordance with generally accepted accounting principles. (f) That the costs of production and financial situation of the sector or industry under investigation are not distorted in relation to asset depreciation, bad debts, barter trade and debt compensation payments, or other factors deemed relevant106. \u160\'00\'A0Regarding the cost of precursors, Akra argued that based on an article produced by Rioux, Bertrand in May 2019, dubbed "The Economic Impact of Price Controls on China's Natural Gas Supply Chain," assessed economic distortions caused by regulated prices in China's natural gas supply market, and concluded that in highly concentrated, upstream and middle markets, dominant players can undermine the implementation of reform initiatives (such as price reforms) if they outs their interests. 107.\u160\'00\'A0Noted that a report published in ICIS by John Richardson "China purified terephthalic net exports to reach 2.7m tones by 2025" (2019),He argues that from 2009 China launched a major wave of investment in new PTA plants, during the 2009-2017 economic stimulus program. At the time, according to the author, it was not a question of building capabilities based on supply and demand in the long term; In addition, it assumes that by 2018 not all PTA producers were in good business, due to the rapid growth of local competition, but local governments issued debt cancellation decisions allowing them to operate only covering variable costs. The author also noted that "Enhanced spreads indicate that variable costs were more easily covered in 2018 compared to 2017," despite the slowdown. Akra concluded that this is an example of the cost distortions throughout its control of the entire value chain of the relevant industries. 108.\u160\'00\'A0With respect to assets, Akra mentioned that according to the publication "China's Financial Repression: Symptoms, Consequences and Causes", developed by Xu, Guangdong in 2018, investments made by both local government and traditional state-owned enterprises have played a key role in pushing the economy after the impact of the financial crisis, although unfortunately the efficiency of these investment projects is dubious. In general, on financial conditions in China, the author concludes that the Chinese government "needs a repressed financial system that allows it to create and distribute rents to reward its central electorate (such as managers and employees of state-owned enterprises) and buy the support of new emerging forces (such as private entrepreneurs). ... Given the importance of financial resources to the PCCh, it is difficult to imagine that it will eventually adopt a liberalisation strategy and cede its control over the financial system.\u160\'00\'A0From this perspective, as long as the Party can retain power, China may never have a chance to free itself from financialrepression." \u160\'00\'A0Regarding bad debts, Akra noted that the reaction of the government and its financial institutions to the increase in bad debt problems in recent years has been to expire, transferring debt and providing debt remissions or restructuring to avoid defaults.\u160\'00\'A0He stated that a measurable effect is so-called permanent credit, where lenders essentially transfer loan maturities or provide loans simply to pay off old debts. 110.\u160\'00\'A0\uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  measured it by calculating what companies owe each year in terms of capital payments and interest expenses. It then evaluates the resources to make these payments, such as cash flow, equity, and excess cash. He added that Frangos Alex's note "How China's Big Loan Boost Falls Short," published by The Wall Street Journal in February 2016, notes that the result is a massive deficit in debt service compared to cash sources, and that gap is filled with more loans. 111.\u160\'00\'A0It stated that according to the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document 2017, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  analysed the degree of corporate debt perennization in China that has increased since 2010 and is more prevalent in the overcapacity sectors, as would be the case in the chemical industry. The bank estimates that these companies have been increasing leverage despite falling profitability. 112.\u160\'00\'A0Noted that in May 2017, in Wildau's note, G. "Regulator urges Chinese banksto save sick companies," he reported that perennial practices were confirmed during a press conference at which the director of the local office of \uc2 China's Banking Regulatory Commission\uc2  noted that his agency had coordinated with creditors to transfer loans to coal and steel companies that cannot afford, but is considered likely to regain health, proving of the PCCh's failed promise to shut down zombie companies. 113.\u160\'00\'A0He clarified that zombie companies record losses or fail to meet their interest repayment obligations, and still obtain loans. A situation outside a market economy, in which debt conditions of this type would force companies to improve their efficiency or exit the market, not to remain sponsored by loans outside competitive schemes. 114.\u160\'00\'A0Emphasized that throughout the Study it was demonstrated that there is direct government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the pricing of capital, labour, land, raw materials and basic inputs related to the chemical, petrochemical and subject merchandise. It concluded that, although end-product prices might be determined by supply and demand in an open market, the reality is that price distortions along the value chain create incorrect price signals that do not reflect reality in the industry and distort the profit or loss balances of all economic operators involved. The above, as established by Markus Taube (2017), in his report "Analysis of Market Distortions in the Chinese Non-Ferrous Metals Industry". ii. Determination115. \u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat conducted a comprehensive analysis of the arguments and evidence provided in this investigation in the administrative record. In principle, the Secretariat notes that, pursuant to paragraph 15(d) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, only Subparagraph (a) of Romaite (ii) expired in December 2016. However, paragraph 15(a) and Romaite (i) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO remain as a current text. Subparagraph (a) provides for the possibility of applying a price or cost-based methodology in China, Chinese producers, or a methodology that is not based on those prices or costs. Thus, the Secretariat considers that the single expiry of the validity of paragraph 15 (a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO does not mean that there has been no longer the possibility of using a methodology that is not based on a strict comparison with domestic prices or costs in China. 116.\u160\'00\'A0Indeed, the methodological basis for determining price comparability in anti-dumping proceedings investigating products of Chinese origin are expressly contained, in principle, in paragraph 15(a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, as well as, like Romanite (i), has not expired. Pursuant to subparagraph (a), there is a legal possibility to use the prices or costs of the chinese producers investigated, in China, or to employ a methodology that is not based on a strict comparison with the prices or costs in that country. 117.\u160\'00\'A0Accordingly, the Secretariat considers that there was a legal basis for assessing Akra's proposal to consider China as a non-market economy in the manufacture, production and sale of PFTT and to analyze the provenance of applying a methodology that is not based on a strict price comparison orcosts in China, for the following reasons:a. \u160\'00\'A0 the Secretariat confirmed that, at the macroeconomic level, there were distortions in variables such as foreign investment, exchange rate, wages and land:(i). \u160\'00\'A0 warns that according to the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document 2017, while the Chinese government has implemented reforms around a single and comprehensive foreign investment law, these reforms do not reduce the role of the state in managing private investment. On the contrary, they are used as a means to strengthen state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by making its influence on the economy more specific and more efficient. With regard to inputs, it is noted that in the gas and energy sectors that are precursors of raw materials for the manufacture of PFTT, they are restricted to foreign investment; ii. \u160\'00\'A0 the exchange rate policy established by the government, while progressing in convertibility of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the international \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the WTO indicates that the government continues to participate in exchange rate formation. The Chinese authority itself has acknowledged that it maintains control over the exchange rate which results in facilitating the undervaluation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; iii. \u160\'00\'A0 the labour market by being subject to government controls prevents wages from being established through free negotiation between employees and employers, and(iv). \u160\'00\'A0 according to article 10 of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Constitution, land is owned by the state. State \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in only one of the factors of production results in direct distortions in the production of final goods. b. \u160\'00\'A0 this makes it possible to presume that active \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Chinese government may lead to distortions that come from government policies, regulatory provisions or direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that selectively discriminate between companies owned by it, which have an impact on the formation of prices and costs of the factors of production in which the manufacture of PFTT is intensive, either by cracking the costs of capital and energy or the absence of markets in land ownership; c. \u160\'00\'A0 consequently, the support that in China and, specifically, in the PFTT production industry, cost and price structures prevail that are not determined according to market principles, is subject to the analysis of the arguments and evidence that Akra provided to support it, of which the Secretariat noted:(i). \u160\'00\'A0 of the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  document 2017, which the Five-Year Plan number 13 on Petrochemical and Chemical Industries (2016-2020), provides for instruments to accompany in the commercial area, which have the potential to influence the market, prices, costs and structures of the value chain, where financial policy supports the chemical industry;\u160\'00\'A0ii. \u160\'00\'A0 of the revision of that document, identified in the list of the 20 largest chemical companies in China that more than half of them are state-owned; iii. \u160\'00\'A0 based on the statistics of installed capacities to produce PFTT, PTA and MEG, published by Wood Mackenzie, of the 20 companies listed above, 3 state-owned industrial groups were identified, which are involved along the value chain in the production of PTA, MEG and PFTT. This may confer artificial competitive advantages, costs and prices would appear to be controlled by theStatus, which makes it possible to assume that the PFTT value chain could be distorted; iv. \u160\'00\'A0 the companies identified in the previous point are companies that Akra identified in its request for investigation as investigated exporters; v. \u160\'00\'A0 it also noted that major oil and natural gas production companies identified as SOEs maintain control over the prices and supply of the main inputs that can transfer them to the value chain in PFTT production.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat noted that in the case of CNPC, it maintained a monopoly on gas production in China, which accounts for 71% of China's total production; I saw. \u160\'00\'A0 In addition, on 3 May 2019, the USDOC's preliminary ruling on subsidies number C570-098 against China's imports of the same product, where support from the Chinese government was identified, such as: (i) preferential loans and interest rates, (ii). tax-cut income, and (iii). import tax reduction, among others, resulting in a more advantageous market position, andvii. \u160\'00\'A0 the Secretariat identified in THE SIC-M statistics, during the period of investigation, 16 exporting companies that the USDOC listed in its investigation for PFTT subsidies against China and which AKRA also noted in its request for this investigation. This informs the presumption that the prices and costs of Chinese PFTT may be distorted, because as there are public policies or direct government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as subsidies, product price and cost formation is passed on. 118.\u160\'00\'A0On the basis of the above, the Secretariat considers that the information provided by domestic production raises the presumption that, in the production, manufacture and sale of PFTT in China, cost and price structures prevail that are not determined according to market principles, because there were market distortions of factors affecting resource allocation in the production of the PFTT and interfere with the determination of costs and prices of the production factors in which it is intensive. 119.\u160\'00\'A0Having identified state-owned companies producing PFTT and its raw materials, it pays the presumption that prices of the good could be controlled by the State, which makes it possible to assume that the costs and selling prices are not determined by the free interaction of supply and demand. 120.\u160\'00\'A0On the basis of the above and pursuant to paragraph 15(a) of China's Protocol of Accession to the WTO, the Secretariat analyzed a methodology that was not based on a strict comparison with prices or costs in China for PFTT. For this purpose, Akra proposed to use a substitute country of China for the calculation of normal value, in accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the ERC. b. Selection of the substitute country121. \u160\'00\'A0The Applicant proposed the United States as the market-economy country that meets the characteristics to be used as reasonable for the purpose of determining normal value in this investigation. 122.\u160\'00\'A0To demonstrate that the United States is operating under the principles of a market economy, Akra argued that based on the United States Trade Policy Review (WT/TPR/S/382/Rev.1) of 27 March 2019, it is established that there are no federal policies directed atspecifically in the manufacturing sector and that the main objective of the policy is to create a framework in which market forces can operate in an equitable and competitive environment. With respect to foreign investment, it is indicated that the United States maintains an open regime, with some sectoral constraints. 123.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, based on information it obtained from the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.theusaonline.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.theusaonline.com}}}\uc2 , it indicated that, in the United States economic system, it refers to laws and institutions such as those of a nation that determines who owns the economic resources, how those resources are purchased and sold, and how the production process makes use of resources to provide goods and services. In addition, the U.S. economy is made up of business and labor organizations, and social institutions. People function as consumers, workers, savers and investors. 124.\u160\'00\'A0Trade unions, which represent some workers in collective bargaining with employers, are another important type of economic organization. So are cooperatives, organizations made up of producers or consumers who come together to share resources. In addition, individual producers and consumers determine the types of goods and services produced, as well as their prices. 125.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat also found that the country's report prepared by HSBC Global Connections indicated that the United States is a market-oriented economy and that the dollar in HSBC Global Connections remains one of the strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world, and the most popular \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of reserves.\u160\'00\'A0This report also confirms that most businesses enjoy flexibility in decisions to expand their capital, labor decisions and new product development, as well as an openness to foreign direct investment, and the federal government accords equal treatment to both foreign and domestic investment. 126.\u160\'00\'A0In order to select the United States as a substitute country, Akra based its focus on effective competition in the PFTT industry, according to which at least four necessary conditions must apply: (i) five comparable bidders who can exert mutual pressure and avoid implicit or explicit coordination between them; (ii) absence of a dominant signature; iii. entry and exit barriers must be low, and iv. consumers should be able to switch suppliers without significant costs. 127.\u160\'00\'A0It noted that in assessing the INDICATOR indicators of the PFTT in the United States and its environment, it was reiterated that the product is the same as that manufactured in China. Based on a comparative analysis between the United States and China on various economic variables of the PFTT industry, such as installed capacity, production, imports, exports, which it obtained from Wood Mackenzie and Trade statistics for international business development (ITC-Trade Map) for 2018, it reached the following conclusions:a. \u160\'00\'A0 there are a large number of PFTT manufacturers in both countries; b. \u160\'00\'A0 in the United States, there is no large company that could be considered dominant and that could influence pricing. There are no technical or other standards that discriminate against the demand for the product manufactured by one company to the detriment of another; c. \u160\'00\'A0 there are no barriers to entry into this industry in the United States that could limit competition. The country is one of the most open economies. Its tariffimport is only 4.3% on average for the NW-FC subheading, allowing 65 thousand tonnes to be imported in 2016. There is enough raw material to manufacture the quantities that are required of the product in question, andd. \u160\'00\'A0 The United States has considerable domestic demand, and like China, its domestic production would basically be sufficient to meet it. 128.\u160\'00\'A0It noted that, it follows from the foregoing that in the United States the prices of THE PFTT are determined under effective competition, and therefore considers it to be the substitute country for the purpose of this investigation. i. \u160\'00\'A0Similarity in the production process129. \u160\'00\'A0Akra explained that PFTT is a petrochemical commodity that is manufactured from a globally standard technology and is a capital-intensive industry. 130.\u160\'00\'A0He pointed out that PFTT is manufactured in a polymerization reactor, whose raw materials are the PTA and MEG. Liquid polyester can be spun directly or into a solid state to be then spun into extruders by pressure and temperature. The spinning produces POY, which can have different characteristics such as thickness and filaments depending on the spreas used. The POY moves to texturing machines, which give the final finish to the polyester filament by stretching, temperature and textured. 131.\u160\'00\'A0It added that, according to the USITC, in its anti-dumping investigation on PFTT originating in China and India, in its document no. 4858, it concluded that the production process is very similar for all producers, regardless of countries, the same raw materials, as well as the same inputs, are used. This is: i. they are manufactured from PET, manufactured from PTA and MEG, directly or from chips or granules; ii. PET melts at very high temperatures to be extruded and obtain partially oriented yarn, POY, and iii. POY goes through a process of stretching and textured, and sometimes dyeing. 132.\u160\'00\'A0With regard to the cost structure for manufacturing PFTT that Akra provided, he explained that both in China, the United States and India, it is similar, since PFTT is manufactured through production processes and similar technologies; it is a capital-intensive industry, regardless of the country of production, which would hardly allow the cost structure to vary significantly between countries, which reasonably proves that the production costs in both markets should be similar. He clarified that the level of efficiency is very similar in companies worldwide even though the inefficient ones tend to go out of the market, being a very competitive market, therefore the cost structure must also be very similar. ii. \u160\'00\'A0The United States is a producer of PFTT133. \u160\'00\'A0It provided data on the volume of production, capacity and consumption, as well as the number of producers in the United States and China in 2018 and 2019, among other economic indicators of PFTT. The information was obtained from Wood Mackenzie and the United Nations Commodity Trade Statistics Database (the "UN Comtrade"). iii. \u160\'00\'A0Availability of the main raw materials and inputs134. \u160\'00\'A0It stated that the United States, like China, had the main raw materials for the production of PFTT. Provided data on PTA and MEG production, capacity and consumption, in the United States and China, for 2018 and 2019, which it obtained from Wood Mackenzie Chemicals DataExtract. 135.\u160\'00\'A0He explained that both countries are large producers of oil and gas, which are the precursors of the RAW material of PFTT. He provided data for both countries in 2018, which he obtained from the U.S. Central Intelligence Agency (CIA) published on The World FactBook. 136.\u160\'00\'A0It pointed out that the data show that both China and the United States produce PFTT, have installed capacity and consume PFTT, export and import PFTT, use the same raw materials, PTA and MEG, demonstrating that the United States is a reasonable substitute for China. iv. \u160\'00\'A0Other elements137. \u160\'00\'A0Akra stated that there are no investigations or measures for dumping or countervailing duties by other countries against the United States over its PFTT exports, the above, based on the information on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://enforcement.trade.gov/trcs/foreignadcvd/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://enforcement.trade.gov/trcs/foreignadcvd/index.html}}}\uc2  website, which lists US products that are subject to anti-dumping or countervailing measures. This information comes from the WTO's semi-annual reports as of August 2019, concluded that no countervailing quotas are applied, nor are us-led investigation proceedings against US PFTT underway. v.\u160\'00\'A0Determination138. \u160\'00\'A0The third paragraph of Article 48 of the RLCE states that a substitute country shall mean a third country with a market economy similar to the exporting country with a non-market economy. The similarity between the substitute country and the exporting country shall be reasonably defined, so that normal value in the exporting country can be approximated on the basis of the domestic price in the substitute country, taking into account economic criteria. 139.\u160\'00\'A0In order to comply with this provision, the Secretariat conducted a comprehensive analysis of the information provided by Akra to consider the United States as a substitute country for China. It found that both countries are the main producers and consumers of PFTT, have input availability and similarity in production processes. In this way, it can be reasonably inferred that the intensity in the use of production factors is similar in both countries. 140.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the analysis described in the previous points of this Resolution, and in accordance with Articles 33 of the LCE and 48 of the RLCE, the Secretariat confirmed the selection of the United States as China's substitute market economy country for the purposes of calculating normal value. c. Domestic prices in the United States141. \u160\'00\'A0Akra provided prices in the U.S. domestic market, based on information from the international specialized publication Fibres Global Montly Market Overview of the international consultancy Wood Mackenzie, for each of the months covering the period investigated, corresponding to the PFTT. 142.\u160\'00\'A0Prices are expressed in cents per pound, are at the level of sale delivered to average consumers in the U.S. market, and are net prices for discounts, bonuses, and refunds. It got the average price from the maximum and low prices in the period investigated and eventually converted it to dollars per kilogram.\u160\'00\'A0143.\u160\'00\'A0The Applicant argued that reported prices are a reasonable basis for normal value calculation in the United States, since they come from a publication that has a recognized global prestige, which provides an overview and detailed picture of theglobal textile industry, including prices for raw materials and fibres; price trends for filaments and polyester fibers; as well as economic, commercial, production and price data, and recent market developments in major raw materials for polyester fibers, such as PTA and MEG. 144.\u160\'00\'A0It explained that prices come from consultations made by PFTT producers, suppliers and buyers in the United States, and that, apart from being a relevant, valid and reliable reference, it is the best available information to demonstrate sufficient evidence of the existence of the practice of discrimination at this stage of the proceeding. 145.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat reviewed and accepted the information provided by the Applicant to calculate the price at which the PFTT is sold for consumption in the domestic market in the United States, on the basis of Article 33 of the LCE. The Secretariat calculated the average normal value of PFTT in dollars per kilogram for the period investigated. i. \u160\'00\'A0Normal value adjustments in United States146. \u160\'00\'A0Akra proposed adjusting the normal value by terms and conditions of sale, in particular for land freight from the plant to where customers are located. 147.\u160\'00\'A0To establish the adjustment, Akra considered the information from the anti-dumping investigation on imports of PFTT originating in China and India conducted in the United States. It clarified that the proposed adjustment is applicable, since the US investigating authority obtained the data from the information provided by the US producers. 148.\u160\'00\'A0It explained that the cost of transport fluctuates between 1 and 4% of the value of the goods, which it assumes represents the difference in distances for delivery of the product. He pointed out that he conservatively applied the highest rate. 149.\u160\'00\'A0Added that the main producers of PFTT in the United States (Miliken & Company, Nan Ya Plastics Corp. America, O'Mara Incorporated; Sapona Manufacturing Co. Inc. and Unifi Manufacturing, Inc.), are located in the same geographic area, North and South Carolina. It justified that, by concentrating those producers in the same geographical area, the adjustment would apply to all of them. 150.\u160\'00\'A0It clarified that on the basis of Article 5.2 and 5.3 of the AD Agreement, it was the information reasonably available to it. ii. \u160\'00\'A0Determination151. \u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat considered the information and methodology that Akra provided to adjust the normal domestic freight value, and applied it on the basis of Articles 2.4 of the AD Agreement, 36 of the LCE, and 53 and 54 of the ERCE. d. India152. \u160\'00\'A0Akra provided price references in the Indian domestic market that it obtained from various companies on the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.alibaba.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.alibaba.com}}}\uc2 . He also noted that Alibaba.com is a leading platform in global wholesale trade, created in 1999 ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://activities.alibaba.com/alibaba/following-about-alibaba.php?spm=a2700.8293689.0.0.2ce267afU7XGdj&tracelog=footer_alibaba"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://activities.alibaba.com/alibaba/following-about-alibaba.php?spm=a2700.8293689.0.0.2ce267afU7XGdj&tracelog=footer_alibaba}}}\uc2 ), offering hundreds of millions of products in more than 40 different categories, including electronics, machinery and clothing, both from finished products and their inputs. This platform reaches buyers and sellers in more than 190 countries and regions, whose main suppliers are located on the Asian continent and, among them, highlights India. 153.\u160\'00\'A0Developed a list of PFTT prices,manufacturers that offer and quote the product on that website, based on the following methodology:a. \u160\'00\'A0 provided the profiles of the companies for which it provided prices including information on plant size, payment terms, delivery times, as well as the type of merchandise it offers, among other information; b. \u160\'00\'A0 considered various product characteristics, such as density, yarn thickness, filaments, layers, finish, color, dyeing method, texturing method and packaging. It argued that those peculiarities are consistent in accordance with the description of the subject merchandise; c. \u160\'00\'A0 the price list includes minimum and maximum prices, terms of sale, supplier, type of company, whether producer or producer/marketer; d. \u160\'00\'A0 got the average price from the lows and highs in dollars per kilogram, and e. \u160\'00\'A0 prices are at the FOB (Free On Board) commercial level. It clarified that there was no need to apply adjustments, because under Article 2.4 of the AD Agreement, when comparing with the export price both prices are at the same commercial level. 154.\u160\'00\'A0Because price references were consulted on 3 January 2020, they are outside the period investigated. Akra provided the data and methodology to deflate them to the period investigated. It contributed the Consumer Price Index in India that it obtained from Trading Economics, on the website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.tradingeconomics.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.tradingeconomics.com}}}\uc2 , until November 2019. He clarified that it was the most recent information that was available. However, in corroborating this information, it noted that the index was available until December 2019 and considered it to implement the adjustment. The Secretariat adjusted the normal value for inflation in accordance with Article 58 of the ERCE. 155.\u160\'00\'A0Akra clarified that the use of price references obtained from the Alibaba website is acceptable, in accordance with the standard for initiating an anti-dumping investigation, in addition to the best available information that it reasonably had available, as provided for in Articles 5.2 and 5.3 of the AD Agreement. 156.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat replicated Akra's methodology for calculating PFTT prices in India and accepted its proposal, on the basis that it is a reasonable basis for determining normal value, since prices are for producers and producers/marketers, and relate to prices for domestic consumption in India. 157.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, Akra provided information and evidence for the calculation of the reconstructed value of PFTT in the Indian market. Based on Akra's cost structure. It provided available information on international costs and prices of raw materials and inputs. Statistical information and prices were obtained from GlobalPetrolPrices.com, the Ministry of Oil and Natural Gas of the Indian government and Tecnon OrbiChem Ltd ("Tecnon OrbiChem"). 158.\u160\'00\'A0Information on general and administrative selling expenses, as well as profit, was obtained from two PFTT producers in India, based on their financial statements. 159.\u160\'00\'A0It noted that the reconstruction of normal value was developed by the consulting company Compa\u241\'00\'F1\u237\'00\'EDa Promotora de Negocios Internacionales, S.A. de C.V.with more than 45 years of experience in the synthetic fiber industry and its raw materials. It stated that this information was reasonably available to it. i. Determination160. \u160\'00\'A0On the basis of Article 31 of the LCE, the Secretariat considered the domestic selling prices of India obtained from Alibaba as an adequate basis for determining normal value, and also calculated the average normal value in dollars per kilogram for the period investigated. 3. Price discrimination margin161. \u160\'00\'A0In accordance with the above analysis and based on Articles 2.1 of the AD Agreement and 30 of the LCE, the Secretariat compared the normal value with the export price and determined that there was sufficient evidence, based on positive evidence, to presume that, during the period investigated, imports of PFTT originating in China and India were made under alleged conditions of price discrimination with margins higher than the de minimis. G. Damage and Causation Analysis162. \u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat analysed the arguments and evidence presented by Akra, with the aim of determining sufficient evidence to support the existence of imports of PFTT originating in China and India under alleged dumping conditions causing material injury to the domestic industry of the like product. This assessment, inter alia, includes an examination of:a. the volume of imports under alleged price dumping conditions, their price and their effect on domestic prices of the like domestic product, and(b) the impact of the volume and price of such imports on the economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry of the like product. 163.\u160\'00\'A0The analysis of the economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry corresponds to the information provided by Akra, since Akra constitutes the domestic industry of the product similar to the one under investigation, as determined in point 173 of this Resolution. 164.\u160\'00\'A0To that end, the Secretariat considered data for the periods July 2016-June 2017, July 2017-June 2018 and July 2018-June 2019, which constitute the period analyzed and include that investigated for the dumping analysis. Unless otherwise indicated, the behaviour of economic and financial indicators in a given year or period is analysed against the comparable previous immediate. 1. Similarity of product165. \u160\'00\'A0Pursuant to Articles 2.6 of the AD Agreement and 37 ii of the RLCE, the Secretariat assessed the information and evidence that Akra provided to determine whether the domestically manufactured PFTT is similar to the product under investigation. 166.\u160\'00\'A0Akra indicated that domestically manufactured PFTT and that imported from China and India under price discrimination are similar in terms of applicable law, since those products are considered commodities and have the same uses and functions, in addition to presenting the same consumer destination. 167.\u160\'00\'A0In support of the above, in addition to the information referred to in the section on the investigated product, Akra submitted: i. information from its website that observes both the characteristics of the goods it manufactures and their uses; ii. a description of its production process and inputsused in the manufacture of PFTT certified in the specialized opinion in the textile field referred to in point 14 of this Resolution; (iii) a comparative table between the physical and chemical characteristics, uses, consumers, customers and production processes and inputs used in the manufacture of the PFTT, relating to both the subject merchandise and the goods manufactured in Mexico; (iv) the list of imports of the SAT, corresponding to tiGIE tariff fraction 5402.33.01, for the months covered by the period analyzed, which was provided to it by ANIQ; v. a list of their customers to whom they sold PFTT in the period analyzed, and I saw. a table identifying the names of the customers and countries from which they acquired imported PFTT. 168.\u160\'00\'A0In this regard, the Secretariat noted the following:a. \u160\'00\'A0 as far as the comparative analysis could be made, based on the information in the administrative record, the Secretariat noted that for both the PFTT under investigation and the nationally manufactured PFTT, the main physical and chemical characteristics are decitex or denier, the number of filaments, luster and the level of braiding; b. \u160\'00\'A0 regarding the comparative analysis between the production processes and inputs used in the production of the PFTT, the Secretariat noted that Akra's production process and the inputs used therein were similar to those of the subject merchandise; c. \u160\'00\'A0 with respect to uses and functions, based on the information in the administrative file, the Secretariat noted that they were the same for both goods, andd. \u160\'00\'A0 as far as the existence of common customers, Based on Akra's customer sales information and the list of siC M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Secretariat noted that fifteen customers of that company also made imports of PFTT originating in the two countries investigated during the period analyzed, so that, in addition to the previous subparagraph, there are indications that they reach the same markets and serve the same type of consumers, allowing them to be commercially interchangeable. 169.\u160\'00\'A0As described in the previous points of this Resolution, the Secretariat had sufficient evidence to initially determine that the domestically manufactured PFTT is similar to the product under investigation, because they have similar physical characteristics, they are manufactured with the same inputs and through production processes that do not show substantial differences, they also serve the same markets and consumers, allowing them to fulfil the same functions and be commercially interchangeable, so that they can be considered similar, in terms of Articles 2.6 of the AD Agreement and 37 fraction II of the RLCE. 2. Domestic production and representativenessbranch 170. \u160\'00\'A0Pursuant to Articles 4.1 and 5.4 of the AD Agreement, 40 and 50 of the LCE and 60, 61 and 62 of the ERCE, the Secretariat identified the domestic industry of the like product as the one investigated, as a significant proportion of the total domestic production of PFTT, taking into account whether the manufacturing companies are importers of the product under investigation or if there are elements to be presumed to be linked to undertakings importing or exporting PFTT. 171.\u160\'00\'A0Akra noted that it is the most important PFTT manufacturer in Mexico by representing more than 90%of total domestic production and supported by the rest of the domestic production, in addition to not being linked to any importer or exporter of the subject merchandise and that, although it made imports of PFTT during the period analyzed, these were not significant, since those originating in China corresponded to specialties, while those from origins other than the countries investigated were due to returns of goods exported by Akra. To support its arguments, it submitted: i. an ANIQ letter recognizing the Applicant as a producer of PFTT; (ii) a letter of support from the other national producer Antextextil, S.A. de C.V.; (iii) an estimate of the domestic production of PFTT based on the production levels of the Akra and Antextextil companies carried out in the period analysed; iv. the figures of their imports of PFTT made in the period analyzed, and v. a copy of a pediment of an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  relating to the goods it imported. 172.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the information in the administrative file, including the information submitted by Akra and the figures obtained from the list of SIC-M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  relating to imports made through TIGIE tariff fraction 5402.33.01, the Secretariat noted:a. \u160\'00\'A0 Akra production accounts for a significant proportion of domestic production (more than 90%). This, taking into account both the information submitted by ANIQ at the request of the Secretariat, and the estimate submitted by Akra, which was considered to be appropriate and relevant, since they start from the information observed in the period analyzed and contemplate the average production reported by Antextextil), andb. \u160\'00\'A0 while it was noted that Akra imported during the period analyzed through TIGIE tariff fraction 5402.33.01, these were not significant, as those for PFTT originating in China accounted for 0.05% of the subject imports made in the period analyzed; while imports from different sources to China and India accounted for 0.4% of imports from different sources to China and India, made throughout the period analyzed. 173.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the results described above, the Secretariat initially determined that Akra constitutes the domestic industry of PFTT, since in the period investigated it produced 91% of the total domestic production of that product, in accordance with Articles 4.1 and 5.4 of the LCE Anti-Dumping Agreement, 40 and 50 and 60, 61 and 62 of the RLCE, it is also supported by the domestic producer Antextextil, so, overall, the application is supported by 100% of total domestic production. In addition, the Secretariat did not have evidence to indicate that Akra is linked to exporters or importers of the subject merchandise or that its imports were the cause of the distortion of domestic prices or the alleged injury. 3. International market174. \u160\'00\'A0With respect to the international market, the Applicant noted that the consumption of PFTT does not respond to economic cycles, and stated that:a. \u160\'00\'A0 According to data obtained from the consultancy Wood Mackenzie for 2019, China was the main producer of PFTT and accounted for 75% of the world's production, followed by India with 9%, while the rest of the supply was pulverized between several countries, within which Mexico occupied the 15th site; b. \u160\'00\'A0 agreeing with the"Global Polyester Fibers & Feedstock -SupplementSupply/Demand Issue 102" report from August 2019, the world's main PFTT consumer regions, for the period investigated, were Northeast and Southeast Asia, while the main consumer countries of pfTT, Indonesia, South Korea, Taiwan, Thailand, The United States, Japan and Malaysia, andc. \u160\'00\'A0 according to international trade centre (ITC) data, by 2018, Akra noted that: (i) China and India were the world's leading exporters of PFTT by making 47% and 23% of the total volume exported, respectively; ii. imports of PFTT are less concentrated than the production and exports of PFTT products, since, for example, Turkey (the main importer) accounted for only 13% of the total volume of PFTT products, and (iii). with positive balances of China and India by 1,035 thousand and 511 thousand tonnes, respectively, were net exporters. 175.\u160\'00\'A0To support the above, Akra submitted tables with volumes and shares with figures from 2018, from major producers, whose source is the consultancy Wood Mackenzie, imports, exports and trade balance obtained from ITC, as well as tables with figures of: i. supply/demand of PFTT worldwide, by region and country from 2016 to 2019, whose source is Wood Mackenzie); ii. demand for PFTT by regions and countries with quarterly figures for 2018 and estimated for 2019 and 2020, obtained from IHS;\u160\'00\'A0iii. export and import volumes for the years 2016 to 2018, as well as the 2018 trade balance by country (information relating to subheading 5402.33 obtained from ITC), and (iv). Monthly average PFTT prices in China obtained from Wood Mackenzie, as the Applicant indicated that there is no price information for all countries or regions of the world in which the product is produced or marketed. 176.\u160\'00\'A0On the other hand, the Secretariat received quarterly FIGUREs from ITC-Trade Map on world export and import statistics by country for the period analyzed for tariff subheading 5402.33. On the basis of that information and that indicated in the previous point, the Secretariat noted the following:a. \u160\'00\'A0 the main exporters in the period analyzed were the same as in the researched period, during which time China (56%), India (19%), Taipei (7%), Thailand (4%) Malaysia (3%) contributed 88% of total exports. In this regard, Mexico ranked 13th with less than 1% of the total; b. \u160\'00\'A0 the main importers in the period analyzed were the same as in the research, during which time Turkey (18%), Brazil (16%), Korea (8%), Italy (4%) United States (4%) 50 per cent of total imports. For its part, Mexico ranked 11th with 3% of the world total, andc. \u160\'00\'A0 PFTT prices in China performed increasingly in the period analyzed when there was 12% growth, while in the period investigated they fell by 2%. 4. Domestic market177. \u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat assessed the conduct of the domestic market on the basis of the information in the administrative record, including the domestic production, domestic sales and exports figures submitted by Akra, both own and estimated, and the figures reached by the Secretariat, relating to imports made through tariff fraction 5402.33.01 of THE TIGIE obtained from the list of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operationsSIC\b0 \highlight\uc2 -M for the period under review, which were purged by the Secretariat, as noted in points 186 and 187 of this Resolution. 178.\u160\'00\'A0Due to the point of point 171 of this Resolution, in relation to the existence of another national producer of PFTT (Antextextil) during the period analysed, Akra calculated the national production volumes of PFTT for the period analysed, pre-estimating the volumes of Antextextil's production figures, from its average production. In this regard, as noted in point 172 of this Resolution, the Secretariat considered Akra's estimates as the best available information at this stage of the investigation, concerning the national production figures of PFTT, since the estimates submitted by Akra were adequate and relevant, since they were based on the information observed in the period analyzed and correspond specifically to Antextextil. 179.\u160\'00\'A0Considering the information set out in the previous point, the Secretariat noted that The Apparent National Consumption (NAC), measured as Domestic Domestic Production Oriented to the Internal Market (PNOMI) plus imports, the national supply of PFTT decreased by 3% from the period July 2016-June 2017 to the period of July 2017-June 2018 and increased by 1% in the period investigated, accumulating a 2% drop in the period analyzed. 180.\u160\'00\'A0For its part, the total imported volume of PFTT decreased by 4% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and increased by 7% in the period investigated, accumulating an increase of 2% in the period analyzed.\u160\'00\'A0Also, during the period analyzed, total imports of PFTT were from 44 countries, the main origins of PFTT imports were China (61%) (18%), as well as the United States (4%), Italy, Indonesia and Malaysia (3% each), which together concentrated 92% of total imports. 181.\u160\'00\'A0With regard to the volume of domestic production of PFTT, PFTT increased by 2% in the period July 2017-June 2018, but decreased by 10% in the period investigated, accumulating a fall of 7% in the period analyzed. In addition, PNOMI behaved similarly by accumulating a 15% drop in the period analyzed, due to the period being virtually constant in the period July 2017-June 2018 and decreased by 15% in the period investigated.\u160\'00\'A0182.\u160\'00\'A0Exports increased by 6% in the period July 2017-June 2018, but in the period investigated they decreased by 2%, which cumulatively meant growth of 4% in the period analyzed. 183.\u160\'00\'A0Akra indicated that PFTT is distributed and marketed throughout the country, in addition to being consumed mainly by fabric, tape or rope manufacturers, clothing designers, textile suppliers of the automotive industry, resellers/distributors. It added that PFTT consumption does not respond to economic cycles or have any seasonal sales patterns. 5. Import Analysis184. \u160\'00\'A0In accordance with Articles 3.1, 3.2 and 3.3 of the AD Agreement, 41 LCE fraction I and 43, and 64 fraction I and 67 of the ERC, the Secretariat assessed the performance and trend of imports of the product under investigation during the period under investigation, both in absolute terms and in relation to domestic production or consumption. a. Imports under analysis185. \u160\'00\'A0The Applicant indicated that the tariff fraction for which the PFTT enters is specific, sothat all transactions should correspond to that good, however, Akra identified that through it they entered products other than the PFTT. As a support for the foregoing, it submitted the list of imports of the SAT, corresponding to tariff fraction 5402.33.01 of the TIGIE, for the months covered by the period analyzed, which was provided to it by ANIQ. 186.\u160\'00\'A0Because, through TIGIE tariff fraction 5402.33.01, products other than the subject matter of investigation were entered over the period under review, the Applicant submitted the purification criteria referred to in point 25 of this Resolution. 187.\u160\'00\'A0For its part, the Secretariat analyzed the methodology submitted by the Applicant and considered it reasonable, so it calculated the value and volume of imports of PFTT using that methodology, based on the import base of TIGIE tariff fraction 5402.33.01 obtained from the SIC-M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  list. In contrasting this information with that submitted by Akra, no significant differences were observed; however, for the analysis of this paragraph, it was determined to use the figures obtained from the list of siC-M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , for the reasons set out in point 28 of this Resolution. b. Import accumulation188. \u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat assessed whether the effects of imports of PFTT originating in China and India should be accumulated for the analysis of material injury to the domestic industry. In this regard, it analysed whether the dumping margins of those imports were greater than those considered de minimis and whether their volumes were not negligible, as well as their effects in the light of the conditions of competition between them and the domestically manufactured product. In this regard, the Secretariat noted the following:a. \u160\'00\'A0 in accordance with the price discrimination analysis described in this Resolution, the Secretariat had evidence of dumping margins greater than de minimis for imports of PFTT originating in China and India during the period investigated; b. \u160\'00\'A0 imports originating in China and India were significant, accounting for 61% and 18% of total IMPORTS of PFTT, respectively, during the period analyzed.\u160\'00\'A0Similarly, during the period investigated, such imports accounted for 66% and 16% of total imports of PFTT, respectively, and(c). \u160\'00\'A0 based on the information in the administrative file, concerning Akra's list of PFTT sales to its customers who in turn purchased goods imported from the investigated countries, in addition to the import figures obtained from the SIC-M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  list, the Secretariat noted that: i. there were import companies that during the period under review acquired product from the investigated countries interchangeably, and ii. as noted in point 168 of this Resolution, it was also noted that common customers purchased investigated goods originating in China and India, and domestic; reflecting a reasonable degree of competition and interchangeability between products originating in China and India, and domestically produced. 189.\u160\'00\'A0From the results described in the previous point of this Resolution, the Secretariat considered it appropriate to accumulate imports of PFTT originating in China and India for the purposes of the material injury analysis to the domestic industry that produces the goodssimilar, as the investigated imports were made with dumping margins higher than de minimis; import volumes from each country are not negligible, and products compete in the samemarkets with those made in Mexico, reach common customers and have very similar characteristics and composition, so they are colliding with each other and with the domestically produced PFTT. c. Import Analysis190. \u160\'00\'A0Akra noted that the presence in the Mexican market of imports of PFTT originating in China and India, carried out under conditions of price discrimination, and their increase in absolute and related terms to the ANC cause material injury to the domestic industry that manufactures the same product; negative effects on the main economic indicators of the domestic industry, and is a direct cause of such imports. 191.\u160\'00\'A0The Applicant added that, if the situation continues, imports under price discrimination would continue to increase, affecting various indicators of domestic industry, such as the volume of production and sales of domestic merchandise, which would continue to decline significantly in subsequent years, especially when the level of undercutting is significant, which does not allow domestic production to increase its prices, even if it lost sales volume against those imports. 192.\u160\'00\'A0Considering points 187, 188 and 189 of this Resolution, the Secretariat noted that the total specific imports of PFTT decreased by 4% in the period July 2017-June 2018, but increased by 7% in the period investigated, accumulating an increase of 2% in the period analyzed. 193.\u160\'00\'A0Total import growth during the period analyzed is explained by the performance of the subject imports, which had an increasing trend over the period analyzed: they increased less than 1% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 12% in the period investigated, accumulating an increase of 12% in the period analyzed. They also maintained a significant share of total imports over the period analyzed, representing 75% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 79% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 83% in the period investigated. 194.\u160\'00\'A0By contrast, imports of origin other than the countries investigated behaved the opposite, as they decreased by 18% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 13% in the period investigated, to accumulate a 29% fall in the period analyzed. In this regard, these imports decreased their share of total IMPORTS of PFTT from 25% in the period July 2016-June 2017 to 17% in the period investigated. 195.\u160\'00\'A0In terms of the domestic market, the Secretariat noted that the investigated imports increased their share of the ANC, the PNOMI and Akra's domestic sales.\u160\'00\'A0For the ANC, these imports accounted for 55% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 57% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 63% in the period investigated, as illustrated in the following graphic; PNOMI, accounted for 201% in the period July 2016-June 2017, 202% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 264% in the period investigated; in addition to the 243% sales to Akra's domestic market in the period July 2016-June 2017, 230% in the periodJuly 2017-June 2018 and 350% in the period investigated. In addition, imports from other origins decreased their market share from 18% of the ANC in the period July 2016-June 2017 to 13% in the period investigated; whereas, with respect to the PNOMI, these imports went from 66% in the period July 2016-June 2017 to 55% in the period investigated; in addition to the 79% sales to the domestic market of Akra in the period July 2016-June 2017, 61% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 73% in the period investigated. Apparent domestic consumption in The Mexican PFTT market\par \par \uc2 Source: SIC-M, Akra and Secretariat calculations.196.\u160\'00\'A0Furthermore, as stated in point 168 of this Resolution, fifteen Akra customers imported the goods investigated during the period investigated, however, Akra provided only specific figures of securities and sales volumes to the domestic market for its top ten customers. In this regard, based on these figures and those obtained from the LIST of SIC-M \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Secretariat noted that eight of the latter made imports of the goods investigated during the period analyzed, which increased their imports of PFTT, both in the period investigated (24%) as in the period analyzed (9%), while in both periods their purchases of domestic merchandise decreased (21% in the period analyzed and 18% in the period investigated), which could indicate the possibility of a shift in domestic sales resulting from the increase in investigated imports. 197.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the results described in the previous points of this Resolution, the Secretariat initially determined that there was an increase in the period analyzed of the investigated imports in both absolute terms and in relation to the domestic industry's domestic market, production and sales. In addition, Akra's sales-per-customer information provides elements indicating the possible existence of a displacement of goods manufactured in Mexico caused by imports of PFTT originating in the countries investigated. 6. Price effects198. \u160\'00\'A0Pursuant to Articles 3.1 and 3.2 of the AD Agreement; 41 LCE fraction II, and 64 fraction II of the ERCE, the Secretariat analysed whether the investigated imports occurred on the domestic market at prices considerably lower than those of the like domestic product and the effect of these imports on domestic prices, as well as whether the level of import prices was decisive in explaining the conduct on the domestic market. 199.\u160\'00\'A0The Applicant argued that the presence on the Mexican market of imports of PFTT originating in China and India, carried out under conditions of price discrimination, and their increase in absolute and relative terms in the ANC cause material injury to the domestic industry that manufactures the same product; negative effects on the main economic indicators of the domestic industry, and is a direct cause of such imports. The above, taking into view the existence of a significant level of undercutting over the period analysed, of the price of such imports with respect to domestic prices, which does not allow domestic production to increase its prices, without losing sales volume. 200.\u160\'00\'A0Akra added that this occurs because the investigated imports areMexican market in price discrimination, due to the overproduction of production companies in China and India; companies that, in order to position themselves in the Mexican market and gain participation in it, export PFTT to their customers in Mexico at dumping prices, prices that are well below the prices at which the domestic merchandise is sold.\u160\'00\'A0201.\u160\'00\'A0To support the above, Akra submitted figures for its economic and financial indicators for the period analyzed, as well as import figures accompanied by the list of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the SAT, for tiGIE tariff fraction 5402.33.01, which was provided to them by ANIQ. 202.\u160\'00\'A0As noted in points 187, 188 and 189 of this Resolution, the Secretariat considered as the best available information concerning import prices, it is obtained from the list of import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of THE SIC-M. Also, in order to analyze the prices of imports at the same level of competition, the payment, where appropriate, of the corresponding tariff and of customs duties to put them on the Mexican market was included. 203.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the above information, the Secretariat noted an increasing performance in the prices of imports in the Mexican market in both the period investigated and the period analyzed.\u160\'00\'A0In this regard, the average price of the investigated imports increased 7% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 5% in the period investigated, accumulating an increase of 13% in the period analyzed. Meanwhile, the average price of imports from other origins grew by 7% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 5% in the period investigated, accumulating an increase of 12% in the period analyzed. In addition, in comparing the two prices, the Secretariat noted that the prices of the investigated merchandise were 52% below the prices of PFTT imported from other sources, consistently over the period analyzed. 204.\u160\'00\'A0On the other hand, based on information obtained from the domestic sales figures of the domestic industry, the Secretariat noted that the average price of domestic sales of domestic merchandise, measured in dollars, increased by 9% in both the period July 2017-June 2018 and in the period investigated, accumulating a growth of 19% in the period analyzed. However, when compared to the prices of the investigated imports, it was noted that the latter were 7%, 9% and 12% below the domestic price in the july 2016-June 2017 periods, July 2017-June 2018 and the period investigated, respectively, as illustrated in the graph below. Prices in the Mexican PFTT market\par \par \uc2 Source: SIC-M, Akra and Secretariat calculations.205.\u160\'00\'A0With regard to the increasing performance of domestic prices, Akra stated that it was mainly due to the increase in the cost of major inputs for the manufacture of PFTT (PTA and MEG), behaviour around the world, so that it was also reflected in the evolution of import prices. The Applicant also insisted on: (i) that the conduct of its domestic sales prices did reflect the increase in costs in raw materials, while the prices of the investigated imports did not do so in the same way, and (ii). which, despite the increase in prices during the period analyzed, the prices of the subject imports continued to be consistently below those of the goodsNational. To support the above, Akra presented figures on the monthly prices of the domestic good and the imports investigated, as well as the average annual prices to which the PTA and MEG acquired during the period analyzed, and the prices of both inputs in the Markets of China and India, corresponding to the period analyzed, the latter, obtained from the specialized publication Tecnon Orbichem. 206.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat confirmed Akra's point in increasing the prices of both premium prices acquired by the Applicant and inputs in the Chinese and Indian markets, in addition to the figures for the unit costs of raw materials and manufacturing submitted by Akra, relating to its sales to the domestic market, which are indicated in point 220 (b) of this Resolution, significant increases were also observed in the period analysed, including, greater than the increase in the price of Akra (both observed at constant weights of June 2019). 207.\u160\'00\'A0On the other hand, on the basis of sales figures to Akra's main customers in the period analyzed and the import figures obtained from the list of SIC-M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Secretariat noted that, while the prices of investigated imports by the eight common customers and those of their domestic purchases (expressed in dollars) increased during the period analyzed, the prices of the investigated imports were always below the prices at which they purchased domestic merchandise (between 3% and 8%) throughout the period analyzed. This underpins the existence of the possible displacement of Akra's sales due to the investigated imports. 208.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the results described in this paragraph, the Secretariat noted the following: (i) the existence of undervaluation of the average price of the investigated good with respect to the average price of domestic sales of the domestic industry over the period analyzed; ii. that, while there was an increase in the domestic price, it was less than the increase in unit costs due in particular to the increase in the prices at which Akra acquired its raw material during the period analyzed, and (iii). Based on figures from common customers, the undercutting of the prices of the imports investigated when compared to the prices at which they acquired the domestic product;\u160\'00\'A0situations arising from the low price levels to which investigated imports occurred on the domestic market under alleged dumping conditions, the evidence of which was previously established. In addition, the low price level of investigated imports observed in the period analyzed is associated with increasing volumes of imports, a greater share of the domestic market and the possible displacement of sales of merchandise manufactured by Akra. 7. Effects on the domestic industry209. \u160\'00\'A0On the basis of Articles 3.1 and 3.4 of the AD Agreement; 41 LCE fraction III, and 64 fraction III of the ERCE, the Secretariat assessed the effects of subject imports on the economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry of the like product. 210.\u160\'00\'A0Akra argued that the presence in the Mexican market of imports of PFTT originating in China and India, carried out under conditions of price discrimination, and their increase in absolute and relative terms within the ANC caused material injury to the domestic industry that manufactures the same product;reflected in negative effects on the main economic indicators of the domestic industry, and was directly caused by those imports. To support the above, Akra presented figures of its economic and financial indicators for the period analyzed. 211.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the information identified in the previous point, the Secretariat noted that Akra's production volume increased an 8% drop in the period analyzed, resulting from a 3% increase in the period July 2017-June 2018 and a decrease of 10% in the period investigated. In addition, the volume of its domestic market-oriented production increased a 17% drop in the period analyzed, resulting from being kept constant in the period July 2017-June 2018 and decreased by 17% in the period investigated. 212.\u160\'00\'A0Further, in the context of the anC's conduct in the period analysed and referred to in point 179 of this Resolution, Akra's domestic-oriented production decreased its share of the ANC in the period analysed by 3 percentage points, from representing 23% in the period July 2016-June 2017 to 20% in the period investigated. 213.\u160\'00\'A0For its part, the total sales of the domestic industry showed a cumulative drop of 11% in the period analyzed, increasing by 6% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and decreasing 16% in the period investigated. The Secretariat noted that the performance of total sales was largely explained by the performance of domestic sales, which had a cumulative drop of 22% in the period analyzed, increasing by 6% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and decreasing by 26% in the period investigated; while sales to the export market grew by 4% in the period analyzed, increasing by 6% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and decreasing by 2% in the period investigated. Akra domestic sales and PFTT imports\par \par \uc2 Source: SIC-M, Akra and Secretariat calculations.214.\u160\'00\'A0Furthermore, on the basis of sales figures to Akra's main customers in the period analyzed and the import figures obtained from the siC-M import \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  list, as noted in points 196 and 207 of this Resolution, the eight common customers not only increased the acquisition of investigated imports in the period analyzed but in turn reduced their domestic purchases, setting the possibility of a displacement of the latter, due to the price differential observed between the two goods. 215.\u160\'00\'A0On the other hand, employment of the domestic industry decreased by 15% in the period analyzed, due to an increase of 2% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and a 16% fall in the period investigated; while the wage mass exhibited similar performance by increasing 4% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and falling 11% in the period investigated, accumulating a 7% drop in the period analyzed. Employment productivity also increased by 1% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and 7% in the period investigated, resulting in a cumulative increase of 8% in the period analyzed;\u160\'00\'A0influenced by the decreases observed in both employment and production of Akra, during the period investigated and analyzed. 216.\u160\'00\'A0In relation to the inventories of the domestic industry, cumulative inventory growth of 33% was observed in the period analysed, resulting from a12% decrease in the period July 2017-June 2018 and an increase of 52% in the period investigated. In addition, Akra's share of inventories to domestic sales increased in the period analyzed from 18% for the period July 2016-June 2017 to 31% in the period investigated. 217.\u160\'00\'A0With regard to the installed capacity of the domestic industry for the manufacture of PFTT, the Secretariat noted that it remained constant throughout the period under review. However, the percentage of utilization of the same decreased in the period analyzed, influenced by the decreasing behavior of production, from 77% in the period July 2016-June 2017 to 70% in the period investigated. In this regard, Akra indicated that the installed capacity figure is representative of the actual mixture of products (low/high denier) with which it met its annual demand and was calculated taking into account the use of its available equipment (texturing machines) occurred during the period investigated. To support it, Akra presented the worksheet which notes that the calculation of its installed capacity corresponds exclusively to PFTT. 218.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat assessed Akra's financial position on the basis of the information submitted by Akra, concerning: i. cost statements, sales and profits of the product similar to the one investigated, both total and unit, in three versions (of its sales to the domestic market, its export sales and all its sales to the domestic market plus exports), for the period analyzed; (ii) Akra's financial statements for 2016, 2017 and 2018, and (iii). information on an investment project related to the goods similar to that investigated (cash flows for the project in dollars, amount of investment, net present value, internal rate of return and cost of affectation, in two scenarios: one, excluding imports of the goods investigated under dumping conditions and another, considering them). 219.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat updated the financial information in order to make its figures comparable, which was done through the method of changes in the general level of prices, based on the National Consumer Price Index published by the National Institute of Statistics and Geography. 220.\u160\'00\'A0With regard to the information referred to in point 218 of this Resolution, the Secretariat analysed the status of costs, sales and profits of the goods similar to that investigated for the market, however, of the proportional share of the total operating costs and expenses to the domestic market, the Secretariat shall request appropriate clarifications. 221.\u160\'00\'A0Without detriment to the foregoing, the Secretariat noted the following:a. \u160\'00\'A0 in relation to the operating results of sales exclusively for the domestic market, it was observed that operating losses increased 9.5% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and decreased 3.6% in the period investigated, accumulating an increase of 5.5% in the period analyzed, and that the operating margin fell 0.3 percentage points in the period July 2017-June 2018 from -15.2% in the period July 2016-June 2017 to -15.5% in the period July 2017-June 2018, and 3.3 percentage points in the period investigated, to be located at -18.8% in that period, accumulating a fall of 3.7 percentage points in the period analyzed. This behaviour was determined by the observed sales revenue, which increased 7.1% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and fell 20.6% in the period investigated,accumulating a 15% drop in the period analyzed, while operating costs increased by 7.4% in the period July 2017-June 2018 and decreased 18.3% in the period investigated, accumulating a fall of 12.3% in the period analyzed, andb. \u160\'00\'A0 as far as the cost status is, sales and unit profits of the goods similar to that investigated, corresponding to sales to the domestic market expressed at constant pesos of June 2019 per kilogram, it was observed that unit manufacturing costs increased by 2.3% for the period July 2017-June 2018 and 11.3% in the period investigated, accumulating an increase of 13.8% for the period analyzed; while the prices of merchandise manufactured by Akra, while also increasing over the period analyzed, did so to a lesser extent than costs, by increasing 1.4% for the period July 2017-June 2018 and 8% in the period investigated, to accumulate an increase of 9.4% in the period analyzed. 222.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, the Secretariat assessed the Variables Return of the Investment in Assets (ROA), cash flow and capital raising, based on Akra's financial statements, taking into account that they consider the group or range of products more restricted to include the like product, in accordance with Articles 3.6 of the Anti-Dumping Agreement and 66 of the RCECE. 223.\u160\'00\'A0With regard to the domestic industry's ROA, calculated at the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  level, the Secretariat noted that this yield was 8.9% for 2016, -2.8% for 2017 and 7.9% for 2018, which represented a 1 percentage point drop in the period analyzed. 224.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the state of change in Akra's financial situation, the Secretariat initially noted that cash flow at the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  level had an increasing performance in reporting an increase of 165% during the period analyzed, as a result of the increased generation of working capital. 225.\u160\'00\'A0On the other hand, the ability to raise capital measures a producer's chance of relying on the monetary resources necessary for productive activity and is analysed through the behaviour of circulating indices, acid testing, leverage and debt. In this sense, solvency and liquidity are initially considered to be adequate, if the relationship between current assets and liabilities is 1 to 1 or higher. In this regard, the Secretariat noted rates relating to Akra's current ratio (relationship between current assets and short-term liabilities) of 2.86, 0.88 and 0.79 for 2016, 2017 and 2018, respectively; whereas, in the acid test (current asset minus the value of inventories, relative to the short-term liability), the indices recorded in the same periods were 2.35, 0.69 and 0.5, respectively. Based on the results obtained, the Secretariat initially considered that the solvency and liquidity levels of the domestic industry were not adequate. 226.\u160\'00\'A0In addition, a proportion of total liabilities with respect to book capital of less than 100% is considered manageable, in this case it is initially concluded that leverage (total liability to book capital) was not manageable when reporting a percentage structure of 159% in 2016, 328% in 2017 and 291% in 2018, while the ratio of total liability to total asset or debt, was at appropriate levels in the same years, with 61%, 77% and 74%, respectively. 227.\u160\'00\'A0With regard to the investment project, Akra argued that in 2018 it carried outplanning an investment project for a fourth spinning line, which it completed in 2019, thereby providing for better filament quality and lower costs, however, this was not achieved, due to the presence of imports in price discrimination. The Secretariat reviewed the information submitted by the Applicant for the investment project and noted that the determination of cash flows was unclear and did not include any explanation, which was why the Secretariat would request that such information be required in the next procedural stage. 228.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the performance of the economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry described above, the Secretariat noted that, in both the period analyzed and the investigation, the concurrence of the investigated imports, under alleged dumping conditions, adversely affected the relevant economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry:a. \u160\'00\'A0 during the period analyzed, deterioration in economic and financial variables was observed mainly in various indicators of the domestic PFTT industry, such as production, PNOMI, total sales and the domestic market, as well as revenue from such sales, employment, wage mass, inventory level, inventory-to-sales ratio, utilization of installed capacity, operating losses and operating margin. In addition, with regard to the indicators determined by the financial statements issued, Akra recorded a performance on the declining investment and a ability to raise limited capital, because leverage levels are greater than 100%, in all years contained in the period analyzed; while the current and acid test ratio was less than 1 time short-term assets and liabilities in 2017 and 2018, andb. \u160\'00\'A0 Comparing the period investigated with the previous similar period, the following indicators saw a deterioration: production, PNOMI, total sales and the domestic market, as well as revenue from such sales, sales to the external market, employment, wage mass, inventory level, inventory-to-sales ratio, utilization of installed capacity, operating losses and operating margin. 8. Other damage factors229. \u160\'00\'A0Pursuant to Articles 3.5 of the AD Agreement, 39 of the LCE and 69 of the ERCE, the Secretariat examined the concurrence of factors other than imports originating in China, under alleged dumping conditions, which at the same time could be caused by injury to the domestic pfTT industry. 230.\u160\'00\'A0Akra stated that the material injury reflected in negative effects of its main economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry was caused directly by the presence in the Mexican market of imports of PFTT originating in China and India, under conditions of price discrimination, the increasing performance of their volumes in absolute and relative terms in the ANC and the low prices at which they were made. In this sense, he stated the following:a. \u160\'00\'A0 imports from origins other than those investigated did not have a negative effect on the performance of the domestic industry, since during the period analyzed, as an effect of the investigated imports, they have lost share in both the Mexican market and total imports; in addition to the price of the investigated imports that were well below the pricesimports from different origins to China and India during the period investigated, which clearly explains the displacement suffered by both the latter and sales by the domestic industry, andb. \u160\'00\'A0 during the period under review, there was no change in consumption structures; there were no technological changes in the production processes of the subject merchandise, and there are no restrictive trade practices of foreign or domestic producers to which the Applicant is aware. 231.\u160\'00\'A0In this regard, the Secretariat considered:a. \u160\'00\'A0 imports from origins other than those investigated, it was confirmed that they decreased their share by 8 percentage points compared to total imports, and by 5 percentage points from the ANC, by comparing the period July 2016-June 2017 with the period investigated; in addition to the effect of, over the period under review, they were at prices higher than those of the goods investigated, confirming what Akra pointed out that such imports could not be regarded as a cause of injury to the domestic industry; b. \u160\'00\'A0 regarding the existence of a possible contraction in demand in the Mexican PFTT market, the Secretariat noted that while the ANC decreased by 2% in the period analyzed, the behaviour presented by the investigated imports was contrary, as there was a 12% increase in the same period, imports that, as noted throughout this Resolution, were allegedly made, presumably under dumping, at prices lower than those of domestic manufacturing; c. \u160\'00\'A0 Regarding Akra's export performance, the Secretariat noted that as sales to the domestic market did, its exports also declined in the period investigated and that, while they increased by 4% in the period analyzed, in absolute terms that increase represented only 14% of the total sales decline observed over the same period, and (d). \u160\'00\'A0 Finally, the domestic industry's productivity performance was positive over the period analyzed, a situation explained by the observed performance in production and employment, which was determined by the volume growth and low prices at which the investigated imports were made during the same period. 232.\u160\'00\'A0Based on the arguments and evidence presented by the Applicant, as well as the analysis in the previous point, the Secretariat initially had no evidence to presume the existence of factors other than imports originating in China and India under alleged dumping conditions that, at the same time, could be the cause of material injury to the domestic industry. H. Conclusions233. \u160\'00\'A0Based on the results of the analysis of the arguments and evidence described in this Resolution, the Secretariat concluded that there was sufficient evidence to presume that during the period investigated imports of PFTT originating in China and India were made under alleged dumping conditions and caused material injury to the domestic industry of the like product. Among the main elements evaluated in a comprehensive way, which underpin this conclusion, without these being considered exhaustive or limiting, the following stand out:a. \u160\'00\'A0 The investigated imports were made with a margin of dumpingde minimis provided for in Article 5.8 of the AD Agreement. In the period investigated, imports originating in China and India accounted for 66% and 16% of totals, respectively. b. \u160\'00\'A0 Investigated imports saw a growing trend in both absolute and relative terms, displacing domestic sales from the domestic industry. During the period analyzed they recorded growth of 12%, and increased their share of the ANC by 8 percentage points, from a contribution of 55% in the period July 2016-June 2017 to 63% in the period investigated. As a result, the investigated imports increased their share of total imports by 8 percentage points, from a contribution of 75% in the period July 2016-June 2017 to 83% in the period investigated. c. \u160\'00\'A0 The prices of the investigated imports were below the average domestic sales price of the domestic industry over the period analyzed (in percentages ranging from 7% to 12%) and while there was an increase in domestic prices, this was less than the increase in costs, influenced by the increase in prices at which Akra acquired its raw material during the period analyzed. This, considering that the low price level of the investigated imports observed in the period analyzed is associated with increasing volumes thereof, a greater share of the domestic market and the possible displacement of sales of goods manufactured by Akra. d. \u160\'00\'A0 The concurrence of imports of PFTT originating in China and India, under alleged dumping conditions, adversely affected the relevant economic and financial indicators of the domestic industry in both the period analyzed and the period investigated. Among the main indicators affected are the following:(i). \u160\'00\'A0 During the period under review, impairment was observed in indicators such as production, PNOMI, total sales and the domestic market, as well as revenue from such sales, sales to the external market, employment, wage mass, inventory level, inventory-to-sales ratio, utilization of installed capacity, operating losses and operating margin;\u160\'00\'A0in addition to the Akra's performance on declining investment and a limited capital-gathering ability, due to the 100% leverage levels, in all the years of the period analyzed, while the current ratio and acid test were less than 1 time short-term assets and liabilities in 2017 and 2018. ii. \u160\'00\'A0 While, during the period investigated, there was a deterioration in production, PNOMI, total sales and the domestic market, as well as revenue from such sales, sales to the external market, employment, wage mass, inventory level, inventory-to-sales ratio, utilization of installed capacity, operating losses and operating margin. E. \u160\'00\'A0 No injury factors other than imports originating in China and India allegedly under dumping were identified. 234.\u160\'00\'A0As a foregoing, and on the basis of Articles 5 of the AD Agreement and 52 fractions I and II of the LCE, it is appropriate to issue the followingSOLUTION235.\u160\'00\'A0The interested party's request is accepted and the initiation of the anti-dumping investigation on imports of PFTT, originating in China and India, is declared,regardless of the country of origin, which is entered by the TIGIE tariff fraction 5402.33.01, or by any other. 236.\u160\'00\'A0The investigation period is fixed from 1 July 2018 to 30 June 2019, and as an injury analysis period from 1 July 2016 to 30 June 2019. 237.\u160\'00\'A0The Secretariat may, where appropriate, apply the final countervailing quotas on products declared for consumption no later than 90 days before the date of application of the provisional measures, in accordance with Articles 10.6 of the AD Agreement and 65A of the LCE. 238.\u160\'00\'A0On the basis of Articles 6.1, 12.1 and footnote 15 of the AD Agreement, 3 last paragraph and 53 of the LCE, domestic producers, importers, exporters, foreign moral persons or any person attesting to have a legal interest in the outcome of this investigation shall have a period of 23 working days to prove their legal interest and submit their response to the official form established for that purpose, as well as the arguments and evidence they deem relevant.\u160\'00\'A0For persons and Governments referred to in point 17 of this Resolution, the 23 working days period shall begin to be 5 days after the date of dispatch of the office of notification of the initiation of this investigation. For other interested parties, the period will begin to count 5 days after the publication of this Resolution in the DOF. In both cases, the deadline will end at 14:00 on the day of its expiration. 239.\u160\'00\'A0The official form referred to in the previous point of this Resolution may be obtained from the officialship of parts of the UPCI, located in Insurgentes Sur No. 1940, ground floor, Col. Florida, C.P. 01030, in Mexico City, Monday to Friday from 9:00 to 14:00 hours. It is also available on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gob.mx/se/acciones-y-programas/industria-y-comercio-unidad-de-practicas-comerciales-internacionales-upci"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.gob.mx/se/acciones-y-programas/industria-y-comercio-unidad-de-practicas-comerciales-internacionales-upci}}}\uc2  website. 240.\u160\'00\'A0Notify companies and governments of knowledge of this Resolution. Transfer copies are made available to any party attesting to their legal interest in these proceedings, at the address and times indicated in the previous point of this Resolution. 241.\u160\'00\'A0Contact this Resolution to the SAT for applicable legal purposes. 242.\u160\'00\'A0This Resolution shall enter into force on the day following its publication in the DOF. Mexico City, as of March 18, 2020.- The Secretary of Economy, Graciela M\u225\'00\'E1rquez Col\u237\'00\'EDn.-Rubric.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020200401eg3v000n3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
