\par\par \b \uc2 Westpac\uc2  Sees NZ House Price Rises Peaking at 10% -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2020\par 23:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1751 ET - Increases in New Zealand house prices will peak at 10% in the middle of this year and then start easing off as rising fixed mortgage rates crimp demand, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  says. The bank says its forecast of prices rising 7% in 2020 has already been met in January and the market "has proved slightly stronger than we anticipated." It doesn't expect a repeat of 2016's big gains, but says the market's behavior over the past year is an example of financial factors exerting more influence than physical supply and demand. (stephen.wright@wsj.com)Despite widening concerns about the spread of COVID-19, Australian consumer confidence gained 1.2% last week, more than reversing the prior week's loss, according to the ANZ Roy Morgan Survey. ANZ's Head of Australian Economics, \uc2 David Plank\uc2  , says sentiment was possibly buoyed by the continued gains in asset prices.\par \par \uc2 The fact the coronavirus hasn't become established in Australia might be a contributing factor, although the measures--such as travel bans--taken to help ensure this remains the case will have economic consequences. This week will bring domestic data on wages and employment. Mr. Plank isn't expecting the news to be good, which may see confidence dip over the coming week, continuing sawtooth pattern.\par \par \uc2 (james.glynn@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1727 ET - Former \uc2 Australian Treasury\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Ken Henry\uc2  says the country's tax system has fallen into disrepair and is badly in need of reform. In a report in the Australian Financial Review, Henry says: "If you look at what has happened to the Australian tax system over the past decade ... you'd have to say things have gotten very much worse, not better." Henry was talking to a tax forum. Income taxes account for a rising proportion of government revenue. That's not sustainable, he added. As productivity growth flags and living standards fall, calls for a broader economic reform agenda are growing in Australia, sharpening the focus on the government's 2020-21 budget, due out in May. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1626 ET - The lift in New Zealand house prices is unlikely to last as long as previous upturns and prices won't peak as high as the last episode, says \uc2 Kiwibank\uc2 , after real estate institute data showed prices and sales volumes rising again in January, spurred by low interest rates. New housing supply is entering the market, the bank says, and population growth is slowing with annual gains from net migration about 20,000 less than the 2016 peak. Polices designed to crimp property speculation and high prices putting ownership out of reach for segments of the population also act as headwinds. (stephen.wright@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1545 ET - Minutes of the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's Feb. 4 policy meeting, due at 0030 GMT, will show a high bar for more easing. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept interest rates on hold at a record low 0.75% at the meeting, and indicated a growing reluctance to cut further. Unemployment has been falling, giving policy makers more time to consider what to do next. The message from the \uc2 RBA\uc2 's leadership has been upbeat, downplaying the impact of bush fires and the coronavirus (for now at least). The market has now priced out interest rate cuts in the first half of 2020. The \uc2 RBA\uc2  is likely to restate that a rising jobless rate, and inflation moving away from its 2-3% target band will be needed before it cuts again. (james.glynn@wsj.com; @JamesGlynnWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1211 ET - European shares rise modestly as the prospect of fresh Chinese economic stimulus helps stabilize risk appetite and the U.S. market stays closed for a public holiday. The Stoxx Europe 600, FTSE 100, CAC-40 and DAX all gained about 0.3% after stock markets in China surged on hopes that Beijing authorities will try to bolster the economy. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  cut the interest rate on one-year loans to support credit markets and the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would inject liquidity into the market. "Sadly the health crisis in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is deepening, but traders are reacting to Beijing's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says David Madden at \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  . (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1020 ET - The "weak" eurozone economy will improve this year and next, \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  says, forecasting subdued GDP growth of 1.2% in 2020 and 1.4% in 2021. However, the pace of growth won't be strong enough to sustainably strengthen inflation from current levels, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. Therefore, it expects the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain "a very loose" monetary policy, with interest rates on hold until at least the end of 2021. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says the balance of risk remains to the downside due to the potential escalation of trade tensions with the US and a worse-than-expected fallout from the coronavirus on global growth and trade. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1016 ET - The coronavirus creates new risks to prospects of a stabilization of global growth in 2020, through trade and tourism channels, \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  says. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has cut its global-growth forecasts by two-tenths of a percentage point, expecting G-20 economies to collectively grow 2.4% in 2020. "Extended lockdowns in China would have a global impact given the country's importance and interconnectedness in the global economy," says \uc2 Moody's\uc2  vice president \uc2 Madhavi Bokil\uc2 . Although the outbreak will first and foremost hurt China, if the rate of infections doesn't abate and the death toll continues to rise, the domestic and international supply-chain disruptions are likely to become significant, amplifying the shock to the global economy, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0852 ET - The key focus for investors regarding German politics is what coalition will be eventually, not who will succeed current German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  as head of the Christian Democratic Union party, says Berenberg's economist \uc2 Holger Schmieding\uc2  . The only outcome that would significantly change the outlook for Germany would be a coalition between the Greens, the Social Democratic Party and the left-wing Linke party, he says. "Such a federal government may seriously harm the economy," with the potential for a "barrage of reform reversals and regulations along the lines of the cap on housing rents [being imposed in Berlin]," Schmieding says. Germany is set to hold regular elections in autumn 2021. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 ET - The German ZEW indicator of current macroeconomic conditions will show "a moderate" decline from January due to the coronavirus, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. The ZEW's assessment of the current situation in Germany rose to minus 9.5 points in January, and data for February will be published on Tuesday. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expects more negative assessments of the current situation amid coronavirus concerns, which would be consistent with the decline recorded in the Sentix indicator of current conditions, which fell in February, it says. For the ZEW index of economic expectations, which improved significantly in January reaching its highest level since 2015, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expects "a slightly larger decline" in February compared with its expectations for current conditions. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 ET - The Swiss franc could rise against the dollar through 2020 due the growing reluctance of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to use measures to contain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally, UBS Global Wealth Management. The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  in January put Switzerland on its watchlist of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators, which is likely to discourage the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit further appreciation of the franc, \uc2 UBS\uc2  analysts say in a note. The dollar also "looks overvalued at current levels" against the franc and could weaken on a "modest softening" in U.S. economic growth, which would raise the prospect of an interest rate cut, they say. \uc2 UBS\uc2  expects USD/CHF to end 2020 at 0.92. USD/CHF is last flat at 0.9819. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0643 ET - The euro should rise to $1.19 by the end of 2020, from $1.0848 now, as the gap between U.S. and eurozone economic growth narrows, UBS Global Wealth Management says. The U.S. should see a "modest softening" in economic growth over the course of 2020 whereas the eurozone will "remain close to parity," \uc2 UBS\uc2  analysts say in a note. "We expect the recent weak patch for the eurozone economy to pass, with growth picking in the latter quarters of the year," the analysts say. The phase one trade deal between U.S. and China should support eurozone exports while trade disruptions from the coronavirus outbreak are likely to abate in the second quarter, they say. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 17, 2020 17:51 ET (22:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200217eg2h001um\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
