\par\par \b \uc2 Europe Stocks Edge Higher as China Stimulus Lifts Mood -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,538 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Februar 2020\par 18:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2020, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1211 ET - European shares rise modestly as the prospect of fresh Chinese economic stimulus helps stabilize risk appetite and the U.S. market stays closed for a public holiday. The Stoxx Europe 600, FTSE 100, CAC-40 and DAX all gained about 0.3% after stock markets in China surged on hopes that Beijing authorities will try to bolster the economy. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  cut the interest rate on one-year loans to support credit markets and the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would inject liquidity into the market. "Sadly the health crisis in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is deepening, but traders are reacting to Beijing's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says David Madden at \uc2 CMC Markets\uc2  . (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1020 ET - The "weak" eurozone economy will improve this year and next, \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  says, forecasting subdued GDP growth of 1.2% in 2020 and 1.4% in 2021. However, the pace of growth won't be strong enough to sustainably strengthen inflation from current levels, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. Therefore, it expects the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain "a very loose" monetary policy, with interest rates on hold until at least the end of 2021. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says the balance of risk remains to the downside due to the potential escalation of trade tensions with the US and a worse-than-expected fallout from the coronavirus on global growth and trade. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1016 ET - The coronavirus creates new risks to prospects of a stabilization of global growth in 2020, through trade and tourism channels, \uc2 Moody's Investors Service\uc2  says. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  has cut its global-growth forecasts by two-tenths of a percentage point, expecting G-20 economies to collectively grow 2.4% in 2020. "Extended lockdowns in China would have a global impact given the country's importance and interconnectedness in the global economy," says \uc2 Moody's\uc2  vice president \uc2 Madhavi Bokil\uc2 . Although the outbreak will first and foremost hurt China, if the rate of infections doesn't abate and the death toll continues to rise, the domestic and international supply-chain disruptions are likely to become significant, amplifying the shock to the global economy, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0852 ET - The key focus for investors regarding German politics is what coalition will be eventually, not who will succeed current German Chancellor \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  as head of the Christian Democratic Union party, says Berenberg's economist \uc2 Holger Schmieding\uc2  . The only outcome that would significantly change the outlook for Germany would be a coalition between the Greens, the Social Democratic Party and the left-wing Linke party, he says. "Such a federal government may seriously harm the economy," with the potential for a "barrage of reform reversals and regulations along the lines of the cap on housing rents [being imposed in Berlin]," Schmieding says. Germany is set to hold regular elections in autumn 2021. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 ET - The German ZEW indicator of current macroeconomic conditions will show "a moderate" decline from January due to the coronavirus, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says. The ZEW's assessment of the current situation in Germany rose to minus 9.5 points in January, and data for February will be published on Tuesday. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expects more negative assessments of the current situation amid coronavirus concerns, which would be consistent with the decline recorded in the Sentix indicator of current conditions, which fell in February, it says. For the ZEW index of economic expectations, which improved significantly in January reaching its highest level since 2015, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expects "a slightly larger decline" in February compared with its expectations for current conditions. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 ET - The Swiss franc could rise against the dollar through 2020 due the growing reluctance of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to use measures to contain the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rally, UBS Global Wealth Management. The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  in January put Switzerland on its watchlist of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulators, which is likely to discourage the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit further appreciation of the franc, \uc2 UBS\uc2  analysts say in a note. The dollar also "looks overvalued at current levels" against the franc and could weaken on a "modest softening" in U.S. economic growth, which would raise the prospect of an interest rate cut, they say. \uc2 UBS\uc2  expects USD/CHF to end 2020 at 0.92. USD/CHF is last flat at 0.9819. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0643 ET - The euro should rise to $1.19 by the end of 2020, from $1.0848 now, as the gap between U.S. and eurozone economic growth narrows, UBS Global Wealth Management says. The U.S. should see a "modest softening" in economic growth over the course of 2020 whereas the eurozone will "remain close to parity," \uc2 UBS\uc2  analysts say in a note. "We expect the recent weak patch for the eurozone economy to pass, with growth picking in the latter quarters of the year," the analysts say. The phase one trade deal between U.S. and China should support eurozone exports while trade disruptions from the coronavirus outbreak are likely to abate in the second quarter, they say. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 ET - The Czech koruna faces volatility due to speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may need to raise interest rates again in March to tackle higher inflation, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. In early February, the \uc2 Czech Republic's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprisingly raised its key interest rate by a quarter point to 2.25%, sending EUR/CZK koruna to its lowest level since October 2012 at 24.8767, according to Factset. While \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor \uc2 Jiri Rusnok\uc2  signalled his preference for unchanged rates for the foreseeable future, the prospect of further tightening could rise if February inflation is above expectations, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's Melanie Fischinger says. "Things will remain interesting, and that could mean further trading days with high levels of volatility for the koruna." EUR/CZK falls 0.3% to 24.7979.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0551 ET - Cyprus is well positioned to substantially reduce its public debt burden but it faces various credit-relevant risks, says Scope Ratings. "These four risks, and their impact on public finances should they materialize, are credit-relevant and will require careful monitoring in the coming months," says Alvise Lennkh, Scope's lead analyst for Cyprus. Contingent liabilities stemming from the banking sector present a "considerable" risk to public finances, while unexpected increases in expenditure could weigh on fiscal outcomes, Scope says. A further risk is the long-term sustainability of the newly-installed General Health System as the government is liable to cover any deficits arising from the new system, and that could result in some fiscal slippage, the rating firm says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0540 ET - European shares rise after Chinese markets closed higher, with the prospect of further economic stimulus helping drive upside for global stocks. The Stoxx Europe 600, the DAX and the FTSE 100 gain 0.2% apiece and the CAC 40 is up 0.1%. China's Shenzhen A-Share rose more than 3% and Hong Kong's Hang Seng advanced 0.5% after Beijing pledged to reduce taxes and cut rates on one-year bank loans. Still, all other major markets in Asia traded lower, with Japan's Nikkei leading the fallers following news of a 1.6% drop in the country's economic growth in the fourth quarter. Meanwhile, IG says it expects the Dow Jones Industrial Average to follow Europe's lead, likely opening Monday's session 55 points higher at 29,453. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0521 ET - The number of employees in the manufacturing sector in Germany declined in December compared with the previous year, German statistics agency Destatis says. At the end of December, 5.6 million people worked in local units of manufacturing with 50 or more people employed. This represented a fall of 30,000, or 0.5%, from December 2018. The number of hours worked in the manufacturing sector also contracted 0.5% in December from a year earlier, reaching 598 million, according to Destatis. However, there was a 0.7% year-on-year increase in earnings in the manufacturing sector to EUR24.3 billion, Destatis says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0508 ET - Uncertainty over the U.K.'s future trading relationships after Brexit will continue to drag on sterling even if the government delivers a big fiscal spending boost, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. The pound has "regained some upward momentum" with EUR/GBP holding support at the 0.8300-level and GBP/USD at 1.3000 due to "building expectations for even looser fiscal policy" after the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  announced a cabinet reshuffle on Thursday, \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says. However, concerns about U.K.-EU trade tensions remain a "downside risk for the pound and casts doubt on the sustainability of recent gains beyond the near-term," he says. GBP/USD falls 0.1% to 1.3040 and EUR/GBP rises 0.2% to 0.8319. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 17, 2020 12:11 ET (17:11 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020200217eg2h001i2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
