S$         13,705         -35         INR US$             71.11        -0.211\par  MYR US$         4.109          -0.006      TWD US$             30.207       -0.125\par  CNY US$         6.9982         -0.0229     HKD US$             7.7653       -0.0007\par  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --     \par  Commodities                    Net Chng                                     Net Chng\par  Spot Gold       1,553.6772     -22.3728    Silver (Lon)        17.5959      -0.0607\par  U.S. Gold Fut   1,557.8        -24.6       Brent Crude         54.14        -0.31\par  Iron Ore        CNY587.0       -19.5       TRJCRB Index        -            -\par  TOCOM Rubber    JPY177.5       2.8         \f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2  Copper          5,618        95\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --  \par     ** indicates closing price \par     All prices as of 20:50 GMT\par     \par     EQUITIES\par \par     GLOBAL - Crude oil prices rebounded and global equity markets surged on Tuesday as\par China's efforts to minimize the economic impact from the coronavirus epidemic spurred\par investors' risk appetite.\par \par The price of gold and government debt slid on views China will do whatever it can to\par alleviate the economic toll from an outbreak that has killed more than 420 people and\par infected more than 20,000. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     NEW YORK - The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2  hit a record high on Tuesday and the S&P 500 headed for its\par biggest one-day gain in about six months as fears of a heavy economic impact from the\par coronavirus outbreak waned after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \par The Dow was on pace for its biggest single-day rise in about eight months, as the stock\par marked recovered from steep losses in the prior week.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - European shares clocked their biggest one-day gain in nearly four months\par on Tuesday as upbeat earnings updates from \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2  along with\par China's moves to support its markets lifted sentiment.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  injected 1.7 trillion yuan ($243 billion) on Monday and Tuesday to\par stabilise markets that fell heavily on heightened fears over the potential economic\par fallout from the fast-spreading coronavirus.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO -  Japanese stocks edged up on Tuesday, erasing early losses as Chinese\par shares steadied somewhat after crumbling the previous day, but sentiment remained\par fragile as investors fretted over the growing economic and human costs of a virus\par outbreak.\par \par The Nikkei index ended up 0.49% at 23,084.59, stepping off from a 10-week low hit in\par the previous session. Shares in the technology and consumer stables sectors rose.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI -  Chinese stocks ended higher on Tuesday as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  vowed to\par stabilise the market, regaining some ground from the coronavirus-led rout that erased\par almost $400 billion in market value from the Shanghai benchmark in the previous\par session.\par \par The Shanghai Composite index closed up 1.3% at 2,783.29 \u8212\'20\'14 the biggest daily\par gain since Dec. 13, 2019. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - Australian shares are set to rise on Wednesday, as China's efforts to\par counter economic impact from the coronavirus outbreak by injecting money into the\par financial system is likely to restore investor confidence in riskier assets.  \par     \par The local share price index futures rose 0.7%, a 13.7-point discount to the\par underlying S&P/ASX 200 index close. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL -  South Korean shares rallied on Tuesday on increasing bets that China would\par roll out more stimulus measures to counter the economic fallout from coronavirus.\par \par The Seoul stock market's main KOSPI ended up 39.02 points, or 1.84%, at\par 2,157.90, posting its steepest daily gain since Jan. 9, 2019.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par     NEW YORK - The safe-haven yen and Swiss franc fell for a second straight session\par against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday, with risk appetite growing as investors were\par encouraged that the Chinese government was taking measures to contain the coronavirus\par and limit its economic fallout.\par \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Australian dollar and offshore Chinese yuan climbed.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     CHINA - China's yuan managed to staunch further losses on Tuesday after skidding to\par a near two-month low in the previous session, with traders assessing Beijing's efforts\par to stabilise confidence in markets battered by the coronavirus epidemic in the country.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has flooded the economy with cash while trimming some key lending\par rates, but analysts suspect more will have to be done to offset the economic fallout\par from the virus as the death toll rose to 425.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - The Australian dollar rallied on Tuesday after the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held interest rates steady and stayed upbeat on the economic outlook despite the\par impact of bushfires at home and the coronavirus in China.\par \par The Aussie climbed 0.45% to $0.6720 and away from a four-month low of $0.6679,\par a modest bounce following almost three weeks of relentless selling.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - The Korean won and the benchmark bond yield rose on Tuesday.\par \par In offshore trading, the won was quoted at 1,186.7 per U.S. dollar, up 0.3% from\par the previous day, while in non-deliverable forward trading its one-month contract\par  was quoted at 1,186.1 per dollar.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TREASURIES\par     \par     NEW YORK - \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields rose on Tuesday as investors grew more comfortable\par with risk, encouraged by steps taken by China in response to the coronavirus.\par \par The benchmark 10-year yield was up 8.4 basis points in afternoon trading at\par 1.6043%.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - Core European government bond yields rose on Tuesday, following their U.S.\par counterparts after U.S. factory activity unexpectedly rebounded in January after\par contracting for five straight months, raising some hopes the global impact of the virus\par in China may be less severe than expected.\par \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Institute for Supply Management (ISM) \f28 \uc2 \f2 said on Monday its index of national factory\par activity increased to a reading of 50.9 last month, the highest level since July, from\par an upwardly revised 47.8 in December. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Prices for most Japanese government bonds edged lower on Tuesday as\par equities and other risky assets showed signs stabilisation after a sharp sell-off in\par the previous session on worries over the outbreak of a new coronavirus in China.\par \par Asian stocks bounced on Tuesday with Chinese markets reversing some of their previous\par plunge amid official efforts to calm virus fears.\par     For a full report, click on\par  \par     \par     COMMODITIES\par     \par     GOLD\par \par      Gold slid more than 1% on Tuesday as China's steps to mitigate the economic impact\par of the coronavirus epidemic drove some investors away from safe havens and back into\par riskier assets.\par \par Spot gold fell 1.61 % to $1,550.69 per ounce by 1:40 p.m. EST (1840 GMT), after\par hitting its lowest since Jan. 21 at $1,548.70. U.S. gold futures settled down\par 1.7% at $1,555.50.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     IRON ORE\par     \par      Iron ore futures in China fell to their lowest level in nearly three months on\par Tuesday, as worries mounted over the economic impact of a coronavirus outbreak, but\par benchmark steel futures rose after the previous session's selloff.\par \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dalian Commodity Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 's most-traded iron ore contract slumped as much\par as 6.1% to 569.50 yuan ($81.12) a tonne, its lowest since Nov. 12, before closing down\par 2.6%.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     BASE METALS\par     \par     Copper bounced on Tuesday as traders and funds reversed bets on lower prices after\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moved to offset the damage to economic growth and demand from the\par coronavirus in the top consumer.\par \par Traders said consumers not wanting to miss the opportunity to hedge their needs at low\par prices also contributed to the rise.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     OIL\par     \par     Oil prices edged lower on Tuesday as fears that energy demand would take a\par long-term hit from the coronavirus outbreak offset prospects for more cuts in crude\par production from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  and its allies.\par \par Brent crude slid 4 cents to $54.41 a barrel by 1:40 p.m. EST (1840 GMT) while\par U.S. West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude lost 9 cents to $50.02.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     PALM OIL\par     \par     Malaysian palm oil futures rose on Tuesday as prospects of a steep drop in\par production helped prices, outweighing the potential impact of a fast-spreading\par coronavirus on Chinese demand.\par \par The benchmark palm oil contract for April delivery on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bursa Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par Derivatives Exchange closed up 59 ringgit, or 2.26%, at 2,672 ringgit ($650.44) a\par tonne, the highest since Thursday.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     RUBBER\par     \par     Tokyo Commodity Exchange (TOCOM) futures ended higher on Tuesday, after slumping to\par a more than 3-month low, as investors looked for bargains and Shanghai pared early\par losses, though investors remained cautious due to the spreading coronavirus outbreak in\par China.\par \par TOCOM's rubber contract for July delivery, finished 1.7 yen higher at\par 174.7 yen ($1.60) per kg, after falling to as low as 165.6 yen, the lowest since Oct.\par 23, 2019 earlier in the session.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par \par  (Bengaluru Bureau; +91 80 6749 1130)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-2-4T21:51:42.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200204eg2403m0l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
