R US$         13,705         -35         INR US$             71.11        -0.211\par  MYR US$         4.109          -0.006      TWD US$             30.207       -0.125\par  CNY US$         6.9982         -0.0238     HKD US$             7.7654       -0.0006\par  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --     \par  Commodities                                                                 \par  Spot Gold       1,550.826      -25.224     Silver (Lon)        17.569       -0.0876\par  U.S. Gold Fut   1,555.9        -26.5       Brent Crude         54.58        0.13\par  Iron Ore        CNY587.0       -19.5       TRJCRB Index        -            -\par  TOCOM Rubber    JPY177.5       2.8         \f28 \uc2 \f2 LME\f28 \uc2 \f2  Copper          5,618        95\par  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par --  \par     ** indicates closing price \par     All prices as of 18:33 GMT\par     \par     EQUITIES\par \par     GLOBAL - Crude oil prices rebounded and global equity markets surged on Tuesday as\par China's efforts to minimize the economic impact from the coronavirus epidemic spurred\par investors' risk appetite.\par \par The price of gold and government debt slid on views China will do whatever it can to\par alleviate the economic toll from an outbreak that has killed more than 420 people and\par infected more than 20,000. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     NEW YORK - U.S. stocks surged on Tuesday and the S&P 500 and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nasdaq\f28 \uc2 \f2  were on pace to\par unwind losses from last week as fears of a heavy economic impact from the coronavirus\par epidemic tapered off after \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the second day.\par \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  injected 1.7 trillion yuan ($242.74 billion) via reverse repos on Monday and\par Tuesday, helping Chinese stocks reverse some losses and lifting the world equity index\par .\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - European shares clocked their biggest one-day gain in nearly four months\par on Tuesday as upbeat earnings updates from \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Glencore\f28 \uc2 \f2  along with\par China's moves to support its markets lifted sentiment.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  injected 1.7 trillion yuan ($243 billion) on Monday and Tuesday to\par stabilise markets that fell heavily on heightened fears over the potential economic\par fallout from the fast-spreading coronavirus.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO - Japanese stocks edged up on Tuesday, erasing early losses as Chinese shares\par steadied somewhat after crumbling the previous day, but sentiment remained fragile as\par investors fretted over the growing economic and human costs of a virus outbreak.\par \par The Nikkei index ended up 0.49% at 23,084.59, stepping off from a 10-week low hit in\par the previous session. Shares in the technology and consumer stables sectors rose.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - SHANGHAI/HONG KONG, Feb 4 (Reuters) - Chinese stocks ended higher on\par Tuesday as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  vowed to stabilise the market, regaining some ground from\par the coronavirus-led rout that erased almost $400 billion in market value from the\par Shanghai benchmark in the previous session.\par     \par The Shanghai Composite index <.SSEC closed up 1.3% at 2,783.29 \u8212\'20\'14 the biggest daily gain\par since Dec. 13, 2019. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - Australian shares on Tuesday recouped some of the sharp losses suffered\par in the previous session, driven by financial and healthcare stocks, although worries\par over the fast-spreading coronavirus outbreak capped gains.\par \par The S&P/ASX 200 index rose 0.4% to 6,948.7 points at the close of trade, having\par lost 1.3% on Monday.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - South Korean shares rallied on Tuesday on increasing bets that China would\par roll out more stimulus measures to counter the economic fallout from coronavirus. \par     \par The Seoul stock market's main KOSPI ended up 39.02 points, or 1.84%, at\par 2,157.90, posting its steepest daily gain since Jan. 9, 2019.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREIGN EXCHANGE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par \par     NEW YORK - The safe-haven yen and Swiss franc fell for a second straight session\par against the U.S. dollar on Tuesday, with risk appetite growing as investors were\par encouraged that the Chinese government was taking measures to contain the coronavirus\par and limit its economic fallout.\par \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the Australian dollar and offshore Chinese yuan climbed.\par     For a full report, click on\par \par     - - - -\par     \par     SHANGHAI - China's yuan managed to staunch further losses on Tuesday after skidding\par to a near two-month low in the previous session, with traders assessing Beijing's\par efforts to stabilise confidence in markets battered by the coronavirus epidemic in the\par country.\par \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has flooded the economy with cash while trimming some key lending\par rates, but analysts suspect more will have to be done to offset the economic fallout\par from the virus as the death toll rose to 425.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     AUSTRALIA - The Australian dollar rallied on Tuesday after the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  held interest rates steady and stayed upbeat on the economic outlook despite the\par impact of bushfires at home and the coronavirus in China.\par \par The Aussie climbed 0.45% to $0.6720 and away from a four-month low of $0.6679,\par a modest bounce following almost three weeks of relentless selling.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     SEOUL - The Korean won and the benchmark bond yield rose on Tuesday. \par      \par In offshore trading, the won was quoted at 1,186.7 per U.S. dollar, up 0.3% from\par the previous day, while in non-deliverable forward trading its one-month contract\par  was quoted at 1,186.1 per dollar.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TREASURIES\par     \par     NEW YORK - \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields rose on Tuesday as investors were encouraged by\par steps taken by China in response to the coronavirus.\par \par The benchmark 10-year yield was up 6.5 basis points in morning trading at\par 1.5854%.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     LONDON - Core European government bond yields rose on Tuesday, following their U.S.\par counterparts after U.S. factory activity unexpectedly rebounded in January after\par contracting for five straight months, raising some hopes the global impact of the virus\par in China may be less severe than expected.\par \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Institute for Supply Management (ISM) \f28 \uc2 \f2 said on Monday its index of national factory\par activity increased to a reading of 50.9 last month, the highest level since July, from\par an upwardly revised 47.8 in December. \par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     TOKYO -  Prices for most Japanese government bonds edged lower on Tuesday as\par equities and other risky assets showed signs stabilisation after a sharp sell-off in\par the previous session on worries over the outbreak of a new coronavirus in China.\par \par Asian stocks bounced on Tuesday with Chinese markets reversing some of their previous\par plunge amid official efforts to calm virus fears.\par     For a full report, click on\par  \par     \par     COMMODITIES\par     \par     GOLD\par \par     Gold slid more than 1% on Tuesday as Chinese steps to mitigate the economic impact\par from the coronavirus epidemic drove some investors away from safe havens and back into\par riskier assets.\par \par Spot gold was down 1.4% at $1,553.79 per ounce by 1555 GMT, after hitting its\par lowest since Jan. 22 at $1,550.75. U.S. gold futures fell 1.6% to $1,557.50.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     IRON ORE\par     \par      Iron ore futures in China fell to their lowest level in nearly three months on\par Tuesday, as worries mounted over the economic impact of a coronavirus outbreak, but\par benchmark steel futures rose after the previous session's selloff.\par \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dalian Commodity Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 's most-traded iron ore contract slumped as much\par as 6.1% to 569.50 yuan ($81.12) a tonne, its lowest since Nov. 12, before closing down\par 2.6%.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     BASE METALS\par     \par      Copper bounced on Tuesday as traders and funds reversed bets on lower prices after\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moved to offset the damage to economic growth and demand from the\par coronavirus in the top consumer.\par \par Traders said consumers not wanting to miss the opportunity to hedge their needs at low\par prices also contributed to the rise.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     OIL\par     \par     Oil prices bounced on Tuesday a day after falling to their lowest in more than a\par year, edging up on hopes that more cuts in crude production from \f28 \uc2 \f2 OPEC\f28 \uc2 \f2  and its allies\par would offset demand destruction related to the coronavirus outbreak.\par \par Brent crude <LCOc1< was up 65 cents, or 1.2%, to $55.10 a barrel by 11:45 a.m. EST\par (1645 GMT), while U.S. West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude gained 52 cents, or\par 1%, to $50.63.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     PALM OIL\par     \par      Malaysian palm oil futures rose on Tuesday as prospects of a steep drop in\par production helped prices, outweighing the potential impact of a fast-spreading\par coronavirus on Chinese demand.\par \par The benchmark palm oil contract for April delivery on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bursa Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par Derivatives Exchange closed up 59 ringgit, or 2.26%, at 2,672 ringgit ($650.44) a\par tonne, the highest since Thursday.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par     \par     RUBBER\par     \par     Tokyo Commodity Exchange (TOCOM) futures ended higher on Tuesday, after slumping to\par a more than 3-month low, as investors looked for bargains and Shanghai pared early\par losses, though investors remained cautious due to the spreading coronavirus outbreak in\par China.\par \par TOCOM's rubber contract for July delivery, finished 1.7 yen higher at\par 174.7 yen ($1.60) per kg, after falling to as low as 165.6 yen, the lowest since Oct.\par 23, 2019 earlier in the session.\par     For a full report, click on\par     \par     - - - -\par \par  (Bengaluru Bureau; +91 80 6749 1130)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-2-4T19:34:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200204eg2403c29\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
