\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-Europe  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - January 15\b0\par\par\uc2 1,434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2020\par 05:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Majors consolidate, await US-China trade deal details Market Briefs \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. to maintain tariffs on Chinese goods until Phase 2 deal -Mnuchin \u8226\'20\'22 Documents to show U.S.-China trade deal fully enforceable - Mnuchin \u8226\'20\'22 China to ramp up U.S.\par \par \uc2  buys under trade deal, but skeptics question targets \u8226\'20\'22 Trump administration moves toward blocking more sales to Huawei-sources \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 China \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injects $58 bln of loans but keeps rates steady \u8226\'20\'22 BOJ's Kuroda says won't hesitate to ease further as risks loom large \u8226\'20\'22 Democrats add last-minute evidence to Trump impeachment case before Senate trial \u8226\'20\'22 Sanders and Biden clash on foreign policy, trade in debate in Iowa \u8226\'20\'22 Forecast storms bring both relief and new risks for fire-stricken Australia Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 09:00 DE 2019 Full year GDP, 0.6% f'cast, 1.5% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 GB Dec CPI YY, 1.5% f'cast, 1.5% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 GB Dec RPI YY, 2.3% f'cast, 2.2% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 GB Dec Core CPI YY, 1.7% f'cast, 1.7% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 GB Dec PPI Input Prices YY NSA, -0.8% f'cast, -2.7% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 GB Dec PPI Output Prices YY NSA, 0.9% f'cast, 0.5% prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 GB Dec PPI Core Output YY NSA, 1.0% f'cast, 1.1% prev \u8226\'20\'22 10:00 EZ Nov Industrial Production YY, -1.1% f'cast, -2.2% prev \u8226\'20\'22 10:00 EZ Nov Eurostat Trade NSA (EUR), 23.3 bln f'cast, 28.0 bln prev Looking Ahead - Events, Auctions, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 N/A Roundtable with \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 's \uc2 Jon Cunliffe\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 08:40 Austrian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor \uc2 Robert Holzmann\uc2  speaks at conference in Vienna \u8226\'20\'22 08:40 Bank of England Member of the Monetary Policy Committee's \uc2 Michael Saunders\uc2  speaks at Southern Eastern Regional College, Bangor, Belfast \u8226\'20\'22 17:00 \uc2 Dallas Fed\uc2 's \uc2 Robert Kaplan\uc2  speaks before Economic Club of New York Signature Luncheon in New York \u8226\'20\'22 16:00 \uc2 Philadelphia Fed\uc2 's \uc2 Patrick Harker\uc2  speaks on "Monetary Policy Normalization: Low Interest Rates and the New Normal" in New York Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY, crosses off highs but still buoyant, consolidation \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY, JPY crosses in consolidation mode just below highs yesterday \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY 109.82-110.01 EBS, EUR/JPY 122.20-39 EBS, highs 110.22, 122.76 \u8226\'20\'22 Feel-good effects of US-China trade deal signing on wane \u8226\'20\'22 That said, risk not off - US yields steady, Nikkei off only on pos-adjusts \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY above hourly supports - @55-HMA, above also ascending 100-HMA \u8226\'20\'22 Options supportive too, more expiries below than above today/going forward \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY, for one, looking to see some Japanese investor demand EUR/USD-Consolidates in narrow range ahead of US-China deal \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD barely moved in Asia trading in a 1.1126/31 range \u8226\'20\'22 Most of the action in regionals as USD moved up on Mnuchin comments \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance at 10-day MA at 1.1140 with option selling ahead of 1.1150 \u8226\'20\'22 Large option maturities 1.1100/50 defining recent range \u8226\'20\'22 Break above 1.1150 targets 61.8 fibo of 1.1240/1.1085 at 1.1181 \u8226\'20\'22 EZ IP later today, but US-China trade deal likely to be main event AUD/USD-Settles below 0.6900 in mild risk-off session \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened 0.6904 and slipped to 0.6891 when risk assets moved lower \u8226\'20\'22 Risk-off tone prompted by Mnuchin comments on tariffs \u8226\'20\'22 AXJ equity index eased around 0.40% and S&P futures fell 0.25% \u8226\'20\'22 Buyers around 0.6890 held and AUD/USD settled 0.6895/0.6900 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD vulnerable to 'sell the rumour/buy the fact' around trade deal \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance at 10-day MA at 0.6907 and 21-day MA at 0.6924 \u8226\'20\'22 Support at triple bottom at 0.6850 and 100-day MA at 0.6837 GBP/USD-Treads water into inflation data, bias is lower \u8226\'20\'22 Little changed in a very tight 1.3015-1.3023 range with only light interest \u8226\'20\'22 Investment in UK's tech sector jumped 44% in 2019, London led \u8226\'20\'22 Techs - daily charts are negative, though at low end of recent ranges \u8226\'20\'22 Momentum studies, 5, 10 & 21 DMAs head lower and 21 day Bolli bands slip \u8226\'20\'22 1.2900, December low then 1.2887 lower 21 day Bolli band are major support \u8226\'20\'22 Close above 1.3074, 38.2% of the 2020 fall would end downside bias \u8226\'20\'22 1.3000 701M strikes and NY 1.3034 high are initial support-resistance \u8226\'20\'22 Inflation data will be key for BoE expectations in London USD/JPY to consolidate on higher plane @110 for now The USD/JPY rally to a near eight-month high of 110.22 on EBS Tuesday was a relatively sedate affair, with option barriers tripped at 110.00 early in Asia after those tipped at 109.80 were taken out the night before. Stops were likely tripped above both levels but trading volume to and above these levels is reported to have been heavy, and the moves look to have been much less volatile than when key levels were breached in the past. With further gains on hold pending fresh news on the U.S.-China trade deal signing in the U.S. later today, USD/JPY is likely to consolidate around 110.00. Asia's range so far has been 109.82-110.01, and Tokyo players suggest 109.80-110.20 or 109.70-110.10 should contain the pair for now. These ranges or a slightly broader 109.50-110.50 range could be the new normal for USD/JPY indefinitely, with euphoria over a Phase I trade deal fading and the deal itself really not all that impressive as the U.S. plans to maintain tariffs on Chinese imports until the completion of a Phase 2 trade deal. Are the days of the supposedly stable CNY over? The days of the stable yuan could be over, or maybe not. U.S. Treasury Secretary Mnuchin said the details of the Phase 1 trade deal to be signed today will show that China's pledge to refrain from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation is fully enforceable. The U.S. could demand the yuan appreciate, but it's unlikely China would swallow such a bitter pill. More probable is that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  will have to report instances of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which may be allowed to some extent to quell excessive volatility. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities have a history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to guide the yuan, and as much as Beijing still claims to want \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability, actions speak louder than words. Chief among these was the August 2015 devaluation, as well as subsequent moves to stem rapid CNY appreciation. To be fair, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has also regularly arrested yuan declines, often by holding down its daily USD/CNY reference rate. These strong-arm \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  management policies could change in line with the Phase 1 deal. Instead of overt yuan policy shifts that irk trade partners, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  might evolve towards a more tactical application of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls, similar to Singapore's policy of closely managing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against an undisclosed trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . INR woes grow as economy heads towards stagflation The Indian rupee just cannot catch a break, with economic stagflation now adding to its long list of travails. India's December consumer inflation galloped to 7.35%, its highest in more than five years, economic growth slowed to 4.5% in the July-September quarter, the weakest since 2013 and rising unemployment is contributing to social unrest. The three signs of stagflation are firmly in place and the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2  and government appear helpless to turn the economy around due to monetary and fiscal policy constraints. A surge in food prices has carried inflation well above the \uc2 Reserve Bank of India\uc2 's 2-6% target range and will prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from cutting rates further to stimulate the economy. Some analysts now expect the RBI to shift to a neutral bias from its accommodative stance. To make matters worse, the government is likely to cut spending as it faces one of the biggest tax shortfalls in recent years, further weighing on growth. USD/INR's three-month 70.35-72.24 range is likely to remain intact and buying dips to 70.35-50 with a 69.90 stop loss can be considered, for a target of 72.20-40.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-1-18T04:35:12.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200115eg1f00ga5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
