\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-U.S. Treasury drops China  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  manipulator label ahead of trade deal signing\b0\par\par\uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2020\par 01:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Lighthizer comment)\par \par \uc2 By Andrea Shalal and Alexandra Alper\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Jan 13 (Reuters) - The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  on Monday dropped its designation of China as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator days before top officials of the world's two largest economies were due to sign a preliminary trade agreement to ease an 18-month-old tariff war.\par \par \uc2 The widely expected decision came in a long-delayed semi-annual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, reversing an unexpected move by Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2  last August at the height of U.S.-China trade tensions.\par \par \uc2 Mnuchin had accused China of deliberately holding down the value of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to create an unfair trade advantage, just hours after President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , angered at the lack of progress in trade negotiations, had also accused China of manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Department had not labeled China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator since 1994. Beijing had recently met just one of the department's three criteria needed for such a designation - a large bilateral trade surplus with the United States.\par \par \uc2 In its latest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, the Treasury said that as part of the Phase 1 trade deal, China had made "enforceable commitments to refrain from competitive devaluation" and agreed to publish relevant data on exchange rates and external balances.\par \par \uc2 Chinese Vice Premier Liu He arrived in Washington on Monday for a White House ceremony to sign the trade deal with Trump. People familiar with the negotiations said that although the manipulator designation had no real consequences for Beijing, its removal was an important symbol of goodwill for Chinese officials.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Trade Representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2  on Monday told Fox Business that the translation of the U.S.-China trade agreement was almost completed and the text of the deal would be made public on Wednesday before the ceremony.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report said the Chinese yuan, also known as the renminbi, had depreciated as far as 7.18 per U.S. dollar in early September, but had rebounded in October and was currently trading at about 6.93 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 "In this context, Treasury has determined that China should no longer be designated as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator at this time," the report said.\par \par \uc2 It said, however, China should take decisive steps to avoid a persistently weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allow greater market openness to strengthen its long-term growth prospects.\par \par \uc2 There was no immediate reaction from Beijing. In August, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  denied it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yuan, and said Washington's designation of China as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator seriously harmed international rules.\par \par \uc2 Mark Sobel, a former senior Treasury official and adviser to the London-based OMFIF economy policy think tank, said China "was errantly designated at a moment of presidential pique."\par \par \uc2 "It should never have happened in the first place," he said. "China manages, but does not manipulate its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Sobel said China's current account surplus was small as a share of gross domestic product and it had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for years. The August move came at a time when the yuan had fallen against the dollar because of market apprehension over Trump's "ratcheting up of trade tariffs," he said.\par \par \uc2 SWITZERLAND ADDED TO MONITORING LIST\par \par \uc2 The Treasury report also cited continued concerns about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices of eight other countries - Germany, Ireland, Italy, Japan, Malaysia, Singapore, South Korea and Vietnam - and added a ninth, Switzerland, to its list.\par \par \uc2 It raised particular concerns about Germany, the world's fourth largest economy, which it said continued to have the world's largest current account surplus and was slipping into recession. It said the \uc2 German government\uc2  had a responsibility to undertake tax cuts and boost domestic investment.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury report said the continued strength of the U.S. dollar was "concerning," given the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's judgment that the dollar was overvalued on a real effective basis.\par \par \uc2 It said the real dollar remains about 8% above its 20-year average, noting that sustained dollar strength would likely exacerbate persistent trade and current account imbalances for the United States.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Senate Democratic leader Chuck Schumer, a fierce critic of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and trade practices, blasted the Trump administration for its decision to "back down" from labeling China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 "China is a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator - that is a fact," Schumer said in a statement. "Unfortunately, President Trump would rather cave to President Xi (Jinping) than stay tough on China."\par \par \uc2 The yuan reached five-month highs earlier on Monday ahead of the expected signing of the trade deal. (Reporting by Andrea Shalal and Alexandra Alper in Washington Additional reporting by David Lawder and Eric Beech in Washington, and Kanishka Singh in Bengaluru; Editing by Leslie Adler and Rosalba O'Brien) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-1-14T01:45:22.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200113eg1d03rhu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
