\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan strengthens to a 5-mth high ahead of signing of China-U.S. trade deal\b0\par\par\uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Januar 2020\par 05:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2020 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     SHANGHAI, Jan 13 (Reuters) - China's yuan made five-month\par highs on Monday amid heavy demand ahead of the signing later\par this week of a trade deal with the United States that includes\par clauses against competitive devaluations.\par     Seasonal dollar-selling by Chinese companies needing to make\par yuan payments ahead of the Lunar New Year holiday also helped\par fuel the yuan's rise, traders say.\par     The yuan rose 0.2% against the greenback to 6.9011 at\par midday, after \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the strongest midpoint\par  since August 5, 2019. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has gained roughly\par 2% since early December. \par     Traders said they saw few signs of \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb the yuan's gains. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  vowed on Jan. 5\par to let market forces "play a determinant role" in exchange rates\par in 2020.\par     On Jan. 15, President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Donald Trump\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Chinese Premier Liu\par He are scheduled to meet in Washington to sign a Phase I trade\par deal to ease the 18-month trade war. The agreement will include\par clauses that seek to prevent China from devaluing the yuan to\par "unfairly compete against U.S. exporters," the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. government\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par has said.\par     Trump labeled China a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator last August,\par after \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  allowed the yuan to weaken past the key 7-per-dollar\par level for the first time in more than a decade. \par     "The U.S. hopes to see the yuan strengthen and China wants\par to see a stable \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that doesn't depreciate too much,\u8221\'20\'1D said\par Ding Jianping, professor at the Shanghai University of Finance\par and Economics. \par     Therefore, the yuan's recovery from last year's excessive\par fall "chimes in easily with both sides."\par     Xie Dongming, analyst at OCBC Bank, said an expected decline\par in the dollar index this year, and continuous foreign inflows\par into China's capital markets could help push the yuan up further\par to 6.7-6.8 per dollar this year. \par     A Reuters poll of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists published last Friday,\par however, predicted the yuan will still be buffeted over the\par coming year as U.S.-China trade relations are expected to remain\par rocky despite recent hopes for some resolution.   \par     The Thomson Reuters/HKEX Global CNH index, which\par tracks the offshore yuan against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a\par daily basis, stood at 93.13, firmer than the previous day's\par 92.91. \par     The global dollar index fell to 97.35 from the\par previous close of 97.356. \par     The offshore yuan was trading 0.02 percent away\par from the onshore spot at 6.8995 per dollar. \par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par (NDFs), considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.9697, 0.62 percent away from the midpoint.\par     One-year NDFs are settled against the midpoint, not the spot\par rate.\par \par \par     The yuan market at 3:54AM GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.9263   6.9351    0.13%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.9011   6.9191    0.26%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    -0.36%             \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       0.90%\par  Spot change since 2005                19.93%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \par  Item            Current     Previous  Change\par                                        \par  Thomson         93.13       92.91     0.2\par  Reuters/HKEX                          \par  CNH index                             \par  Dollar index    97.35       97.356    0.0\par  \par     \par     \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.8995    0.02%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              6.9697    -0.62%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore\par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par .\par \par  (Reporting by Samuel Shen and Andrew Galbraith; Editing by\par Simon Cameron-Moore)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2020-1-13T05:22:48.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020200113eg1d00cf9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
