\par\par \b \uc2 MORNING BID EUROPE-A breather but no real respite\b0\par\par\uc2 1,263 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2019\par 10:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nov 15 (Reuters) - Markets have been likened to a drug addict looking for the next fix. Or maybe it's like being in a relationship with an alcoholic and always hoping for recovery (according to one analyst). Well, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stimulus has for years been that fix, but these days there is also the trade war, with markets swinging up or down on every headline as the Dec. 15 deadline for extra tariffs approaches. After U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's disappointing speech on Tuesday, they've latched on to comments by his economics adviser, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 , that Beijing and Washington were nearing a deal. Oft-repeated words, maybe, but what the heck - risk is back on, bond yields and the yen are lower, stock markets and oil are higher. An index of world shares has risen 0.2%, though it's on track to snap a five-week winning streak, given the stream of weak economic data out of almost everywhere.\par \par \uc2  The S&P 500 shrugged off dire earnings yesterday to reach a record high, Japanese shares rose 0.7% and Europe is opening higher.\par \par \uc2 But markets can only rise so much. The latest Chinese economic numbers yesterday showed that industrial production and retail sales were continuing to slow. The \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly added 200 billion yuan in liquidity injections in what\u8217\'20\'19s seen as an attempt to ease conditions without cutting rates. Some reckon the move sets the stage for a cut in the new lending benchmark rate next week.\par \par \uc2 Then we have the events in Hong Kong and the fears of what might happen next. That pushed Hong Kong shares 4.6% lower this week, the biggest weekly loss since early August. Chinese shares were down, too, shrugging off the trade noise to head for the biggest weekly decline in three months.\par \par \uc2 Concern is growing that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the increasingly violent protests, the ripples from which are starting to be felt far and wide. Hong Kong itself has confirmed its economy has plunged into recession for the first time in a decade. Money-market rates have rocketed 100 basis points this week, approaching highs touched in July when the protests took off. Of course, much of that is driven by the impending Alibaba IPO, which is expected to suck money out of the system, but watch this space if the protests escalate further and capital flight picks up. So far, there's no sign of flight, apparently.\par \par \uc2 As for knock-on effects, sales of \uc2 Burberry\uc2  provided an insight of what the impact could be on luxury markets. Sales to Hong Kong were down in the first half, and Ferragamo has said the turmoil has severely hit sales from July onward. More of this is likely, too.\par \par \uc2 Still, Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  sounded upbeat yesterday about the U.S. economy and in no mood to cut interest rates further. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President \uc2 Luis de Guindos\uc2  said he considered a euro zone recession highly unlikely. Today we\u8217\'20\'19ll get to see if that optimism is justified - headline euro zone inflation is expected to be confirmed at 0.7%, well below the \uc2 ECB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s target rate of just under 2%, with core prices higher at 1.1%. We also get euro zone trade figures. U.S. retail sales figures may show if Powell\u8217\'20\'19s cheerful comments hold water.\par \par \uc2 Yields in Germany and the United States were set to end the week 6 to 8 basis points lower, with Bund yields headed for their biggest weekly decline since late September.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , polls implying a majority for the Conservatives in Dec. 12 elections have taken sterling to a six- month high to the euro but no further moves from those levels. The dollar has risen from 10-day lows against the yen, but it\u8217\'20\'19s still set for the biggest weekly drop against the yen since early October. The Chinese yuan is headed for the first weekly decline in six weeks.\par \par \uc2 European stock markets were opening higher and Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX, a typical gauge of trade war fear, were up a quarter percent. Autos, a key sector to play the U.S.-China feud were up 0.3%. The pan-European STOXX 600 is only 11 points from its April 2015 record high of 415.18 points. It\u8217\'20\'19s rising today but still off the four-year highs touched earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2 The big news in the UK market is BT\u8217\'20\'19s 2% fall after Labour said it would nationalise parts of telecoms provider BT's network if it won power in the Dec. 12 election, to provide free full-fibre broadband for all. Labour is not expected to win the election, though.\par \par \uc2 Price action is expected in Scandinavia, where Nordic property firm SBB launched a $2.42 billion cash and shares bid for its rival \uc2 Hemfosa\uc2 . The price constitutes a premium of 22.7% to the closing price on Thursday, so all eyes will be on how investors arbitrage the price gap. Another share under the spotlight will be Europcar after Reuters reported its main shareholder, France\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Eurazeo\uc2 , hired \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  for a possible sale of its 29.8% stake.\par \par \uc2 Altice is expected to rise at the open after it said its sale of its Portuguese fibre network was progressing well.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Delivery Hero\uc2  said it would continue to look at every single acquisition and that Europe would break even for the full year.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market stocks edged up 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s lira was 0.3% weaker after Washington proposed Ankara get rid of the Russian S-400 missile defences it purchased, while the latest Turkish data showed unemployment rose to 14%, but still below a 10-year peak. The Republican chairman of the U.S. Senate Foreign Relations Committee said he did not want lawmakers to pass legislation imposing sanctions on Turkey for now.\par \par \uc2 South Africa's rand is up 0.3%, its strongest level in a week. Workers at \uc2 South African Airways\uc2  began strikes on Friday over wages and job cuts that have forced the state-owned carrier to cancel all flights and left its future unclear.\par \par \uc2 Chile's peso slid to a low against the dollar on Thursday and is on course for its worst week since 2011 as the government warned as many as 300,000 jobs could be lost because of the impact of four weeks of rioting.\par \par \uc2 For its third meeting in a row, \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates, lowering its key interest rate by 25 basis points to 7.50% and flagging that the economic growth outlook had probabl y worsened in recent months.\par \par \uc2 In Lebanon, three major Lebanese parties agreed to nominate Mohammad Safadi, a former finance minister, to become prime minister.\par \par \uc2 China Oct house prices SKorea Oct trade balance Japan Sept industrial output Hong Kong Q3 GDP \uc2 Sri Lanka \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy decision Europe corp events: LEG Immobilien, Simcorp, \uc2 AP Moeller-Maersk\uc2  EZ, Italy Sept trade balance, Oct inflation Turkey Oct budget balance \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Mersch speaks in Paris; ECB supervisor Jochnick speaks in Frankfurt ECOFIN budget meeting in Brussels US Oct retail sales, industrial production, export/import prices \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  chief Poloz speaks in San Francisco Sovereign credit rating reviews \u8211\'20\'13 S&P Global reviews Netherlands, Iceland, Burkino Faso. \uc2 Moody\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  reviews Spain, Zambia, Republic of Congo, Isle of Man. Fitch reviews Ireland, Belarus; DBRS reviews Italy, Cyprus, Finland (Reporting by Sujata Rao) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-11-15T10:22:54.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191115efbf01i0h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
