\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Stocks fall as Hong Kong protests go on; Turkish lira weakens\b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2019\par 11:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * HK stocks down about 1% as protests continue\par \par \uc2 * Turkey's lira down after Trump-Erdogan meeting\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds interest rates\par \par \uc2 * Rouble up after strong third-quarter GDP numbers\par \par \uc2 By Sagarika Jaisinghani and Susan Mathew\par \par \uc2 Nov 14 (Reuters) - Anti-government protests in Hong Kong led to a decline by emerging-market stocks on Thursday, while the Turkish lira fell after President \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 's visit to Washington did little to ease concerns about ties with the United States.\par \par \uc2 The lira lost 0.5% as U.S. President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  described his meeting with Erdogan as "wonderful," but fell short of explaining how they would overcome such issues as Syria policy and Turkey's purchase of Russian defence systems.\par \par \uc2 "The market is assessing the possibility that the U.S. may be inclined to impose sanctions on Turkey for a number of different issues, primarily the purchase of the Russian S-400 systems," said Cristian Maggio, head of emerging market strategy at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A stronger Indian rupee and Philippine peso helped limit declines on a broader index of emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng index fell nearly 1% to a one-month low as protesters paralysed parts of the Asian financial hub for a fourth day, closing schools and blocking highways.\par \par \uc2 The index is on track for its biggest weekly decline in three-and-a-half months. It's shed nearly 8% since the protests turned violent in June.\par \par \uc2 "The biggest risk here is that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some point loses patience and decides to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  (and) that would definitely precipitate an increase in friction with the U.S.," Maggio said.\par \par \uc2 As trade tensions between the world's two largest economies heightened this week, China said it was holding "in-depth" discussions with Washington and that cancelling tariffs was an important condition to reaching a deal.\par \par \uc2 Chinese stocks rebounded from six-month lows after a slowdown in industrial activity in October led to bets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again after cutting a key policy rate this month.\par \par \uc2 The Russian rouble gained against a weaker dollar for the first time in five sessions, as data showed Russia's economic growth picked up in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso also strengthened for the first time in nearly two weeks after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its benchmark interest rate unchanged, as expected, following stronger-than-expected growth in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 For GRAPHIC on emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  performance 2019, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/2egbfVh}}}\uc2  For GRAPHIC on MSCI emerging index performance 2019, see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/2OusNdX"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://tmsnrt.rs/2OusNdX}}}\par \par \uc2 For TOP NEWS across emerging markets\par \par \uc2 For CENTRAL EUROPE market report, see\par \par \uc2 For TURKISH market report, see\par \par \uc2 For RUSSIAN market report, see (Reporting by Sagarika Jaisinghani and Susan Mathew in Bengaluru; editing by Larry King) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-11-14T11:23:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191114efbe020zt\par }\page