\par\par \b \uc2 China  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Stayed on Sidelines as Outflows Eased: CE -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,359 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2019\par 10:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0923 GMT - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to refrain from direct \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, thanks to easing trade tensions and widening \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. interest-rate spreads, says Julian Evans-Pritchard, an economist with \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . He estimates China's capital outflows dropped to $5 billion in October, down from $21 billion in September. The decline in outflows should give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  less reason to worry about the impact of monetary easing on the balance of payments and exchange rates, he adds. (grace.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0919 GMT - A broadly weaker dollar due to reports that a U.S.-China trade deal could be delayed until December helps EUR/USD edge higher, but the euro's rise is limited following earlier weak German industrial production data. EUR/USD is last up 0.1% at 1.1081 but this is "mostly dollar driven," says \uc2 London Capital Group\uc2  's Ipek Ozkardeskaya, as the DXY dollar index falls 0.1% to 97.87. EUR/USD earlier hit a three-week low of 1.1055 and Ozkardeskaya says the bigger-than-expected fall in German industrial production may give traders a stronger incentive to sell. Industrial output fell 0.6% on the month in September, a bigger drop than the 0.3% decline forecast in a WSJ poll. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says the German data confirm manufacturers "are still firmly in a recession," adding: "We expect this to drag on until well into next year." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0909 GMT - German GDP data due next week will show a slight contraction in 3Q, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's \uc2 Ralph Solveen\uc2  says. Industrial production data showed 3Q output down 1.1% compared with 2Q for the manufacturing sector and the producing sector, which includes construction and energy. "The figures support the expectation that real GDP shrank slightly in the third quarter as well, thus fulfilling a frequently used definition of a recession," says Solveen. German GDP contracted in 2Q and a technical recession is defined at two negative quarters consecutively. Manufacturing orders on Wednesday showing an increase in September briefly gave cause for optimism, but \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  nevertheless expects a slight drop in 4Q production. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0843 GMT - \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees potential for further interest rate cuts but won't be rushed into reducing rates too fast, Governor \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2  says. Last month, the bank lowered its key rate for the fourth time since June, to 6.5% from 7%, highlighting the risk of a "substantial" slowdown in global economic growth. Ms. Nabiullina says on Thursday that further cuts are possible given declining inflation expectations. But, she adds: "we will not make a decision automatically, hastening to reduce the rate as much as possible." (georgi.kantchev@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0830 GMT - A correction in rates has on aggregate moved the yield the iBoxx EUR corporate index in all maturity categories out of negative-yield territory, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . With rates tentatively stabilizing, the increased yield levels "should therefore now impose less of a floor" on the spreads of higher-rated and shorter-dated maturities such as subordinated 5-year single As than in previous months, the bank says. Easing trade tensions, latest Brexit developments and better-than-expected macro economic data have pushed government bond yields higher. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT - German industrial production data indicates that any optimism on the industry's outlook is premature, ING Germany Chief Economist \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  says. Industrial output fell 0.6% in September from August in calendar-adjusted terms. Manufacturing orders data a day ago, which showed a 1.3% adjusted increase, provided a dash of optimism for the sector. "Hopes for a quick rebound of German industry were short-lived," Brzeski says. ING expects German industry to rebound in the first quarter of 2020. "So far, we only know that industry is in recession but not necessarily the entire economy," he says. For ING, the German economy's direction in 3Q will depend on the export sector. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT - "The decline in industrial production in September confirms that German manufacturers are still firmly in a recession," says Andrew Kenningham, chief economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . Industrial output fell 0.6% in September from August in calendar-adjusted terms, slightly below Capital Economics ' forecast. Excluding energy and construction, output fell more than 1.3%, suggesting the underlying situation is worse than the headline figures, Kenningham says. Industrial production fell around 1.0% in the third quarter, the fifth successive quarterly contraction. "Both the Manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  output index and the manufacturing component of the Ifo Business Climate Index edged up in October, but they both still point to production contracting," says Kenningham. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0803 GMT - Eurozone government-bond yields rise, shrugging off weaker-than-expected German industrial production data for September, which disappoint after Wednesday's better-than-forecast manufacturing-orders data for the same month. Industrial production fell by 0.6% in adjusted terms in September over August, the Federal Statistical Office said, while economists in The Wall Street Journal's survey forecast a 0.3% decline. Supply will come from Spain and France, with the former selling up to EUR4.25 billion in 2024-, 2029- and 2035-dated nominal and 2023-dated inflation-linked bonds, while the latter auctioning up to EUR10 billion in 2029-, 2034-, 2050- and 2066-dated OATs. The 10-year Bund yield is trading at -0.312%, up 1.4 basis points, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT - Sterling dips against the euro ahead of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's interest rate decision, policy meeting minutes and latest economic projections Thursday at 1200 GMT, followed by Governor \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  's press conference at 1230 GMT. The \uc2 BoE\uc2  is expected to keep its key interest rate at 0.75% so the focus is on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  provides any clues on future policy action in its statements. "Any signs of the \uc2 BoE\uc2  indicating an easing policy will likely lead to a further selloff in the pound," \uc2 Hussein Sayed\uc2  at FXTM says. EUR/GBP rises 0.1% to 0.8620 while GBP/USD is broadly flat at 1.2850. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT - Momentum for USD/SGD is mildly bullish for now, Maybank says, as news of a possible delay in the signing of a partial U.S.-China trade deal opens up cracks in investors' optimism that the conflict will be resolved. Maybank expects USD/SGD will be reluctant to follow USD/\uc2 CNH\uc2  moves downward due to the elevated SGD NEER, reckoning the pair will lean more toward tracking upward moves in USD/\uc2 CNH\uc2 . Maybank puts USD/SGD support at 1.3540 and resistance at 1.3660. USD/SGD is flat at 1.3596 versus 1.3592 late Wednesday in New York. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0432 GMT - China's economy needs to expand by at least 6.1% next year--roughly the same as this year's growth rate--to fulfill Beijing's commitment to double the size of the economy from 2010, China Merchants Securities says. That estimate factors in inflation and the government's adjustments in calculating GDP, it says. CMS concurs with other economists that China's economy will struggle to meet that goal without extra policy supports such as increased government spending on infrastructure projects and rebuilding of slum areas. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0258 GMT - Australia's trade surplus has been setting records through the year and has led to the country posting a current account surplus in the second quarter, its first since 1975. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  expects the economy to register a comfortable surplus in the third quarter as well, helped by all-time-high commodity prices. Notably, surging gold prices are offsetting smaller declines in other major commodity exports. Iron-ore exports were 5.5% lower on month in September, though in annual terms they expanded 64%. (rachel.pannett@wsj.com @rachelpannett)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 07, 2019 04:23 ET (09:23 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020191107efb70016t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
