\par\par \b \uc2 Global Equities Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2019\par 10:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering Equities. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0935 GMT - The Stoxx Europe 600 gains 0.4%, or 1.57 points to 406.64 on hopes of progress in U.S.-China trade relations and as oil prices rise. The DAX is up 0.7% and the CAC-40 advances 0.3%. Reports suggest Beijing and Washington have agreed to gradually roll back costly tariffs imposed during the last few months of the trade row to secure "phase one" of a deal. "Again this isn't the same as action, and is yet to be confirmed by the U.S.," says Connor Campbell at \uc2 Spreadex\uc2  . "However, it continues a string of news stories pointing in the right direction, helping the European indices pop higher after the bell." Meanwhile, the price of a barrel of Brent crude climbs 0.9% to $62.30 after a study predicted a slowdown in U.S. shale-oil output in the next couple of years. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT - Singapore stocks closed higher, with the FTSE Straits Times Index up 0.7% at 3285.72, following news that China and the U.S. agreed to cancel tariffs in phases. Developments on the U.S.-China trade deal are likely to continue playing a key role in guiding market action in Asian markets, IG says. Among bank stocks, DBS gained 1.0%, while \uc2 OCBC\uc2  and \uc2 UOB\uc2  were each 0.3% higher. Property stocks were also up, as \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  rose 1.9%, \uc2 City Developments\uc2  gained 0.3% and \uc2 UOL\uc2  edged up 0.4%. Telco stocks also fared better, with \uc2 StarHub\uc2  up 2.1% and \uc2 Singtel\uc2  0.9% higher. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0927 GMT - Lagardere's third-quarter organic growth was decent and beat market expectations for the French media company, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. "While some of this is one-off in nature (in particular in Publishing) we think the robust performance in Travel Retail will settle nerves," the brokerage says. Travel retail performance was especially strong in Asia, with limited impact from trade tensions or disorder in Hong Kong, it says. \uc2 Lagardere\uc2  trades 2.1% up at EUR19.88. (olivia.bugault@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0926 GMT - Malaysia's benchmark index rose after news that the U.S. and China agreed to roll back existing tariffs in phases. The FTSE Bursa Malaysia KLCI gained 0.4% to 1609.33. Conglomerate Sime Darby was the top gainer in the index, up 3.1%. Plantation stocks also advanced, with \uc2 Kuala Lumpur Kepong\uc2  up 2.7% while casinos-to-plantation group Genting gained 2.5%. Financials also traded higher, with \uc2 Hong Leong Financial Group\uc2  up 1.6%, while \uc2 AMMB Holdings\uc2  closed 1.2% higher. Conglomerate IOI Corp was the biggest loser of the index, down 0.9% (yiwei.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0923 GMT - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to refrain from direct \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, thanks to easing trade tensions and widening \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. interest-rate spreads, says Julian Evans-Pritchard, an economist with \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . He estimates China's capital outflows dropped to $5 billion in October, down from $21 billion in September. The decline in outflows should give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  less reason to worry about the impact of monetary easing on the balance of payments and exchange rates, he adds. (grace.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0921 GMT - \uc2 Lufthansa\uc2  's third-quarter results beat market consensus, with adjusted EBIT 6% ahead of expectations, Berenberg says. Investors should be reassured today thanks to the market beat and guidance's confirmation, the brokerage says. "Revenue appears to be the source of the beat alongside better unit costs in the group's Network Airlines segment," it says. Berenberg notes that the strike impact is still uncertain. The German carrier trades 6.5% higher at EUR17.20. (olivia.bugault@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0909 GMT - German GDP data due next week will show a slight contraction in 3Q, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's \uc2 Ralph Solveen\uc2  says. Industrial production data showed 3Q output down 1.1% compared with 2Q for the manufacturing sector and the producing sector, which includes construction and energy. "The figures support the expectation that real GDP shrank slightly in the third quarter as well, thus fulfilling a frequently used definition of a recession," says Solveen. German GDP contracted in 2Q and a technical recession is defined at two negative quarters consecutively. Manufacturing orders on Wednesday showing an increase in September briefly gave cause for optimism, but \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  nevertheless expects a slight drop in 4Q production. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT - The FTSE 100 rises 0.4% to 7423 on the back of a broadly positive reaction to a flurry of corporate results. Auto Trader is the blue-chip index's top riser, up 3.7% after the automotive listings publisher reported a 12% gain in first-half profit and voiced confidence about meeting annual growth expectations. U.K. home-builder \uc2 Persimmon\uc2  gains 1.3% as it reported in-line summer trading and said it is experiencing the usual pick-up in customer activity towards the autumn season. \uc2 J.Sainsbury\uc2  is up 1.6% after it said a decline in its food sales was slowing and said it continues to expect profits in the second half to benefit from normalization of costs. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT - Vestas shares hit the top of the Stoxx Europe 600 index after 3Q orders, revenue and Ebit all beat expectations. "The clear beat on orders and Ebit and the retained 2019 guidance will be seen as the key positives, especially against the backdrop of the share price weakness into numbers," says \uc2 Citi\uc2 . Order intake came in 32% above consensus and while order average selling price was just below consensus, sales beat by 10% and pre-items Ebit margin by 15%, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. Guidance was reiterated but the service outlook improved and the EUR200 million buyback is lower than the EUR400 million \uc2 Citi\uc2  had expected. "While the debate on 2020 will remain until Vestas initiates 2020 guidance in February, we see these results as very re-assuring, especially after recent results from peers." Shares trade 8.8% higher at DKK590.80. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT - \uc2 Sodexo\uc2  's FY19 results were mixed, \uc2 Citi\uc2  analysts say as shares in the French company rise. \uc2 Sodexo\uc2  delivered 3.6% organic growth for the year, beating its guidance of about 3% but implying a slight slowdown in 4Q growth compared to 3Q, the bank says. Even so, \uc2 Citi\uc2  is disappointed by the company's FY20 guidance for a stable margin, which it believes casts doubt on whether \uc2 Sodexo\uc2  's earlier higher margin aspiration is achievable. \uc2 Sodexo\uc2  trades 6.3% higher at EUR106.05. (cristina.roca@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0839 GMT - Shares in Auto Trader rise 7.5% after the automotive listings publisher reported a 12% gain in first-half profit and voiced confidence about meeting its growth expectations for the year. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  says group revenue was broadly in line and pretax profit before interest was a touch above consensus expectations. "Auto Trader has a dominant position in the U.K. and attractive 6% free cash-flow yield," says \uc2 RBC\uc2 's Sherri Malek, re-iterating the brokerage's sector-perform recommendation. "However, valuation prevents us from being more constructive." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0833 GMT - Hong Kong shares rose after a dull start as investors cheered the news that the U.S. and China agreed to lift existing tariffs in stages. The Hang Seng Index, which started to climb after the news broke in afternoon, ended up 0.6% at 27847.23, rising for the sixth consecutive session. \uc2 CSPC Pharmaceutical\uc2  and \uc2 Sino Biopharmaceutical\uc2  were the top gainers on the HSI with shares up 4.4% and 2.2%, respectively. The two Chinese drug makers have reported a slew of drug approvals recently, boosting investors' confidence in their outlooks. Bank stocks were also broadly stronger, with \uc2 Hang Seng Bank\uc2  2.0% higher and \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  1.7% higher. (martin.mou@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 07, 2019 04:35 ET (09:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020191107efb7001cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
