\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,460 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2019\par 10:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2019, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0930 GMT - Despite easing trade tensions with the U.S., softening demand probably weighed on China's trade of goods with the rest of the world, economists say. China's exports likely fell 3.1% in October from a year earlier, following September's 3.2% decrease, according to the median forecast of 15 economists polled by WSJ. Imports likely slipped 8.6% after an 8.5% decline in September, the poll shows. China's trade surplus likely widened to $42.6 billion from $39.7 billion. The data are due Friday. (liyan.qi@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0925 GMT - Investor demand for new sales of euro-denominated corporate bonds remains "robust" and should not discourage further issuance, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . All of Wednesday's sales showed bonds were oversubscribed by 3-4 times, with companies adding EUR2.4 billion of iBoxx EUR corporates index-eligible debt, says the bank. This brought the week's total to EUR10.9 billion and \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  expects activity in November to stay "lively". (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0923 GMT - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to refrain from direct \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month, thanks to easing trade tensions and widening \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 -U.S. interest-rate spreads, says Julian Evans-Pritchard, an economist with \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  . He estimates China's capital outflows dropped to $5 billion in October, down from $21 billion in September. The decline in outflows should give the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  less reason to worry about the impact of monetary easing on the balance of payments and exchange rates, he adds. (grace.zhu@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0919 GMT - A broadly weaker dollar due to reports that a U.S.-China trade deal could be delayed until December helps EUR/USD edge higher, but the euro's rise is limited following earlier weak German industrial production data. EUR/USD is last up 0.1% at 1.1081 but this is "mostly dollar driven," says \uc2 London Capital Group\uc2  's Ipek Ozkardeskaya, as the DXY dollar index falls 0.1% to 97.87. EUR/USD earlier hit a three-week low of 1.1055 and Ozkardeskaya says the bigger-than-expected fall in German industrial production may give traders a stronger incentive to sell. Industrial output fell 0.6% on the month in September, a bigger drop than the 0.3% decline forecast in a WSJ poll. \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says the German data confirm manufacturers "are still firmly in a recession," adding: "We expect this to drag on until well into next year." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0909 GMT - German GDP data due next week will show a slight contraction in 3Q, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's \uc2 Ralph Solveen\uc2  says. Industrial production data showed 3Q output down 1.1% compared with 2Q for the manufacturing sector and the producing sector, which includes construction and energy. "The figures support the expectation that real GDP shrank slightly in the third quarter as well, thus fulfilling a frequently used definition of a recession," says Solveen. German GDP contracted in 2Q and a technical recession is defined at two negative quarters consecutively. Manufacturing orders on Wednesday showing an increase in September briefly gave cause for optimism, but \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  nevertheless expects a slight drop in 4Q production. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0843 GMT - \uc2 Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees potential for further interest rate cuts but won't be rushed into reducing rates too fast, Governor \uc2 Elvira Nabiullina\uc2  says. Last month, the bank lowered its key rate for the fourth time since June, to 6.5% from 7%, highlighting the risk of a "substantial" slowdown in global economic growth. Ms. Nabiullina says on Thursday that further cuts are possible given declining inflation expectations. But, she adds: "we will not make a decision automatically, hastening to reduce the rate as much as possible." (georgi.kantchev@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0830 GMT - A correction in rates has on aggregate moved the yield the iBoxx EUR corporate index in all maturity categories out of negative-yield territory, says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  . With rates tentatively stabilizing, the increased yield levels "should therefore now impose less of a floor" on the spreads of higher-rated and shorter-dated maturities such as subordinated 5-year single As than in previous months, the bank says. Easing trade tensions, latest Brexit developments and better-than-expected macro economic data have pushed government bond yields higher. (lorena.ruibal@wsj.com; @lorena_rbal)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT - German industrial production data indicates that any optimism on the industry's outlook is premature, ING Germany Chief Economist \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2  says. Industrial output fell 0.6% in September from August in calendar-adjusted terms. Manufacturing orders data a day ago, which showed a 1.3% adjusted increase, provided a dash of optimism for the sector. "Hopes for a quick rebound of German industry were short-lived," Brzeski says. ING expects German industry to rebound in the first quarter of 2020. "So far, we only know that industry is in recession but not necessarily the entire economy," he says. For ING, the German economy's direction in 3Q will depend on the export sector. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0823 GMT Currently trading at JPY 109.03, the US Dollar is reversing up thanks to the upside breakout of a declining trend line in place since Nov. 5 top and the breakout of the 50-period moving average on a 30-minute chart at JPY 108.90. Moreover, the intraday Relative Strength Index (RSI, 14) has landed on it support at 30% and is turning up, pushing above the 50% area. As a consequence, further rebound is expected toward Nov. 5 top at JPY 109.25 and toward Fibonacci projection at JPY 109.45 in extension. Only a push below horizontal support at JPY 108.62 would invalidate this bullish view and call for a down move toward horizontal support at JPY 108.24. [This piece contains the opinions of Trading Central and does not constitute personalized investment advice or form part of any invitation or inducement to buy or sell any security. The author has been prohibited by Trading Central from purchasing or otherwise directly or indirectly acquiring any direct or indirect beneficial ownership of any instruments or markets for which Trading Central or its affiliates issues recommendations. To read more, visit bit.ly/1MehCU9.] (analysts-europe@tradingcentral.com)\par \par \uc2 0812 GMT - "The decline in industrial production in September confirms that German manufacturers are still firmly in a recession," says Andrew Kenningham, chief economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 . Industrial output fell 0.6% in September from August in calendar-adjusted terms, slightly below Capital Economics ' forecast. Excluding energy and construction, output fell more than 1.3%, suggesting the underlying situation is worse than the headline figures, Kenningham says. Industrial production fell around 1.0% in the third quarter, the fifth successive quarterly contraction. "Both the Manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  output index and the manufacturing component of the Ifo Business Climate Index edged up in October, but they both still point to production contracting," says Kenningham. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0803 GMT - Eurozone government-bond yields rise, shrugging off weaker-than-expected German industrial production data for September, which disappoint after Wednesday's better-than-forecast manufacturing-orders data for the same month. Industrial production fell by 0.6% in adjusted terms in September over August, the Federal Statistical Office said, while economists in The Wall Street Journal's survey forecast a 0.3% decline. Supply will come from Spain and France, with the former selling up to EUR4.25 billion in 2024-, 2029- and 2035-dated nominal and 2023-dated inflation-linked bonds, while the latter auctioning up to EUR10 billion in 2029-, 2034-, 2050- and 2066-dated OATs. The 10-year Bund yield is trading at -0.312%, up 1.4 basis points, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT - Sterling dips against the euro ahead of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's interest rate decision, policy meeting minutes and latest economic projections Thursday at 1200 GMT, followed by Governor \uc2 Mark Carney\uc2  's press conference at 1230 GMT. The \uc2 BoE\uc2  is expected to keep its key interest rate at 0.75% so the focus is on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  provides any clues on future policy action in its statements. "Any signs of the \uc2 BoE\uc2  indicating an easing policy will likely lead to a further selloff in the pound," \uc2 Hussein Sayed\uc2  at FXTM says. EUR/GBP rises 0.1% to 0.8620 while GBP/USD is broadly flat at 1.2850. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 07, 2019 04:30 ET (09:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020191107efb7001cl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
