\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-U.S.-China  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  deal won't change dollar-yuan dynamics -experts\b0\par\par\uc2 953 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Oktober 2019\par 13:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2019 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, Oct 11 (Reuters) - A U.S.-China \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  agreement being floated as a symbol of progress in this week's trade talks between the world's two largest economies would largely repeat past pledges by China, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts say, and will not change the dollar-yuan relationship that has been a thorn in the side of President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Such a deal would, however, provide the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  an opportunity to climb down from what \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts say was a misguided declaration in August that Beijing was a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator," reducing the yuan's value to gain "unfair competitive advantage in international trade."\par \par \uc2 Little is known about the structure of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deal that the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2  said American and Chinese negotiators were working toward on Thursday in their first high-level trade talks since July, but it is widely expected to include a promise from both to sides to refrain from devaluing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain a competitive trade advantage.\par \par \uc2 As members of the \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  major economies, both the United States and China agreed to such language starting in 2010.\par \par \uc2 At that time, China was widely seen to be deliberately holding down the value of its heavily managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the renminbi (RMB). But this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifted to mainly prop up the yuan's value after a sharp devaluation in 2015.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear how a pact with the United States might change Beijing's behavior.\par \par \uc2 "In essence, I don't see anything in a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deal that will cause a significant change in the present RMB/dollar \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market dynamics," said Mark Sobel, a longtime former Treasury and \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  official who is now U.S. chairman of the Official Monetary and Financial Institutions Forum, a London-based think tank.\par \par \uc2 Myron Brilliant, head of international affairs at the \uc2 U.S. Chamber of Commerce\uc2 , said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deal would likely be accompanied by the United States' canceling a planned tariff increase to 30% from 25% on $250 billion in Chinese imports.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. official confirmed that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely be discussed in the talks, but it was premature to say whether an announcement would be made on the issue this week. Optimism over the talks had improved after the first day's negotiations wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary \uc2 Steven Mnuchin\uc2 , a principal negotiator in the talks along with U.S. Trade Representative \uc2 Robert Lighthizer\uc2 , said as early as February that Chinese Vice Premier Liu He had largely agreed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  language. But talks on a broader deal to end the trade war between the United States and China broke down in May, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  provisions in limbo.\par \par \uc2 YUAN STABILITY\par \par \uc2 Mnuchin has long pushed his Chinese counterparts for increased transparency in yuan market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to maintain a stable yuan value against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In August, after the yuan fell below the psychologically important level of 7 to the dollar in response to a new round of U.S. tariffs, the Treasury Department declared China a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator for the first time in 25 years following Trump's own tweets that China was manipulating the yuan.\par \par \uc2 The designation under a 1988 law requires the Treasury to enter into negotiations with the offending country to correct the situation -- an effort that had been under way for more than two years.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  disagreed with the designation, arguing that the yuan's value was in line with China's trade-battered fundamentals. Beijing had actually been spending reserves to prop up the yuan's value, not push it down to make exports cheaper.\par \par \uc2 Eswar Prasad\uc2 , former head of the \uc2 IMF\uc2 's China department and now a trade professor at Cornell University, said it would be convenient time for both sides to agree on the need for maintaining a stable dollar/yuan value -- even though this goes against longstanding U.S. demands that China allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float freely.\par \par \uc2 If so, the United States would get an assurance that yuan devaluation would not undermine its tariffs, while China would reduce the risk of capital outflows from perceptions that it is trying to weaken the yuan, he said.\par \par \uc2 "This issue could give both sides cover to strike a mini deal that has little substance but de-escalates tensions at least temporarily, which could stabilize market sentiment and have some positive effects on both economies," Prasad said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  REPORT\par \par \uc2 An agreement could also allow the Treasury to declare that its manipulator designation on Beijing had been resolved. The Treasury will need to address the designation in its semi-annual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  report, due on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Mnuchin has said that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  provisions in the new U.S.-Mexico Canada Agreement represent a model for future trade deals, although these were not repeated in a newly signed limited trade deal with Japan.\par \par \uc2 The USMCA language closely tracks the G20 language. The North American trade deal, which still needs ratification by the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 , requires the three countries to disclose monthly data on international reserve balances and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, along with quarterly balance-of-payments data and other public reporting to the \uc2 IMF\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  publishes \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data and is more transparent about its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices, that's a positive," said Sobel of the Official Monetary and Financial Institutions Forum, although he noted that China already publishes reserves data, which show that it has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yuan from rising for years. (Reporting by David Lawder; additional reporting by \uc2 Jeff Mason\uc2  Editing by Leslie Adler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2019-10-11T12:00:00.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020191011efab01so0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
